# Companies Law — نظام الشركات

<!-- Written 2026-09-04 from the Bureau of Experts Arabic consolidated text (laws.boe.gov.sa), the Bureau of Experts official English translation (MISA-hosted copy) and the Ministry of Commerce Implementing Regulations 1444H/2023 (Arabic). Rows combining the Law and the Regulations carry both source tags. -->

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
| Instrument | Companies Law / نظام الشركات, together with its Implementing Regulations / اللائحة التنفيذية لنظام الشركات (Ministry of Commerce, 1444H/2023). Rows sourced from the Regulations are marked "Regs Art. N". |
| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/132 of 1/12/1443H (30 June 2022), approving Council of Ministers Resolution No. 678 of 29/11/1443H (both reproduced at the head of the portal text). Implementing Regulations: Ministerial Decision No. 284 of 23/6/1444H `[model knowledge — verify]` — the decision number and date do not appear on the face of the Regulations text read; the text is headed only "1444هـ / 2023م". |
| Issuing authority | Ministry of Commerce (وزارة التجارة) is "the competent authority" for all companies except joint-stock companies listed on the Exchange, for which it is the Capital Market Authority (Art. 1). Decree cl. 2 preserves the powers of the Saudi Central Bank and the CMA. |
| Effective date | 2023-01-19 (Art. 281: in force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette; the portal records publication on 23/12/1443H = 22 July 2022; day 180 after 22 July 2022 is 18 January 2023, so "after 180 days" gives 19 January 2023 = 26/6/1444H, the date the Ministry of Commerce announced `[model knowledge — verify]` the one-day counting convention). Regulations: Regs Art. 95 — apply from the date the Law enters into force. |
| Amendment history | none shown on the portal (see post-cutoff check). Decree cl. 3 and Council of Ministers Resolution 678 cl. 3: existing companies had two years from the effective date to conform (ended 19 January 2025); the Ministry and the CMA were to specify which provisions applied to them during the transition. |
| Official source (authoritative language) | https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/a8376aea-1bc3-49d4-9027-aed900b555af/1 (Arabic). Implementing Regulations: Ministry of Commerce website (mc.gov.sa), regulations page; the PDF was read from a locally saved copy. |
| Official translation | Bureau of Experts Official Translation Department, "Companies Law, Royal Decree No. M/132, June 30, 2022" (English; the same PDF also contains a French version). Read from the MISA-hosted copy; the portal page for the Arabic text carries a "Translated document" link to the same translation. No `boe-en` text file was available for this instrument. |
| Text last read from the official source | 2026-09-04 |
| Post-cutoff amendment check | 2026-09-04: portal page shows issue date 1443/12/01H (30/06/2022), publication date 1443/12/23H (22/07/2022), status "ساري" (in force), `<!-- articles marked as amended on the portal: 0 -->`; the only issuing instruments named are Royal Decree M/132 and Council of Ministers Resolution 678; no amending instrument is named anywhere in the 281 articles. The Bureau of Experts "recent updates" listing (saved page 2) contains a Companies Law entry; in that listing each entry is followed by its own issue/publication dates and the Companies Law entry is followed by 1443/12/01H / 1443/12/23H, while the 1447/03/12H issue date and 1447/03/27H publication date that precede it belong to the adjacent entry (the Expropriation Law). I therefore found no evidence of a post-2022 amendment; the portal's "الإصدارات" (versions) tab was not opened. Treat any 2025 amendment as unconfirmed and ask counsel to check the Umm al-Qura index. |

## Scope

The Companies Law governs every company incorporated in the Kingdom (Art. 3: a company incorporated under the Law is Saudi and must have its head office in the Kingdom) and, except for the incorporation provisions, foreign companies operating in the Kingdom through a branch or representative office (Arts. 235-241). It recognises five forms — general partnership, limited partnership, joint-stock company (JSC), simplified joint-stock company (SJSC) and limited liability company (LLC) (Art. 4) — plus non-profit companies (Part 7), professional companies (Part 8) and holding/subsidiary rules (Part 9), and it regulates transformation, merger and division (Part 10), dissolution and liquidation (Part 12) and criminal and administrative penalties (Part 13). It replaced the 2015 Companies Law (M/3 of 1437H) and the Professional Companies Law (M/17 of 1441H) (Art. 280). It does not cover listed-company market conduct (Capital Market Law and CMA regulations govern trading, disclosure and takeovers of listed JSCs; the CMA is the competent authority for them), foreign-investment licensing (Investment Law), commercial registration formalities (Commercial Register Law), insolvency (Bankruptcy Law) or beneficial-ownership reporting (Ministry rules under Art. 274(3)). The Implementing Regulations read for this file cover unlisted JSCs, LLCs, non-profit and professional companies and restructurings; the CMA issues separate regulations for listed JSCs.

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 1 | Definitions. "Regulations" are the regulations issued under the Law; "Ministry/Minister" = Commerce; "CMA" = Capital Market Authority; "competent authority" = the Ministry, except for JSCs listed on the Exchange, where it is the CMA. "Relatives" = parents and grandparents (ascending), children and their descendants, and spouses. "Day" = calendar day, whether or not a working day. | الجهة المختصة: الوزارة، إلا ما يتعلق بشركات المساهمة المدرجة في السوق المالية فتكون الهيئة. الأقارب: الآباء والأمهات والأجداد والجدات وإن علوا، الأولاد وأولادهم وإن نزلوا، الأزواج والزوجات. اليوم: اليوم التقويمي، سواء أكان يوم عمل أم لا. | Every time period in this file (21-day notice, 30-day pre-emption, 60/180-day losses windows) runs in calendar days including Fridays, Saturdays and Eid holidays. "Relatives" is the statutory perimeter for related-party and director-loan rules (Arts. 27, 71, 72) and is narrower than IFRS "close family": siblings are not relatives for this Law. Listed JSCs answer to the CMA, so a listed-company question must be routed to the CMA regulations, not to the Ministry's Regulations rows below. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 2, 4, 3, 5 | A company is a legal entity formed under articles of incorporation or articles of association by two or more persons who each contribute money or work to a profit-seeking venture; exceptionally a company may be formed by the unilateral will of one person, and non-profit companies may be formed under Part 7 (Art. 2). Permitted forms: general partnership, limited partnership, JSC, SJSC, LLC (Art. 4). A company incorporated under the Law is Saudi and its head office must be in the Kingdom (Art. 3). Trade name may be in Arabic or another language, derived from the object, distinctive, or the name of present or former partners (with their or their heirs' consent for former partners); the name must be accompanied by the company form; a name change follows the amendment procedure and does not affect existing rights, obligations or proceedings (Art. 5). | تتخذ الشركة التي تؤسس وفقًا لأحكام النظام أحد الأشكال الآتية: شركة التضامن، شركة التوصية البسيطة، شركة المساهمة، شركة المساهمة المبسطة، الشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة. تعد الشركة التي تؤسس وفقًا لأحكام النظام سعودية الجنسية، ويجب أن يكون مركزها الرئيس في المملكة. يجب أن يقترن بالاسم التجاري ما يبين شكل الشركة. | A one-person JSC, SJSC or LLC is lawful (see Arts. 98, 150, 157); there is no "one-person company" form of its own. A counterparty name that lacks the form indicator (e.g. "ش.م.م", "شركة مساهمة مبسطة") is a drafting defect worth checking against the Commercial Register extract. A name change is not a change of entity; contracts survive it without novation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 6 | A founder is anyone who actually participates in incorporation and contributes cash or in kind. Founders file the incorporation and registration application with the Commercial Register with the articles and required documents; the Register decides on a complete application; a refusal must be reasoned; founders may appeal to the Ministry within 60 days of notification, and to the competent judicial authority if the appeal is refused or not decided within 30 days. | يقدم المؤسسون طلب تأسيس الشركة وقيدها إلى السجل التجاري ... في حال رفض الطلب يجب أن يكون مسببًا، ويحق للمؤسسين التظلم أمام الوزارة خلال (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ إبلاغهم برفض الطلب. | Incorporation is a single Commercial Register filing (in practice through the Ministry's online platform `[model knowledge — verify]`); the statutory refusal-appeal route is Ministry then Commercial Court. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 7 | Every company has articles of incorporation (عقد تأسيس), except a JSC, an SJSC and a single-owner LLC, which each have articles of association (نظام أساس). The articles must contain the provisions required by the Law for the form; they must be in Arabic and may be accompanied by a translation; the Ministry issues model articles. | يكون لكل شركة تؤسس وفقًا لأحكام النظام عقد تأسيس، عدا شركة المساهمة وشركة المساهمة المبسطة والشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة المملوكة لشخص واحد، فيكون لكل منها نظام أساس. يجب أن يكون عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس باللغة العربية، ويجوز أن يكون مقرونًا بترجمة إلى لغة أخرى. | The Bureau of Experts translation renders عقد التأسيس as "articles of incorporation" and النظام الأساس as "articles of association"; this file follows that usage. The Arabic text is the governing constitutional document; an English "shareholders' agreement" that contradicts the Arabic articles is subordinate (Art. 11). A multi-member LLC has articles of incorporation, not a "memorandum and articles". | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
| Art. 8; Regs Art. 2 | Articles and every amendment must be in writing or are void; incorporation or amendment takes effect after the requirements in the Law and Regulations are met. Founders, partners, managers or directors (as applicable) must register the articles and every amendment with the Commercial Register, which publishes what the Law requires; those who cause non-registration are jointly liable for resulting damage. Registered data are available to third parties and are evidence against the company and third parties; unregistered articles or amendments cannot be relied on against third parties, and an unregistered particular alone is ineffective against them. Regs Art. 2: identity of founders/partners is verified through the National Information Center, the Commercial Register, or the notarisation authority in cases the Ministry sets with the Ministry of Justice; amendment of a JSC/SJSC's articles of association requires submitting the extraordinary general assembly (or shareholders') decision with the required documents to the Commercial Register. | يجب أن يكون عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس، وأي تعديل يطرأ عليه، مكتوبًا، وإلا كان ... باطلًا. لا يجوز الاحتجاج على الغير بعقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس أو بأي تعديل عليه إلا بعد القيد لدى السجل التجاري. (اللائحة م2) يكون التحقق من هوية المؤسسين أو الشركاء ... من خلال البيانات الموثقة لدى مركز المعلومات الوطني، أو من خلال السجل التجاري، أو من خلال الجهة المختصة بالتوثيق في الحالات التي تحددها الوزارة بالتنسيق مع وزارة العدل. | The Law itself does not require notarisation of the articles before a notary public; it requires writing and Commercial Register registration, with identity verification that may be electronic. In practice amendments and share transfers are executed through the Ministry's electronic platform and notarised electronically via the Ministry of Justice integration `[model knowledge — verify]`. For a closing checklist the operative test is "is the amended article registered on the Commercial Register?" — an executed but unregistered transfer or amendment binds the parties but not third parties. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 9, 10, 12 | Legal personality arises on registration with the Commercial Register; during incorporation the company has personality to the extent needed to incorporate, provided incorporation is completed. On registration all contracts and acts done by the founders for its account pass to the company, which bears the incorporation expenses. If incorporation is not completed, those who dealt in the company's name are personally and jointly liable for their acts (Art. 9). The company may carry on its objects after registration and after obtaining any required licences (Art. 10). Contracts, receipts and other documents issued by the company must state its name, form, head-office address, e-mail (if any) and Commercial Register number, and its capital and paid-up capital (not required for general and limited partnerships), and "under liquidation" during liquidation (Art. 12). | تكتسب الشركة الشخصية الاعتبارية بعد قيدها لدى السجل التجاري ... يجب أن يوضع على العقود والمخالصات وغيرها من الوثائق التي تصدرها الشركة البيانات الآتية: اسم الشركة وشكلها وعنوان مركزها الرئيس ... ورقم قيدها لدى السجل التجاري؛ رأس مال الشركة ومقدار المدفوع منه؛ عبارة (تحت التصفية). | Pre-registration contracts signed "for a company under formation" become the company's on registration automatically; if registration never happens the signatories are personally liable. Contract reviewers should check the counterparty block for the Art. 12 particulars; omission is a fineable violation (Art. 262(i)) but does not invalidate the contract. Licences (MISA, municipal, sectoral) are a separate gate from registration. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 11; Regs Art. 4 | Founders, partners or shareholders may, during or after incorporation, (a) conclude one or more agreements regulating their relations among themselves or with the company, including how their heirs enter the company, and (b) conclude a family charter (ميثاق عائلي) regulating family ownership, governance, management, work and family-employment policies, dividends, disposal of interests or shares, and dispute resolution. The agreement or charter is binding and may form part of the articles; it must not contradict the Law or the articles. Regs Art. 4: where the agreement or charter forms part of the articles, its amendment quorum is that for amending the articles of the relevant company form. | يكون الاتفاق أو الميثاق العائلي ملزمًا، ويجوز أن يكون جزءًا من عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس. ويشترط ألا يخالف النظام أو عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس. (اللائحة م4) يكون نصاب تعديل اتفاق الشركاء أو المساهمين أو الميثاق العائلي إذا كان جزءًا من عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس وفقًا للأوضاع المقررة لتعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس. | This is the statutory basis for shareholders' agreements: they bind the signatories but yield to the Law and to the registered articles. In diligence, compare the SHA's transfer, drag/tag, reserved-matter and dispute provisions against the registered Arabic articles; a conflict is resolved in favour of the articles, so key protections should be mirrored in the articles (Arts. 113, 151-153, 181 provide the statutory hooks). Whether a Saudi court will enforce an SHA governed by foreign law or providing for foreign-seated arbitration is a practice question `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 13-15 | Contributions may be cash, in kind or both; except in JSCs and SJSCs a partner may contribute work in return for a share of profits fixed in the articles, but never reputation or influence; only cash and in-kind contributions form the capital; interests or shares may be issued to a person for work or services benefiting the company (Art. 13). Warranty rules for in-kind contributions follow sale (ownership/real rights) or lease (personal right of use) unless agreed otherwise; a work contributor owes his work and its earnings, but not IP rights unless agreed (Art. 14). Every partner is a debtor for the contribution promised; on delay the company may demand performance or suspend the rights attached to the interests (dividends, voting), without prejudice to damages (Art. 15). | تكوِّن الحصص النقدية والحصص العينية وحدها رأس مال الشركة. إذا تأخر الشريك عن تقديم حصته في رأس مال الشركة، في الأجل المحدد لذلك، كان للشركة مطالبته بتنفيذ ما تعهد به تجاهها، أو تعليق نفاذ الحقوق المتصلة بحصصه كالحق في الحصول على أرباح أو حق التصويت. | "Sweat equity" is permitted in LLCs and partnerships as a profit share, not as capital; in JSC/SJSC it must be structured as shares for services under Art. 13(4). Unpaid capital is a diligence item: check the Art. 158 declaration that interests are paid and any suspension of voting rights that could affect quorum in a partners' resolution. IP created by a work-contributing partner stays with the partner absent an express assignment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 16-17; Regs Art. 5 | Fiscal year is 12 months fixed in the articles; the first fiscal year may be 6 to 18 months from registration (Art. 16). The company must keep accounting records and supporting documents at its head office or a place fixed by the manager/board; financial statements must be prepared at the end of each fiscal year under the accounting standards approved in the Kingdom and deposited, as the Regulations specify, within six months of the fiscal year end; a controlling or holding company may require information from its subsidiary/investee for its own statements (Art. 17). Regs Art. 5: the manager or chairman deposits the financial statements and the auditor's report (if any) with the Saudi Business Center through the electronic financial-statements deposit programme. | يجب إعداد قوائم مالية للشركة في نهاية كل سنة مالية وفق المعايير المحاسبية المعتمدة في المملكة، وإيداع هذه القوائم وفقًا لما تحدده اللوائح خلال (ستة) أشهر من تاريخ انتهاء السنة المالية. (اللائحة م5) على مدير الشركة أو رئيس مجلس إدارتها إيداع القوائم المالية للشركة، وتقرير مراجع الحسابات -إن وجد- لدى المركز السعودي للأعمال الاقتصادية من خلال برنامج الإيداع الإلكتروني للقوائم المالية. | Annual compliance item: audited (where required) financial statements filed electronically within six months of year end — the platform is known as "Qawaem" `[model knowledge — verify]`. Failure to keep records or to prepare/deposit statements is a fineable violation (Art. 262(e)). For the calendar cross-reference see `filing-calendar.md`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 18-20; Regs Arts. 6-9 | Every company has one or more licensed auditors appointed (with fees, term and scope) by the partners, general assembly or shareholders; the Regulations cap the term. The appointing body may remove the auditor (subject to damages if unjustified) and the manager/chairman must notify the competent authority of the removal and its reasons within 5 days; an auditor may resign by written notice, must state reasons to the company and the competent authority, and the manager/board must convene the partners/assembly to consider the reasons and appoint a replacement (Art. 18). Micro and small companies are exempt from mandatory appointment unless their articles require it, they are listed, issue tradeable debt/sukuk, preferred or redeemable shares, are required by other laws, are foreign, or are a parent/subsidiary (unless the whole group is micro/small); the exemption requires the status in the first fiscal year or two consecutive years; holders of 10% of voting interests may demand an auditor; general partnerships are exempt except in stated cases (Art. 19). The auditor must be independent, may not be a founder, director, manager, partner, employee or relative, may not trade the company's shares during the audit, may not do technical, administrative or consulting work except as the Regulations allow, has access to all records at any time, must report obstruction and may ask the manager/board to convene the partners/assembly and may convene it himself if they fail to do so within 30 days; he reports on the financial statements, management's cooperation and violations found, reads the report or a summary at the annual assembly (or presents it by circulation), must keep secrets, and is liable (jointly if several) for errors (Art. 20). Regs Art. 6: individual auditor max 10 consecutive fiscal years; audit firm max 10, extendable to 20 with audit-committee recommendation or competing bids, engagement partner max 10; a 2-year cooling-off. Regs Art. 7: micro/small = any two of annual revenue ≤ SAR 10 million, assets ≤ SAR 10 million, employees ≤ 49; a statement of exemption accompanies the deposited financial statements. Regs Art. 8: the 10% demand is made in writing to the manager/board at least 30 days before fiscal year end, and the manager/board must then convene a vote. | يكون للشركة مراجع حسابات (أو أكثر) من المراجعين المرخص لهم في المملكة يعينه ويحدد أتعابه ومدة عمله ونطاقه الشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمون بحسب الأحوال. لا يسري على الشركة متناهية الصغر والصغيرة الحكم المتعلق بإلزامية تعيين مراجع الحسابات. (اللائحة م7) تعد الشركة متناهية الصغر أو صغيرة ... عند تحقق اثنين من الآتي: ألا يتجاوز مجموع إيراداتها السنوية مبلغ (عشرة) ملايين ريال سعودي؛ ألا تتجاوز أصولها مبلغ (عشرة) ملايين ريال سعودي؛ ألا يتجاوز عدد الموظفين بها (تسعة وأربعين) موظفًا. | Diligence: confirm the auditor's appointment resolution, term against the rotation caps, and the 5-day removal notification if the auditor changed. A subsidiary of a foreign group is not micro/small-exempt unless the whole group qualifies, so most foreign-owned LLCs need an audit. The auditor's power to convene the assembly is a safety valve where management stalls the annual meeting. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 22-24; Regs Art. 10 | Annual or interim dividends may be distributed out of distributable profits in JSCs, SJSCs and LLCs; if distributed otherwise, creditors may claim against the company and the company may reclaim from every partner or shareholder, even in good faith; lawfully distributed dividends need not be returned if later losses occur (Art. 22). Partners share profits and losses in proportion to capital; a clause excluding a partner from profit or exempting him from loss is void, but the articles may set unequal ratios; a work-only partner may be exempted from loss if unpaid (Art. 23-24). Regs Art. 10: interim dividends require an enabling provision in the articles, an annual delegation to the manager/board, reasonable liquidity, and distributable profits per the latest financial statements; distributable profits = retained earnings in the last statement of financial position plus distributable reserves (profit-based reserves not earmarked or whose purpose was cancelled). | يجوز توزيع أرباح سنوية أو مرحلية من الأرباح القابلة للتوزيع ... إذا وزعت أرباح ... بالمخالفة لحكم الفقرة (1) من هذه المادة، جاز لدائني الشركة مطالبتها، وللشركة مطالبة كل شريك أو مساهم -ولو كان حسن النية- برد ما قبضه منها. فإن اتفق على حرمان أي منهم من الربح أو على إعفائه من الخسارة، عُدَّ هذا الاتفاق كأن لم يكن. | Unlawful dividends are recoverable from good-faith recipients, so a buyer should test historic distributions against retained earnings. Preferred-return or waterfall structures must be drafted as unequal ratios or share classes, never as a total exclusion from profit or loss. The share-premium account cannot be paid out as cash dividends (Regs Art. 33). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 25 | Ownership of interests in general partnerships, limited partnerships and LLCs passes by registration with the Commercial Register and is not effective against the company or third parties before that date. Shares in unlisted JSCs and SJSCs are transferred by entry in the shareholders' register under Art. 112 with the same effect; listed shares transfer under the Capital Market Law. | تنتقل ملكية الحصص في شركة التضامن وشركة التوصية البسيطة والشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة بالقيد لدى السجل التجاري، ولا يُعتد بنقل ملكية الحصة في مواجهة الشركة أو الغير إلا من تاريخ هذا القيد. تتداول أسهم شركة المساهمة غير المدرجة في السوق المالية وشركة المساهمة المبسطة بالقيد في سجل المساهمين. | The closing deliverable for an LLC share sale is the updated Commercial Register (amended articles showing the buyer), not the signed transfer; for an unlisted JSC/SJSC it is the updated shareholders' register (and the Art. 112 data update to the Register within 15 days). Escrow and completion mechanics should be built around the registration event. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 26; Regs Art. 11 | Managers and directors owe duties of care and loyalty, in particular: act within their powers; work for the company's interest and success; decide and vote independently; exercise reasonable care, attention, diligence and skill; avoid conflicts; disclose any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings; accept no benefit from third parties in connection with their role. Regs Art. 11 elaborates: exercise powers for their proper purpose, act in good faith to maximise value for partners/shareholders, act objectively, perform with the skill of a prudent person and the knowledge expected of the office, avoid and disclose conflicts, and not exploit the position for benefits from third parties. | يجب على مدير الشركة، أو عضو مجلس إدارتها، الالتزام بواجبات العناية والولاء، وبوجه خاص: ممارسة مهماته في حدود الصلاحيات المقررة له؛ العمل على مصلحة الشركة، وتعزيز نجاحها؛ اتخاذ القرارات أو التصويت عليها باستقلال؛ بذل العناية والاهتمام والحرص والمهارة المعقولة والمتوقعة؛ تجنب حالات تعارض المصالح؛ الإفصاح عن أي مصلحة له مباشرة أو غير مباشرة؛ عدم قبول أي منفعة ممنوحة له من الغير. | Codified fiduciary duties apply equally to LLC managers and JSC directors, and to nominee directors of a foreign parent. Board minutes should evidence the decision process (information received, independence, conflicts declared) because Art. 31 protects good-faith decisions only where those elements are present. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 27; Regs Arts. 12-13, 16-20 | A manager or director may not have a direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts, nor compete with the company in any branch of its activity, except with authorisation from the partners, general assembly or shareholders or their delegate; he may not exploit the company's assets, information or investment opportunities offered to him or the company. Exceptions to the interest bar: public tenders; personal-needs dealings on the company's standard terms within its ordinary activity; other cases set by the Regulations. Breach of the interest bar: the company may seek annulment and disgorgement; breach of the competition bar: damages (Art. 27). Regs Art. 12: an opportunity is off-limits if within the company's usual activities or if the company wishes or is expected to benefit from it. Regs Art. 13: a general-partnership manager needs authorisation from all partners for self-dealing (case by case) and for competing activity (renewed annually). Regs Art. 16: the general assembly may delegate the authorisation of related-party dealings to the board only where the dealing (or aggregate for the year) is below 1% of revenue per the latest financial statements and below SAR 10 million, is in the ordinary course and on non-preferential terms; delegation lasts at most one year or to the end of the board term; directors may not vote on the delegation. Regs Art. 17: indirect interest includes dealings benefiting the director's relatives, partnerships/LLCs in which he or a relative is a partner, JSCs/SJSCs in which he or relatives hold 5% or more, non-company establishments owned or managed by him or relatives, and companies where he or a relative is a manager, director or senior executive. Regs Arts. 18-19: competition authorisations may be delegated to the board with criteria, for at most one year; the director must notify the board (recorded in the minutes), abstain, and the licence is renewed annually. Regs Art. 20: if authorisation is refused the director must resign within the period set or his membership ends, unless he abandons the dealing or competition. | لا يجوز لمدير الشركة، ولا لعضو مجلس إدارتها، أن تكون له أي مصلحة مباشرة أو غير مباشرة في الأعمال والعقود التي تتم لحساب الشركة، إلا بترخيص من الشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمين أو من يفوضونه. (اللائحة م16) أن يكون إجمالي مبلغ العمل أو العقد -أو مجموع الأعمال والعقود خلال السنة المالية- أقل من (واحد في المائة) من إيرادات الشركة وفقًا لآخر قوائم مالية، وأن يكون أقل من (عشرة) ملايين ريال سعودي. | Related-party approval is a shareholder-level matter by default; board-level approval is valid only under a delegation meeting the Regs Art. 16 thresholds. Diligence should list every contract with a director, manager, their relatives or their other companies and match each to a partners'/assembly authorisation; unauthorised contracts are voidable at the company's election with disgorgement. Non-compete undertakings by directors are statutory, not just contractual. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 28 | Managers and directors are jointly liable to the company, partners/shareholders and third parties for damage caused by breach of the Law or the articles or by their errors, negligence or default; any contrary clause is void. Liability is personal to the individual, or shared by all where the decision was unanimous; where taken by majority, dissenters who expressly recorded their objection in the minutes are not liable; absence is no defence unless the absentee proves he did not know of the decision or could not object once he knew. The company may insure its managers/directors against liability arising from their capacity. | يكون المدير وأعضاء مجلس الإدارة مسؤولين بالتضامن عن تعويض الشركة أو الشركاء أو المساهمين أو الغير ... وكل شرط يقضي بغير ذلك يعد كأن لم يكن. وإذا صدر القرار بأغلبية الآراء فلا يسأل المديرون أو الأعضاء المعارضون متى أثبتوا اعتراضهم صراحة في محضر الاجتماع. | Minutes must record named dissents to give directors the statutory safe harbour; "the resolution was passed by majority" without names is not enough. Contractual exculpation of managers is void, but D&O insurance is expressly permitted. Absent directors should send a written objection promptly on learning of a decision. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 29-32 | The company may sue managers/directors for breach of the Law or the articles or for errors, negligence or default; the partners/assembly/shareholders decide to sue and appoint the representative (the liquidator during liquidation; the legal representative in Bankruptcy Law proceedings). Partners/shareholders holding 5% of capital (or less if the articles so provide) may bring the company's action if the company does not, provided the action serves the company, has a proper basis, is in good faith and the claimant holds at the time of filing; they must notify the manager/board at least 14 days before filing; a partner may also sue personally for special damage (Art. 29). A discharge (إبراء ذمة) voted by the partners/assembly does not bar these actions; except for forgery and fraud, the action is time-barred five years from the end of the fiscal year in which the harmful act occurred or three years from the end of the manager's/director's tenure, whichever is later (Art. 30). Business-judgement rule: a manager/director who decided or voted in good faith is deemed to have performed his duty if he had no interest in the matter, was informed to the extent appropriate in the circumstances, and rationally and firmly believed the decision served the company; the claimant bears the burden of proving otherwise (Art. 31). The court may order the company to bear a good-faith claimant's costs whatever the outcome (Art. 32). | يجوز لشريك أو مساهم أو أكثر يمثلون (خمسة في المائة) من رأس مال الشركة ... رفع دعوى المسؤولية المقررة للشركة في حال عدم قيام الشركة برفعها. لا تحول موافقة الشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمين -بحسب الأحوال- على إبراء ذمة المدير أو أعضاء مجلس الإدارة دون إقامة الدعاوى. لا تسمع دعوى المسؤولية بعد مضي (خمس) سنوات ... أو (ثلاث) سنوات من انتهاء عمل المدير ... أيهما أبعد. | The customary annual "discharge of the board" resolution has no protective effect; it does not stop a derivative claim. Post-closing claims against outgoing managers remain open for at least three years after they leave. A 5% minority has statutory derivative standing that cannot be raised by the articles, only lowered. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 33-34 | A partner's or shareholder's personal creditor may ask the court to be paid from the debtor's share of distributed net profits and, on dissolution, from his share of the surplus (Art. 33). Subject to the Movable Assets Security Law, the creditor may also ask the court to sell enough of the debtor's interests (other partners have a statutory right to redeem them) or shares; in an unlisted JSC or SJSC the other shareholders have priority to buy within 15 days of the offer if the articles so provide (Art. 34). | للدائن الشخصي للشريك أو المساهم ... أن يطلب من الجهة القضائية المختصة ... بيع ما يلزم من حصص ذلك الشريك ليتقاضى حقه من حصيلة بيعها، ويكون لباقي الشركاء الحق في استرداد تلك الحصص وفقًا لأحكام النظام. | Enforcement against LLC interests triggers the Art. 178 redemption mechanics for the other partners; a JSC/SJSC pre-emption on forced sale exists only if written into the articles. Lenders taking security over interests should read this with the pledge rules (Art. 180; Regs Arts. 68-69). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 35-38, 42-46, 50 | General partnership (شركة التضامن): two or more natural or legal persons personally and jointly liable with all their assets for the company's debts; each partner is a merchant (Art. 35). Managed by the partners or by one or more managers (partners or not) appointed in the articles or a separate contract; multiple managers may each act alone unless otherwise provided, with objections resolved by majority of managers, then by the partners; the manager conducts all business within the objects and represents the company, and the company is bound by his acts within its objects unless the counterparty acted in bad faith (Art. 37). Partners' decisions pass by numerical majority; amendment of the articles requires unanimity unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 38). Removal of a partner-manager named in the articles requires unanimity of the other partners; other managers by numerical majority; court removal always possible; removal does not dissolve the company unless the articles say so (Art. 42); a manager may resign on 60 days' written notice (Art. 43). Interests cannot be represented by negotiable instruments; assignment requires compliance with the articles' restrictions or the consent of the other partners, is void otherwise, and must be registered and published (Art. 44). An incoming partner is liable for prior debts unless exempted by unanimous agreement registered with the Register; a withdrawing or expelled partner remains liable for debts before registration of his exit; an assignor is released unless creditors object within 30 days of notice (Art. 45). A partner may withdraw on 60 days' notice unless the articles provide otherwise; expulsion follows the articles or a court order on application of a numerical majority; the court may dissolve where continuation is impossible (Art. 46). Death, incapacity, insolvency, expulsion or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the partnership unless the articles so provide; if only one partner remains he has 90 days to admit another partner or transform, failing which the company is dissolved by operation of law (Art. 50). | شركة التضامن: هي شركة يؤسسها شخصان أو أكثر ... يكونون فيها مسؤولين شخصيًّا في جميع أموالهم وبالتضامن عن ديون الشركة والتزاماتها، ويكتسب الشريك فيها صفة التاجر. تصدر قرارات الشركاء بالأغلبية العددية، إلا إذا كان القرار متعلقًا بتعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة فيجب أن يصدر بإجماع الشركاء، ما لم ينصّ في عقد التأسيس على غير ذلك. | Rarely used by foreign investors because of unlimited liability; where a target group contains one, diligence should confirm that all partners consented to past assignments and that creditor-objection notices were given. A corporate partner in a general partnership exposes its whole balance sheet. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 51, 53, 55-56 | Limited partnership (شركة التوصية البسيطة): at least one general partner with unlimited joint liability and at least one limited partner liable only to the extent of his contribution and who is not a merchant; general-partnership rules apply to general partners and, absent special provision, to the company (Art. 51). A limited partner may inspect twice a year but may not take part in external management, even under a power of attorney, on pain of unlimited liability for the resulting obligations; internal management participation is allowed if the articles so provide, unless it leads third parties to believe he is a general partner (Art. 53). Unless the articles provide otherwise, amendments require unanimity of general partners plus the majority in capital of limited partners; other decisions by numerical majority of general partners; a limited partner may not seek dissolution or vote on appointment/removal of the manager (Art. 55). A limited partner may assign to other partners freely; to third parties only with consent of all general partners and the majority in capital of limited partners, unless the articles provide otherwise; a general partner assigns under the same rule; new partners are admitted with the consent of all general partners (Art. 56). | لا يجوز للشريك الموصي التدخل في أعمال الإدارة الخارجية ولو صدر له توكيل، فإن تدخل كان مسؤولًا شخصيًّا في جميع أمواله وبالتضامن عن ديون الشركة والتزاماتها التي ترتبت على ما أجراه من أعمال. | A fund-style structure using a limited partnership must keep limited partners out of external management; signing authority given to a limited partner converts him into an unlimited guarantor for those acts. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 58-60 | JSC (شركة المساهمة): formed by one or more persons, capital divided into tradeable shares, the company alone liable for its debts, shareholders liable only to pay up their shares (Art. 58). Issued capital must be at least SAR 500,000 and at least one quarter must be paid at incorporation (Art. 59). The company has issued capital (subscribed shares) and may have authorised capital fixed in the articles; the board may increase issued capital within the authorised capital by board resolution provided the issued capital is fully paid (Art. 60). | يجب ألا يقلّ رأس مال شركة المساهمة المصدر عن (خمسمائة ألف) ريال، ويجب ألا يقلّ المدفوع منه عند التأسيس عن (الربع). يجوز بقرار من مجلس إدارة الشركة زيادة رأس المال المصدر في حدود رأس المال المصرح به، على أن يكون رأس المال المصدر قد دُفع بالكامل. | The old distinction between a regular JSC and one formed with public subscription is gone; SAR 500,000 with 25% paid is the floor for any JSC. Authorised-capital headroom lets the board issue shares (e.g. for ESOPs or conversions) without an extraordinary assembly, so diligence should check the authorised figure and any board issuances since the last registered capital. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 61-66; Regs Art. 3 | The JSC articles of association must state at least: name, head office, objects, authorised (if any), issued and paid-up capital, number, types and classes of shares with nominal value and rights, term (if any), management and number of directors, fiscal year, and other agreed terms; the application must attach founders' names/addresses/nationalities, a statement of incorporation expenses, the founders' declaration of subscription to all issued shares and amounts paid, a bank certificate of deposit of the paid amount with a licensed Saudi bank, the founders' decision appointing the first board (names, nationalities, addresses, dates of birth) and, where required, the first auditor, a compliance declaration, and an accredited valuer's report on any in-kind contributions with the other founders' acceptance (Art. 61). Shares not taken by founders must be offered under the Capital Market Law (Art. 62); the paid amount is deposited in the name of the company under incorporation and may be used only by the board after registration; if registration fails subscribers recover their money and founders are jointly liable (Art. 64). Once registered the company is validly incorporated and no nullity action lies for breach of the Law or the articles (Art. 65). In-kind contributions at incorporation or capital increase must be valued by one or more accredited valuers; the report is put to the founders or the extraordinary assembly; contributors may not vote on it; a reduction of the agreed consideration needs the contributors' consent; the Regulations cap the time between report and issue (Art. 66) — Regs Art. 3: six months for JSC, SJSC and LLC. | يجب أن يرفق بالنظام الأساس عند تقديم طلب تأسيس الشركة الآتي: ... شهادة إيداع القدر المدفوع من رأس المال المصدر لدى أحد البنوك المرخص لها في المملكة ... تقرير مُعد من مقيّم معتمد أو أكثر يُبين فيه القيمة العادلة للحصص العينية إن وجدت. تعد الشركة مؤسسة تأسيسًا صحيحًا بعد قيدها لدى السجل التجاري، ولا تسمع بعد ذلك الدعوى ببطلان الشركة. (اللائحة م3) يجب ألا تتجاوز المدة من إصدار تقرير المقيّم المعتمد ... إلى إصدار الحصص أو الأسهم المقابلة لها، (ستة) أشهر. | A valuation report older than six months at the date of issue is a defect in any capital increase by contribution in kind; check dates in the capital-increase file. Art. 65 means historic incorporation defects in a JSC cannot be used to unwind it. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 67-70; Regs Arts. 14-15, 22 | A JSC is managed by a board of at least three members; any shareholder may nominate himself or others (Art. 67). Directors are elected by the ordinary general assembly, must be natural persons; the Regulations fix the voting method; the articles may set a board-formation method within Regulation limits; the articles fix the term, not exceeding four years, with re-election unless the articles provide otherwise; the ordinary assembly may remove all or some directors even if the articles provide otherwise, and must then elect replacements (Art. 68). The board must call the ordinary assembly in good time before its term ends; if no election takes place the outgoing board continues for the period set by the Regulations; a director resigns by written notice to the chairman (the chairman to the other members and the secretary), effective on the date stated; casual vacancies may be filled by the board on a temporary basis, notified to the Commercial Register (and the CMA if listed) within 15 days and put to the next ordinary assembly, the appointee completing his predecessor's term; if numbers fall below the quorum minimum the remaining members must call the assembly within 60 days; failing an election any interested person may ask the court to appoint supervisors to convene an assembly within 90 days or to dissolve the company (Art. 69). The assembly, on the board's recommendation, may end the membership of a director absent from three consecutive or five separate meetings without an excuse accepted by the board (Art. 70). Regs Art. 14: election is by cumulative voting unless the articles provide for ordinary voting; the articles may give a shareholder above a stated threshold the right to reserve board seats, in which case he does not vote for the other seats. Regs Art. 15: an expired board continues at most 90 days; a resigning whole board continues at most 120 days. Regs Art. 22: a director removed for absence forfeits remuneration after his last meeting attended and must repay amounts received for that period; remuneration based on incorrect or misleading information must be repaid. | يدير شركة المساهمة مجلس إدارة على ألا يقل عدد أعضائه عن (ثلاثة). يحدد نظام الشركة الأساس مدة عضوية مجلس الإدارة، على ألا تتجاوز (أربع) سنوات. يجوز للجمعية العامة العادية عزل جميع أعضاء مجلس الإدارة أو بعضهم ولو نص نظام الشركة الأساس على غير ذلك. (اللائحة م14) يكون انتخاب أعضاء مجلس إدارة الشركة من قبل الجمعية العامة العادية بالتصويت التراكمي. ومع ذلك، يجوز انتخابهم بالتصويت العادي إذا نص نظام الشركة الأساس على ذلك. | The Law fixes only the minimum of three directors; the text read contains no statutory maximum (the eleven-member cap familiar from the 2015 Law is not in Art. 67). A maximum of eleven for listed companies comes from the CMA Corporate Governance Regulations `[model knowledge — verify]`. Corporate directors are not permitted: a shareholder company must nominate individuals. Cumulative voting is the default for unlisted JSCs, which protects minorities; a shareholders' agreement promising board seats should be paired with an articles provision under Regs Art. 14(2). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 71-72 | A director must inform the board immediately of any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts; the disclosure is recorded in the minutes; he may not vote on the matter in the board or the assemblies; the board reports such dealings to the assembly with a special auditor's report; failure to disclose entitles the company or any interested person to seek annulment or disgorgement; liability for damage falls on the interested director and on directors who were negligent or where the dealing was unfair; dissenters recorded in the minutes are exempt (Art. 71). A JSC may not lend to a director in any form or guarantee or secure his loans with third parties, nor to his relatives; such contracts are void and the company may claim damages; exceptions for banks and finance companies in the ordinary course, and for employee incentive programmes approved under the articles or by the assembly; the competent authority may restrict loans or guarantees to shareholders (Art. 72). | يجب على عضو مجلس الإدارة فور علمه بأي مصلحة له سواء مباشرة أو غير مباشرة في الأعمال والعقود التي تكون لحساب الشركة، أن يبلغ المجلس بذلك، ويثبت هذا الإبلاغ في محضر اجتماع المجلس عند اجتماعه. ولا يجوز لهذا العضو الاشتراك في التصويت. لا يجوز لشركة المساهمة أن تقدم قرضًا من أي نوع إلى أيّ من أعضاء مجلس إدارتها ... ويعد باطلًا كل عقد يتم بالمخالفة لذلك. | Board-minutes practice: a standing "declarations of interest" item, recorded by name and matter, with the interested director abstaining; the annual assembly agenda needs a related-party item supported by the auditor's special report. Intra-group loans to a director-shareholder's own company are caught if the director has an indirect interest (Regs Art. 17); loans to directors are void, not merely voidable. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 73-79 | Shareholders supervise the board through the assembly and may not interfere in board or executive management unless they sit on the board or work in management (Art. 73). The board may borrow for any term, sell or mortgage the company's assets or business, and release debtors, unless the articles or the assembly restrict it (Art. 74); sale of assets exceeding 50% of total assets, in one or several transactions within the preceding 12 months, needs assembly approval, the approval attaching to the transaction that crosses the threshold (Art. 75). The board has the widest powers within the objects except matters reserved to the assembly by the Law or the articles, may delegate specific acts to members or third parties, and the company is bound by the board's acts even outside its powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew the act was outside them (Art. 77). At its first meeting the board appoints a chairman from among its members and may appoint a managing director or a CEO from among them; the articles allocate their powers, failing which the board does; a listed JSC must appoint a vice-chairman; the board appoints a CEO and a secretary (members or not) and sets their powers and pay if the articles are silent; the board may relieve any of them of office without ending board membership (Art. 78). The chairman represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties; the articles may give representation to the managing director or CEO; any of them may delegate representation; the chairman may delegate some powers in writing to other members or third parties unless the articles provide otherwise; the vice-chairman deputises (Art. 79). | يكون لمجلس الإدارة أوسع السلطات في إدارة الشركة بما يحقق أغراضها ... تلتزم الشركة بجميع الأعمال والتصرفات التي يجريها مجلس الإدارة باسمها ولو كانت خارج اختصاصه، إلا إذا كان من تعامل معه سيء النية أو كان يعلم أن تلك الأعمال خارج اختصاصات المجلس. يشترط حصول مجلس الإدارة على موافقة الجمعية العامة عند بيع أصول للشركة تتجاوز قيمتها (خمسين في المائة) من قيمة مجموع أصولها. | The Art. 78 text read contains no bar on one person being both chairman and CEO or managing director; the separation requirement for listed companies comes from the CMA Corporate Governance Regulations, not from this Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. Authority checks on a JSC counterparty: chairman signs by default; a CEO or MD signs only if the articles or a written chairman's delegation say so. An asset sale crossing the 50%-of-assets line in a rolling 12 months needs an assembly resolution — a change-of-control/major-transaction flag for diligence and closing checklists. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 76; Regs Art. 21 | The articles set the method of directors' remuneration (fixed sum, attendance allowance, benefits in kind, a percentage of net profits, or a combination) and may cap it; the ordinary assembly fixes the amount, which must be fair, motivating and proportionate to performance; the board's annual report must itemise everything each director received (remuneration, allowances, expenses, executive or consulting pay) and attendance at each meeting. Regs Art. 21: remuneration must be proportionate to duties, activity, company size and experience, may differ between members, must not for independent directors be a percentage of profits or otherwise tied to profitability, and the policy and amounts must be disclosed in the annual report. | يبين نظام الشركة الأساس طريقة مكافأة أعضاء مجلس الإدارة ... وتحدد الجمعية العامة العادية مقدار تلك المكافآت. (اللائحة م21) يجب ألا تكون مكافآت أعضاء مجلس الإدارة المستقلين نسبة من الأرباح التي تحققها الشركة. | Board remuneration is an assembly reserved matter; a management agreement fixing a director's fee without an assembly resolution is a compliance gap. The Law no longer contains a fixed monetary cap per director; caps for listed companies are a CMA matter `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 80-81 | The JSC board meets at least four times a year on the chairman's call under the articles (the competent authority may vary the number); the chairman must call a meeting when any member so requests in writing. Quorum is at least half the members present in person or by proxy, unless the articles require more. Resolutions pass by majority of those present in person or by proxy, the chair of the meeting having a casting vote unless the articles provide otherwise. The board fixes the venue and may meet by modern technology (Art. 80). A director may not appoint a proxy to attend or vote, except that the articles may allow him to appoint another director, who may hold only one proxy; a board resolution takes effect on issue unless it specifies another time or conditions (Art. 81). | لا يكون اجتماع مجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة صحيحًا إلا إذا حضره نصف الأعضاء (أصالة أو نيابة) على الأقل، ما لم ينص نظام الشركة الأساس على نسبة أكبر. تصدر قرارات مجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة بأغلبية أصوات الأعضاء الحاضرين (أصالة أو نيابة) على الأقل، وعند تساوي الأصوات يرجح الجانب الذي صوت معه رئيس الاجتماع. | Board minutes should record: the call and its date, members present/represented (with proxies under an articles provision), quorum against the articles' threshold, each resolution with votes for/against/abstaining and named dissents (Art. 28). Video meetings are valid without special articles wording. Fewer than four meetings a year is a violation for an unlisted JSC unless the Ministry has varied the number. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 82 | The board may pass resolutions on urgent matters by circulation to all members, unless a member requests in writing that the board meet to deliberate. Such resolutions pass with the approval of a majority of the board's members (not merely of those responding), unless the articles require a higher proportion or number, and must be tabled at the next board meeting for recording in that meeting's minutes. | لمجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة أن يصدر قراراته في الأمور العاجلة بعرضها على جميع الأعضاء بالتمرير، ما لم يطلب أحد الأعضاء -كتابة- اجتماع المجلس للمداولة فيها. وتصدر تلك القرارات بموافقة أغلبية أصوات أعضائه ... وتعرض هذه القرارات على المجلس في أول اجتماع تالٍ له لإثباتها في محضر ذلك الاجتماع. | A written board consent must be circulated to every director, state the urgency, carry approvals from a majority of all directors, and be ratified in the next meeting's minutes; any single director can force a meeting instead. The text limits circulation to "urgent matters", so routine business by written consent is open to challenge. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 83 | Board deliberations and resolutions are recorded in minutes prepared by the secretary and signed by the chair of the meeting, the directors present and the secretary; minutes are entered in a special register signed by the chairman and the secretary; modern technology may be used for signature, recording of deliberations and keeping the minutes. | تُثبت مداولات مجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة وقراراته في محاضر يعدها أمين السر ويوقعها رئيس الاجتماع وأعضاء مجلس الإدارة الحاضرون وأمين السر. تدون المحاضر في سجل خاص يوقعه رئيس مجلس الإدارة وأمين السر. | Signature by all attending directors (not only the chair and secretary) is the statutory form; electronic signature is acceptable. The Law fixes the language of the articles as Arabic (Art. 7) but is silent on the language of minutes; bilingual minutes with an Arabic text are the safe practice because Ministry inspections and courts work in Arabic `[model knowledge — verify]`. Failure to prepare and record minutes is fineable (Art. 262(h)). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 84; Regs Arts. 24-30 | The general assembly is chaired by the chairman, the vice-chairman in his absence, or a board delegate, failing which a person chosen by the shareholders by vote; every shareholder may attend regardless of the articles and may appoint a proxy who is not a director; meetings, deliberation and voting may be held by modern technology (Art. 84). Regs Art. 24: electronic participation requires live audio-visual transmission and real-time ability to hear, follow, speak and vote; the company may offer electronic voting on agenda items to shareholders who do not attend, opening after the notice and for at least three days before the meeting, closing per item as each is voted; electronic meetings do not remove the right to attend in person at the venue in the notice. Regs Art. 25: the board must verify the identity and entitlement of electronic participants and voters. Regs Art. 26: electronic participants and e-voters count towards quorum and majorities. Regs Arts. 27-30: proxies in writing to a shareholder or non-shareholder who is not a director, on the form annexed (Form 1) or under a notarised power of attorney expressly covering assembly attendance and voting; a proxy is valid for the next assembly and any adjournment; a resident natural person or a Saudi-incorporated legal person must have the signature authenticated by the chamber of commerce, a licensed bank or CMA-licensed person where the principal holds an account, a notary or licensed notarising person, or another body approved by the Ministry; non-residents and foreign entities authenticate through a Saudi mission abroad, except where an international convention exempts; a shareholder may not both attend and appoint a proxy; a copy of the proxy must reach the company at least two days before the meeting (the company may accept later proxies received before registration closes). | لكل مساهم حق حضور اجتماع الجمعية العامة ولو نص نظام الشركة الأساس على غير ذلك، وله في ذلك أن يوكل عنه شخصًا آخر من غير أعضاء مجلس الإدارة. يجوز عقد اجتماع الجمعية العامة واشتراك المساهم في المداولات والتصويت على القرارات بوساطة وسائل التقنية الحديثة. (اللائحة م24) أن تكون مشاركة المساهم عن طريق نقل مرئي وصوتي لحظي لاجتماع الجمعية العامة أو الخاصة. | Virtual assemblies are lawful but need the Regs Art. 24-26 controls documented in the minutes (platform, identity verification, e-voting window). Foreign parent shareholders must plan proxy authentication through a Saudi embassy/consulate (or apostille where the Hague Convention applies `[model knowledge — verify]`) well before the meeting; a proxy not lodged two days before can be refused. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 85-88 | The extraordinary general assembly (EGA) amends the articles of association, except that it may not deprive a shareholder of, or modify, his fundamental rights (dividends, liquidation surplus, attending and voting, disposing of shares under the Law, inspection, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging assembly decisions), and amendments increasing shareholders' financial burdens require all shareholders' consent; the EGA also decides continuation or dissolution and approves buybacks (Art. 85); the EGA may decide ordinary matters under ordinary rules (Art. 86). The ordinary general assembly (OGA) handles everything else, in particular election and removal of directors, appointment, fees, reappointment and removal of the auditor, board and auditor reports, financial statements, dividend proposals, and reserves (Art. 87). The annual OGA must be held at least once within six months after fiscal year end, with a mandatory agenda: board report, financial statements, auditor's report, dividend proposal; an EGA held within the six months with that agenda satisfies the requirement (Art. 88). | تنعقد الجمعية العامة العادية السنوية مرة على الأقل خلال الأشهر (الستة) التالية لانتهاء السنة المالية للشركة. التعديلات التي من شأنها زيادة الأعباء المالية للمساهمين، ما لم يوافق على ذلك جميع المساهمين. | Entity-compliance calendar: annual assembly by the end of the sixth month after year end (30 June for a calendar-year company); the same deadline governs the financial-statements deposit (Art. 17). Failure to call the annual assembly on time is a fineable violation (Art. 262(b)) and allows the Ministry to convene it (Art. 90). Any clause obliging shareholders to fund the company (capital calls beyond issue price) needs unanimity — see also Art. 116. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 90-91; Regs Art. 23 | General and special assemblies are convened by the board under the articles; the board must call the OGA within 30 days of a request by the auditor or by shareholders holding at least 10% of voting shares, and the auditor may convene it himself if the board fails to do so within 30 days of his request; the request must state the items to be voted on. The competent authority may itself call the OGA where the six-month period passed without a meeting, where violations of the Law or the articles or management defects (including a board below quorum) appear, or where the board ignored a valid request; it may take the steps needed to hold the meeting and chair it. (Art. 90). Notice of an assembly (Art. 91) must be given at least 21 days before the meeting under the Regulations: by registered letters to the addresses in the shareholders' register or by announcement through modern technology; a copy of the notice and agenda goes to the Commercial Register (and to the CMA if listed) on the date of the notice. The notice must state who may attend and the right to appoint a non-director proxy, the right to discuss and question and how to vote; place, date and time; the type of assembly; and the agenda with the items to be voted on. In an unlisted JSC, shareholders representing all voting shares may hold an assembly without observing the notice formalities and periods. Regs Art. 23: electronic notice must allow proof of content, date and time of sending and identification of sender and recipient. | على مجلس الإدارة أن يدعو الجمعية العامة العادية للانعقاد خلال (ثلاثين) يومًا من تاريخ طلب مراجع الحسابات أو مساهم أو أكثر يمثلون (عشرة في المائة) من أسهم الشركة التي لها حقوق تصويت على الأقل. يكون توجيه الدعوة لانعقاد الجمعية قبل الميعاد المحدد لها (بواحد وعشرين) يومًا على الأقل ... إرسال صورة من الدعوة وجدول الأعمال إلى السجل التجاري. يجوز للمساهمين في شركة المساهمة غير المدرجة في السوق المالية الذين يمثلون جميع أسهم الشركة التي لها حقوق تصويت؛ أن يعقدوا جمعية عامة دون مراعاة للأوضاع والمدد المقررة للدعوة. | A 10% shareholder has a hard right to force an OGA within 30 days; the Ministry can step in for unlisted JSCs (the CMA for listed) — a lever for minority investors in deadlock. Board-minutes/written-consent workflow: 21 calendar days' notice, a copy lodged with the Commercial Register the same day, and the full statutory content; the only shortcut is a 100% attendance meeting (waiver by all holders of voting shares). E-mail notice is acceptable if it is provable as to content, time and parties. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 92 | OGA quorum: shareholders representing at least one quarter of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding one half. If not met, a second meeting is called in the same manner within the 30 days following the first date; it may be held one hour after the first meeting's slot if the articles allow and the first notice said so; the second meeting is valid whatever the number of voting shares represented. OGA resolutions pass by majority of the voting rights represented at the meeting. | لا يكون انعقاد اجتماع الجمعية العامة العادية صحيحًا إلا إذا حضره مساهمون يمثلون (ربع) أسهم الشركة التي لها حقوق تصويت على الأقل، ما لم ينص نظام الشركة الأساس على نسبة أعلى، بشرط ألا تتجاوز (النصف). تصدر قرارات الجمعية العامة العادية بموافقة أغلبية حقوق التصويت الممثلة في الاجتماع. | Formula for written-consent/board-minutes checks: first OGA valid if represented voting shares ≥ 25% (or the articles' figure, max 50%); resolution passes if votes for > 50% of voting rights represented. A "one hour later" second meeting is valid only with both an articles provision and a statement in the notice. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 93 | EGA quorum: shareholders representing at least half of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding two thirds. If not met, a second meeting (one hour later if the notice said so) is valid with at least one quarter of voting shares; if still not met, a third meeting is valid whatever the number represented. EGA resolutions pass by two thirds of the voting rights represented, except that resolutions to increase or reduce capital, extend the company's term, dissolve before the end of its term, merge with another company or divide into two or more companies require three quarters of the voting rights represented. The board must register with the Commercial Register the EGA resolutions specified by the Regulations within 15 days of their issue. | تصدر قرارات الجمعية العامة غير العادية بموافقة (ثلثي) حقوق التصويت الممثلة في الاجتماع، إلا إذا كان القرار متعلقًا بزيادة رأس المال أو تخفيضه أو بإطالة مدة الشركة أو بحلها قبل انقضاء المدة المحددة في نظامها الأساس أو باندماجها مع شركة أخرى أو تقسيمها إلى شركتين أو أكثر، فلا يكون صحيحًا إلا إذا صدر بموافقة (ثلاثة أرباع) حقوق التصويت الممثلة في الاجتماع. | Formula: EGA first meeting quorum ≥ 50% (articles up to 66.7%); ordinary amendment passes at ≥ 2/3 of represented voting rights; capital change, term extension, early dissolution, merger and division pass at ≥ 3/4 of represented voting rights. A 25% holder can therefore block a merger or capital change only if he attends (the thresholds are of represented, not issued, shares). Register the resolution within 15 days — a closing-checklist item. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 94-98 | Assembly resolutions take effect on issue unless the Law, the articles or the resolution provide otherwise (Art. 94). The articles fix the voting method; directors may not vote on resolutions concerning dealings in which they have a direct or indirect interest or a conflict (Art. 95). The board must consider items shareholders wish to add; holders of 10% of voting shares may add items when the agenda is prepared; each subject must be a separate item; substantially different matters may not be bundled, and related-party dealings may not be bundled into one item; every shareholder may discuss and question the board and the auditor, any contrary articles clause is void, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 96). Minutes must record the number of shareholders present in person or by proxy, the shares and votes they hold, the resolutions adopted with votes for and against, and an adequate summary of the discussion; minutes are entered regularly after each meeting in a special register signed by the chair of the assembly, its secretary and the vote collectors; the competent authority may regulate minutes, secretaries and vote collectors (Art. 97). Where a JSC has a single shareholder (at incorporation or later), he exercises the assemblies' powers; his decisions are issued in writing without convening an assembly and are entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 98). | يحرر باجتماع الجمعية محضر يتضمن عدد المساهمين الحاضرين بالأصالة أو النيابة، وعدد الأسهم التي في حيازتهم بالأصالة أو النيابة، وعدد الأصوات المقررة لها، والقرارات التي اتخذت، وعدد الأصوات التي وافقت عليها أو عارضتها، وخلاصة وافية للمناقشات التي دارت في الاجتماع. وتدون المحاضر بصفة منتظمة عقب كل اجتماع في سجل خاص يوقعه رئيس الجمعية وأمين سرها وجامعو الأصوات. | Assembly minutes have a statutory content list and three signatories (chair, secretary, vote collectors); a sole-shareholder JSC still needs a written decision entered in the register for every assembly-level matter. Bundled resolutions ("approve all related-party contracts") are non-compliant. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 99, 102, 170 | Any JSC shareholder may ask the court to annul an assembly resolution contrary to the Law or the articles, if he objected at the meeting or was absent with an acceptable excuse; the action is not heard after 90 days from the resolution; the claimant must remain a shareholder throughout (Art. 99). Shareholders holding at least 5% of capital may ask the court to order an inspection of the company where the conduct of the directors or the auditor raises suspicion; the court may order inspection at the applicant's expense (with security if the company requests), and if the complaint is proved may order precautionary measures, convene the assembly, remove directors and the auditor, and appoint supervisors to convene an assembly for a new board (Art. 102). The same 90-day annulment action lies for LLC partners, but only those who objected in writing or could not object after learning of the resolution (Art. 170). | لا تسمع دعوى البطلان بعد انقضاء (تسعين) يومًا من تاريخ صدور القرار. يحق لمساهم أو أكثر يمثلون (خمسة في المائة) على الأقل من رأس مال الشركة، تقديم طلب إلى الجهة القضائية المختصة للتفتيش على الشركة. | Defective resolutions become unchallengeable after 90 days unless void for other reasons; for diligence, resolutions older than 90 days carry reduced risk of annulment. LLC partners must object in writing to preserve the right — a reason to send written objections to circulated resolutions. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 100-101 | In an unlisted JSC the articles may allow the chairman to propose a general-assembly resolution by circulation to the shareholders without a meeting, unless any shareholder requests a meeting in writing; election and removal of directors, appointment and removal of the auditor, and review and discussion of the financial statements always require a meeting; the company must send the proposed resolution with the relevant documents to all shareholders, stating how to approve and the date by which the resolution is to be issued (Art. 100). Circulated resolutions pass, for OGA matters, with the approval of shareholders holding a majority of voting rights and, for EGA matters, with at least 75% of voting rights, unless the articles require more; they are recorded in minutes and entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 101). | يجوز أن يُنص في نظام الشركة الأساس على أن يكون لرئيس مجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة غير المدرجة في السوق المالية اقتراح إصدار قرار الجمعية العامة بعرضه على المساهمين بالتمرير ... فيما يتعلق بالقرار الذي يدخل في اختصاص الجمعية العامة غير العادية: يصدر بموافقة مساهم أو أكثر يمثلون (خمسة وسبعين في المائة) على الأقل من حقوق التصويت. | Written shareholder consents in a JSC require (i) an enabling articles clause, (ii) a chairman's proposal, (iii) circulation to all shareholders with documents, deadline and approval method, (iv) absolute majorities of all voting rights (> 50% ordinary; ≥ 75% extraordinary) — higher than the meeting thresholds, which count only represented shares — and (v) entry in the minutes register. The annual accounts, board elections and auditor changes cannot be done by written consent. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 103-112; Regs Arts. 51-55 | JSC shares are registered (nominal) and indivisible as against the company; co-owners appoint one representative and are jointly liable; the articles fix the nominal value, equal within a type or class; shares may be split or consolidated under the competent authority's rules; an unlisted JSC must issue paper or electronic share certificates (Art. 103). Subscription implies acceptance of the articles and assembly resolutions (Art. 104). Shares are issued against cash or in kind; at least one quarter of the nominal value of cash shares must be paid, the certificate showing the amount paid, and the balance within five years of issue; in-kind shares are issued only after full payment and transfer of the assets (Art. 105). Shares may not be issued below nominal value; a premium is allowed if the articles or the EGA permit, credited to a separate equity item used per the Regulations (Art. 106). Attached rights include disposal, attendance and voting, dividends, electing directors, inspection of records without breaching confidentiality, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging resolutions and a share of assets on liquidation, subject to the Law and the articles (Art. 107). Share types: ordinary, preferred and redeemable, with classes carrying different rights, privileges or restrictions under the articles and the Regulations (Art. 108); conversion between types/classes needs an articles provision and EGA approval (or automatic conversion terms), and conversion into redeemable shares needs all shareholders (Art. 109); modification of class rights requires a special assembly of the affected class under EGA rules plus EGA approval; new shares ranking ahead of preferred or redeemable shares need the affected class's consent (Arts. 89, 110). The CMA may restrict transfers in companies seeking listing; the articles may restrict share transfers, including redemption rights, but may not impose an absolute ban (Art. 111). An unlisted JSC keeps a shareholders' register (names, nationalities, details, residence, occupations, number, numbers and paid amount of shares), which may be outsourced but must be kept in the Kingdom; the Commercial Register must be given the register data and every change within 15 days of registration or of the change (Art. 112). Regs Art. 51: preferred or redeemable shares (and classes of them) may be issued only if the articles allow, issued capital is fully paid, the affected class's special assembly and the EGA approve, the proposal with the rights, privileges or restrictions accompanies the EGA notice and is reflected in the articles, the preferred and redeemable shares together never exceed 50% of capital, and they are fully paid on subscription; the EGA resolution states the redemption terms including the company's option, and redeemed shares are cancelled with a capital reduction. Regs Art. 52: conversion between types/classes needs an articles clause, must be into a type in Art. 108, needs the affected class's special assembly and the EGA, keeps preferred and redeemable shares within 50% of capital, and is supported by a board statement on the conversion ratio and its effect. Regs Art. 53: preferred and redeemable shares carry preferential rights but no vote at general assemblies; if a fixed or enhanced dividend is not paid for three consecutive years while profits suffice, their special assembly may resolve that they attend and vote (one vote per share) until arrears are paid. Regs Art. 54: share splits or consolidations need an articles clause, a board proposal sent to shareholders 21 days before the EGA, and EGA approval. Regs Art. 55: a shareholder in default has 30 days from the board's demand to pay; the auction is notified to the Ministry, advertised 15 to 30 days ahead in a daily newspaper or as the Ministry directs, and minuted. | يجوز أن ينص في نظام الشركة الأساس على قيود تتعلق بتداول الأسهم، بما في ذلك تقرير حق طلب استرداد الأسهم للمساهمين، ويشترط في جميع الأحوال ألا يكون من شأن ذلك الحظر المطلق لهذا التداول. تُعِد شركة المساهمة غير المدرجة في السوق المالية سجلًّا خاصًّا بأسماء المساهمين ... ويجب حفظه في المملكة. على الشركة تزويد السجل التجاري ببيانات السجل ... وأي تعديل يطرأ عليه خلال (خمسة عشر) يومًا. | Diligence on an unlisted JSC: obtain the shareholders' register and reconcile with Commercial Register filings (15-day updates); check paid-up status per share (five-year deadline for balance) and the class-rights matrix in the articles. Transfer restrictions (consent, pre-emption, lock-ups) are valid if in the articles and short of an absolute ban; an SHA-only restriction does not bind a transferee registered in the register. Preferred shares are non-voting by regulation (Regs Art. 53), which matters for investor term sheets: a preferred investor keeps control through reserved matters in the articles rather than votes, and preferred plus redeemable shares are capped at 50% of capital. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 113, 181 | With the approval of holders of at least 90% of voting shares (JSC) or 90% of capital (LLC), the articles may provide (a) drag-along: the majority may compel the minority to accept a bona fide buyer's offer for all shares/interests at the same price, terms and conditions as the majority's; (b) tag-along: the minority may compel the majority to procure the sale of the minority's shares/interests when the majority sell, at the same price, terms and conditions. | يجوز أن ينص في نظام الشركة الأساس بعد موافقة المساهمين الذين يمثلون (تسعين في المائة) من أسهم الشركة التي لها حقوق تصويت على الأقل، على الآتي: أن يكون لأكثرية المساهمين إلزام الأقلية بقبول عرض من مشترٍ حسن النية لشراء جميع أسهم الشركة بذات السعر والشروط والأحكام. | Drag and tag rights are enforceable only if inserted in the articles with a 90% vote; an SHA-only drag against a non-signatory or a transferee is weak. Diligence and closing checklists should verify the registered articles contain the clause and that the 90% approval was minuted when it was inserted. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 114-116, 180; Regs Arts. 34-39, 43-50, 66-69 | A JSC may buy back or take a pledge over its own shares if the articles allow; treasury shares carry no vote (Art. 114(1)); shares may be pledged, the pledgee collecting dividends and exercising share rights unless the pledge agreement provides otherwise, but never attending or voting at assemblies (Art. 114(2)). A shareholder who defaults on a call may, after notice, have his shares sold at public auction (or on the Exchange), the articles may give other shareholders priority; rights are suspended until sale or payment; the company recovers its dues and pays over the balance (Art. 115). The company may not demand more than the shareholder committed on issue, even if the articles say otherwise (Art. 116). An LLC may buy or take a pledge over its own interests if the articles allow; purchased interests carry no vote; a pledgee of interests collects dividends unless agreed otherwise (Art. 180). Regs Arts. 34-39: an unlisted JSC may buy its ordinary or preferred shares if the articles allow, only to reduce capital or to hold ordinary shares as treasury shares; treasury shares may never exceed 10% of the class, must be fully paid, and their cost may not exceed retained earnings; the buyback is approved by the EGA with a maximum number and purposes and a board authority to complete within 12 months, announced immediately; treasury shares may be used only to satisfy convertible debt/sukuk, as consideration for acquiring shares, interests or assets, for an employee share programme, for cancellation, or for another Ministry-approved purpose; shareholders must receive adequate information and a fair opportunity to tender; purchased preferred shares are cancelled; the EGA may fix a holding period. Regs Arts. 43-45: treasury shares are sold by board resolution if the articles allow and consistently with the EGA resolution, existing shareholders having pro rata priority on a cash sale; the company may take a pledge over its own shares only if the articles allow, as security for a debt owed to it, in the interest of the company and shareholders as the board judges, with general-assembly approval (which may be given in advance for several transactions). Regs Arts. 46-50: a shareholder may pledge his shares after any required regulatory approvals; a pledge is created by a written contract stating the parties and their identity numbers and addresses, the number, value and numbers of the pledged shares and the issuer's Commercial Register number, the secured debt and any cap, the debtor if different, the date, release terms and other terms; the pledgee and pledgor each file an application with the chairman (or the outsourced registrar) on Forms 2 and 3 with authenticated signatures (chamber of commerce, licensed bank or CMA-licensed person, notary or licensed notarising person, or a Saudi mission abroad for non-residents, subject to treaty exemptions); the pledge is entered in the shareholders' register and annotated on the certificates; release is by expiry or by the pledgee's Form 4; the pledge is effective against third parties only once entered in the shareholders' register; Regs Art. 66: an LLC buyback needs an articles clause, partners' approval with waiver of redemption rights and a manager's authority to complete within 30 days; Regs Art. 67: an LLC may take a pledge over its own interests only if the articles allow, as security for a debt to the company, in the interest of the company and partners, with partners' approval at the amendment quorum; Regs Arts. 68-69: a partner's pledge of interests requires the necessary regulatory approvals and a written contract stating the parties, the number and value of pledged interests and the Commercial Register number, the secured debt, the debtor, date, release terms and other agreed terms. | يجوز رهن الأسهم، ويكون للدائن المرتهن قبض الأرباح واستعمال الحقوق المتصلة بالسهم، ما لم يتفق في عقد الرهن على غير ذلك. ولا يجوز للدائن المرتهن حضور اجتماعات جمعيات المساهمين ولا التصويت فيها. لا يجوز للشركة أن تطالب المساهم بدفع مبالغ تزيد على مقدار ما التزم به عند إصدار السهم ولو نص نظام الشركة الأساس على ذلك. | Security packages over Saudi shares/interests: pledge over LLC interests is by written contract meeting Regs Art. 69 and registered under the Movable Assets Security Law regime `[model knowledge — verify]`; pledge over unlisted JSC shares is perfected only by entry in the shareholders' register on the Regulations' forms (Regs Art. 50), so a lender's closing checklist needs the registrar's confirmation of entry, not just the signed pledge. Pledgees never vote. Treasury shares dilute voting thresholds because they carry no votes. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 121-122, 125; Regs Arts. 31-32 | At the end of each fiscal year the board prepares the financial statements and a report on the company's activity and financial position with the proposed dividend, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual OGA; they are signed by the chairman, the CEO and the CFO (if any) and copies are kept at the head office for shareholders (Art. 121). The chairman must provide shareholders with the signed statements, board report and auditor's report at least 21 days before the annual OGA unless published by modern technology, and deposit them as the Regulations specify (Art. 122). The assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; the resolution states the entitlement (record) date and the distribution date; dividends belong to holders registered at the end of the record date; the Regulations cap the time for the board to implement (Art. 125). Regs Art. 31: the chairman sends the board report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual OGA. Regs Art. 32: dividends must be paid to registered shareholders within 15 days of the entitlement date. | يجب على مجلس الإدارة في نهاية كل سنة مالية للشركة أن يعد القوائم المالية للشركة وتقريرًا عن نشاطها ومركزها المالي ... ويضع المجلس هذه الوثائق تحت تصرف مراجع الحسابات إن وجد، قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة العادية السنوية (بخمسة وأربعين) يومًا على الأقل. (اللائحة م32) يجب على مجلس إدارة شركة المساهمة تنفيذ قرار الجمعية العامة بشأن توزيع الأرباح ... خلال مدة لا تزيد على (خمسة عشر) يومًا من تاريخ استحقاق تلك الأرباح. | Annual JSC timeline working back from the assembly date D: statements to auditor by D-45; notice, statements and board report to shareholders and board report to the Ministry by D-21; assembly by month 6; financial statements deposited by month 6; dividends paid within 15 days of the record date. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 126-131; Regs Arts. 40-42, 57-61 | Capital is increased by new shares for cash or in kind; by shares for ascertained and due debts with the creditors' consent, at a value set by the EGA after an expert or accredited valuer's opinion and a board statement with an auditor's report; by capitalising reserves (bonus shares pro rata); or by shares for debt instruments or sukuk (Art. 126). The EGA may increase issued or authorised capital only if issued capital is fully paid (except unpaid shares from pending conversions); it may allocate new shares to employees of the company and its subsidiaries, without pre-emption for shareholders; new shares have the same nominal value as existing shares of the type/class (Art. 127). Shareholders at the date of the EGA (or board) resolution have pre-emptive rights over new cash shares in proportion to their type/class and are notified by registered letter or technology of the terms and subscription period (Art. 128); the EGA may suspend pre-emption or grant it to non-shareholders if the articles allow (Art. 129); pre-emption rights may be sold or waived under the Regulations (Art. 130); allocation is pro rata to rights, then to over-subscribers, then to third parties unless the EGA or the Capital Market Law provide otherwise (Art. 131). Regs Art. 40: a company holding treasury shares (other than for an employee programme), or with an unrevoked EGA buyback approval, may not increase issued capital by a rights issue; Regs Art. 41: treasury shares participate in a capitalisation issue. Regs Art. 42: an employee share programme using treasury shares needs an articles clause, EGA approval of the programme (the board may be delegated its terms and the allocation price), exclusion of non-executive directors, and abstention of executive directors from board votes on it. Regs Arts. 57-61: pre-emption rights accrue to shareholders registered at the end of the day of the EGA (or board) resolution, in proportion to their shares and by type/class, but not for employee-allocated shares or where the EGA has suspended or reallocated the right; each right entitles the holder to subscribe for one new share at the price fixed in the resolution; subject to the articles, a registered shareholder may sell or assign rights to anyone, shareholder or not, at an agreed price during the subscription period, which may not be shorter than 15 days; the seller must notify the company in writing with the buyer's name, nationality, residence, occupation and number of rights, and the buyer steps into the subscription right; unexercised rights lapse at the end of the last subscription day. | للمساهم مالك السهم -وقت صدور قرار الجمعية العامة غير العادية بالموافقة على زيادة رأس المال المصدر أو قرار مجلس الإدارة بالموافقة على زيادته في حدود رأس المال المصرح به- الأولوية في الاكتتاب بالأسهم الجديدة التي تصدر مقابل حصص نقدية. يحق للجمعية العامة غير العادية -إذا نص على ذلك في نظام الشركة الأساس- وقف العمل بحق الأولوية. | A third-party investment round in a JSC needs either pre-emption waivers from all shareholders or an articles clause allowing the EGA (3/4 majority, Art. 93) to suspend pre-emption. Debt-to-equity conversions require creditor consent, a valuation and an auditor's report. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 132 | If a JSC's losses reach half of its issued capital, the board must within 60 days of learning of it disclose the fact and its recommendations, and call the EGA to meet within 180 days of that date to decide whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | إذا بلغت خسائر شركة المساهمة (نصف) رأس المال المصدر، وجب على مجلس الإدارة الإفصاح عن ذلك وعما توصل إليه من توصيات بشأن تلك الخسائر خلال (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ علمه ببلوغها هذا المقدار، ودعوة الجمعية العامة غير العادية إلى الاجتماع خلال (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ العلم بذلك للنظر في استمرار الشركة ... أو حلّها. | Losses trigger for diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of issued capital (from the latest financial statements or management accounts) → check for the board disclosure within 60 days and the EGA within 180 days. Unlike the 2015 Law there is no automatic dissolution for failing to act `[model knowledge — verify]`, but failure to call the assembly is a criminal offence under Art. 261(h) and the assembly may resolve to dissolve. Continuation usually involves a capital reduction (Art. 134 allows reduction below SAR 500,000 for losses) or shareholder recapitalisation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 133-137 | Capital is reduced by cancelling shares, reducing nominal value by the amount of losses, reducing nominal value by refund or release of unpaid amounts, or by buying and cancelling shares (Art. 133). The EGA may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses (only then below the SAR 500,000 minimum), after a board statement of reasons, liabilities and the effect on them with an auditor's report is read at the assembly (or circulated where the resolution is by circulation) (Art. 134). Where the reduction is for surplus capital, creditors must be invited to object at least 45 days before the EGA, with a statement of capital before and after and the effective date; an objecting creditor who lodges his documents in time must be paid if due or given adequate security if not, and may apply to the court before the EGA, which may order payment, security or postponement; the reduction is not effective against a timely-objecting creditor until he is paid or secured (Art. 135). Shareholders of the same type/class are treated equally (Art. 136); reduction by buyback requires an invitation to all shareholders, pro rata scaling and, for unlisted companies, purchase at fair value (Art. 137). | إذا كان تخفيض رأس المال نتيجة زيادته على حاجة الشركة، وجبت دعوة الدائنين إلى إبداء اعتراضاتهم -إن وجدت- على التخفيض قبل (خمسة وأربعين) يومًا على الأقل من التاريخ المحدد لعقد اجتماع الجمعية العامة غير العادية لاتخاذ قرار التخفيض. | A return of surplus capital to a JSC shareholder is a 45-day creditor process plus a 3/4 EGA vote; a loss-absorbing reduction has no creditor window. Diligence should check that a historic reduction for surplus capital was preceded by the creditor invitation, otherwise unpaid creditors can treat it as ineffective against them. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 138-141 | The SJSC (شركة المساهمة المبسطة) is governed by the JSC rules except Arts. 61, 63, 67-71, 74-88, 90-94, 95(1), 96-98, 100, 101, 111(2), 121 and 122; shareholders organise the structure and operation in the articles; the shareholders replace the OGA and EGA and may allocate those powers in the articles; the president, manager or board exercises the JSC board's powers (Art. 138). The articles fix issued and paid-up capital and may provide authorised capital; the JSC minimum capital does not apply (Art. 139). Articles must cover name, head office, objects, capital, shares (types/classes/rights), term, management, share assignment, shareholders' meetings and their quorum, shareholders' decisions and their quorum, fiscal year; attachments mirror the JSC list including a bank deposit certificate and the appointment of the president/manager/board (Art. 140). In-kind contributions not exceeding half the capital need no accredited valuer unless the founders/shareholders decide otherwise; above half, an accredited valuer's report is required, contributors do not vote, and a reduction of the agreed value needs their consent; without a proper valuation the founders/shareholders are personally liable to third parties for the fairness of the valuation and for paying the difference in cash, for five years from registration or the increase (Art. 141). | تسري على شركة المساهمة المبسطة فيما لم يرد به نص خاص في هذا الباب، وبما يتفق مع طبيعتها، أحكام شركة المساهمة عدا المواد: (الحادية والستين)، و(الثالثة والستين)، ومن (السابعة والستين) إلى (الحادية والسبعين) ... لا يسري متطلب الحد الأدنى لرأس المال المقرر لشركة المساهمة على شركة المساهمة المبسطة. | The SJSC is the venture-capital vehicle: no minimum capital, share classes, freedom in governance and reserved matters, and (because Art. 71 is excluded) related-party approvals fall back on the general Art. 27 regime. Because the JSC assembly articles (84-88, 90-94, 96-98, 100-101) do not apply, quorum, majority and notice rules for an SJSC come from its articles and Arts. 145-149 — a reviewer must read the specific articles of association rather than assume JSC thresholds. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 142-144 | The articles fix how the SJSC is managed — a president, one or more managers, a board or otherwise — and how the managers are appointed and removed, their powers and working method; if silent, the shareholders decide; the president/manager/board has the widest powers except matters reserved to shareholders, may delegate specific acts; the president, manager or chairman represents the company and may delegate representation if the articles allow; the company is bound even by acts outside the manager's powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew (Art. 142). JSC board liability rules apply to the president, manager or board (Art. 143), as does the Art. 72 prohibition on loans to them (Art. 144). | تحدد طريقة إدارة شركة المساهمة المبسطة في نظامها الأساس، ويجوز أن يتولى إدارتها رئيس أو مدير أو أكثر أو مجلس إدارة أو غير ذلك. | A single director (president or manager) is permitted; there is no minimum board size for an SJSC. Counterparty authority check: identify from the articles whether a president, manager or chairman signs, and whether delegation to a CEO is authorised by the articles. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 145-149 | The SJSC articles list the matters reserved to shareholders, but capital increase or reduction, transformation, merger, division, dissolution, auditor appointment, discussion of the financial statements, dividends and amendment of the articles must always be decided by the shareholders; the articles set the quorum for meetings and decisions, may set different quorums for different matters, and must specify the matters requiring unanimity (Art. 145). Meetings are called by the president/manager/board under the articles, and may be requested by the auditor or by holders of 10% of voting shares; notice to all shareholders at least 5 days before, with place, date, time and agenda (and optionally a second-meeting date); by registered letter or technology unless the articles provide otherwise; for reserved matters each shareholder may obtain the relevant documents during the 5 days before the meeting (or longer per the articles); meetings may be held at the head office, elsewhere or by technology; holders of all voting shares may meet without notice formalities (Art. 146). Financial statements and the activity report (with dividend proposal) plus the auditor's report are presented to shareholders within six months of fiscal year end and deposited per the Regulations (Art. 147). Deliberations and decisions, including those by circulation, are recorded in minutes in a special register signed by the president/manager/board; technology may be used; the decisions specified by the Regulations must be registered with the Commercial Register within 15 days (Art. 148). The articles may allow decisions by circulation: the proposed decision and documents are sent to all shareholders (registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide otherwise) stating how to approve and the decision date; the articles fix the quorum for circulated decisions (Art. 149). | يجب أن تتخذ من المساهمين القرارات الداخلة في اختصاصات الجمعية العامة العادية أو غير العادية لشركة المساهمة فيما يتعلق بزيادة رأس المال أو تخفيضه أو تحول الشركة إلى شكل آخر أو اندماجها أو تقسيمها أو حلها أو تعيين مراجع الحسابات أو مناقشة القوائم المالية أو توزيع الأرباح أو تعديل نظام الشركة الأساس. توجه الدعوة إلى الاجتماع إلى جميع المساهمين قبل الميعاد المحدد له (بخمسة) أيام على الأقل. | SJSC written consents are valid only under an articles clause and at the quorum the articles fix; there is no statutory fallback majority, so a silent articles document means no circulation route. Notice is only 5 calendar days. The statutory list of non-delegable shareholder matters is the minimum reserved-matters list for any SJSC SHA. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 150-154 | A single-shareholder SJSC: the owner's liability is limited to the capital allocated; he exercises the shareholders' powers by written decisions entered in a special register (Art. 150). The articles may restrict share disposals by (a) a lock-up of up to 10 years from issue, extendable by unanimity, and (b) a requirement of the company's or shareholders' consent before disposal; a disposal in breach is void (Art. 151). The articles may set conditions on which a shareholder can be compelled to transfer his shares, at fair value unless the articles provide otherwise, with non-financial rights suspended until transfer (Art. 152). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between shareholders or between the company and its president/manager/directors to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 153). Inserting or amending Arts. 151-153 provisions in the articles requires unanimity (Art. 154). | يجوز أن يُنص في نظام الشركة الأساس على قيود على التصرف في الأسهم تتعلق بالآتي: حظر التصرف فيها لمدة لا تتجاوز (عشر) سنوات من تاريخ إصدارها ... اشتراط موافقة الشركة أو المساهمين قبل التصرف فيها. ويعد باطلًا كل تصرف في الأسهم يقع بالمخالفة لتلك القيود. يشترط موافقة المساهمين بالإجماع على تضمين نظام الشركة الأساس أحكام المواد (الحادية والخمسين بعد المائة) و(الثانية والخمسين بعد المائة) و(الثالثة والخمسين بعد المائة) من النظام. | Founder lock-ups, consent rights, good/bad-leaver compulsory transfers and arbitration clauses are statutorily enforceable in an SJSC when placed in the articles — but only with unanimous consent when inserted or changed, so diligence should look for the unanimous resolution. Transfers in breach of a registered lock-up or consent clause are void, not merely a breach of contract. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 156-159, 174, 183-184 | LLC (الشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة): formed by one or more natural or legal persons; its estate is separate from the partners'; only the company is liable for its debts; a partner or sole owner is liable only to the extent of his interest in the capital (Art. 156). Single-owner LLC: the owner exercises the powers of the manager, board of managers and general assembly by written decisions entered in a special register; he may appoint one or more managers who represent the company and answer to him; a single-owner LLC has articles of association and references to articles of incorporation are read accordingly (Art. 157). Articles of incorporation must state: partners' names and details, name, head office, objects, capital and its division, the partners' declaration that the interests are paid, term (if any), management, assignment of interests, the means of notifying partners, partners' decisions, profit/loss distribution, fiscal year, dissolution, and other agreed terms; attachments: a compliance declaration and an accredited valuer's report on in-kind contributions (Art. 158), valued under Art. 141 (Art. 159). The partners fix the capital in the articles (no statutory minimum); it is divided into interests of equal value, indivisible and non-tradeable; co-owned interests must have a single representative or the company may suspend rights and ultimately sell the interest (Art. 174). A fixed-term LLC may be extended before expiry by partners holding half the interests (or more if the articles say so); if it continues trading without a resolution it is extended for a like term; a dissenting partner may exit at a value fixed under Art. 178, the extension taking effect only after his interests are sold and paid; third parties with an interest may object (Art. 183). Death, incapacity, insolvency, bankruptcy proceedings or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the LLC unless the articles so provide (Art. 184). | يحدد الشركاء مقدار رأس مال الشركة في عقد تأسيسها، ويقسم إلى حصص متساوية القيمة، وتكون الحصة غير قابلة للتجزئة والتداول. يكون للشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة المملوكة من شخص واحد نظام أساس. | The LLC has no minimum capital under the Law; MISA licensing may nonetheless expect a stated capital for certain activities `[model knowledge — verify]`. The Law text read contains no cap on the number of LLC partners and no bar on an LLC owned by another single-owner LLC. The old 2015-Law requirement that a sole owner may not own more than one LLC is not in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 160-162 | An LLC is managed by one or more managers, partners or not, appointed by the partners in the articles or in a separate contract, for a fixed or indefinite term; the partners may constitute a board of managers where there are several (Art. 160). The articles or a partners' decision fix the management method and the majority for managers' decisions (Art. 161). The manager represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties and may delegate specific acts; a decision appointing, replacing or restricting the powers of a manager is effective against third parties only after registration with the Commercial Register; the company is bound by the manager's acts within its objects (Art. 162). | لا يسري في مواجهة الغير أي قرار يصدر بتعيين المدير أو بتغييره أو بتقييد سلطاته؛ إلا بعد قيده لدى السجل التجاري. تلتزم الشركة بأعمال المدير التي تدخل في غرض الشركة. | Counterparty authority for an LLC: the manager named on the Commercial Register binds the company within its objects; internal limits (SHA reserved matters, approval thresholds) do not protect the company against third parties unless registered. Closing checklist: register the change of manager and any power limits on the Register on completion. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 163-164; Regs Art. 63 | If a sole manager's office falls vacant, the partners must appoint a new manager within 15 days of learning of it; the auditor or any partner may call the general assembly for that purpose (Art. 163). The partners may remove the manager(s), whether appointed in the articles or a separate contract, and must appoint successors; a manager who is a partner may not vote on his own removal; partners holding at least one quarter of capital may petition the court for removal (Art. 164). Regs Art. 63: subject to the articles, removal of an LLC manager, even a partner named in the articles, is decided at the quorum the Law sets for partners' decisions (Art. 166: more than half of capital). | يجوز للشركاء عزل المدير أو المديرين، سواء أكانوا معينين في عقد تأسيس الشركة أم في عقد مستقل ... وإذا كان المدير شريكًا في الشركة فلا يجوز له أن يشترك في التصويت على القرار المتعلق بعزله. (اللائحة م63) يكون قرار عزل مدير الشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة وإن كان شريكًا فيها معينًا في عقد تأسيسها أو في عقد مستقل وفقًا للنصاب المقرر لصحة صدور قرارات الشركاء المنصوص عليها في النظام. | Removal of an LLC manager is an ordinary partners' decision (> 50% of capital), not an amendment of the articles, even where he is named in the articles; a manager-partner's interests are excluded from the vote on his removal. A 25% partner can seek court removal. The registered articles then need updating (Art. 162). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 165 | An LLC has a general assembly of all partners, convened by the manager(s) as the articles provide, at least once a year within six months after fiscal year end; it may be called at any time at the request of the managers, the auditor, or partners holding at least 10% of capital; notice goes to all partners by registered letter, modern technology or any other means in the articles at least 21 days before the meeting; partners representing all interests may meet without observing notice formalities and periods; deliberations, decisions and decisions by circulation are recorded in minutes entered in a special register kept for the purpose, and technology may be used to record them; meetings and voting may be held by technology. | تنعقد الجمعية العامة للشركاء بدعوة من المدير أو المديرين ... على أن تنعقد مرة على الأقل في السنة خلال الأشهر (الستة) التالية لنهاية السنة المالية للشركة ... وذلك قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة (بواحد وعشرين) يومًا على الأقل. تثبت مداولات الجمعية العامة للشركاء وقراراتها أو قرارات الشركاء بالتمرير في محاضر تدون في سجل خاص تعده الشركة لهذا الغرض. | LLC annual compliance: assembly within six months of year end (same window as the financial-statements deposit), 21 calendar days' notice unless 100% of interests attend, and a minutes register (paper or electronic) containing every resolution including written ones. The absence of a minutes register is a common diligence finding in foreign-owned LLCs. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
| Arts. 166, 171 | Partners' decisions are taken in the general assembly but may be taken by circulation without a meeting: the manager sends each partner the proposed decisions and documents for a written vote, by registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide another means. In all cases a decision is valid only if approved by partners holding more than half of the capital, unless the articles require a larger majority; if that majority is not reached in the first deliberation or consultation, the partners must be called to a meeting at which decisions pass by a majority of the interests represented, whatever their proportion of capital, unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 166). Each partner takes part in deliberations and voting with votes equal to his interests, and no contrary agreement is allowed; a partner may appoint another partner in writing as proxy unless the articles provide otherwise, and the articles may allow non-partner proxies; a non-manager partner may advise the manager and may inspect the company's business, records and documents at the head office twice per fiscal year, the company complying within 15 days; contrary clauses are void; information obtained is confidential (Art. 171). | في جميع الأحوال لا تكون القرارات صحيحة إلا إذا وافق عليها شريك أو أكثر يمثلون أكثر من (نصف) رأس المال على الأقل، ما لم ينص عقد تأسيس الشركة على أغلبية أكبر. إذا لم تتوافر في المداولة أو في المشاورة الأولى الأغلبية المنصوص عليها ... وجبت دعوة الشركاء إلى الاجتماع، وتصدر القرارات في هذه الحالة بموافقة أغلبية الحصص الممثلة فيه أيًّا كانت النسبة التي تمثلها في رأس المال. | Written-consent formula for an LLC ordinary decision: approvals from holders of > 50% of the capital (an absolute majority, not of those voting); if not reached, a called meeting decides by majority of interests present (the second-round rule can let a minority decide, so articles often exclude it — check). Voting proxies to non-partners (e.g. a parent's lawyer) need an articles clause. One-interest-one-vote cannot be varied. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
| Arts. 167-169; Regs Art. 65 | The manager prepares for each fiscal year the financial statements, an activity and financial-position report and dividend proposals, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual assembly; he provides partners with the statements, report and auditor's report (by technology or as the articles provide) at least 21 days before the annual assembly and deposits them as the Regulations specify (Art. 167). The annual agenda must include the manager's report, the financial statements, the auditor's report and the dividend proposal (Art. 168). The assembly may not deliberate outside the agenda unless facts emerge at the meeting; a partner may require an item to be added, and may appeal to the assembly if refused; every partner may discuss and question, the manager must answer, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 169). Regs Art. 65: the manager sends the activity report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual assembly. | يعد مدير الشركة عن كل سنة مالية القوائم المالية للشركة وتقريرًا عن نشاطها ومركزها المالي ... ويضع المدير هذه الوثائق تحت تصرف مراجع الحسابات -إن وجد- قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة في اجتماعها السنوي (بخمسة وأربعين) يومًا على الأقل. (اللائحة م65) على مدير الشركة أن يزود الوزارة بتقرير عن نشاط الشركة قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة السنوي (بواحد وعشرين) يومًا على الأقل. | LLC annual timeline mirrors the JSC: D-45 to auditor; D-21 to partners and the Ministry; assembly and deposit within six months of year end. The Ministry filing of the manager's activity report is frequently overlooked in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 172-173; Regs Art. 64 | The articles of incorporation, including capital increases and reductions, may be amended with the approval of partners holding at least three quarters of the capital, unless the articles require more; on a cash increase by new interests each partner has pre-emption pro rata to his holding under the Regulations; an increase by raising the nominal value of interests, or suspension of pre-emption, requires unanimity (Art. 172). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between partners or between the company and its managers to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 173). Regs Art. 64: a partner who takes less than his entitlement leaves the balance to the other partners pro rata; the partners file a draft amendment with a declaration that the new interests are paid, and the increase is effective on registration and publication with the Commercial Register. | يجوز تعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة بما في ذلك زيادة رأس مالها أو تخفيضه بموافقة شريك أو أكثر يمثلون (ثلاثة أرباع) رأس المال على الأقل، ما لم ينص عقد التأسيس على نسبة أكبر. لا يجوز زيادة رأس المال عن طريق رفع القيمة الاسمية لحصص الشركاء أو وقف العمل بحق الأولوية، إلا بإجماع الشركاء. | Formula for LLC special decisions: ≥ 75% of capital (absolute) to amend the articles, change capital, transform, merge or divide (Arts. 220, 225, 232 refer back to the amendment quorum); unanimity to suspend pre-emption or raise nominal value. The 2015-Law rule requiring unanimity to change the company's nationality or increase partners' obligations is not in the Art. 172 text read `[model knowledge — verify]`; Art. 3 fixes Saudi nationality in any event, and a JSC-style "no increased financial burdens without all shareholders" rule appears only in Art. 85. A 25%+1 partner holds a veto over every amendment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 175-177 | Interests carry equal rights to net profits and liquidation surplus unless the articles provide otherwise; the assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; entitlement and payment dates are stated in the resolution (Art. 175). The assembly may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses; in the loss case a manager's statement of reasons, liabilities and effect with an auditor's report is read (or circulated); where the reduction returns surplus capital, every manager must sign a solvency statement confirming, on examination, that nothing would render the company unable to pay its debts and that it can pay debts falling due within the next 12 months, delivered to partners at least 15 days before the decision; the partners file a draft amendment with the Register within 15 days of the decision, attaching the statements, and the reduction is effective on registration and publication (Art. 176). The articles may provide for a reserve; the annual assembly may create reserves and allocate amounts for employees' social purposes (Art. 177). | إذا كان تخفيض رأس المال نتيجة زيادته على حاجة الشركة، فيجب على كل مدير في الشركة إعداد بيان بملاءة الشركة المالية ... أن الشركة قادرة على سداد ديونها والتزاماتها التي تستحق خلال (الاثني عشر) شهرًا التي تلي تاريخ إعداد البيان. ويكون قرار التخفيض نافذًا بعد قيده وشهره لدى السجل التجاري. | An LLC has no creditor-objection window on a capital reduction (contrast Art. 135 for JSCs); the safeguard is the managers' personal solvency statement, which exposes managers criminally under Art. 260(a) if false. The statutory reserve of the 2015 Law is no longer mandatory in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`; reserves are optional. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Art. 178 | A partner may assign his interest to another partner on the terms in the articles. To assign to a non-partner, with or without consideration, the partner must notify the other partners through the manager of the assignee's or buyer's name and the terms; the manager notifies the partners immediately; each partner may within 30 days of the manager's notification of the agreed price demand to redeem the interest and pay its value, or have the company buy it; if several partners redeem, the interest is divided pro rata to their capital; if the value is disputed it is fixed by an accredited valuer's fair-value report at the cost of the redeeming partner or the company; if the period expires without redemption, payment or company purchase, the partner may assign to the third party. The articles may provide other notification procedures, another valuation method, or a longer period for redemption, payment or company purchase. The redemption right does not apply to transfers by inheritance, will or court judgment. | يجوز لكل شريك أن يطلب استرداد تلك الحصة وسداد قيمتها أو قيام الشركة بشرائها خلال (ثلاثين) يومًا من تاريخ إبلاغ المدير بالثمن الذي يتفق عليه ... وإذا انقضت المدة المحددة لممارسة حق الاسترداد دون أن يطلب أي من الشركاء استرداد الحصة أو إذا لم يقم طالب الاسترداد بسداد قيمتها أو إذا لم تقم الشركة بشرائها خلال تلك المدة، كان لصاحبها الحق في التنازل عنها للغير. لا يسري حق الاسترداد المنصوص عليه في هذه المادة على انتقال ملكية الحصص بالإرث أو بالوصية أو بموجب حكم من الجهة القضائية المختصة. | Statutory pre-emption in every multi-partner LLC: a third-party sale of interests requires the manager-routed notice and the 30-day redemption window (or waivers from all other partners). Closing checklist for an LLC acquisition: written waivers of Art. 178 rights (and any longer articles period) from every non-selling partner, then the amended articles registered with the Commercial Register (Art. 25). A change of control of a corporate partner is not an "assignment" under the text and does not trigger Art. 178 `[model knowledge — verify]`, though SHAs often add it contractually. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
| Art. 182 | If an LLC's losses reach half of its capital, the manager must call the general assembly of partners to meet within 60 days of learning that losses reached that level, to consider whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | إذا بلغت خسائر الشركة نصف رأس مالها، وجب على مدير الشركة دعوة الجمعية العامة للشركاء إلى الاجتماع خلال (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ العلم ببلوغ الخسارة هذا المقدار للنظر في استمرار الشركة مع اتخاذ أي من الإجراءات اللازمة لمعالجة تلك الخسائر، أو حلها. | Losses trigger for LLC diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of registered capital → assembly within 60 days of knowledge. The text imposes no automatic dissolution and no partner liability for the company's debts for failing to act (the 2015-Law joint liability of partners has been dropped in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`), but the manager commits an offence under Art. 261(h) if he fails to call the assembly. Foreign-owned LLCs funded by shareholder loans often sit above the threshold; a recapitalisation or loss-absorbing reduction (Art. 176) is the usual cure. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
| Arts. 185-186, 197-199, 216-218 (notes) | Non-profit companies: a public non-profit company must be a JSC and spend its profits on public-benefit purposes fixed with the National Center for Non-Profit Sector; a private non-profit company may be an LLC, JSC or SJSC spending profits on stated non-profit purposes; neither may offer shares to the public; otherwise the rules of the chosen form apply (Arts. 185-186). Professional companies: formed by one or more persons licensed to practise a liberal profession, alone or with others, in any of the five forms; the form's rules apply save as Part 8 provides; a partner is not a merchant by reason of his holding (Arts. 197-199); Regs Part 5 sets conditions for single-owner professional companies, multi-profession firms, participation of non-licensed persons and foreign professional firms (headings read). Holding companies: a JSC, SJSC or LLC that forms or holds subsidiaries; a company is a subsidiary where the holder has a majority of voting rights, alone controls appointment or removal of the manager or a majority of the board, controls a majority of votes by agreement with other holders, or is a subsidiary of a subsidiary; a subsidiary may not hold interests or shares in its parent (acquisitions to the contrary are void) and must dispose of pre-existing holdings within 12 months without voting them, with exceptions for CMA-licensed persons (Arts. 216-218). | لا يجوز للشركة التابعة امتلاك حصص أو أسهم في الشركة القابضة. ويعد باطلًا كل تصرف من شأنه نقل ملكية الحصص أو الأسهم من الشركة القابضة إلى الشركة التابعة. | Group structuring: circular holdings are void; a control test (voting majority, board control, or control by agreement) defines subsidiaries for Art. 17 information rights and for the Art. 19 audit exemption. Professional-firm and non-profit specifics beyond these definitions were not read in detail. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 220-224 | A company may transform into another form by a resolution passed under the rules for amending its articles, after satisfying the incorporation, registration and publication requirements of the new form; transformation into an SJSC requires unanimity; sole proprietors may transfer their assets into any company form without being released from prior debts unless creditors expressly agree; a general partnership, limited partnership or LLC wholly owned by relatives (or including waqf/bequest interests) may transform into a JSC on the request of holders of more than half the capital (Art. 220). A private non-profit company may transform, spending surplus on its non-profit purposes and refunding exemptions; any company may become a non-profit company by unanimity (Art. 221; Regs Art. 86 requires proof and a special auditor's report). Partners or shareholders who object to the transformation may exit by written request within 15 days of the resolution, paid at the agreed value or an accredited valuer's fair value at the transformation date, unless the articles provide otherwise, with recourse to the court (Art. 222). Transformation creates no new legal person; the company keeps its rights and remains bound by prior obligations (Art. 223). General partners are not released from pre-transformation debts unless creditors expressly agree or fail to object within 30 days of notice by registered letter or technology (Art. 224). | لا يترتب على تحول الشركة نشوء شخص ذي صفة اعتبارية جديد، وتظل الشركة محتفظة بحقوقها ومسؤولة عن التزاماتها السابقة للتحول. للشركاء أو المساهمين الذين يعترضون على قرار التحول التخارج من الشركة بناء على طلب مكتوب يقدم إليها خلال (خمسة عشر) يومًا من تاريخ صدور القرار. | An LLC-to-JSC/SJSC conversion (common pre-IPO or pre-VC round) needs a 3/4 partners' vote (unanimity for SJSC), keeps contracts in place without assignment, and gives dissenters a 15-day appraisal exit — a cash-out contingency for the deal model. Contract reviewers: change-of-form clauses in counterparties' contracts are not triggered by a new legal entity, but may still be drafted to bite. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 225-229; Regs Art. 87 | Merger is by absorption into an existing company or by amalgamation into a new company; a merger proposal must be approved by each party under its articles-amendment rules and must set out the terms, the nature and value of the consideration including the interests/shares allotted, and each party's ability to pay its debts; a company in liquidation may merge; the merger is valid only after valuation of each party's assets; consideration is interests or shares in the absorbing or new company; the competent authority may regulate cash for fractions, compensation of dissenters and voting by conflicted holders (Art. 225). The Regulations govern mergers into a 100% parent or between companies with identical owners (Art. 226): Regs Art. 87 — a wholly-owned subsidiary is merged by a resolution of the parent alone, with a managers'/board solvency statement for each party, without a merger proposal or asset valuation; sister companies with identical owners merge by resolutions of each, without asset valuation. Each party must announce the merger at least 30 days before the date set for the decision; creditors of the merged company may object by registered letter (or the means stated in the announcement) within 15 days of the announcement, and must be paid if due or adequately secured if not; an unpaid/unsecured objector may apply to the court no later than 10 days before the decision date, and the court may order payment or security, or suspend or postpone the merger before it takes effect, or award damages afterwards (Art. 227). The merger takes effect on registration of the merged company's data in the absorbing company's Commercial Register entry, or on registration of the new company (Art. 228); all rights, obligations, assets and contracts of the merged company pass to the absorbing/new company as successor (Art. 229). | على كل شركة طرف في الاندماج الإعلان عنه قبل مدة لا تقل عن (ثلاثين) يومًا على الأقل من التاريخ المحدد لاتخاذ القرار بشأن مقترح الاندماج والتصويت عليه. يكون لأي من دائني الشركة المندمجة الاعتراض على الاندماج ... خلال (خمسة عشر) يومًا من تاريخ الإعلان. يسري قرار الاندماج ويعد نافذًا من تاريخ قيد بيانات الشركة المندمجة في سجل الشركة الدامجة لدى السجل التجاري. | Integration timeline for a statutory merger: valuation → proposal approved by each company (LLC 3/4 of capital; JSC 3/4 of represented votes at an EGA) → public announcement ≥ 30 days before the vote → creditor objections within 15 days of the announcement → court applications up to 10 days before the vote → vote → registration (effective date; universal succession, so contracts transfer without counterparty consent unless they contain change-of-control or anti-assignment-by-merger terms). Intra-group mergers of wholly-owned subsidiaries are simplified. Competition clearance (GAC economic-concentration filing) and CMA rules for listed parties are separate gates `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Art. 230; Regs Art. 88 | Squeeze-out and sell-out in JSCs (subject to the Capital Market Law): a person, or persons acting in concert, who reach 90% or more of voting shares directly or indirectly, or unconditionally contract to buy that proportion, must disclose it to shareholders; any shareholder may within 90 days of the disclosure require the holder to make an offer for his shares (sell-out). The 90% holder may within 60 days apply to the competent authority for approval of a compulsory offer forcing the remaining shareholders to sell (squeeze-out); shareholders may contest the price in court within 60 days of the offer; only the competent authority may suspend a compulsory offer; settlement within 7 days after the objection period unless the court orders otherwise. Regs Art. 88: the disclosure goes through the board, which informs the other shareholders; a sell-out offer must be made in cash within 60 days of the request at not less than the highest price the holder paid in the preceding 12 months; the squeeze-out application to the Ministry must attach the offer and price, likewise not below the highest price paid in the preceding 12 months; after the objection period the purchaser completes through the board, which places the cash in a bank account for the affected shareholders and updates the register. | يجب عند قيام شخص، أو أكثر من شخص يتصرفون بالاتفاق، بزيادة ملكيتهم إلى حد تبلغ فيه ... نسبة (تسعين في المائة) أو أكثر من أسهم شركة المساهمة التي لها حقوق تصويت ... الإفصاح عن ذلك لمساهمي الشركة. | Acquisitions of 90%+ of an unlisted JSC carry both a minority put (90 days) and a squeeze-out call (application within 60 days), priced at no less than the highest price paid in the prior 12 months — relevant to price-protection and earn-out structures. There is no equivalent for LLCs or SJSCs in the text read; drag-along under Art. 113/181 is the LLC/SJSC route. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 231-234; Regs Arts. 89-92 | A company, even in liquidation, may divide into two or more companies of any form (Art. 231); the division resolution is passed under the articles-amendment rules and must state the number of partners/shareholders, their shares in the resulting and the divided company, and the allocation of rights, assets and liabilities (Art. 232). Resulting companies succeed the divided company to the extent allocated, but creditors of the divided company may claim against any of the resulting companies, which are jointly liable, unless creditors agree to the transfer of their claims to a specific resulting company (Art. 233). Regs Arts. 89-92: the division resolution requires the amendment quorum; owners receive interests/shares pro rata unless they agree otherwise; the manager/board prepares a proposal stating reasons, the assets and liabilities divided, an accredited valuer's fair-value report (not needed for a pro rata division), the shares to be received, and any creditor agreements; the proposal goes to owners at least 21 days before the vote; the division takes effect on registration of the amended articles of the divided company and of the resulting company. | تكون الشركة الناشئة عن التقسيم خلفًا للشركة محلّ التقسيم في حدود ما آل إليها وفقًا لقرار التقسيم. ومع ذلك، يكون لدائني الشركة محل التقسيم مطالبة الشركتين أو الشركات الناشئة عن التقسيم بأداء الديون والالتزامات ... وتكون الشركتان أو الشركات مسؤولة بالتضامن. | Carve-outs by statutory division leave all resulting companies jointly liable to pre-division creditors unless each creditor consents to the allocation — a post-closing indemnity point for buyers of a divided entity. No creditor objection period is stated for division in the text read; the protection is joint liability. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 235-241 | Foreign companies carrying on activity in the Kingdom are subject to the Law (other than its incorporation provisions), without prejudice to treaties and other laws (Art. 235), and operate through a branch, representative office or other form under the Investment Law (Art. 236). Each branch/representative office must show its Saudi address and the foreign company's full name, address and head office on all papers (Art. 237). The branch registration application must state its fiscal year; a branch (not a representative office) must prepare financial statements for its Saudi activity under Saudi standards and deposit them with the auditor's report within six months of the fiscal year end per the Regulations; the branch manager may appoint the auditor under delegation from the foreign company (Art. 238). The branch/office is the foreign company's domicile for its Saudi activity and subject to all Saudi laws (Art. 239). If the foreign company operates before licensing and registration, or beyond its licence, the company and the persons who carried on the activity are jointly liable (Art. 240). A foreign company present to perform specific works for a limited period is registered temporarily and deregistered on completion after settling its rights and obligations, unless it regularises for continuation; the Ministry and the Ministry of Investment set the rules (Art. 241). | فيما عدا مكاتب التمثيل، يجب على فرع الشركة الأجنبية إعداد القوائم المالية الخاصة بنشاطه داخل المملكة وفق المعايير المحاسبية المعتمدة في المملكة، وإيداع هذه الوثائق وتقرير مراجع الحسابات عنها، خلال (ستة) أشهر من تاريخ انتهاء السنة المالية الخاصة بنشاط ذلك الفرع. | A branch needs a MISA licence and Commercial Register entry before signing Saudi contracts; the individuals who sign for an unregistered branch are personally liable with the company. Branch annual compliance: audited branch accounts deposited within six months; the branch manager's authority comes from the foreign company's registered power of attorney `[model knowledge — verify]` and should be checked on counterparty authority reviews. Temporary registrations (project branches) lapse on project completion. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 242-245 | Before the partners, assembly or shareholders resolve to dissolve, the managers or directors must prepare a statement confirming they have examined the company's position and that its assets suffice to pay its debts by the end of the proposed liquidation and that the company is not distressed under the Bankruptcy Law; it is put to the owners within 30 days of preparation; if it shows insufficiency or distress, the owners may not resolve to dissolve, on pain of joint liability for remaining debts (Art. 242). General grounds of dissolution: expiry of term unless extended; agreement of the partners/shareholders to dissolve; a final judgment dissolving or annulling the company (Art. 243). On dissolution the company enters liquidation and keeps personality for that purpose; the owners must start liquidation; the Art. 242 statement must be prepared (or be no older than 30 days); an insolvent or distressed company must apply to the court to open Bankruptcy Law liquidation; liquidation in breach makes the owners/managers/directors jointly liable for remaining debts; a public non-profit company needs Ministry approval (Art. 244). Liquidation follows the articles or the owners' agreement, failing which the Law (Art. 245). | يلتزم مديرو الشركة أو أعضاء مجلس إدارتها -قبل اتخاذ الشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمين قرارًا بحل الشركة- بإعداد بيان يفيد بقيامهم بفحص أوضاع الشركة، ويتضمن التأكيد على أن أصول الشركة تكفي لسداد ديونها بنهاية مدة التصفية المقترحة وأن الشركة غير متعثرة وفقًا لنظام الإفلاس. | Voluntary liquidation is available only for solvent companies, gated by a managers' solvency statement (false statements are criminal under Art. 260(a)); insolvent entities must go through the Bankruptcy Law. Post-acquisition clean-up of dormant entities: use the Regs Art. 93 short-form route (see Arts. 252-259 row). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 246-251 | On dissolution the managers'/board's authority ends but they continue to manage and are treated as liquidators towards third parties until a liquidator is appointed; assemblies continue with powers not conflicting with the liquidator's; owners keep inspection rights (Art. 246). One or more liquidators (owners or not) are appointed; liquidation may not exceed three years, extendable only by court order (Art. 247). The liquidator is appointed by the owners under the articles-amendment rules within 60 days of dissolution, failing which by the court on application of any owner or interested person; where dissolution is by judgment, the court appoints; the court first requires the Art. 242 statement or equivalent evidence within 30 days and opens Bankruptcy Law proceedings if assets are insufficient; the appointment decision must fix powers, fees, restrictions and the liquidation period (Art. 248). The liquidator registers and publishes his appointment with the Commercial Register, without which it cannot be relied on against third parties (Art. 249); he is removed in the manner appointed, or by the court on an owner's or creditor's application, with a replacement named (Art. 250); multiple liquidators act jointly and unanimously unless authorised otherwise (Art. 251). | يكون تعيين المصفي بقرار من الشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمين وفقًا للأوضاع المقررة لتعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس بحسب شكل الشركة، وذلك خلال مدة لا تتجاوز (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ انقضاء الشركة. يجب ألا تتجاوز مدة التصفية بموجب النظام (ثلاث) سنوات. | Liquidation checklist: solvency statement (≤ 30 days old) → dissolution and liquidator appointment resolution at the amendment quorum (3/4 for an LLC) within 60 days → Commercial Register registration → maximum three years. Directors remain de facto liquidators, with liquidator liability, until the appointment is registered. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 252-259; Regs Art. 93 | The liquidator represents the company, converts assets to cash (auction or best-price sale), may sell assets en bloc or contribute them to another company only with the appointing body's authority, may not start new business except to complete existing business, binds the company within his powers, and his powers end with the liquidation or its period, whichever first (Art. 252). Managers/directors hand over records; within 90 days of starting he prepares an inventory of assets, rights and liabilities with an auditor's report (extendable by the appointing body); annually he prepares financial statements and a report, files copies with the Commercial Register and submits them to the owners (Art. 253). If assets prove insufficient he must immediately inform owners and creditors and apply to open Bankruptcy Law proceedings (Art. 254). He pays due debts by priority, reserves for deferred or disputed debts (liquidation debts rank first), returns capital and distributes the surplus per the articles or pro rata (Art. 255). On completion he files a detailed financial report; liquidation ends on the appointing body's approval; he registers and publishes the end of liquidation, effective against third parties only on deregistration (Art. 257). He is liable for exceeding his powers or for errors (Art. 258), with a five-year limitation from deregistration except for forgery and fraud (Art. 259). Regs Art. 93: a company that never carried on any activity may be dissolved by a unanimous owners' resolution submitted to the Ministry containing a declaration that it never traded, has no assets, debts (present, future or disputed), zakat, tax or fees due, plus a joint and several undertaking by all owners to pay any debts that emerge from their own funds; the undertaking counts as the final liquidation report, the closing accounts and the deregistration request under Art. 257. | يعد المصفي خلال (تسعين) يومًا من مباشرته أعماله جردًا بجميع أصول الشركة وما لها من حقوق وما عليها من التزامات. يجب على المصفي قيد وشهر انتهاء التصفية لدى السجل التجاري. ولا يعتد بانتهاء التصفية في مواجهة الغير إلا من تاريخ شطب قيد الشركة من السجل التجاري. (اللائحة م93) يجوز للشركاء أو الجمعية العامة أو المساهمين إصدار قرار بالإجماع لحل الشركة إذا لم تزاول أي نشاط ولم تقم بأي عمل. | Dormant-shell clean-up after integration: the Regs Art. 93 short-form route replaces a full liquidation but makes all owners jointly liable for any later-discovered debt. Zakat/tax clearance in practice is required before deregistration `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 260-261 | Imprisonment up to three years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000 for: (a) any manager, officer, director, auditor or liquidator who deliberately records false or misleading information in the financial statements, reports, capital-reduction statements, solvency statements or other statements put to the owners, or deliberately omits a material fact to misrepresent the financial position; (b) any manager, officer or director who uses the company's funds, powers or votes knowingly against its interests for personal ends, to favour a company or person, or to benefit from a transaction in which he has an interest; (c) a liquidator who does likewise or prefers a creditor without lawful cause (Art. 260). Imprisonment up to one year and/or a fine up to SAR 1,000,000 for, among others: an auditor who fails to report apparent criminal violations to the company; vote-buying; falsely announcing registration of an unregistered company; a public official disclosing company secrets; publishing false names to attract subscriptions; deciding, paying or receiving dividends in bad faith contrary to the Law or the articles (and an auditor who fails to report it); false declarations on in-kind valuations, allocation of interests/shares or payment of their value; any manager, officer, director or auditor who fails to call (or to take steps to call) the assembly on learning that losses reached the Arts. 132/182 threshold; exploiting or disclosing company secrets to harm the company; deliberately obstructing persons entitled to inspect records; a court-appointed inspector who falsifies or omits material facts (Art. 261). | يعاقب بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (ثلاث) سنوات وبغرامة لا تزيد على (5,000,000) خمسة ملايين ريال، أو بإحدى هاتين العقوبتين: كل مدير أو مسؤول أو عضو مجلس إدارة أو مراجع حسابات أو مصفّ، سجَّل متعمدًا بيانات أو معلومات كاذبة أو مضللة في القوائم المالية. يعاقب بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (سنة) وبغرامة لا تزيد على (1,000,000) مليون ريال ... كل مدير أو مسؤول أو عضو مجلس إدارة أو مراجع حسابات، لم يدعُ الجمعية العامة للشركاء أو المساهمين ... عند علمه ببلوغ الخسائر الحدود المقدرة. | Personal criminal exposure of nominee managers/directors: false financial statements or solvency statements (3 years/SAR 5m) and failure to convene the losses assembly (1 year/SAR 1m). Diligence on a loss-making target should therefore check the losses-assembly minutes; integration plans should regularise before new managers are appointed. Public Prosecution investigates these offences (Art. 265). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 262-267, 269-271; Regs Art. 94 | Fine up to SAR 500,000 for violations including: obstructing the calling or holding of an assembly or preventing an owner from attending or voting; failing to call the annual assembly within the statutory period; accepting or retaining JSC board membership contrary to the Law (and directors who knew and did not object); a director obtaining a loan or guarantee contrary to the Law; failing to keep accounting records or to prepare or deposit financial statements; failing to provide the competent authority with required documents; failing to make documents available to owners; failing to prepare and record minutes; omitting the Art. 12 particulars; acting as auditor despite a disqualification; failing to register the company or an amendment of its articles with the Commercial Register; deliberately recording untrue data in the articles, the application or supporting documents (and those who sign or register them knowingly); professional-company breaches; a liquidator failing to register his appointment or the end of liquidation; failing to take corrective action after notification of a violation; an auditor failing in statutory duties; any company or officer failing to apply the Law and Regulations or to comply with the competent authority's rules and decisions without reasonable cause (Art. 262). A Ministry committee of at least three members chaired by a legally qualified person imposes these fines (except for listed JSCs, where the CMA Board does); the Minister may designate violations penalised directly without the committee; the penalised person may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of notification; Regs Art. 94: notification may be electronic to a mobile number or e-mail registered with the Commercial Register or the National Information Center (Art. 267). Penalties are graduated by gravity and circumstances; repeat offences under Arts. 260-261 within three years of a final conviction double the penalty (Art. 263). The court may, in addition to or instead of Arts. 260-261 penalties, warn, order preventive or corrective steps, order cessation, or ban from the board of a listed JSC; the competent authority may warn or order corrective steps instead of Art. 262 fines (Art. 264). The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes Arts. 260-261 offences (Art. 265). The competent court hears all civil and criminal cases under the Law and Regulations except for listed JSCs, which go to the Committee for the Resolution of Securities Disputes (Art. 266). Penalties do not prejudice civil claims for damages (Art. 269). The competent authority supervises companies' compliance with the Law and their articles, may inspect, examine accounts and demand records and minutes through its officers or experts, and may send observers to general assemblies (Art. 270); all company officers must give Ministry (and, for listed or listing companies, CMA) representatives access to records and information (Art. 271). | يعاقب بغرامة لا تزيد على (500,000) خمسمائة ألف ريال: ... كل من لم يؤدِّ واجبه في دعوة الجمعية العامة للشركاء أو المساهمين إلى الانعقاد خلال المدة المقررة لانعقادها؛ ... كل من أهمل في أداء واجبه في إعداد محاضر الاجتماعات وتدوينها؛ ... كل من تخلف عن قيد تعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس لدى السجل التجاري. تتولى المحكمة المختصة النظر والفصل في جميع الدعاوى المدنية والجزائية والمنازعات الناشئة عن تطبيق أحكام النظام واللوائح ... وذلك فيما عدا ما يتعلق بشركات المساهمة المدرجة في السوق المالية. | The administrative-fine list is effectively the entity-compliance checklist: annual assembly on time, financial statements deposited, minutes kept, amendments registered, documents bearing the Art. 12 particulars, auditor properly qualified. Committee decisions are notified electronically to the registered mobile/e-mail, so keep Commercial Register contact data current or appeal windows (30 days) will be missed. Forum: Commercial Court for unlisted companies (the "competent court" under the Commercial Courts Law `[model knowledge — verify]`), CRSD for listed JSCs. Ministry inspection powers extend to minutes and registers, which is why registers must exist in Arabic-readable form. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
| Arts. 274, 277 | The CMA supervises listed JSCs and issues their rules, including merger rules where a party is listed; the competent authority issues a corporate-governance regulation for JSCs (and the Ministry may issue one for other companies); the Ministry sets rules to obtain beneficial-ownership (المستفيد الحقيقي) information from companies other than listed JSCs; the Minister and the CMA Board issue the necessary rules (Art. 274). The Minister and the CMA Board issue the Regulations within 180 days of publication, covering rules, periods, procedures, documents and the use of technology for notices, meetings, participation and voting; any procedure under the Law or Regulations may be electronic, including incorporation and amendment applications, registration and publication, signature of applications, documents and registers, and deposit of financial statements (Art. 277). | للوزارة وضع القواعد والإجراءات اللازمة التي تكفل حصولها على معلومات المستفيد الحقيقي من الشركات الخاضعة لأحكام النظام وذلك فيما عدا ما يتعلق بشركات المساهمة المدرجة في السوق المالية. يجوز أن يكون أي من الإجراءات المنصوص عليها في النظام، أو اللوائح؛ إلكترونيًّا. | Closing checklist: the UBO register/filing with the Ministry must be updated after a share transfer; the Ministry's UBO rules (issued under Art. 274(3)) were not read for this file `[model knowledge — verify]` — obtain them before advising on thresholds or deadlines. Electronic execution and filing of corporate documents has express statutory backing. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
| Arts. 280-281; Royal Decree M/132 cl. 2-3; Regs Art. 95 | The Law replaces the Companies Law (M/3 of 28/1/1437H) and the Professional Companies Law (M/17 of 26/1/1441H) and repeals conflicting provisions (Art. 280); it enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 281). Decree cl. 2: the Law does not prejudice the powers of the Saudi Central Bank and the CMA. Decree cl. 3: companies existing at the effective date must conform to the Law within two years from that date; exceptionally, the Ministry and the CMA determine which provisions apply to them during that period. Regs Art. 95: the Regulations apply from the Law's effective date. | على الشركات القائمة عند نفاذ النظام ... تعديل أوضاعها وفقًا لأحكامه خلال مدة لا تزيد على (سنتين) تبدأ من تاريخ نفاذه. يعمل بالنظام بعد (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ نشره في الجريدة الرسمية. (اللائحة م95) تسري أحكام اللائحة من تاريخ نفاذ النظام. | The transition ended 19 January 2025; a company whose registered articles still follow the 2015-Law templates (e.g. old statutory-reserve or partner-liability-on-losses wording, "one-person company" terminology) is now non-conforming and its amendment is a closing-checklist item. Corporate documents dated before 19 January 2023 must be read against the 2015 Law, whose rules differed on several points flagged in this file. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- Post-2022 amendments: the portal marks no article as amended and names no amending instrument, but the Bureau of Experts "recent updates" listing includes the Companies Law and the portal has a "versions" tab that was not opened. Confirm from the Umm al-Qura index whether any royal decree has amended the Law since M/132 (the brief mentioned a 1447/03/12H listing date, which on my reading belongs to the adjacent Expropriation Law entry).
- Implementing Regulations: confirm the decision number and date (Ministerial Decision 284 of 23/6/1444H) and whether the Ministry has amended or re-issued the Regulations since 2023 (the text read is the 1444H/2023 edition); several rows rely on Regs Arts. 3, 6-8, 10, 14-33, 62-69, 86-95.
- Board size: the Law fixes only a minimum of three directors for a JSC; confirm whether any Ministry rule imposes a maximum for unlisted JSCs (the CMA governance regulations do for listed companies).
- Chairman/CEO separation: Art. 78 as read does not prohibit combining the roles in an unlisted JSC; confirm whether the Ministry's corporate-governance regulation for unlisted companies (Art. 274(2)) exists and imposes separation or other board rules.
- LLC unanimity matters: Art. 172 requires unanimity only for raising nominal value and suspending pre-emption; confirm that no other provision (or Ministry model articles) reintroduces the 2015-Law unanimity for changing nationality or increasing partners' obligations, and whether Ministry model articles are mandatory in content.
- Losses rule consequences: confirm in practice whether the Ministry treats a failure to hold the Art. 182/132 assembly as grounds for administrative action beyond the Art. 261(h) offence, and whether partners face any residual liability (the 2015-Law joint liability is absent from the text read).
- Change of control of a corporate partner: Art. 178 speaks of assignment of the interest; confirm whether Saudi courts or the Ministry treat an indirect transfer as triggering statutory redemption.
- Enforceability of shareholders' agreements governed by foreign law or providing for foreign-seated arbitration against the registered Arabic articles (Art. 11), and the treatment of English-language SHAs in the Commercial Court.
- Practical notarisation: whether the Ministry's platform still requires Ministry of Justice electronic notarisation for LLC share transfers and amendments, and which cases fall within Regs Art. 2's "notarisation authority" category.
- Beneficial-ownership rules under Art. 274(3): the Ministry's UBO rules (thresholds, filing deadline after a transfer, sanctions) were not read.
- Language of minutes and registers: the Law is silent; confirm the Ministry's inspection practice on English-only minutes and whether Arabic minutes are required for registration of resolutions with the Commercial Register.
- Filing platform names and deadlines for the manager's/board's activity report to the Ministry (Regs Arts. 31, 65) and the financial-statements deposit (Regs Art. 5), and whether late-filing fines are being imposed in practice.
- SJSC and JSC interaction with CMA rules where the company is "seeking listing" (Arts. 111(1), 271): confirm at what point CMA rules displace the Ministry's Regulations.

## How skills should use this file

- **Stop rule (all skills):** if the row needed is missing, or the relevant sentence carries `[model knowledge — verify]`, the skill must say so explicitly in its output and either stop or flag the point for Saudi counsel; it must never fill the gap from memory. Rows tagged `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` may be cited by article number; anything in the Practical effect column that carries an inline `[model knowledge — verify]` tag must be quoted with that tag attached.
- **corporate-legal `entity-compliance`** — load at the step where the annual compliance calendar is built. Cite Arts. 16-17 (financial statements within six months; Regs Art. 5 electronic deposit), Arts. 18-20 (auditor appointment and rotation), Art. 88 (JSC annual assembly within six months), Art. 165 (LLC annual assembly within six months, 21-day notice), Arts. 121-122 and 167 with Regs Arts. 31, 65 (45-day auditor and 21-day shareholder/Ministry deliveries), Art. 147 (SJSC six-month presentation), Art. 238 (branch accounts within six months), Art. 112 (JSC register updates within 15 days), Arts. 93 and 148 (registration of resolutions within 15 days), Arts. 262-267 (fines and appeal window). Cross-reference `filing-calendar.md` for the consolidated dates; where that file and this file differ, this file's article rows control and the discrepancy must be flagged. Computed dates: "annual assembly deadline = fiscal year end + 6 months (Art. 88 / Art. 165)"; "auditor pack date = assembly date − 45 calendar days (Art. 121 / Art. 167)"; "shareholder/Ministry pack date = assembly date − 21 calendar days (Art. 122 / Art. 167; Regs Arts. 31, 65)"; all days are calendar days (Art. 1). Attach the tag of the rows used to every computed date.
- **corporate-legal `written-consent`** — load when drafting or validating a resolution by circulation. JSC board: Art. 82 (urgent matters only; majority of all members; ratification at next meeting) and Art. 83 (minutes register). JSC shareholders: Arts. 100-101 (articles clause, chairman's proposal, circulation to all, > 50% / ≥ 75% of all voting rights, excluded matters, register entry). SJSC: Art. 149 (articles clause and articles quorum only; no statutory fallback). LLC: Art. 166 (written vote; > 50% of capital; second-round meeting rule), Art. 172 (≥ 75% for amendments; unanimity items), Art. 165(5) (minutes register), Regs Art. 63 (manager removal at > 50%). Single-owner entities: Arts. 98, 150, 157 (written decisions entered in the register). Formula outputs must state the article and the tag. If the articles of incorporation/association are not available to the skill, it must say that the statutory default is being applied and that the articles may set a higher threshold.
- **corporate-legal `board-minutes`** — load at the template-selection step. JSC board: Art. 80 (call, quorum half, majority, casting vote, technology), Art. 81 (proxies only under an articles clause, one per member), Art. 83 (secretary drafts; signed by chair, attending directors and secretary; register), Art. 28 (named dissents), Art. 71 (interest declarations recorded, abstention), Art. 76 (attendance disclosure in board report). Assemblies: Arts. 90-91 and Regs Art. 23 (convening, 21-day notice content), Arts. 92-93 (quorum and majorities), Art. 97 (minutes content and signatories), Art. 84 and Regs Arts. 24-30 (electronic participation, proxies, authentication). LLC assemblies: Arts. 165, 168-169. Language of record: Art. 7 requires Arabic only for the articles; the Law is silent on minutes — the skill must flag the `[model knowledge — verify]` note in the Art. 83 row and default to bilingual minutes with an Arabic text.
- **corporate-legal `diligence-issue-extraction`** and **`tabular-review`** — load at the corporate-documents category step. Extraction targets and rows: share-transfer restrictions (Art. 178 LLC pre-emption and 30-day window; Art. 111 and Arts. 151-154 JSC/SJSC restrictions; Art. 44 and Art. 56 partnerships; Art. 25 registration as the transfer event); change-of-control and major-transaction approvals (Art. 75 sale of > 50% of assets; Art. 93 three-quarters matters; Art. 172 three-quarters matters; Art. 220 transformation; Art. 230 90% squeeze-out/sell-out); drag/tag (Arts. 113, 181, 90% insertion vote); losses trigger (Art. 132 JSC: 60-day disclosure and 180-day EGA; Art. 182 LLC: 60-day assembly; Art. 261(h) offence); related-party approvals (Art. 27 with Regs Arts. 16-17 thresholds; Art. 71 disclosure and abstention; Art. 72 director loans void; Regs Art. 62 LLC application); auditor status (Arts. 18-19; Regs Arts. 6-7); registers and minutes (Arts. 83, 97, 112, 148, 165); unpaid capital (Arts. 15, 105, 115); capital reductions (Arts. 135, 176); historic dividends (Art. 22); pledges (Art. 114, Art. 180, Regs Arts. 46-50, 66-69); conformity with the 2022 Law after the transition (Decree cl. 3 row). Materiality wording must cite the article; where a finding depends on the target's articles, the skill must request them rather than assume the statutory default.
- **corporate-legal `closing-checklist`** — load at checklist generation. LLC share purchase: Art. 178 waivers or expiry of the redemption window; Art. 172 approval of the amended articles (≥ 75% or higher per articles); Art. 8 and Regs Art. 2 (written amendment, identity verification, Commercial Register registration); Art. 25 (transfer effective on registration); Art. 162 (register new manager and any power limits); Art. 274(3) UBO update (`[model knowledge — verify]` for the Ministry's rules); Art. 12 stationery and document particulars; Art. 11 SHA/family-charter conformity. Unlisted JSC/SJSC: Art. 112 register entry and 15-day Commercial Register update; Art. 91(3) or Art. 146(6) waiver meeting; Art. 93(5) or Art. 148(2) 15-day registration of resolutions; Regs Arts. 46-50 pledge entry or release in the shareholders' register. Pre-closing corporate approvals of the seller/buyer: Art. 75 (asset sale > 50%), Art. 93 (3/4 matters), Art. 27/71 (related-party authorisation). MISA licence amendment and other regulator consents are outside this file and must be flagged as such.
- **corporate-legal `integration-management`** — load at the legal-entity rationalisation step. Statutory merger: Arts. 225-229 and Regs Art. 87 (proposal at the amendment quorum; valuation; 30-day announcement before the vote; 15-day creditor objection from the announcement; court application ≥ 10 days before the vote; effective on Commercial Register registration; universal succession; simplified intra-group merger). Division: Arts. 231-234 and Regs Arts. 89-92 (21-day proposal circulation; joint liability to pre-division creditors). Transformation: Arts. 220-224 (amendment quorum, unanimity for SJSC, 15-day dissenter exit, 30-day creditor notice for partnerships). Dissolution of surplus entities: Arts. 242-259 and Regs Art. 93 (solvency statement, 60-day liquidator appointment, 3-year cap, short-form for never-traded companies). Managers'/directors' personal exposure: Arts. 28, 260-261. Competition, CMA and MISA gates are flagged `[model knowledge — verify]` in the merger row and must be raised separately.
- **commercial-legal `review`, `vendor-agreement-review`, `nda-review`, `saas-msa-review`** — load only for counterparty capacity and authority checks: Art. 12 (document particulars), Arts. 77-79 (JSC signatory: chairman by default, MD/CEO if the articles say so), Art. 142 (SJSC president/manager/chairman), Art. 162 (LLC manager as registered; internal limits not effective against third parties unless registered), Art. 37 (partnership manager), Art. 240 (unregistered foreign branch — joint personal liability), Art. 9 (companies under formation). These skills must not cite the governance rows for anything beyond authority and capacity.
- **employment-legal skills** — this file is not a source for employment questions; the only relevant rows are Art. 27/Regs Art. 13 (statutory non-compete of managers and directors) and Art. 127 (employee share allocations without pre-emption), and they may be cited only for corporate-side approval requirements.
# Anti-Concealment Law — نظام مكافحة التستر

## Instrument

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Jurisdiction | `ksa` |
| Instrument | Anti-Concealment Law (Anti-Commercial-Concealment / "Tasattur" Law) / نظام مكافحة التستر |
| Issuing instrument | Royal Decree No. M/4 dated 1/1/1442H (20/08/2020), approving Council of Ministers Resolution No. 785 dated 28/12/1441H (both printed in the portal text) |
| Issuing authority | Council of Ministers (law); Ministry of Commerce is "the Ministry" that administers it (Art. 1); the Implementing Regulation is issued by the Minister of Commerce in agreement with the Minister of Interior (Art. 19) |
