## Scope

The Law criminalises "tasattur": any agreement or arrangement by which a person enables a non-Saudi to practise, for the non-Saudi's own account, an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, using the licence, approval, commercial register, or name issued to the concealer (Arts. 2–3). "Economic activity" is drawn widely: any profit-seeking activity that requires an approval or licence, whether commercial, investment, service, professional, industrial, or agricultural (Art. 1). Both the enabler and the non-Saudi beneficiary commit a crime, as does anyone who knowingly incites, assists, or advises (Art. 3(c)), and a separate tier of administrative "violations" catches the mechanics of concealment even without a completed crime: granting a non-Saudi instruments of absolute disposal over an establishment, or running the business through a bank account that does not belong to it (Art. 4). Enforcement is split between the Ministry of Commerce (oversight, reports, detection, and an internal committee for Art. 4 violations), the Public Prosecution (investigation of crimes), and the Criminal Court (trial of crimes) (Art. 5).

The Law does not itself say who counts as a properly licensed foreign investor; that question is answered by the Investment Law (Royal Decree No. M/19 dated 16/1/1446H) and its registration regime, which this file cross-references. The Law does not cover the Implementing Regulation's detail on good-faith cases, travel bans, deportation procedure, or reward procedures, all of which it delegates to that Regulation (Arts. 4, 8, 11, 18).

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 1 (definitions) | "Non-Saudi" means a natural or legal person who does not hold Saudi nationality and is not treated as a holder of it. "Economic activity" means any activity aimed at profit for which approvals or licences from the competent authorities are required, whether commercial, investment, service, professional, industrial, agricultural, or otherwise. "Proceeds" means funds arising directly or indirectly from the crimes in Art. 3(a) and (b), including funds converted wholly or partly into similar funds. | غير السعودي: الشخص ذو الصفة الطبيعية أو الاعتبارية الذي لا يحمل الجنسية العربية السعودية ولا يعامل معاملة حاملها. النشاط الاقتصادي: كل نشاط يستهدف تحقيق الربح ويشترط لممارسته الحصول على موافقات أو تراخيص من الجهات المختصة؛ سواء أكان تجاريًّا أم استثماريًّا أم خدميًّا أم مهنيًّا أم صناعيًّا أم زراعيًّا أم غير ذلك. | A foreign-owned company (legal person) is itself a "non-Saudi" for this Law, so a Saudi-registered company that is beneficially foreign-controlled without an investment registration is within scope. Which persons are "treated as" Saudi nationals (e.g. GCC nationals, Premium Residency holders for permitted activities) is not defined in the Law; it depends on other instruments and the Implementing Regulation `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 2 | Definition of tasattur: an agreement or arrangement through which a person enables another, non-Saudi, person to practise an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, using the licence or approval issued to the concealer. | يقصد بالتستر اتفاق أو ترتيب يُمكِّن من خلاله شخصٌ شخصًا آخر غير سعودي من ممارسة نشاط اقتصادي في المملكة غير مرخص له بممارسته باستخدام الترخيص أو الموافقة الصادرة للمتستر. | The offence has three elements a reviewer should test: (1) an agreement or arrangement (formal or informal); (2) a non-Saudi practising an activity for which the non-Saudi holds no licence/approval of their own; (3) use of the Saudi party's licence, approval, CR, or name. The gating question is therefore whether the non-Saudi is themselves licensed, which is why the Investment Law registration (see the Investment Law row below) is decisive. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 3(a) | Crime of the enabler: any person enabling a non-Saudi to practise, for the non-Saudi's own account, an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, including enabling the non-Saudi to use the enabler's name, licence or approval, commercial register, trade name, or the like. | قيام شخص بتمكين غير السعودي من أن يمارس -لحسابه الخاص- نشاطًا اقتصاديًّا في المملكة غير مرخص له بممارسته، ويشمل ذلك تمكينه غير السعودي من استعمال: اسمه، أو الترخيص أو الموافقة الصادرة له، أو سجله التجاري، أو اسمه التجاري، أو نحو ذلك. | The enabler is "any person" (شخص), not only a Saudi national: a licensed foreign investor who lends its licence to an unlicensed non-Saudi is also caught. "For the non-Saudi's own account" (لحسابه الخاص) is the dividing line between concealment and a lawful employment or agency relationship; who bears the profit and loss is the practical test `[model knowledge — verify]` for how courts apply it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 3(b)–(d) | (b) Crime of the non-Saudi: practising an economic activity for their own account in the Kingdom without a licence, through the person who enabled them. (c) Participation: anyone who incites, assists, or gives advice for the commission of either crime, with knowledge, is an accomplice whenever the crime is committed or continued on the basis of that incitement, assistance, or advice. (d) Obstructing or preventing enforcement officers from performing their duties by any means, including non-disclosure of information or providing incorrect or misleading information, is a crime. | ب. قيام غير السعودي بممارسة نشاط اقتصادي لحسابه الخاص في المملكة غير مرخص له بممارسته، وذلك من خلال الشخص الممكّن له. ج. الاشتراك في ارتكاب أيّ من الجريمتين ... ويعد شريكًا في الجريمة كل من حرض أو ساعد أو قدم المشورة في ارتكابها مع علمه بذلك. د. عرقلة أو منع ممارسة المكلفين بتنفيذ أحكام النظام من أداء واجباتهم بأي وسيلة، بما في ذلك عدم الإفصاح عن المعلومات، أو تقديم معلومات غير صحيحة أو مضللة. | Paragraph (c) exposes advisers, accountants, and nominee-service providers who knowingly structure a concealment arrangement. Paragraph (d) means that a company's response to a Ministry inspection (withholding records, false statements) is itself a crime carrying the Art. 9 penalties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 4 | Administrative violations (not crimes): (a) an establishment irregularly granting a non-Saudi instruments that lead to absolute disposal over the establishment; (b) a non-Saudi irregularly holding or using such instruments; (c) an establishment using, in the dealings of its economic activity, a bank account that does not belong to it. The Regulation sets the detail, taking into account cases where the instruments were granted or held in good faith. | أ. قيام أي منشأة بمنح غير السعودي بصورة غير نظامية أدوات تؤدي إلى التصرف على نحو مطلق في المنشأة. ب. حيازة أو استخدام غير السعودي بصورة غير نظامية لأدوات تؤدي إلى التصرف على نحو مطلق في المنشأة. ج. استخدام المنشأة في تعاملاتها الخاصة بنشاطها الاقتصادي حسابًا بنكيًّا آخر غير عائد لها. وتحدد اللائحة الأحكام المتعلقة بهذه المادة، مع مراعاة الحالات التي يكون فيها منح الأدوات أو حيازتها قد تم بحسن نية. | These are the statutory red flags a diligence review must check in a Saudi target: (i) unlimited powers of attorney, blank-signed documents, or bank mandates giving a non-Saudi absolute control; (ii) revenue collected into, or expenses paid from, accounts in a person's name rather than the establishment's. Each is a violation on its own even if no Art. 3 crime is proved. The good-faith carve-out lives in the Implementing Regulation, which was not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 5 | Competences: the Ministry (of Commerce) has oversight, receives reports, and detects crimes and violations; the Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes crimes; the Criminal Court hears crimes; a Ministry committee of at least three members, its chair and at least one member legally qualified, decides Art. 4 violations and imposes Art. 14 penalties. | تختص الوزارة بالرقابة وتلقي البلاغات وضبط الجرائم والمخالفات ... تختص النيابة العامة بالتحقيق والادعاء في الجرائم ... تختص المحكمة الجزائية بالنظر والفصل في الجرائم ... تكوّن بقرار من الوزير لجنة من عدد لا يقل عن ثلاثة أعضاء ... للنظر في مخالفات أحكام المادة (الرابعة). | Two tracks: crimes (Art. 3) go through Public Prosecution to the Criminal Court; violations (Art. 4) are decided administratively by the Ministry committee, with appeal to the Administrative Court (Art. 14(5)). A target's litigation disclosure should be checked against both. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 6 | Enforcement officers designated by the Minister from the Ministry, Municipal and Rural Affairs, HRSD, Environment/Water/Agriculture, the General Authority of Zakat and Tax, and other competent bodies have judicial-enforcement status and may: enter and inspect suspected establishments, offices, branches, warehouses, and vehicles; examine and seize records; view CCTV recordings; require disclosure of information from any person or body; seal premises and safes pending inspection; summon and record statements; and call on the police. Inspection procedures are confidential. Rules for financial rewards to enforcement staff are issued by Council of Ministers resolution. | يتولى ضبط الجرائم والمخالفات ... موظفون من: الوزارة، ووزارة الشؤون البلدية والقروية، ووزارة الموارد البشرية والتنمية الاجتماعية، ووزارة البيئة والمياه والزراعة، والهيئة العامة للزكاة والدخل ... وتكون لهم صفة الضبط الجنائي ... الزيارات الرقابية ودخول المنشآت المشتبه بها ... فحص وضبط السجلات ... الاطلاع على تسجيلات كاميرات المراقبة ... تشميع المواقع والخزائن. | Inspections are multi-agency and can arrive via labour (HRSD) or zakat/tax (now ZATCA, the successor to the authority named in the text `[model knowledge — verify]`) channels. A diligence request list should ask for any inspection visit records, seizure receipts, or summonses in the last five years. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 7 | Crimes and violations may be proved by all means of proof, including electronic evidence. | يكون الإثبات في الجرائم والمخالفات المنصوص عليها في النظام بجميع طرق الإثبات، بما فيها الأدلة الإلكترونية. | WhatsApp messages, bank-app logs, and email approvals showing who really directs the business are admissible; there is no documentary-only rule to hide behind. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 8 | The Ministry may ask the Public Prosecution to impose a travel ban on a person suspected of a crime under the Law (conditions per the Regulation). The Public Prosecution may, on its own motion or at the request of enforcement officers, order a precautionary seizure of funds liable to confiscation for up to sixty days, extendable by order of the Criminal Court; the funds may be left under the management of the interested party or transferred to a competent body. | للوزارة أن تطلب من النيابة العامة منع سفر من يشتبه في ارتكابه أيًّا من الجرائم ... للنيابة العامة ... أن تأمر بالحجز التحفظي على الأموال التي قد تصبح محلًا للمصادرة، لمدة لا تتجاوز (ستين) يومًا. ويمكن تمديد المدة بأمر قضائي من المحكمة الجزائية. | Seizure can freeze the target's operating funds mid-transaction; an escrow or closing-funds-flow check should confirm no seizure order is outstanding. Travel bans on non-Saudi principals affect signing logistics. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 9 | Penalty for any crime under the Law: imprisonment up to five years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000, having regard to the size of the activity, its revenues, the period of practice, and the effects. Penalties are doubled for recidivism (a further crime within three years of a final judgment). The Criminal Court may reduce penalties where the accused, after the Ministry learns of the crime, provides evidence or information that could not otherwise have been obtained and that is relied on to prove the crime. | يعاقب كل من يرتكب أيًّا من الجرائم ... بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (خمس) سنوات وبغرامة لا تزيد على (خمسة) ملايين ريال أو بإحدى هاتين العقوبتين ... تضاعف في حالة العود العقوبات ... للمحكمة الجزائية تخفيف العقوبات ... إذا بادر المتهم ... بتقديم دليل أو معلومة لم يكن من المستطاع الحصول عليها بطريق آخر. | Headline exposure for a finding: five years / SAR 5m per convicted person (enabler, beneficiary, and each accomplice), doubled for repeat offenders. The penalty is "without prejudice to any penalty in any other law" (labour, tax, investment). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 10 | On conviction for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime, the proceeds are confiscated by court judgment whether held by the convicted person or another party; if the proceeds cannot be found or are mixed with lawful funds, other funds of equivalent value are confiscated. The Criminal Court may annul or block any act, contractual or otherwise, whose parties knew or should have known it would impair recovery of confiscable proceeds. Confiscated funds go to the Public Treasury, subject to the rights of good-faith third parties. | تصادر بحكم قضائي المتحصلات بصرف النظر عمّا إذا كانت في حيازة أو ملكية المدان أو أي طرف آخر ... فتصادر بحكم قضائي أي أموال أخرى تعادل قيمة تلك المتحصلات ... للمحكمة الجزائية ... إبطال أو منع تنفيذ أي إجراء أو عمل -تعاقدي أو غير ذلك- ... تؤول الأموال المصادَرة إلى الخزينة العامة للدولة. | A purchase price paid for a concealed business can be reached as "proceeds" or "equivalent value", and the sale itself can be annulled if the buyer should have known. Buyer good faith is the only shield, which is why a documented tasattur review is part of the buyer's protection, not just the seller's. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 11 | The judgment convicting for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime must order publication of its summary once final; the Ministry publishes it in the medium it sees fit. A non-Saudi convicted of any Art. 3 crime is deported and barred from re-entry, per the applicable rules and the Regulation, after serving the sentence and paying fees, taxes, and other obligations set by the court. | يضمَّن الحكم ... النص على نشر ملخصه ... وتنشر الوزارة ملخص الحكم النهائي في الوسيلة التي تراها مناسبة. يترتب على الحكم بإدانة غير السعودي ... إبعاده عن المملكة ومنعه من دخولها. | Publication is mandatory, so a conviction is reputationally public; deportation removes the non-Saudi operator, which for a founder-run business is an existential key-person risk. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 12 | Conviction for the enabler's crime (Art. 3(a)) entails: (a) dissolution of the establishment, cancellation of its licence and approval, and deletion of the convicted person's commercial register, unless the Criminal Court decides otherwise; (b) a ban on the convicted person practising the activity concerned and any other commercial business for five years from finality. Those convicted under Art. 3(a) or (b) are jointly liable for zakat, taxes, fees, and any other obligation of the establishment. Judgments are copied to the Ministry and to the zakat and tax authority. | حل المنشأة محل الجريمة، وإلغاء الترخيص والموافقة الصادرة لها ... وشطب السجل التجاري للمدان، ما لم ترَ المحكمة الجزائية خلاف ذلك ... منع المدان من ممارسة النشاط الاقتصادي محل الجريمة وأي عمل تجاري آخر لمدة (خمس) سنوات ... تستوفى، بالتضامن بين المدانين ... الزكاة والضرائب والرسوم، وأي التزام آخر مقرر على المنشأة. | The target entity itself is at risk of liquidation and CR deletion, which is why a tasattur finding is a "deal-structure" issue rather than a price issue: the acquired vehicle may cease to exist. Joint tax liability means back-taxes assessed on the concealed business (income tax on the non-Saudi share, retroactively) attach to both parties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 13 | If a perpetrator reports the crime or the other perpetrators to the competent authorities before discovery, and the report leads to their arrest or to seizure of the funds, instruments, or proceeds, the Criminal Court may exempt them from the Art. 9(1) penalties under rules prepared by the Ministry and issued by Council of Ministers resolution; the exemption does not cover zakat and tax obligations. | إذا أبلغ أي من مرتكبي الجرائم ... عن الجريمة أو عن مرتكبيها الآخرين قبل اكتشافها ... فيجوز للمحكمة الجزائية إعفاؤه من العقوبات الواردة في الفقرة (1) من المادة (التاسعة) ... ولا يشمل ذلك الإعفاء من الالتزامات الزكوية والضريبية. | This is the ongoing "leniency" route after the Royal Decree's one-time correction window closed (see the Royal Decree row). It is discretionary, requires pre-discovery reporting, and never waives tax. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 14 | Penalty for an Art. 4 violation: a fine up to SAR 500,000 and/or closure of the establishment for up to ninety days. The committee may additionally require the violator to submit to the Ministry, for up to five years, the establishment's financial statements, bank statements, and employee payroll, and may penalise non-compliance with that order. Factors: size, revenues, duration, gravity, repetition, effect. If the committee sees indications of a crime it refers that part to the competent authority. A penalised person may appeal to the Administrative Court within sixty days of notification. | غرامة لا تزيد على (خمسمائة ألف) ريال. ب. إغلاق المنشأة لمدة لا تزيد على (تسعين) يومًا ... إلزام المخالف بتقديم ... القوائم المالية للمنشأة. ب- كشوف حسابات المنشأة البنكية. ج- مسيَّرات الرواتب لعاملي المنشأة ... لمدة لا تزيد على (خمس) سنوات ... يحق لمن صدر في حقه قرار بالعقوبة التظلم منه أمام المحكمة الإدارية خلال (ستين) يومًا. | An outstanding five-year reporting order is a continuing compliance obligation that survives a share sale; ask whether the target is under one. Ninety-day closure is a business-interruption risk that belongs in the closing-checklist critical path. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 15 | Without prejudice to the rights of good-faith third parties, every contract or act whose object or purpose is tasattur is void. | دون إخلال بحقوق الغير (الحسن النية)، يعد باطلًا كل عقد أو تصرف يكون محله أو غايته التستر. | Side letters, nominee declarations, profit-split agreements, and "management agreements" that in substance hand the business to an unlicensed non-Saudi are unenforceable between the parties. A diligence reviewer who finds such a document should treat the target's ownership and profit entitlement as unsettled, not merely as a covenant breach. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Arts. 17–18 | Every authority that issues licences for an economic activity must monitor its licensees and report suspected crimes or violations to the Ministry (Art. 17). The identity of reporters is kept in a confidential register and excluded from the case file (subject to the Public Prosecution's right to seek disclosure with safeguards). By decision of the Minister, a reporter who is not an enforcement official and not convicted in the matter receives a reward of up to 30 % of the fine collected, if the information was fit to open an investigation and a final judgment (crime) or final decision (violation) issued; the Regulation sets reporting procedures and division among multiple reporters (Art. 18). | على كل جهة تصدر تراخيص ... إبلاغ الوزارة بما يظهر لها من اشتباه ... يجب الحفاظ على سرية هوية المبلّغين في سجل سري ... تمنح بقرار من الوزير مكافأة مالية لا تزيد على (30%) من الغرامة المحصلة ... لمن يبلغ عنها -من غير المختصين بتطبيق أحكام النظام-. | Disgruntled employees, ex-partners, and competitors have a financial incentive to report; MISA, municipalities, and sector regulators have a statutory duty to. Internal-investigation and HR-separation processes should assume that concealment allegations will reach the Ministry. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Royal Decree M/4, clause "Second" | The Ministries of Commerce and Interior, with HRSD, Investment, and the Premium Residency Center, were to prepare within sixty days a Council of Ministers regulation with a mechanism for violators under the old 1425H law who continued after this Law took effect to correct their status, providing: the available correction options; exemption from the penalties of both laws and from retroactive income tax for anyone who applied to the Ministry of Commerce within 180 days of this Law's effective date; exclusion of those who first offended under this Law, those already caught, and those referred to the Public Prosecution or court; treatment of corrected persons wishing to leave the Kingdom permanently; and preservation of private rights arising from their dealings. | تعد وزارة التجارة ووزارة الداخلية ... لائحة تصدر بقرار من مجلس الوزراء تتضمن آلية لتصحيح أوضاع مخالفي نظام مكافحة التستر ... إعفاء من يتقدم -خلال (180) يومًا من تاريخ نفاذ هذا النظام- إلى وزارة التجارة بطلب تصحيح أوضاعه؛ من العقوبات المقررة في النظامين المشار إليهما أعلاه، ومن دفع ضريبة الدخل بأثر رجعي ... ألا تخل هذه الآلية بالحقوق الخاصة المترتبة على التعاملات التي أبرموها. | The statutory window ran 180 days from 24/02/2021; it was later extended by government decision to August 2021 `[model knowledge — verify]`. It is closed. A target that "corrected its status" should be able to produce the Ministry's correction certificate and evidence of the option taken (e.g. conversion to a MISA-registered foreign entity, sale to a Saudi, or exit); absence of it after a historic concealment is a live exposure. The correction regulation itself was not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Arts. 19–20 | The Minister of Commerce, in agreement with the Minister of Interior, issues the Implementing Regulation within 180 days of publication, to apply from the Law's effective date (Art. 19). The Law replaces the Anti-Concealment Law of Royal Decree No. M/22 dated 4/5/1425H, repeals conflicting provisions, and enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 20). | يصدر الوزير -بالاتفاق مع وزير الداخلية- اللائحة خلال (مائة وثمانين) يومًا ... يحل النظام محل نظام مكافحة التستر الصادر بالمرسوم الملكي رقم (م/22) وتاريخ 4/5/1425هـ ... ويعمل به بعد مضي (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ نشره في الجريدة الرسمية. | Conduct before 24/02/2021 is judged under the 1425H law; conduct after it under this Law. The Implementing Regulation (good-faith cases, travel-ban controls, deportation, reward procedures) governs much of the practical exposure and was not in the source set. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Investment Law (M/19 of 16/1/1446H) Arts. 1, 7, 8, 11, 16 — interplay | "Foreign investor" is any natural or legal person making an investment who is not a "local investor" (a person holding Saudi nationality) (Art. 1). A foreign investor must register with the Ministry of Investment before making any investment, per the Regulation, except investment in securities under the Capital Market Law (Art. 7(2)). Before investing in an activity on the Excluded Activities list (prohibited or restricted), or changing ownership of an investment in a restricted activity, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval (Art. 8). Breaches of Arts. 7–8 carry warning, a fine up to SAR 300,000 (doubled on repetition), or cancellation of registration (Art. 11). The Law took effect 180 days after publication on 16/08/2024 and repealed the Foreign Investment Law of M/1 of 1421H (Art. 16). | المستثمر الأجنبي: أي شخص ذي صفة طبيعية أو اعتبارية يقوم بالاستثمار، ولا يعد مستثمرًا محليًّا ... على المستثمر الأجنبي التسجيل لدى الوزارة قبل القيام بأي استثمار ... على المستثمر الأجنبي، قبل الاستثمار في أي من الأنشطة المحددة في قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة؛ التقدم بطلب إلى الوزارة للموافقة على ذلك. | A non-Saudi who holds a MISA registration covering the activity is "licensed to practise it" and therefore cannot be an Art. 2 concealment beneficiary for that activity; the concealment analysis collapses to: (i) is there a MISA registration (formerly "licence"); (ii) does it cover the activity actually carried on; (iii) does the target's CR activity match. A gap on (ii) or (iii), or activity on the Excluded Activities list, reopens tasattur exposure. The Anti-Concealment Law still speaks of "licence or approval" (ترخيص أو موافقة); reading MISA "registration" as the relevant licence is the natural construction under the 2024 Investment Law but is not stated in either text `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Investment Law text read today from `boe/investment-law-ar.md`; portal shows 0 amended articles) |
| Diligence checklist (practice) | Indicators a reviewer should test against Arts. 2–4 and 15: a non-Saudi (or foreign entity) without MISA registration who (a) holds a general or irrevocable power of attorney or is the sole bank signatory; (b) receives the business's revenues or pays its expenses through personal accounts; (c) appears as "general manager" with de facto ownership rights in side agreements; (d) funds the business by "loans" repaid from all profits; (e) is the counterparty in a trust/nominee declaration over the Saudi partner's shares. A UBO register that names a non-Saudi natural person as controller of a company whose CR shows only Saudi owners is a direct cross-check (see `ultimate-beneficial-ownership-rules.md`). | (لا نص نظامي — قائمة تحقق عملية) | Each indicator maps to an Art. 4 violation (a)–(c) or to the Art. 15 nullity, so findings can be reported by article rather than by impression. Whether Premium Residency holders or GCC nationals are "treated as" Saudi for particular activities must be confirmed under the relevant rules before a red flag is raised against them. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- Implementing Regulation: the Law delegates the good-faith carve-out for Art. 4 instruments, travel-ban conditions (Art. 8), deportation procedure (Art. 11), reporting and reward procedures (Art. 18), and the criteria for enforcement officers (Art. 6) to the Implementing Regulation, which was not in the source set. Confirm the current text and whether it has been amended since 2021.
- Who is "treated as a holder of" Saudi nationality for Art. 1 (GCC nationals, Premium Residency holders, and for which activities)? The Royal Decree's reference to the Premium Residency Center suggests a role, but the Law does not define it.
- Investment Law "registration" versus Anti-Concealment "licence or approval": confirm that MISA registration under the 2024 Investment Law (and legacy MISA licences still in force) is accepted by the Ministry of Commerce and the Criminal Court as the licence that takes a foreign investor outside Art. 2, and how the Ministry treats activity outside the scope of the registration.
- Status and outcome of the one-time correction mechanism under clause "Second" of the Royal Decree: the closing date of the extended window, what evidence of correction the Ministry issues, and whether a corrected establishment is treated as clean for later diligence.
- The amendment-watch lead 2.6 refers to a "ministerial committee for the national anti-concealment programme"; whether that programme has issued binding rules or only enforcement guidance is unknown.
- Application to professional companies and to activities licensed by sector regulators (health, education, engineering): the "economic activity" definition is broad enough to cover them, but sector licensing rules may interact with the Investment Law's Excluded Activities list.
- Whether the Criminal Court in practice exercises the Art. 12(1)(a) discretion not to dissolve the establishment where an innocent buyer has since acquired it.

## How skills should use this file

- `corporate-legal:diligence-issue-extraction` loads this file when the target has any non-Saudi shareholder, director, general manager, bank signatory, or attorney-in-fact, or when the VDR contains powers of attorney, nominee or trust declarations, side letters on profit sharing, or personal bank statements. It must cite Arts. 2–4 for the elements of the offence and violation, Art. 15 for nullity, Arts. 9, 12, and 14 for exposure, and the Investment Law row for the "licensed" test. A finding must state which article each indicator maps to.
- `corporate-legal:tabular-review` uses the Diligence checklist row as the column set for a tasattur screen across group entities, and the Investment Law row for the MISA-registration column; it must not add indicators not in this file without tagging them `[model knowledge — verify]`.
- `corporate-legal:closing-checklist` adds, for any target with a tasattur finding: MISA registration or correction evidence as a condition precedent; a check for outstanding Art. 8 seizure orders or Art. 14 reporting orders; and a specific indemnity/escrow item sized against Art. 9 (SAR 5m per person) and Art. 12 joint tax liability.
- `corporate-legal:entity-compliance` uses Arts. 4(c) and 17 for the standing rule that the entity's own bank accounts must be used and that licensing authorities report; and the Investment Law row for the foreign-investor registration duty (Art. 7(2)) and change-of-ownership approvals in restricted activities (Art. 8).
- `commercial-legal:review` and `vendor-agreement-review` load this file when a counterparty is a Saudi-registered entity with foreign principals acting through a Saudi licence holder, or when a services or "management" agreement in substance transfers the business to a non-Saudi; they must cite Art. 15 (nullity) in the risk assessment and route the issue via `escalation-flagger` to Saudi counsel.
- `employment-legal:hiring-review` and `worker-classification` load this file where a "manager" or "consultant" arrangement with a non-Saudi carries profit participation or control rights that look like ownership (Art. 3(a)–(b), Art. 4).
- No computed numbers arise from this file. Where a skill quotes a penalty it must quote the statutory maximum and the article (Art. 9: five years and/or SAR 5,000,000, doubled for recidivism; Art. 14: SAR 500,000 and/or ninety days' closure) with the `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` tag.
- Stop rule: if a skill needs a rule that is not in this table, or that is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (Premium Residency/GCC treatment, the correction-window extension, the "registration equals licence" construction, the Implementing Regulation's good-faith rules), it must say that the point is unconfirmed, cite this file, and stop or flag for Saudi counsel. It must never fill the gap from memory or promote a tag.
# Investment Law — نظام الاستثمار

<!-- Written 2026-09-04 from the Arabic text on laws.boe.gov.sa and the MISA-hosted Bureau of Experts translation of the Law and the MISA English text of the Implementing Regulations. Arabic controls. -->

