## Scope

The Law governs all investment (local and foreign) in the Kingdom: it defines investment and capital, grants investor rights (equal treatment, fair and equitable treatment, protection against confiscation and expropriation, free transfer of funds), creates a National Register of Investors kept by MISA, and replaces the foreign-investment *licence* of the 2000 Foreign Investment Law with *registration* of the foreign investor before investing (Art. 7). It does not itself list the activities closed or restricted to foreign investors — that list is issued by the Examination Committee and published in the Investor Guide (Art. 8; Reg. Art. 15), and neither was among the sources read. It does not apply to investment in securities governed by the Capital Market Law (Art. 7(2)), and it leaves sector-specific regimes and special economic zones intact subject to a floor of the rights in the Law (Art. 14). Tax, zakat, Saudization and company-law obligations are outside it (Art. 5 simply requires compliance with all laws). Penalties are limited to breaches of the registration and excluded-activities articles (Art. 11).

## Rules

Tag rules: `[settled — last confirmed YYYY-MM-DD]` only where the primary text was read on that date; `[model knowledge — verify]` for anything else, including practical-effect commentary that goes beyond the article's words.

| Article | Rule (English) | Rule (authoritative language) | Practical effect | Tag |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Art. 1 (definitions: الاستثمار، رأس المال، المستثمر الأجنبي); Reg. Art. 2 | "Investment" is the use of capital to establish, expand, develop, finance, own (in whole or part) or manage an investment project in the Kingdom for economic gain. "Capital" is any asset of material value (cash, in-kind or intangible), in particular shares and interests in companies, contractual rights, fixed or movable assets, IP rights, and rights granted by law such as licences; it excludes loans, bonds, financing sukuk and public or private debt instruments. A "local investor" is a natural or legal person investing who holds Saudi nationality; a "foreign investor" is any investor who is not a local investor. | الاستثمار: استخدام رأس المال لإنشاء مشروع استثماري في المملكة أو توسيعه أو تطويره أو تمويله أو تملكه جزئيًّا أو كليًّا أو إدارته من أجل تحقيق منفعة اقتصادية. رأس المال: أي أصل له قيمة مادية نقدية أو عينية أو معنوية ... ولا يشمل رأس المال القروض والسندات والصكوك التمويلية وأدوات الدين. المستثمر المحلي: ... ويتمتع بالجنسية العربية السعودية. المستثمر الأجنبي: أي شخص ... يقوم بالاستثمار ولا يعد مستثمرًا محليًّا. | A lender or sukuk holder is not an "investor" and gets none of the Art. 4 protections. The nationality test is on the person investing, so a Saudi-incorporated company is not automatically "local": MISA practice treats a Saudi company with any non-Saudi (non-GCC) shareholder as a foreign investor that must register `[model knowledge — verify]`, and GCC nationals and GCC-owned companies are treated as local under the GCC Economic Agreement and Council of Ministers decisions `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 2 (الهدف من النظام) | The Law's purposes include facilitating establishment of, ownership of assets in, and exit from or liquidation of an investment; guaranteeing investor rights; guaranteeing equal treatment between local and foreign investors; transparent, efficient and fair procedures; and competitive neutrality. | يهدف النظام إلى ... تسهيل تأسيس الاستثمار وتملك الأصول فيه والتخارج منه أو تصفيته؛ ضمان حقوق المستثمر وتعزيزها؛ ضمان المساواة في المعاملة بين المستثمر المحلي والأجنبي؛ ... دعم مبدأ الحياد التنافسي. | Purpose clause; cite it when a regulator's practice discriminates between local and foreign-owned entities without a statutory basis. It is programmatic and grants no directly enforceable right by itself. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 3 (حرية الاستثمار) | Subject to Arts. 8 and 9 and to other laws, an investor may invest in any sector or activity open to investment. | دون إخلال بأحكام المادتين (الثامنة) و(التاسعة) من النظام وما تقضي به الأنظمة ذات العلاقة، يحق للمستثمر الاستثمار في أي قطاع أو نشاط متاح للاستثمار. | The default is openness; restrictions must come from the excluded-activities list (Art. 8), national-security suspension (Art. 9) or a sector law (Art. 14). Diligence should ask for the specific legal basis of any claimed foreign-ownership cap rather than assume one. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 4(1)(a)–(b), 4(2); Reg. Arts. 3–4 | Investors enjoy equal treatment with other investors, and equal treatment between local and foreign investors, in similar circumstances; and fair and equitable treatment. Reg. Art. 3(2) tests "similar circumstances" by the goods/services produced or consumed, the target sector, investment size, and impact on the local economy or environment; Reg. Art. 3(3) preserves the Ministry's right to regulate for the public interest (national security, public safety, public order). Reg. Art. 4 deems denial of justice, substantial breach of due process, manifest arbitrariness (harassment, compulsion) and unjustified targeted discrimination to be breaches of fair and equitable treatment. Under Art. 4(2) the competent authority must observe these rights when taking public-interest measures. | يتمتع المستثمر بالحقوق الآتية: أ- مساواته في التعامل مع المستثمرين الآخرين، والمساواة في التعامل بين المستثمر المحلي والأجنبي؛ وذلك في الظروف المماثلة. ب- معاملته معاملة عادلة ومنصفة. ... على الجهة المختصة عند اتخاذ أي تدابير لتحقيق المصلحة العامة ... مراعاة الحقوق المنصوص عليها في الفقرة (1). | These are treaty-style standards written into domestic law. Reg. Art. 3(3) is the carve-out a regulator will cite; Reg. Art. 4 gives an investor-side checklist for a complaint under Reg. Arts. 24–26. Domestic enforcement is through the competent court or the MISA complaint system, not an investor–state tribunal, unless a BIT applies (Art. 13). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 4(1)(c); Reg. Art. 5 | No confiscation of an investment, in whole or part, except by final judicial judgment; no direct or indirect expropriation except for the public interest, in accordance with legal procedures, and against fair compensation. Reg. Art. 5 defines indirect expropriation (measure with effect equivalent to direct expropriation, judged by economic impact — adverse effect on value alone is not enough — duration, and nature/purpose) and excludes non-discriminatory measures protecting public health, safety and the environment. | ج- عدم مصادرة استثماره كليًّا أو جزئيًّا إلا بحكم قضائي نهائي، وعدم نزع ملكيته بشكل مباشر أو غير مباشر؛ إلا للمصلحة العامة ووفقًا للإجراءات النظامية ومقابل تعويض عادل. | Expropriation protection now exists in domestic statute for local and foreign investors alike. In a diligence memo, note that the standard of "fair compensation" (تعويض عادل) is undefined in the Law and Regulations; valuation practice is untested `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 4(1)(d); Reg. Art. 6 | Freedom to transfer funds into and out of the Kingdom without delay, through legal channels, in any currency recognised by the Saudi Central Bank, including investment returns, profits, and sale or liquidation proceeds. Reg. Art. 6(1) lists initial and additional capital, profits, capital gains, dividends, royalties, fees, contract and loan payments, liquidation/sale proceeds, and remuneration of personnel engaged from abroad. Reg. Art. 6(2) permits delay or prevention under equitable, non-discriminatory, bona fide laws on bankruptcy/creditor protection, securities dealing, criminal offences, and compliance with judgments; Reg. Art. 6(3) preserves prudential measures in financial services. | د- حرية تحويل أمواله داخل المملكة وخارجها دون تأخير -بما في ذلك على سبيل المثال لا الحصر تحويل عائدات استثماره وأرباحه وبيعه أو تصفيته- عبر القنوات النظامية باستخدام أي عملة معترف بها. | No exchange-control approval is required for dividend or exit repatriation; the practical gate is the bank's AML/KYC and, for dividends, the zakat/tax clearance and withholding-tax position `[model knowledge — verify]`. Contract reviewers can rely on this row when a foreign counterparty asks for a "currency convertibility" representation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 4(1)(e)–(g), 4(3); Reg. Arts. 7, 24–26 | Freedom to manage the investment, dispose of it lawfully and own what is needed to run the business; protection of IP and confidential commercial information; facilitation of administrative procedures and support from the competent authority. The Ministry must provide available statistics (within 30 working days of a request, Reg. Art. 7) and handle investor complaints under a transparent procedure: acceptance or rejection notified within 10 working days, recommendations within 30 working days of acceptance (Reg. Arts. 25–26); a complaint is not a judicial proceeding and does not prejudice court or ADR rights (Reg. Art. 24(2)). | هـ- حرية إدارة استثماره، والتصرف النظامي به، وتملك ما يلزم لتسيير أعماله. و- حماية الملكية الفكرية والمعلومات التجارية السرية. ز- تيسير إجراءاته الإدارية ... توفر الوزارة للمستثمر ... أي معلومات أو بيانات إحصائية متاحة ... وتسعى لمعالجة الشكاوى التي يتقدم بها. | The MISA complaint route is a cheap first step against another government body's decision affecting an investment; it does not stop limitation periods for court or Board of Grievances actions `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 5 (التزامات المستثمر) | The investor must comply with all laws and legislation of the Kingdom and with the Kingdom's obligations under international agreements to which it is party. | يلتزم المستثمر بالتقيد بكافة الأنظمة والتشريعات في المملكة، وبالتزاماتها بموجب الاتفاقيات الدولية التي تكون طرفًا فيها. | The only free-standing obligation article; the operative obligations are in Arts. 7 and 8 (register; obtain approval for excluded activities). Company-law, labour, tax and Saudization compliance are policed by their own laws, not by MISA penalties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 6; Reg. Art. 8 | Investment incentives are granted by the competent authority under objective, specific and fair eligibility criteria; the competent authority publishes the criteria through appropriate channels. | يكون منح المحفزات الاستثمارية للمستثمر من الجهة المختصة وفقًا لمعايير استحقاق موضوعية محددة وعادلة، وتحدد اللائحة الأحكام اللازمة لإنفاذ ذلك. | Incentives (tax holidays, land, RHQ benefits) come from the sector authority or ZATCA under their own instruments, not from this Law. Reg. Art. 36(3) names the Regional Headquarters programme and Special Economic Zones as regimes whose licensing continuity MISA must preserve; the RHQ programme itself (RHQ licence from MISA, 30-year 0% corporate income tax and withholding tax on qualifying RHQ activities, and the 1 January 2024 restriction on government contracting with companies lacking a KSA RHQ) is `[model knowledge — verify]` and is not in the sources read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 7(1), 7(3), 7(4); Reg. Arts. 9–10, 21; Decree para. 5 | The Ministry establishes and maintains a confidential National Register of Investors (السجل الوطني للمستثمرين) recording all information on investors and their investments; competent authorities must supply data, including through platform integration; the Ministry may make its services conditional on the investor updating its register data (Reg. Art. 9(4)). Through its comprehensive service centre (مركز الخدمة الشاملة) the Ministry receives applications for any approvals, licences or permits needed to practise an activity and coordinates their issue with the competent authority. Decree para. 5 requires the Ministry to exercise this function consistently with Art. 4 of the Saudi Business Center organisation (CoM Res. 456 of 11/8/1440H) and by agreement with the Center. | تنشئ الوزارة سجلاً وطنيًّا للمستثمرين، وتقيَّد فيه جميع المعلومات والبيانات المتعلقة باستثماراتهم ... للوزارة، من خلال مركز الخدمة الشاملة المنشأ فيها، استقبال طلبات المستثمر لإصدار أي من الموافقات النظامية اللازمة لممارسة نشاط استثماري. | The register is a MISA database, not a public register; a diligence reviewer cannot search it and must obtain the target's registration certificate and latest annual-update confirmation from the target. Sector licences are still issued by the sector regulator; MISA only routes. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 7(2); Reg. Arts. 11–12; Art. 16 | A foreign investor must register with the Ministry before making any investment, as specified in the Regulations; this does not apply to investment in securities governed by the Capital Market Law. Registration data for a legal person: name and place of incorporation, business scope and economic activities to be practised, capital, expected capital contribution, particulars of owners/shareholders/controllers or the ultimate beneficial owner, plus anything the Investor Guide requires; the applicant must confirm the Ministry's declarations including accuracy of information (Reg. Art. 11). The Ministry notifies registration within 10 working days of completion; an incomplete file must be completed within a period of not less than 15 working days or is deemed cancelled (Reg. Art. 12). Art. 16 repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) under which foreign investors needed a licence. | على المستثمر الأجنبي التسجيل لدى الوزارة قبل القيام بأي استثمار؛ وفقًا لما تحدده اللائحة. ولا يسري ذلك على الاستثمار في الأوراق المالية الخاضعة لأحكام نظام السوق المالية. ... ويُلغِي نظام الاستثمار الأجنبي الصادر بالمرسوم الملكي رقم (م / 1) وتاريخ 5 / 1 / 1421ه. | This is the shift from foreign investment licence (ترخيص الاستثمار الأجنبي) to investment registration. Neither the Law nor the Regulations set a minimum capital, a licence fee, or a licence term; MISA issues a "شهادة تسجيل الاستثمار" (investment registration certificate) and has been removing the minimum-capital requirements that attached to the old licence categories `[model knowledge — verify]`. Portfolio investment through the Tadawul is outside the registration duty. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Reg. Art. 13 (annual update) | The registered investor must submit an annual update of the declarations signed at registration and of any change to its register data; the Ministry notifies the result within 5 working days. The update may be filed within the 60 working days before the deadline; the Ministry reminds the investor 30 working days before; a grace period of up to 30 working days may be granted; a lapsed registration may be reactivated within three years of the end of that period, after which the investor must re-register under the Investor Guide procedure. | (النص العربي للائحة لم يُقرأ؛ الترجمة الإنجليزية الصادرة عن الوزارة:) على المستثمر المسجل تقديم تحديث سنوي للإقرارات الموقعة عند التسجيل ... خلال (60) يوم عمل السابقة لموعد التحديث ... ويجوز طلب إعادة تفعيل التسجيل خلال مدة لا تتجاوز (3) سنوات. | This replaces the annual licence renewal (and its fee) of the old regime `[model knowledge — verify]`. For an entity tracker: the annual update is the MISA compliance event; its due date is investor-specific (anniversary of registration) and must be taken from the registration certificate. A target whose registration has lapsed for more than three years cannot simply update — it must re-register, which is a closing-checklist item. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` |
| Reg. Art. 14 (deregistration) | An investor may apply for voluntary deregistration with the documents in the Investor Guide (incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days). The Ministry must deregister on (a) cessation of all registered investments by a final decision or unappealable judgment, (b) a final national-security suspension decision, or (c) a final committee penalty cancelling registration; notice within 5 working days. A deregistered investor may not carry on any investment activity unless it re-registers; deregistration does not waive third-party claims. | (ترجمة الوزارة:) تشطب الوزارة تسجيل المستثمر في حال توقف جميع الاستثمارات المسجلة بقرار نهائي ... أو صدور قرار نهائي بإيقاف الاستثمار لأسباب تتعلق بالأمن الوطني ... أو صدور قرار نهائي من اللجنة بإلغاء التسجيل. ولا يجوز للمستثمر المشطوب تسجيله ممارسة أي نشاط استثماري. | Exit sequencing: MISA deregistration should follow, not precede, commercial-register deletion and liquidation, because the deregistered investor loses the right to operate while creditors' rights survive. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` |
| Art. 8(1)–(2); Reg. Arts. 15–19 | The competent authority (the Examination Committee, per Decree para. 3) issues and updates the list of excluded activities (قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة) — activities prohibited to, or restricted for, foreign investors — and the Ministry publishes it (Reg. Art. 15: in the Investor Guide). Before investing in a listed activity a foreign investor must apply to the Ministry, which refers the application to the Examination Committee; the Committee may request further information (Reg. Art. 16); incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days (Reg. Art. 17); the decision is notified within 5 working days of receipt (Reg. Art. 18); a rejected applicant may reapply citing the first application and new information (Reg. Art. 19). | تتولى الجهة المختصة إصدار قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة وتحديثها، وتقوم الوزارة بنشرها. على المستثمر الأجنبي، قبل الاستثمار في أي من الأنشطة المحددة في قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة؛ التقدم بطلب إلى الوزارة للموافقة على ذلك. وترفع الوزارة الطلب إلى الجهة المختصة. | Investing in an excluded activity without approval is a "serious violation" (Reg. Art. 30(b)) exposing the investor to cancellation of registration. Diligence must map every activity code on the target's commercial register against the current list, not the list at the time of licensing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Excluded-activities list — contents | The list itself was not among the sources read. From memory it descends from the "negative list" of the 2000 regime and the Investor Guide and includes, among others: oil exploration, drilling and production (except certain services); manufacture of military equipment and explosives; security and investigation services; real-estate investment in Makkah and Madinah; Hajj and Umrah tour guidance and services; recruitment and employment offices; certain fisheries, printing and publishing, and audiovisual/media activities; and restricted (conditional) activities such as wholesale/retail trade, transport, and professional services with Saudi-participation or capital conditions. | (لم يُقرأ نص القائمة؛ من الذاكرة:) تشمل القائمة أنشطة محظورة على المستثمر الأجنبي، منها التنقيب عن النفط وخدمات الحج والعمرة ومكاتب الاستقدام والتحقيقات والأمن والاستثمار العقاري في مكة المكرمة والمدينة المنورة، وأنشطة مقيدة بشروط. | Do not rely on this row for any activity classification. The skill must obtain the current list from MISA's Investor Guide and record which version was used. Restricted activities carry conditions (Saudi shareholding, capital, experience) that also drive the Art. 8(3) ownership-change approval below. | `[model knowledge — verify]` |
| Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20; Reg. Art. 30(c) | Before any change in the ownership of its investment in a *restricted* activity on the list, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval, which the Ministry refers to the Examination Committee under the same procedure as new applications (Reg. Arts. 15–18 apply). Changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval is a serious violation. | على المستثمر الأجنبي، قبل القيام بأي تغيير يطرأ على ملكية استثماره في أي من الأنشطة المقيدة الواردة في قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة؛ التقدم بطلب إلى الوزارة للموافقة على ذلك. | Merger-control-style condition precedent for M&A: any share transfer, capital increase diluting a Saudi partner, or change of control in a foreign-owned entity operating a restricted activity needs MISA/Examination Committee approval before closing. Add to the closing checklist alongside GAC clearance and MoC amendment of the articles. The Law does not set a review period for this approval. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 9; Reg. Arts. 22–23 | The Ministry may suspend any foreign investment to protect national security, on an objective basis, consistently with the Kingdom's international-agreement obligations and under the Regulations' procedure. The Ministry may coordinate with competent authorities including the Examination Committee before, during and after; may request information and must keep it confidential; may discuss alternative measures with the investor before suspending (Reg. Art. 22); the suspension is notified and may be appealed under the laws in force (Reg. Art. 23). | للوزارة الحق في إيقاف أي استثمار أجنبي لحماية الأمن الوطني، على أن يكون قرارها في الإيقاف مبنيًّا على أساس موضوعي وبما يتمشى مع التزامات المملكة بموجب الاتفاقيات الدولية ... ووفقًا للإجراءات التي تحددها اللائحة. | This is a general foreign-investment screening power without sector thresholds or deadlines; the Law gives no review period and no time bar. Reg. Art. 14(3)(b) turns a final suspension into deregistration. Flag for any target in defence, critical infrastructure, data or telecoms even where the activity is not on the excluded list. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 10 (الوسائل البديلة لتسوية المنازعات) | An investor party to a dispute — including a dispute with a competent authority — may resort to the competent court unless the parties agree otherwise; investors may agree to settle disputes by alternative means including arbitration, mediation and conciliation. | للمستثمر الذي يكون طرفًا في أي نزاع -بما في ذلك المنازعات التي تنشأ مع الجهة المختصة- اللجوء إلى المحكمة المختصة؛ ما لم يتفق أطراف النزاع على غير ذلك. للمستثمرين الاتفاق في شأن تسوية منازعاتهم من خلال الوسائل البديلة لتسوية المنازعات بما في ذلك التحكيم والوساطة والمصالحة. | Statutory endorsement of arbitration clauses in investor agreements, including with government bodies "unless agreed otherwise". Arbitration by a government entity still requires the approval mandated by Art. 10(2) of the Arbitration Law (Prime Minister's approval) `[model knowledge — verify]`; the Investment Law does not dispense with it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 11(1)–(3); Reg. Arts. 27–30 | For a non-serious violation of Art. 7 or Art. 8 the Ministry notifies the investor to remedy it within the period set by the Regulations (not less than 30 working days, extendable on justification: Reg. Art. 28; the list of non-serious violations goes in the Investor Guide: Reg. Art. 29). Failure to remedy in time, or a serious violation, is punishable — without prejudice to any severer penalty in another law — by one or more of: warning; a fine of up to SAR 300,000, which may be doubled on repetition; cancellation of registration. Serious violations (Reg. Art. 30): investing without registration; investing in an excluded activity without approval, after its expiry or cancellation, or in breach of its conditions; changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval; giving false or misleading information to the Ministry or competent authority; obstructing detection officers. Detection officers are appointed by the Minister and may obtain records, analyse reports, record complaints, verify supplier/customer contracts and consult the licensing authority (Reg. Art. 27). | يعاقب المستثمر عند عدم إزالة المخالفة غير الجسيمة ... أو عند ارتكابه مخالفة جسيمة لأي من أحكام المادتين (السابعة) أو (الثامنة) من النظام؛ بواحدة -أو أكثر- من العقوبات الآتية: أ- الإنذار. ب- فرض غرامة لا تزيد على (300,000) ثلاثمائة ألف ريال. ويجوز مضاعفتها في حال تكرار المخالفة. ج- إلغاء التسجيل. | The financial penalty is modest; the real exposure is cancellation of registration, which under Reg. Art. 14(5) ends the right to operate. Operating a foreign-owned business through a Saudi nominee to avoid registration is a serious violation here and separately an offence under the Anti-Concealment Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 11(4)–(5); Reg. Arts. 31–35 | The Minister forms one or more committees of at least three members, at least one a legal specialist, to hear violations and impose the Art. 11(2) penalties, weighing gravity, repetition and the size of the enterprise. Members serve three years renewable once (Reg. Art. 31); sessions are in camera, valid with a majority including the chair or deputy, decisions by majority (Reg. Art. 32); representation is under the Code of Law Practice (Reg. Art. 33); the committee decides within 30 working days of receiving the violation report, extendable once, and the Minister or delegate issues the penalty decision within 30 working days of the committee's report (Reg. Art. 34); the decision is notified by the Ministry's approved means (Reg. Art. 35). | تكوّن بقرار من الوزير لجنة -أو أكثر- لا يقل عدد أعضائها عن (ثلاثة)؛ يكون منهم -على الأقل- مختصٌّ في الأنظمة، تتولى النظر في المخالفات وإيقاع العقوبات ... وتراعي اللجنة في تحديد العقوبة جسامة المخالفة وتكرارها وحجم المنشأة. | Expect a decision within roughly 60–90 working days of a violation report. A pending committee matter against a target should be disclosed as a contingent liability and, if it could end in cancellation of registration, treated as a closing condition. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Art. 12 (التظلم) | A person against whom the Ministry issues a penalty decision may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of being notified of the decision. | يجوز لمن صدر بحقه قرار من الوزارة بعقوبة التظلمُ منه أمام المحكمة المختصة خلال (ثلاثين) يومًا من تاريخ إبلاغه بالقرار. | Thirty calendar days, from notification. The competent court for administrative penalty appeals is the administrative court (Board of Grievances) `[model knowledge — verify]`. National-security suspensions (Reg. Art. 23) are appealed "in accordance with the laws in force", which the text does not specify. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Arts. 13–14 | The Law does not prejudice the Kingdom's obligations under any international agreement in force to which it is party; nor the special regimes of other authorities applying to specific economic activities or to special economic zones, provided the investor enjoys at least the rights in the Law. | لا تخل أحكام النظام بأي من التزامات المملكة بموجب أي اتفاقية دولية نافذة تكون المملكة طرفًا فيها. لا تخل أحكام النظام بالأنظمة الخاصة بالجهات الأخرى التي تسري على أنشطة اقتصادية محدَّدة، أو المناطق الاقتصادية الخاصة في المملكة، على أن يتمتع المستثمر بحد أدنى بالحقوق المنصوص عليها في النظام. | A bilateral investment treaty or the GCC framework can give a foreign investor more than this Law; the Law is a floor for SEZ (e.g. King Abdullah Economic City, Riyadh Integrated SEZ) and sector regimes (SAMA, CMA, CST, energy). Check which regime governs the target's licence before applying these rows. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` |
| Art. 15; Art. 16; Decree paras. 2 and 4; Reg. Arts. 36–37 | The Minister issues the Regulations within 180 days of publication, effective with the Law; MISA prepares them after coordinating with relevant bodies and the Ministry of Energy co-drafts the provisions on Art. 6 and Art. 7(3)–(4) (Decree para. 4). The Law takes effect 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette, repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) and conflicting provisions. Rights acquired before the effective date, including any licences or incentives granted, are preserved until their terms expire (Decree para. 2). Reg. Art. 36 requires the Minister to issue and publish an Investor Guide listing MISA services, their classification, method and fees; Reg. Art. 37 brings the Regulations into force with the Law. | يُعمل بالنظام بمضي (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ نشره في الجريدة الرسمية ... لا يخل النظام ... بأي حقوق اكتسبت قبل نفاذه لأي مستثمر، بما في ذلك أي تراخيص أو محفزات ممنوحة له، وذلك إلى حين انتهاء المدد الخاصة بتلك الحقوق. | A pre-2025 MISA foreign investment licence remains valid until its stated expiry; on expiry the holder moves to registration. How MISA migrated existing licence-holders into the register, and whether it did so automatically, is a platform practice not in the text `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) |
| Diligence checklist (synthesis of Art. 7, Art. 8, Reg. Arts. 11, 13, 14, 20, 30) | For a target with any foreign ownership, a corporate diligence must verify: (1) a current MISA investment registration certificate and the date and result of the last annual update (Reg. Art. 13) — a lapse of more than 3 years means re-registration; (2) that the economic activities on the commercial register match the activities registered with MISA (Reg. Art. 11(2)(b)) — a mismatch is a violation of Art. 7; (3) whether any activity is on the excluded list and, if restricted, that Examination Committee approval exists and its conditions are met (Art. 8; Reg. Art. 30(b)); (4) that every past change of ownership in a restricted activity was pre-approved (Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20); (5) that the owner/controller/UBO particulars filed with MISA match the cap table (Reg. Art. 11(2)(e)); (6) any notice of violation, committee proceeding, suspension or deregistration (Reg. Arts. 14, 22, 27–35). | (تجميع) يتحقق فريق الفحص من: سريان شهادة تسجيل الاستثمار والتحديث السنوي؛ تطابق الأنشطة في السجل التجاري مع الأنشطة المسجلة لدى الوزارة؛ وجود موافقة الجهة المختصة على أي نشاط مستثنى أو مقيد؛ وموافقتها المسبقة على أي تغيير في ملكية الاستثمار في نشاط مقيد؛ وتطابق بيانات الملاك والمستفيد الحقيقي؛ وعدم وجود مخالفات أو قرارات إيقاف أو شطب. | Minimum-capital and licence-fee checks that diligence memos carried under the old regime are no longer statutory requirements; where MISA still applies capital conditions to specific restricted activities this comes from the Investor Guide, not the Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. The registration certificate and the annual-update confirmation are MISA portal documents; request both, not just the certificate. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.); practice points inline `[model knowledge — verify]` |

## Open questions for a local practitioner

- **Regulations issuing instrument.** The MISA English text of the Regulations shows no decision number or date. Confirm the Ministerial Decision number and date (understood to be No. 1086 of 7 February 2025), confirm the Official Gazette publication, and obtain the Arabic text — the English rendering is MISA's, and the Bureau of Experts has not published an official translation of the Regulations.
- **Who is a "foreign investor" when the investor is a Saudi company with foreign shareholders?** Art. 1 defines the local investor by Saudi nationality of the person investing. Whether a Saudi-incorporated LLC with a 10% non-GCC shareholder must itself register, and at what ownership level registration is triggered, is a MISA practice rule in the Investor Guide, not in the Law.
- **The excluded-activities list.** The list and its current version were not read. Confirm the current list, its issue date, which activities are "prohibited" versus "restricted", and the conditions attached to restricted activities (Saudi participation, capital, experience), since Reg. Art. 30(b) makes breach of those conditions a serious violation.
- **Minimum capital.** Neither the Law nor the Regulations set minimum capital. Confirm whether MISA still applies capital thresholds to particular restricted activities (e.g. trading, real estate, engineering consultancy) through the Investor Guide, and whether these have been withdrawn.
- **Migration of pre-2025 licence-holders.** Decree para. 2 preserves acquired rights until their terms expire. Confirm how MISA converted existing licences into registrations, whether the annual-update deadline for converted investors runs from the old licence anniversary, and whether the old annual licence fee survives in any form.
- **Timing of Examination Committee approvals.** Reg. Arts. 16–18 set deadlines for MISA's notifications but none for the Committee's decision on an excluded-activity application or an Art. 8(3) ownership change. Ask for typical turnaround so the closing-checklist critical path can be estimated.
- **Appeal forum for national-security suspensions.** Reg. Art. 23 says the investor may appeal "in accordance with the laws in force" without naming the forum or the period; Art. 12's 30-day appeal applies in terms only to Ministry *penalty* decisions.
- **Interaction with the Anti-Concealment Law and the Companies Law branch rules.** Operating without registration is a serious violation here; confirm how MISA and the Ministry of Commerce coordinate on nominee-shareholder cases and whether a branch of a foreign company (Companies Law Arts. 235–239) registers as an investor in the same way as a subsidiary.

## How skills should use this file

- **`diligence-issue-extraction`** (corporate-legal): when the VDR shows non-Saudi ownership at any level, load this file at the regulatory/licensing category step and run the *Diligence checklist* row; cite Art. 7(2), Reg. Art. 13 and Art. 8(3) for each finding; classify a missing or lapsed registration, an activity mismatch, or an unapproved ownership change in a restricted activity as a regulatory finding at the "serious violation" tier (Reg. Art. 30). The excluded-activities row is `[model knowledge — verify]`: the skill must not classify an activity as prohibited or restricted from it — obtain the current list or flag.
- **`closing-checklist`** and **`integration-management`**: add three items for any deal touching a foreign-owned Saudi entity — (i) MISA/Examination Committee approval of the ownership change where the activity is restricted (Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20), (ii) post-closing update of the register data (Reg. Art. 13 — change of owners/UBO), and (iii) for a new foreign buyer, its own registration before closing (Art. 7(2)). Where the checklist needs the Committee's review period, state that the text gives none and mark the estimate `[model knowledge — verify]`.
- **`tabular-review`** and **`material-contract-schedule`**: when a contract contains an assignment or change-of-control clause in a target operating a restricted activity, cross-reference Art. 8(3); when a foreign counterparty asks for a repatriation, expropriation or currency representation, cite Art. 4(1)(c)–(d) and Reg. Arts. 5–6.
- **`entity-compliance`**: the MISA annual update (Reg. Art. 13) is the recurring event for foreign-owned entities; the due date is investor-specific and must be taken from the registration certificate — the skill must not compute it from the fiscal year. See `filing-calendar.md` for the row.
- **`review` / `vendor-agreement-review`** (commercial-legal): Art. 10 supports the enforceability of an arbitration clause with a Saudi investor counterparty; do not use it to clear arbitration with a government entity without the Arbitration Law check noted in that row.
- **Stop rule.** If a skill needs a row that is missing from this file or tagged `[model knowledge — verify]` (in particular the contents of the excluded-activities list, minimum-capital conditions, the Regulations' decision number, or GCC-national treatment), it must say so in its output and stop or flag for Saudi counsel; it must not fill the gap from memory or promote the tag.
- **Arabic rendering rule.** Where the skill quotes an article, quote the Arabic from the Rule (authoritative language) column and give the Bureau of Experts English; for the Regulations only MISA's English was read, so mark any Arabic rendering of a Regulations article as a back-translation.

codex
The extracts identify several potentially deal-affecting issues: the foreign shareholder’s control and profit arrangement, mismatched investment activities, unpaid GOSI contributions, customer termination rights, and LLC losses above half of capital. I’m checking the applicable articles and distinguishing documented problems from missing evidence.
exec
/bin/zsh -lc "python3 -B - <<'PY'
from pathlib import Path
for name in ['companies-law','anti-concealment-law','investment-law','social-insurance-law','saudization-nitaqat','platform-obligations','commercial-agencies-law']:
 print('\\nFILE',name)
 for l in Path('references/jurisdictions/ksa/'+name+'.md').read_text().splitlines():
  if l.startswith('|'):
   c=l.split('|')
   if len(c)>=7: print('|'.join([c[1],c[2],c[-3],c[-2]]))
PY" in /Users/basimkhoja/Projects/Legal Consultant
 succeeded in 0ms:

FILE companies-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Art. 1 | Definitions. "Regulations" are the regulations issued under the Law; "Ministry/Minister" = Commerce; "CMA" = Capital Market Authority; "competent authority" = the Ministry, except for JSCs listed on the Exchange, where it is the CMA. "Relatives" = parents and grandparents (ascending), children and their descendants, and spouses. "Day" = calendar day, whether or not a working day. | Every time period in this file (21-day notice, 30-day pre-emption, 60/180-day losses windows) runs in calendar days including Fridays, Saturdays and Eid holidays. "Relatives" is the statutory perimeter for related-party and director-loan rules (Arts. 27, 71, 72) and is narrower than IFRS "close family": siblings are not relatives for this Law. Listed JSCs answer to the CMA, so a listed-company question must be routed to the CMA regulations, not to the Ministry's Regulations rows below. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 2, 4, 3, 5 | A company is a legal entity formed under articles of incorporation or articles of association by two or more persons who each contribute money or work to a profit-seeking venture; exceptionally a company may be formed by the unilateral will of one person, and non-profit companies may be formed under Part 7 (Art. 2). Permitted forms: general partnership, limited partnership, JSC, SJSC, LLC (Art. 4). A company incorporated under the Law is Saudi and its head office must be in the Kingdom (Art. 3). Trade name may be in Arabic or another language, derived from the object, distinctive, or the name of present or former partners (with their or their heirs' consent for former partners); the name must be accompanied by the company form; a name change follows the amendment procedure and does not affect existing rights, obligations or proceedings (Art. 5). | A one-person JSC, SJSC or LLC is lawful (see Arts. 98, 150, 157); there is no "one-person company" form of its own. A counterparty name that lacks the form indicator (e.g. "ش.م.م", "شركة مساهمة مبسطة") is a drafting defect worth checking against the Commercial Register extract. A name change is not a change of entity; contracts survive it without novation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 6 | A founder is anyone who actually participates in incorporation and contributes cash or in kind. Founders file the incorporation and registration application with the Commercial Register with the articles and required documents; the Register decides on a complete application; a refusal must be reasoned; founders may appeal to the Ministry within 60 days of notification, and to the competent judicial authority if the appeal is refused or not decided within 30 days. | Incorporation is a single Commercial Register filing (in practice through the Ministry's online platform `[model knowledge — verify]`); the statutory refusal-appeal route is Ministry then Commercial Court. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 7 | Every company has articles of incorporation (عقد تأسيس), except a JSC, an SJSC and a single-owner LLC, which each have articles of association (نظام أساس). The articles must contain the provisions required by the Law for the form; they must be in Arabic and may be accompanied by a translation; the Ministry issues model articles. | The Bureau of Experts translation renders عقد التأسيس as "articles of incorporation" and النظام الأساس as "articles of association"; this file follows that usage. The Arabic text is the governing constitutional document; an English "shareholders' agreement" that contradicts the Arabic articles is subordinate (Art. 11). A multi-member LLC has articles of incorporation, not a "memorandum and articles". | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Art. 8; Regs Art. 2 | Articles and every amendment must be in writing or are void; incorporation or amendment takes effect after the requirements in the Law and Regulations are met. Founders, partners, managers or directors (as applicable) must register the articles and every amendment with the Commercial Register, which publishes what the Law requires; those who cause non-registration are jointly liable for resulting damage. Registered data are available to third parties and are evidence against the company and third parties; unregistered articles or amendments cannot be relied on against third parties, and an unregistered particular alone is ineffective against them. Regs Art. 2: identity of founders/partners is verified through the National Information Center, the Commercial Register, or the notarisation authority in cases the Ministry sets with the Ministry of Justice; amendment of a JSC/SJSC's articles of association requires submitting the extraordinary general assembly (or shareholders') decision with the required documents to the Commercial Register. | The Law itself does not require notarisation of the articles before a notary public; it requires writing and Commercial Register registration, with identity verification that may be electronic. In practice amendments and share transfers are executed through the Ministry's electronic platform and notarised electronically via the Ministry of Justice integration `[model knowledge — verify]`. For a closing checklist the operative test is "is the amended article registered on the Commercial Register?" — an executed but unregistered transfer or amendment binds the parties but not third parties. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 9, 10, 12 | Legal personality arises on registration with the Commercial Register; during incorporation the company has personality to the extent needed to incorporate, provided incorporation is completed. On registration all contracts and acts done by the founders for its account pass to the company, which bears the incorporation expenses. If incorporation is not completed, those who dealt in the company's name are personally and jointly liable for their acts (Art. 9). The company may carry on its objects after registration and after obtaining any required licences (Art. 10). Contracts, receipts and other documents issued by the company must state its name, form, head-office address, e-mail (if any) and Commercial Register number, and its capital and paid-up capital (not required for general and limited partnerships), and "under liquidation" during liquidation (Art. 12). | Pre-registration contracts signed "for a company under formation" become the company's on registration automatically; if registration never happens the signatories are personally liable. Contract reviewers should check the counterparty block for the Art. 12 particulars; omission is a fineable violation (Art. 262(i)) but does not invalidate the contract. Licences (MISA, municipal, sectoral) are a separate gate from registration. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 11; Regs Art. 4 | Founders, partners or shareholders may, during or after incorporation, (a) conclude one or more agreements regulating their relations among themselves or with the company, including how their heirs enter the company, and (b) conclude a family charter (ميثاق عائلي) regulating family ownership, governance, management, work and family-employment policies, dividends, disposal of interests or shares, and dispute resolution. The agreement or charter is binding and may form part of the articles; it must not contradict the Law or the articles. Regs Art. 4: where the agreement or charter forms part of the articles, its amendment quorum is that for amending the articles of the relevant company form. | This is the statutory basis for shareholders' agreements: they bind the signatories but yield to the Law and to the registered articles. In diligence, compare the SHA's transfer, drag/tag, reserved-matter and dispute provisions against the registered Arabic articles; a conflict is resolved in favour of the articles, so key protections should be mirrored in the articles (Arts. 113, 151-153, 181 provide the statutory hooks). Whether a Saudi court will enforce an SHA governed by foreign law or providing for foreign-seated arbitration is a practice question `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 13-15 | Contributions may be cash, in kind or both; except in JSCs and SJSCs a partner may contribute work in return for a share of profits fixed in the articles, but never reputation or influence; only cash and in-kind contributions form the capital; interests or shares may be issued to a person for work or services benefiting the company (Art. 13). Warranty rules for in-kind contributions follow sale (ownership/real rights) or lease (personal right of use) unless agreed otherwise; a work contributor owes his work and its earnings, but not IP rights unless agreed (Art. 14). Every partner is a debtor for the contribution promised; on delay the company may demand performance or suspend the rights attached to the interests (dividends, voting), without prejudice to damages (Art. 15). | "Sweat equity" is permitted in LLCs and partnerships as a profit share, not as capital; in JSC/SJSC it must be structured as shares for services under Art. 13(4). Unpaid capital is a diligence item: check the Art. 158 declaration that interests are paid and any suspension of voting rights that could affect quorum in a partners' resolution. IP created by a work-contributing partner stays with the partner absent an express assignment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 16-17; Regs Art. 5 | Fiscal year is 12 months fixed in the articles; the first fiscal year may be 6 to 18 months from registration (Art. 16). The company must keep accounting records and supporting documents at its head office or a place fixed by the manager/board; financial statements must be prepared at the end of each fiscal year under the accounting standards approved in the Kingdom and deposited, as the Regulations specify, within six months of the fiscal year end; a controlling or holding company may require information from its subsidiary/investee for its own statements (Art. 17). Regs Art. 5: the manager or chairman deposits the financial statements and the auditor's report (if any) with the Saudi Business Center through the electronic financial-statements deposit programme. | Annual compliance item: audited (where required) financial statements filed electronically within six months of year end — the platform is known as "Qawaem" `[model knowledge — verify]`. Failure to keep records or to prepare/deposit statements is a fineable violation (Art. 262(e)). For the calendar cross-reference see `filing-calendar.md`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 18-20; Regs Arts. 6-9 | Every company has one or more licensed auditors appointed (with fees, term and scope) by the partners, general assembly or shareholders; the Regulations cap the term. The appointing body may remove the auditor (subject to damages if unjustified) and the manager/chairman must notify the competent authority of the removal and its reasons within 5 days; an auditor may resign by written notice, must state reasons to the company and the competent authority, and the manager/board must convene the partners/assembly to consider the reasons and appoint a replacement (Art. 18). Micro and small companies are exempt from mandatory appointment unless their articles require it, they are listed, issue tradeable debt/sukuk, preferred or redeemable shares, are required by other laws, are foreign, or are a parent/subsidiary (unless the whole group is micro/small); the exemption requires the status in the first fiscal year or two consecutive years; holders of 10% of voting interests may demand an auditor; general partnerships are exempt except in stated cases (Art. 19). The auditor must be independent, may not be a founder, director, manager, partner, employee or relative, may not trade the company's shares during the audit, may not do technical, administrative or consulting work except as the Regulations allow, has access to all records at any time, must report obstruction and may ask the manager/board to convene the partners/assembly and may convene it himself if they fail to do so within 30 days; he reports on the financial statements, management's cooperation and violations found, reads the report or a summary at the annual assembly (or presents it by circulation), must keep secrets, and is liable (jointly if several) for errors (Art. 20). Regs Art. 6: individual auditor max 10 consecutive fiscal years; audit firm max 10, extendable to 20 with audit-committee recommendation or competing bids, engagement partner max 10; a 2-year cooling-off. Regs Art. 7: micro/small = any two of annual revenue ≤ SAR 10 million, assets ≤ SAR 10 million, employees ≤ 49; a statement of exemption accompanies the deposited financial statements. Regs Art. 8: the 10% demand is made in writing to the manager/board at least 30 days before fiscal year end, and the manager/board must then convene a vote. | Diligence: confirm the auditor's appointment resolution, term against the rotation caps, and the 5-day removal notification if the auditor changed. A subsidiary of a foreign group is not micro/small-exempt unless the whole group qualifies, so most foreign-owned LLCs need an audit. The auditor's power to convene the assembly is a safety valve where management stalls the annual meeting. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 22-24; Regs Art. 10 | Annual or interim dividends may be distributed out of distributable profits in JSCs, SJSCs and LLCs; if distributed otherwise, creditors may claim against the company and the company may reclaim from every partner or shareholder, even in good faith; lawfully distributed dividends need not be returned if later losses occur (Art. 22). Partners share profits and losses in proportion to capital; a clause excluding a partner from profit or exempting him from loss is void, but the articles may set unequal ratios; a work-only partner may be exempted from loss if unpaid (Art. 23-24). Regs Art. 10: interim dividends require an enabling provision in the articles, an annual delegation to the manager/board, reasonable liquidity, and distributable profits per the latest financial statements; distributable profits = retained earnings in the last statement of financial position plus distributable reserves (profit-based reserves not earmarked or whose purpose was cancelled). | Unlawful dividends are recoverable from good-faith recipients, so a buyer should test historic distributions against retained earnings. Preferred-return or waterfall structures must be drafted as unequal ratios or share classes, never as a total exclusion from profit or loss. The share-premium account cannot be paid out as cash dividends (Regs Art. 33). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 25 | Ownership of interests in general partnerships, limited partnerships and LLCs passes by registration with the Commercial Register and is not effective against the company or third parties before that date. Shares in unlisted JSCs and SJSCs are transferred by entry in the shareholders' register under Art. 112 with the same effect; listed shares transfer under the Capital Market Law. | The closing deliverable for an LLC share sale is the updated Commercial Register (amended articles showing the buyer), not the signed transfer; for an unlisted JSC/SJSC it is the updated shareholders' register (and the Art. 112 data update to the Register within 15 days). Escrow and completion mechanics should be built around the registration event. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 26; Regs Art. 11 | Managers and directors owe duties of care and loyalty, in particular: act within their powers; work for the company's interest and success; decide and vote independently; exercise reasonable care, attention, diligence and skill; avoid conflicts; disclose any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings; accept no benefit from third parties in connection with their role. Regs Art. 11 elaborates: exercise powers for their proper purpose, act in good faith to maximise value for partners/shareholders, act objectively, perform with the skill of a prudent person and the knowledge expected of the office, avoid and disclose conflicts, and not exploit the position for benefits from third parties. | Codified fiduciary duties apply equally to LLC managers and JSC directors, and to nominee directors of a foreign parent. Board minutes should evidence the decision process (information received, independence, conflicts declared) because Art. 31 protects good-faith decisions only where those elements are present. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 27; Regs Arts. 12-13, 16-20 | A manager or director may not have a direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts, nor compete with the company in any branch of its activity, except with authorisation from the partners, general assembly or shareholders or their delegate; he may not exploit the company's assets, information or investment opportunities offered to him or the company. Exceptions to the interest bar: public tenders; personal-needs dealings on the company's standard terms within its ordinary activity; other cases set by the Regulations. Breach of the interest bar: the company may seek annulment and disgorgement; breach of the competition bar: damages (Art. 27). Regs Art. 12: an opportunity is off-limits if within the company's usual activities or if the company wishes or is expected to benefit from it. Regs Art. 13: a general-partnership manager needs authorisation from all partners for self-dealing (case by case) and for competing activity (renewed annually). Regs Art. 16: the general assembly may delegate the authorisation of related-party dealings to the board only where the dealing (or aggregate for the year) is below 1% of revenue per the latest financial statements and below SAR 10 million, is in the ordinary course and on non-preferential terms; delegation lasts at most one year or to the end of the board term; directors may not vote on the delegation. Regs Art. 17: indirect interest includes dealings benefiting the director's relatives, partnerships/LLCs in which he or a relative is a partner, JSCs/SJSCs in which he or relatives hold 5% or more, non-company establishments owned or managed by him or relatives, and companies where he or a relative is a manager, director or senior executive. Regs Arts. 18-19: competition authorisations may be delegated to the board with criteria, for at most one year; the director must notify the board (recorded in the minutes), abstain, and the licence is renewed annually. Regs Art. 20: if authorisation is refused the director must resign within the period set or his membership ends, unless he abandons the dealing or competition. | Related-party approval is a shareholder-level matter by default; board-level approval is valid only under a delegation meeting the Regs Art. 16 thresholds. Diligence should list every contract with a director, manager, their relatives or their other companies and match each to a partners'/assembly authorisation; unauthorised contracts are voidable at the company's election with disgorgement. Non-compete undertakings by directors are statutory, not just contractual. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 28 | Managers and directors are jointly liable to the company, partners/shareholders and third parties for damage caused by breach of the Law or the articles or by their errors, negligence or default; any contrary clause is void. Liability is personal to the individual, or shared by all where the decision was unanimous; where taken by majority, dissenters who expressly recorded their objection in the minutes are not liable; absence is no defence unless the absentee proves he did not know of the decision or could not object once he knew. The company may insure its managers/directors against liability arising from their capacity. | Minutes must record named dissents to give directors the statutory safe harbour; "the resolution was passed by majority" without names is not enough. Contractual exculpation of managers is void, but D&O insurance is expressly permitted. Absent directors should send a written objection promptly on learning of a decision. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 29-32 | The company may sue managers/directors for breach of the Law or the articles or for errors, negligence or default; the partners/assembly/shareholders decide to sue and appoint the representative (the liquidator during liquidation; the legal representative in Bankruptcy Law proceedings). Partners/shareholders holding 5% of capital (or less if the articles so provide) may bring the company's action if the company does not, provided the action serves the company, has a proper basis, is in good faith and the claimant holds at the time of filing; they must notify the manager/board at least 14 days before filing; a partner may also sue personally for special damage (Art. 29). A discharge (إبراء ذمة) voted by the partners/assembly does not bar these actions; except for forgery and fraud, the action is time-barred five years from the end of the fiscal year in which the harmful act occurred or three years from the end of the manager's/director's tenure, whichever is later (Art. 30). Business-judgement rule: a manager/director who decided or voted in good faith is deemed to have performed his duty if he had no interest in the matter, was informed to the extent appropriate in the circumstances, and rationally and firmly believed the decision served the company; the claimant bears the burden of proving otherwise (Art. 31). The court may order the company to bear a good-faith claimant's costs whatever the outcome (Art. 32). | The customary annual "discharge of the board" resolution has no protective effect; it does not stop a derivative claim. Post-closing claims against outgoing managers remain open for at least three years after they leave. A 5% minority has statutory derivative standing that cannot be raised by the articles, only lowered. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 33-34 | A partner's or shareholder's personal creditor may ask the court to be paid from the debtor's share of distributed net profits and, on dissolution, from his share of the surplus (Art. 33). Subject to the Movable Assets Security Law, the creditor may also ask the court to sell enough of the debtor's interests (other partners have a statutory right to redeem them) or shares; in an unlisted JSC or SJSC the other shareholders have priority to buy within 15 days of the offer if the articles so provide (Art. 34). | Enforcement against LLC interests triggers the Art. 178 redemption mechanics for the other partners; a JSC/SJSC pre-emption on forced sale exists only if written into the articles. Lenders taking security over interests should read this with the pledge rules (Art. 180; Regs Arts. 68-69). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 35-38, 42-46, 50 | General partnership (شركة التضامن): two or more natural or legal persons personally and jointly liable with all their assets for the company's debts; each partner is a merchant (Art. 35). Managed by the partners or by one or more managers (partners or not) appointed in the articles or a separate contract; multiple managers may each act alone unless otherwise provided, with objections resolved by majority of managers, then by the partners; the manager conducts all business within the objects and represents the company, and the company is bound by his acts within its objects unless the counterparty acted in bad faith (Art. 37). Partners' decisions pass by numerical majority; amendment of the articles requires unanimity unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 38). Removal of a partner-manager named in the articles requires unanimity of the other partners; other managers by numerical majority; court removal always possible; removal does not dissolve the company unless the articles say so (Art. 42); a manager may resign on 60 days' written notice (Art. 43). Interests cannot be represented by negotiable instruments; assignment requires compliance with the articles' restrictions or the consent of the other partners, is void otherwise, and must be registered and published (Art. 44). An incoming partner is liable for prior debts unless exempted by unanimous agreement registered with the Register; a withdrawing or expelled partner remains liable for debts before registration of his exit; an assignor is released unless creditors object within 30 days of notice (Art. 45). A partner may withdraw on 60 days' notice unless the articles provide otherwise; expulsion follows the articles or a court order on application of a numerical majority; the court may dissolve where continuation is impossible (Art. 46). Death, incapacity, insolvency, expulsion or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the partnership unless the articles so provide; if only one partner remains he has 90 days to admit another partner or transform, failing which the company is dissolved by operation of law (Art. 50). | Rarely used by foreign investors because of unlimited liability; where a target group contains one, diligence should confirm that all partners consented to past assignments and that creditor-objection notices were given. A corporate partner in a general partnership exposes its whole balance sheet. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 51, 53, 55-56 | Limited partnership (شركة التوصية البسيطة): at least one general partner with unlimited joint liability and at least one limited partner liable only to the extent of his contribution and who is not a merchant; general-partnership rules apply to general partners and, absent special provision, to the company (Art. 51). A limited partner may inspect twice a year but may not take part in external management, even under a power of attorney, on pain of unlimited liability for the resulting obligations; internal management participation is allowed if the articles so provide, unless it leads third parties to believe he is a general partner (Art. 53). Unless the articles provide otherwise, amendments require unanimity of general partners plus the majority in capital of limited partners; other decisions by numerical majority of general partners; a limited partner may not seek dissolution or vote on appointment/removal of the manager (Art. 55). A limited partner may assign to other partners freely; to third parties only with consent of all general partners and the majority in capital of limited partners, unless the articles provide otherwise; a general partner assigns under the same rule; new partners are admitted with the consent of all general partners (Art. 56). | A fund-style structure using a limited partnership must keep limited partners out of external management; signing authority given to a limited partner converts him into an unlimited guarantor for those acts. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 58-60 | JSC (شركة المساهمة): formed by one or more persons, capital divided into tradeable shares, the company alone liable for its debts, shareholders liable only to pay up their shares (Art. 58). Issued capital must be at least SAR 500,000 and at least one quarter must be paid at incorporation (Art. 59). The company has issued capital (subscribed shares) and may have authorised capital fixed in the articles; the board may increase issued capital within the authorised capital by board resolution provided the issued capital is fully paid (Art. 60). | The old distinction between a regular JSC and one formed with public subscription is gone; SAR 500,000 with 25% paid is the floor for any JSC. Authorised-capital headroom lets the board issue shares (e.g. for ESOPs or conversions) without an extraordinary assembly, so diligence should check the authorised figure and any board issuances since the last registered capital. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 61-66; Regs Art. 3 | The JSC articles of association must state at least: name, head office, objects, authorised (if any), issued and paid-up capital, number, types and classes of shares with nominal value and rights, term (if any), management and number of directors, fiscal year, and other agreed terms; the application must attach founders' names/addresses/nationalities, a statement of incorporation expenses, the founders' declaration of subscription to all issued shares and amounts paid, a bank certificate of deposit of the paid amount with a licensed Saudi bank, the founders' decision appointing the first board (names, nationalities, addresses, dates of birth) and, where required, the first auditor, a compliance declaration, and an accredited valuer's report on any in-kind contributions with the other founders' acceptance (Art. 61). Shares not taken by founders must be offered under the Capital Market Law (Art. 62); the paid amount is deposited in the name of the company under incorporation and may be used only by the board after registration; if registration fails subscribers recover their money and founders are jointly liable (Art. 64). Once registered the company is validly incorporated and no nullity action lies for breach of the Law or the articles (Art. 65). In-kind contributions at incorporation or capital increase must be valued by one or more accredited valuers; the report is put to the founders or the extraordinary assembly; contributors may not vote on it; a reduction of the agreed consideration needs the contributors' consent; the Regulations cap the time between report and issue (Art. 66) — Regs Art. 3: six months for JSC, SJSC and LLC. | A valuation report older than six months at the date of issue is a defect in any capital increase by contribution in kind; check dates in the capital-increase file. Art. 65 means historic incorporation defects in a JSC cannot be used to unwind it. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 67-70; Regs Arts. 14-15, 22 | A JSC is managed by a board of at least three members; any shareholder may nominate himself or others (Art. 67). Directors are elected by the ordinary general assembly, must be natural persons; the Regulations fix the voting method; the articles may set a board-formation method within Regulation limits; the articles fix the term, not exceeding four years, with re-election unless the articles provide otherwise; the ordinary assembly may remove all or some directors even if the articles provide otherwise, and must then elect replacements (Art. 68). The board must call the ordinary assembly in good time before its term ends; if no election takes place the outgoing board continues for the period set by the Regulations; a director resigns by written notice to the chairman (the chairman to the other members and the secretary), effective on the date stated; casual vacancies may be filled by the board on a temporary basis, notified to the Commercial Register (and the CMA if listed) within 15 days and put to the next ordinary assembly, the appointee completing his predecessor's term; if numbers fall below the quorum minimum the remaining members must call the assembly within 60 days; failing an election any interested person may ask the court to appoint supervisors to convene an assembly within 90 days or to dissolve the company (Art. 69). The assembly, on the board's recommendation, may end the membership of a director absent from three consecutive or five separate meetings without an excuse accepted by the board (Art. 70). Regs Art. 14: election is by cumulative voting unless the articles provide for ordinary voting; the articles may give a shareholder above a stated threshold the right to reserve board seats, in which case he does not vote for the other seats. Regs Art. 15: an expired board continues at most 90 days; a resigning whole board continues at most 120 days. Regs Art. 22: a director removed for absence forfeits remuneration after his last meeting attended and must repay amounts received for that period; remuneration based on incorrect or misleading information must be repaid. | The Law fixes only the minimum of three directors; the text read contains no statutory maximum (the eleven-member cap familiar from the 2015 Law is not in Art. 67). A maximum of eleven for listed companies comes from the CMA Corporate Governance Regulations `[model knowledge — verify]`. Corporate directors are not permitted: a shareholder company must nominate individuals. Cumulative voting is the default for unlisted JSCs, which protects minorities; a shareholders' agreement promising board seats should be paired with an articles provision under Regs Art. 14(2). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 71-72 | A director must inform the board immediately of any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts; the disclosure is recorded in the minutes; he may not vote on the matter in the board or the assemblies; the board reports such dealings to the assembly with a special auditor's report; failure to disclose entitles the company or any interested person to seek annulment or disgorgement; liability for damage falls on the interested director and on directors who were negligent or where the dealing was unfair; dissenters recorded in the minutes are exempt (Art. 71). A JSC may not lend to a director in any form or guarantee or secure his loans with third parties, nor to his relatives; such contracts are void and the company may claim damages; exceptions for banks and finance companies in the ordinary course, and for employee incentive programmes approved under the articles or by the assembly; the competent authority may restrict loans or guarantees to shareholders (Art. 72). | Board-minutes practice: a standing "declarations of interest" item, recorded by name and matter, with the interested director abstaining; the annual assembly agenda needs a related-party item supported by the auditor's special report. Intra-group loans to a director-shareholder's own company are caught if the director has an indirect interest (Regs Art. 17); loans to directors are void, not merely voidable. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 73-79 | Shareholders supervise the board through the assembly and may not interfere in board or executive management unless they sit on the board or work in management (Art. 73). The board may borrow for any term, sell or mortgage the company's assets or business, and release debtors, unless the articles or the assembly restrict it (Art. 74); sale of assets exceeding 50% of total assets, in one or several transactions within the preceding 12 months, needs assembly approval, the approval attaching to the transaction that crosses the threshold (Art. 75). The board has the widest powers within the objects except matters reserved to the assembly by the Law or the articles, may delegate specific acts to members or third parties, and the company is bound by the board's acts even outside its powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew the act was outside them (Art. 77). At its first meeting the board appoints a chairman from among its members and may appoint a managing director or a CEO from among them; the articles allocate their powers, failing which the board does; a listed JSC must appoint a vice-chairman; the board appoints a CEO and a secretary (members or not) and sets their powers and pay if the articles are silent; the board may relieve any of them of office without ending board membership (Art. 78). The chairman represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties; the articles may give representation to the managing director or CEO; any of them may delegate representation; the chairman may delegate some powers in writing to other members or third parties unless the articles provide otherwise; the vice-chairman deputises (Art. 79). | The Art. 78 text read contains no bar on one person being both chairman and CEO or managing director; the separation requirement for listed companies comes from the CMA Corporate Governance Regulations, not from this Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. Authority checks on a JSC counterparty: chairman signs by default; a CEO or MD signs only if the articles or a written chairman's delegation say so. An asset sale crossing the 50%-of-assets line in a rolling 12 months needs an assembly resolution — a change-of-control/major-transaction flag for diligence and closing checklists. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 76; Regs Art. 21 | The articles set the method of directors' remuneration (fixed sum, attendance allowance, benefits in kind, a percentage of net profits, or a combination) and may cap it; the ordinary assembly fixes the amount, which must be fair, motivating and proportionate to performance; the board's annual report must itemise everything each director received (remuneration, allowances, expenses, executive or consulting pay) and attendance at each meeting. Regs Art. 21: remuneration must be proportionate to duties, activity, company size and experience, may differ between members, must not for independent directors be a percentage of profits or otherwise tied to profitability, and the policy and amounts must be disclosed in the annual report. | Board remuneration is an assembly reserved matter; a management agreement fixing a director's fee without an assembly resolution is a compliance gap. The Law no longer contains a fixed monetary cap per director; caps for listed companies are a CMA matter `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 80-81 | The JSC board meets at least four times a year on the chairman's call under the articles (the competent authority may vary the number); the chairman must call a meeting when any member so requests in writing. Quorum is at least half the members present in person or by proxy, unless the articles require more. Resolutions pass by majority of those present in person or by proxy, the chair of the meeting having a casting vote unless the articles provide otherwise. The board fixes the venue and may meet by modern technology (Art. 80). A director may not appoint a proxy to attend or vote, except that the articles may allow him to appoint another director, who may hold only one proxy; a board resolution takes effect on issue unless it specifies another time or conditions (Art. 81). | Board minutes should record: the call and its date, members present/represented (with proxies under an articles provision), quorum against the articles' threshold, each resolution with votes for/against/abstaining and named dissents (Art. 28). Video meetings are valid without special articles wording. Fewer than four meetings a year is a violation for an unlisted JSC unless the Ministry has varied the number. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 82 | The board may pass resolutions on urgent matters by circulation to all members, unless a member requests in writing that the board meet to deliberate. Such resolutions pass with the approval of a majority of the board's members (not merely of those responding), unless the articles require a higher proportion or number, and must be tabled at the next board meeting for recording in that meeting's minutes. | A written board consent must be circulated to every director, state the urgency, carry approvals from a majority of all directors, and be ratified in the next meeting's minutes; any single director can force a meeting instead. The text limits circulation to "urgent matters", so routine business by written consent is open to challenge. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 83 | Board deliberations and resolutions are recorded in minutes prepared by the secretary and signed by the chair of the meeting, the directors present and the secretary; minutes are entered in a special register signed by the chairman and the secretary; modern technology may be used for signature, recording of deliberations and keeping the minutes. | Signature by all attending directors (not only the chair and secretary) is the statutory form; electronic signature is acceptable. The Law fixes the language of the articles as Arabic (Art. 7) but is silent on the language of minutes; bilingual minutes with an Arabic text are the safe practice because Ministry inspections and courts work in Arabic `[model knowledge — verify]`. Failure to prepare and record minutes is fineable (Art. 262(h)). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 84; Regs Arts. 24-30 | The general assembly is chaired by the chairman, the vice-chairman in his absence, or a board delegate, failing which a person chosen by the shareholders by vote; every shareholder may attend regardless of the articles and may appoint a proxy who is not a director; meetings, deliberation and voting may be held by modern technology (Art. 84). Regs Art. 24: electronic participation requires live audio-visual transmission and real-time ability to hear, follow, speak and vote; the company may offer electronic voting on agenda items to shareholders who do not attend, opening after the notice and for at least three days before the meeting, closing per item as each is voted; electronic meetings do not remove the right to attend in person at the venue in the notice. Regs Art. 25: the board must verify the identity and entitlement of electronic participants and voters. Regs Art. 26: electronic participants and e-voters count towards quorum and majorities. Regs Arts. 27-30: proxies in writing to a shareholder or non-shareholder who is not a director, on the form annexed (Form 1) or under a notarised power of attorney expressly covering assembly attendance and voting; a proxy is valid for the next assembly and any adjournment; a resident natural person or a Saudi-incorporated legal person must have the signature authenticated by the chamber of commerce, a licensed bank or CMA-licensed person where the principal holds an account, a notary or licensed notarising person, or another body approved by the Ministry; non-residents and foreign entities authenticate through a Saudi mission abroad, except where an international convention exempts; a shareholder may not both attend and appoint a proxy; a copy of the proxy must reach the company at least two days before the meeting (the company may accept later proxies received before registration closes). | Virtual assemblies are lawful but need the Regs Art. 24-26 controls documented in the minutes (platform, identity verification, e-voting window). Foreign parent shareholders must plan proxy authentication through a Saudi embassy/consulate (or apostille where the Hague Convention applies `[model knowledge — verify]`) well before the meeting; a proxy not lodged two days before can be refused. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 85-88 | The extraordinary general assembly (EGA) amends the articles of association, except that it may not deprive a shareholder of, or modify, his fundamental rights (dividends, liquidation surplus, attending and voting, disposing of shares under the Law, inspection, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging assembly decisions), and amendments increasing shareholders' financial burdens require all shareholders' consent; the EGA also decides continuation or dissolution and approves buybacks (Art. 85); the EGA may decide ordinary matters under ordinary rules (Art. 86). The ordinary general assembly (OGA) handles everything else, in particular election and removal of directors, appointment, fees, reappointment and removal of the auditor, board and auditor reports, financial statements, dividend proposals, and reserves (Art. 87). The annual OGA must be held at least once within six months after fiscal year end, with a mandatory agenda: board report, financial statements, auditor's report, dividend proposal; an EGA held within the six months with that agenda satisfies the requirement (Art. 88). | Entity-compliance calendar: annual assembly by the end of the sixth month after year end (30 June for a calendar-year company); the same deadline governs the financial-statements deposit (Art. 17). Failure to call the annual assembly on time is a fineable violation (Art. 262(b)) and allows the Ministry to convene it (Art. 90). Any clause obliging shareholders to fund the company (capital calls beyond issue price) needs unanimity — see also Art. 116. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 90-91; Regs Art. 23 | General and special assemblies are convened by the board under the articles; the board must call the OGA within 30 days of a request by the auditor or by shareholders holding at least 10% of voting shares, and the auditor may convene it himself if the board fails to do so within 30 days of his request; the request must state the items to be voted on. The competent authority may itself call the OGA where the six-month period passed without a meeting, where violations of the Law or the articles or management defects (including a board below quorum) appear, or where the board ignored a valid request; it may take the steps needed to hold the meeting and chair it. (Art. 90). Notice of an assembly (Art. 91) must be given at least 21 days before the meeting under the Regulations: by registered letters to the addresses in the shareholders' register or by announcement through modern technology; a copy of the notice and agenda goes to the Commercial Register (and to the CMA if listed) on the date of the notice. The notice must state who may attend and the right to appoint a non-director proxy, the right to discuss and question and how to vote; place, date and time; the type of assembly; and the agenda with the items to be voted on. In an unlisted JSC, shareholders representing all voting shares may hold an assembly without observing the notice formalities and periods. Regs Art. 23: electronic notice must allow proof of content, date and time of sending and identification of sender and recipient. | A 10% shareholder has a hard right to force an OGA within 30 days; the Ministry can step in for unlisted JSCs (the CMA for listed) — a lever for minority investors in deadlock. Board-minutes/written-consent workflow: 21 calendar days' notice, a copy lodged with the Commercial Register the same day, and the full statutory content; the only shortcut is a 100% attendance meeting (waiver by all holders of voting shares). E-mail notice is acceptable if it is provable as to content, time and parties. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 92 | OGA quorum: shareholders representing at least one quarter of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding one half. If not met, a second meeting is called in the same manner within the 30 days following the first date; it may be held one hour after the first meeting's slot if the articles allow and the first notice said so; the second meeting is valid whatever the number of voting shares represented. OGA resolutions pass by majority of the voting rights represented at the meeting. | Formula for written-consent/board-minutes checks: first OGA valid if represented voting shares ≥ 25% (or the articles' figure, max 50%); resolution passes if votes for > 50% of voting rights represented. A "one hour later" second meeting is valid only with both an articles provision and a statement in the notice. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 93 | EGA quorum: shareholders representing at least half of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding two thirds. If not met, a second meeting (one hour later if the notice said so) is valid with at least one quarter of voting shares; if still not met, a third meeting is valid whatever the number represented. EGA resolutions pass by two thirds of the voting rights represented, except that resolutions to increase or reduce capital, extend the company's term, dissolve before the end of its term, merge with another company or divide into two or more companies require three quarters of the voting rights represented. The board must register with the Commercial Register the EGA resolutions specified by the Regulations within 15 days of their issue. | Formula: EGA first meeting quorum ≥ 50% (articles up to 66.7%); ordinary amendment passes at ≥ 2/3 of represented voting rights; capital change, term extension, early dissolution, merger and division pass at ≥ 3/4 of represented voting rights. A 25% holder can therefore block a merger or capital change only if he attends (the thresholds are of represented, not issued, shares). Register the resolution within 15 days — a closing-checklist item. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 94-98 | Assembly resolutions take effect on issue unless the Law, the articles or the resolution provide otherwise (Art. 94). The articles fix the voting method; directors may not vote on resolutions concerning dealings in which they have a direct or indirect interest or a conflict (Art. 95). The board must consider items shareholders wish to add; holders of 10% of voting shares may add items when the agenda is prepared; each subject must be a separate item; substantially different matters may not be bundled, and related-party dealings may not be bundled into one item; every shareholder may discuss and question the board and the auditor, any contrary articles clause is void, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 96). Minutes must record the number of shareholders present in person or by proxy, the shares and votes they hold, the resolutions adopted with votes for and against, and an adequate summary of the discussion; minutes are entered regularly after each meeting in a special register signed by the chair of the assembly, its secretary and the vote collectors; the competent authority may regulate minutes, secretaries and vote collectors (Art. 97). Where a JSC has a single shareholder (at incorporation or later), he exercises the assemblies' powers; his decisions are issued in writing without convening an assembly and are entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 98). | Assembly minutes have a statutory content list and three signatories (chair, secretary, vote collectors); a sole-shareholder JSC still needs a written decision entered in the register for every assembly-level matter. Bundled resolutions ("approve all related-party contracts") are non-compliant. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 99, 102, 170 | Any JSC shareholder may ask the court to annul an assembly resolution contrary to the Law or the articles, if he objected at the meeting or was absent with an acceptable excuse; the action is not heard after 90 days from the resolution; the claimant must remain a shareholder throughout (Art. 99). Shareholders holding at least 5% of capital may ask the court to order an inspection of the company where the conduct of the directors or the auditor raises suspicion; the court may order inspection at the applicant's expense (with security if the company requests), and if the complaint is proved may order precautionary measures, convene the assembly, remove directors and the auditor, and appoint supervisors to convene an assembly for a new board (Art. 102). The same 90-day annulment action lies for LLC partners, but only those who objected in writing or could not object after learning of the resolution (Art. 170). | Defective resolutions become unchallengeable after 90 days unless void for other reasons; for diligence, resolutions older than 90 days carry reduced risk of annulment. LLC partners must object in writing to preserve the right — a reason to send written objections to circulated resolutions. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 100-101 | In an unlisted JSC the articles may allow the chairman to propose a general-assembly resolution by circulation to the shareholders without a meeting, unless any shareholder requests a meeting in writing; election and removal of directors, appointment and removal of the auditor, and review and discussion of the financial statements always require a meeting; the company must send the proposed resolution with the relevant documents to all shareholders, stating how to approve and the date by which the resolution is to be issued (Art. 100). Circulated resolutions pass, for OGA matters, with the approval of shareholders holding a majority of voting rights and, for EGA matters, with at least 75% of voting rights, unless the articles require more; they are recorded in minutes and entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 101). | Written shareholder consents in a JSC require (i) an enabling articles clause, (ii) a chairman's proposal, (iii) circulation to all shareholders with documents, deadline and approval method, (iv) absolute majorities of all voting rights (> 50% ordinary; ≥ 75% extraordinary) — higher than the meeting thresholds, which count only represented shares — and (v) entry in the minutes register. The annual accounts, board elections and auditor changes cannot be done by written consent. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 103-112; Regs Arts. 51-55 | JSC shares are registered (nominal) and indivisible as against the company; co-owners appoint one representative and are jointly liable; the articles fix the nominal value, equal within a type or class; shares may be split or consolidated under the competent authority's rules; an unlisted JSC must issue paper or electronic share certificates (Art. 103). Subscription implies acceptance of the articles and assembly resolutions (Art. 104). Shares are issued against cash or in kind; at least one quarter of the nominal value of cash shares must be paid, the certificate showing the amount paid, and the balance within five years of issue; in-kind shares are issued only after full payment and transfer of the assets (Art. 105). Shares may not be issued below nominal value; a premium is allowed if the articles or the EGA permit, credited to a separate equity item used per the Regulations (Art. 106). Attached rights include disposal, attendance and voting, dividends, electing directors, inspection of records without breaching confidentiality, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging resolutions and a share of assets on liquidation, subject to the Law and the articles (Art. 107). Share types: ordinary, preferred and redeemable, with classes carrying different rights, privileges or restrictions under the articles and the Regulations (Art. 108); conversion between types/classes needs an articles provision and EGA approval (or automatic conversion terms), and conversion into redeemable shares needs all shareholders (Art. 109); modification of class rights requires a special assembly of the affected class under EGA rules plus EGA approval; new shares ranking ahead of preferred or redeemable shares need the affected class's consent (Arts. 89, 110). The CMA may restrict transfers in companies seeking listing; the articles may restrict share transfers, including redemption rights, but may not impose an absolute ban (Art. 111). An unlisted JSC keeps a shareholders' register (names, nationalities, details, residence, occupations, number, numbers and paid amount of shares), which may be outsourced but must be kept in the Kingdom; the Commercial Register must be given the register data and every change within 15 days of registration or of the change (Art. 112). Regs Art. 51: preferred or redeemable shares (and classes of them) may be issued only if the articles allow, issued capital is fully paid, the affected class's special assembly and the EGA approve, the proposal with the rights, privileges or restrictions accompanies the EGA notice and is reflected in the articles, the preferred and redeemable shares together never exceed 50% of capital, and they are fully paid on subscription; the EGA resolution states the redemption terms including the company's option, and redeemed shares are cancelled with a capital reduction. Regs Art. 52: conversion between types/classes needs an articles clause, must be into a type in Art. 108, needs the affected class's special assembly and the EGA, keeps preferred and redeemable shares within 50% of capital, and is supported by a board statement on the conversion ratio and its effect. Regs Art. 53: preferred and redeemable shares carry preferential rights but no vote at general assemblies; if a fixed or enhanced dividend is not paid for three consecutive years while profits suffice, their special assembly may resolve that they attend and vote (one vote per share) until arrears are paid. Regs Art. 54: share splits or consolidations need an articles clause, a board proposal sent to shareholders 21 days before the EGA, and EGA approval. Regs Art. 55: a shareholder in default has 30 days from the board's demand to pay; the auction is notified to the Ministry, advertised 15 to 30 days ahead in a daily newspaper or as the Ministry directs, and minuted. | Diligence on an unlisted JSC: obtain the shareholders' register and reconcile with Commercial Register filings (15-day updates); check paid-up status per share (five-year deadline for balance) and the class-rights matrix in the articles. Transfer restrictions (consent, pre-emption, lock-ups) are valid if in the articles and short of an absolute ban; an SHA-only restriction does not bind a transferee registered in the register. Preferred shares are non-voting by regulation (Regs Art. 53), which matters for investor term sheets: a preferred investor keeps control through reserved matters in the articles rather than votes, and preferred plus redeemable shares are capped at 50% of capital. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 113, 181 | With the approval of holders of at least 90% of voting shares (JSC) or 90% of capital (LLC), the articles may provide (a) drag-along: the majority may compel the minority to accept a bona fide buyer's offer for all shares/interests at the same price, terms and conditions as the majority's; (b) tag-along: the minority may compel the majority to procure the sale of the minority's shares/interests when the majority sell, at the same price, terms and conditions. | Drag and tag rights are enforceable only if inserted in the articles with a 90% vote; an SHA-only drag against a non-signatory or a transferee is weak. Diligence and closing checklists should verify the registered articles contain the clause and that the 90% approval was minuted when it was inserted. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 114-116, 180; Regs Arts. 34-39, 43-50, 66-69 | A JSC may buy back or take a pledge over its own shares if the articles allow; treasury shares carry no vote (Art. 114(1)); shares may be pledged, the pledgee collecting dividends and exercising share rights unless the pledge agreement provides otherwise, but never attending or voting at assemblies (Art. 114(2)). A shareholder who defaults on a call may, after notice, have his shares sold at public auction (or on the Exchange), the articles may give other shareholders priority; rights are suspended until sale or payment; the company recovers its dues and pays over the balance (Art. 115). The company may not demand more than the shareholder committed on issue, even if the articles say otherwise (Art. 116). An LLC may buy or take a pledge over its own interests if the articles allow; purchased interests carry no vote; a pledgee of interests collects dividends unless agreed otherwise (Art. 180). Regs Arts. 34-39: an unlisted JSC may buy its ordinary or preferred shares if the articles allow, only to reduce capital or to hold ordinary shares as treasury shares; treasury shares may never exceed 10% of the class, must be fully paid, and their cost may not exceed retained earnings; the buyback is approved by the EGA with a maximum number and purposes and a board authority to complete within 12 months, announced immediately; treasury shares may be used only to satisfy convertible debt/sukuk, as consideration for acquiring shares, interests or assets, for an employee share programme, for cancellation, or for another Ministry-approved purpose; shareholders must receive adequate information and a fair opportunity to tender; purchased preferred shares are cancelled; the EGA may fix a holding period. Regs Arts. 43-45: treasury shares are sold by board resolution if the articles allow and consistently with the EGA resolution, existing shareholders having pro rata priority on a cash sale; the company may take a pledge over its own shares only if the articles allow, as security for a debt owed to it, in the interest of the company and shareholders as the board judges, with general-assembly approval (which may be given in advance for several transactions). Regs Arts. 46-50: a shareholder may pledge his shares after any required regulatory approvals; a pledge is created by a written contract stating the parties and their identity numbers and addresses, the number, value and numbers of the pledged shares and the issuer's Commercial Register number, the secured debt and any cap, the debtor if different, the date, release terms and other terms; the pledgee and pledgor each file an application with the chairman (or the outsourced registrar) on Forms 2 and 3 with authenticated signatures (chamber of commerce, licensed bank or CMA-licensed person, notary or licensed notarising person, or a Saudi mission abroad for non-residents, subject to treaty exemptions); the pledge is entered in the shareholders' register and annotated on the certificates; release is by expiry or by the pledgee's Form 4; the pledge is effective against third parties only once entered in the shareholders' register; Regs Art. 66: an LLC buyback needs an articles clause, partners' approval with waiver of redemption rights and a manager's authority to complete within 30 days; Regs Art. 67: an LLC may take a pledge over its own interests only if the articles allow, as security for a debt to the company, in the interest of the company and partners, with partners' approval at the amendment quorum; Regs Arts. 68-69: a partner's pledge of interests requires the necessary regulatory approvals and a written contract stating the parties, the number and value of pledged interests and the Commercial Register number, the secured debt, the debtor, date, release terms and other agreed terms. | Security packages over Saudi shares/interests: pledge over LLC interests is by written contract meeting Regs Art. 69 and registered under the Movable Assets Security Law regime `[model knowledge — verify]`; pledge over unlisted JSC shares is perfected only by entry in the shareholders' register on the Regulations' forms (Regs Art. 50), so a lender's closing checklist needs the registrar's confirmation of entry, not just the signed pledge. Pledgees never vote. Treasury shares dilute voting thresholds because they carry no votes. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 121-122, 125; Regs Arts. 31-32 | At the end of each fiscal year the board prepares the financial statements and a report on the company's activity and financial position with the proposed dividend, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual OGA; they are signed by the chairman, the CEO and the CFO (if any) and copies are kept at the head office for shareholders (Art. 121). The chairman must provide shareholders with the signed statements, board report and auditor's report at least 21 days before the annual OGA unless published by modern technology, and deposit them as the Regulations specify (Art. 122). The assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; the resolution states the entitlement (record) date and the distribution date; dividends belong to holders registered at the end of the record date; the Regulations cap the time for the board to implement (Art. 125). Regs Art. 31: the chairman sends the board report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual OGA. Regs Art. 32: dividends must be paid to registered shareholders within 15 days of the entitlement date. | Annual JSC timeline working back from the assembly date D: statements to auditor by D-45; notice, statements and board report to shareholders and board report to the Ministry by D-21; assembly by month 6; financial statements deposited by month 6; dividends paid within 15 days of the record date. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 126-131; Regs Arts. 40-42, 57-61 | Capital is increased by new shares for cash or in kind; by shares for ascertained and due debts with the creditors' consent, at a value set by the EGA after an expert or accredited valuer's opinion and a board statement with an auditor's report; by capitalising reserves (bonus shares pro rata); or by shares for debt instruments or sukuk (Art. 126). The EGA may increase issued or authorised capital only if issued capital is fully paid (except unpaid shares from pending conversions); it may allocate new shares to employees of the company and its subsidiaries, without pre-emption for shareholders; new shares have the same nominal value as existing shares of the type/class (Art. 127). Shareholders at the date of the EGA (or board) resolution have pre-emptive rights over new cash shares in proportion to their type/class and are notified by registered letter or technology of the terms and subscription period (Art. 128); the EGA may suspend pre-emption or grant it to non-shareholders if the articles allow (Art. 129); pre-emption rights may be sold or waived under the Regulations (Art. 130); allocation is pro rata to rights, then to over-subscribers, then to third parties unless the EGA or the Capital Market Law provide otherwise (Art. 131). Regs Art. 40: a company holding treasury shares (other than for an employee programme), or with an unrevoked EGA buyback approval, may not increase issued capital by a rights issue; Regs Art. 41: treasury shares participate in a capitalisation issue. Regs Art. 42: an employee share programme using treasury shares needs an articles clause, EGA approval of the programme (the board may be delegated its terms and the allocation price), exclusion of non-executive directors, and abstention of executive directors from board votes on it. Regs Arts. 57-61: pre-emption rights accrue to shareholders registered at the end of the day of the EGA (or board) resolution, in proportion to their shares and by type/class, but not for employee-allocated shares or where the EGA has suspended or reallocated the right; each right entitles the holder to subscribe for one new share at the price fixed in the resolution; subject to the articles, a registered shareholder may sell or assign rights to anyone, shareholder or not, at an agreed price during the subscription period, which may not be shorter than 15 days; the seller must notify the company in writing with the buyer's name, nationality, residence, occupation and number of rights, and the buyer steps into the subscription right; unexercised rights lapse at the end of the last subscription day. | A third-party investment round in a JSC needs either pre-emption waivers from all shareholders or an articles clause allowing the EGA (3/4 majority, Art. 93) to suspend pre-emption. Debt-to-equity conversions require creditor consent, a valuation and an auditor's report. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 132 | If a JSC's losses reach half of its issued capital, the board must within 60 days of learning of it disclose the fact and its recommendations, and call the EGA to meet within 180 days of that date to decide whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | Losses trigger for diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of issued capital (from the latest financial statements or management accounts) → check for the board disclosure within 60 days and the EGA within 180 days. Unlike the 2015 Law there is no automatic dissolution for failing to act `[model knowledge — verify]`, but failure to call the assembly is a criminal offence under Art. 261(h) and the assembly may resolve to dissolve. Continuation usually involves a capital reduction (Art. 134 allows reduction below SAR 500,000 for losses) or shareholder recapitalisation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 133-137 | Capital is reduced by cancelling shares, reducing nominal value by the amount of losses, reducing nominal value by refund or release of unpaid amounts, or by buying and cancelling shares (Art. 133). The EGA may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses (only then below the SAR 500,000 minimum), after a board statement of reasons, liabilities and the effect on them with an auditor's report is read at the assembly (or circulated where the resolution is by circulation) (Art. 134). Where the reduction is for surplus capital, creditors must be invited to object at least 45 days before the EGA, with a statement of capital before and after and the effective date; an objecting creditor who lodges his documents in time must be paid if due or given adequate security if not, and may apply to the court before the EGA, which may order payment, security or postponement; the reduction is not effective against a timely-objecting creditor until he is paid or secured (Art. 135). Shareholders of the same type/class are treated equally (Art. 136); reduction by buyback requires an invitation to all shareholders, pro rata scaling and, for unlisted companies, purchase at fair value (Art. 137). | A return of surplus capital to a JSC shareholder is a 45-day creditor process plus a 3/4 EGA vote; a loss-absorbing reduction has no creditor window. Diligence should check that a historic reduction for surplus capital was preceded by the creditor invitation, otherwise unpaid creditors can treat it as ineffective against them. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 138-141 | The SJSC (شركة المساهمة المبسطة) is governed by the JSC rules except Arts. 61, 63, 67-71, 74-88, 90-94, 95(1), 96-98, 100, 101, 111(2), 121 and 122; shareholders organise the structure and operation in the articles; the shareholders replace the OGA and EGA and may allocate those powers in the articles; the president, manager or board exercises the JSC board's powers (Art. 138). The articles fix issued and paid-up capital and may provide authorised capital; the JSC minimum capital does not apply (Art. 139). Articles must cover name, head office, objects, capital, shares (types/classes/rights), term, management, share assignment, shareholders' meetings and their quorum, shareholders' decisions and their quorum, fiscal year; attachments mirror the JSC list including a bank deposit certificate and the appointment of the president/manager/board (Art. 140). In-kind contributions not exceeding half the capital need no accredited valuer unless the founders/shareholders decide otherwise; above half, an accredited valuer's report is required, contributors do not vote, and a reduction of the agreed value needs their consent; without a proper valuation the founders/shareholders are personally liable to third parties for the fairness of the valuation and for paying the difference in cash, for five years from registration or the increase (Art. 141). | The SJSC is the venture-capital vehicle: no minimum capital, share classes, freedom in governance and reserved matters, and (because Art. 71 is excluded) related-party approvals fall back on the general Art. 27 regime. Because the JSC assembly articles (84-88, 90-94, 96-98, 100-101) do not apply, quorum, majority and notice rules for an SJSC come from its articles and Arts. 145-149 — a reviewer must read the specific articles of association rather than assume JSC thresholds. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 142-144 | The articles fix how the SJSC is managed — a president, one or more managers, a board or otherwise — and how the managers are appointed and removed, their powers and working method; if silent, the shareholders decide; the president/manager/board has the widest powers except matters reserved to shareholders, may delegate specific acts; the president, manager or chairman represents the company and may delegate representation if the articles allow; the company is bound even by acts outside the manager's powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew (Art. 142). JSC board liability rules apply to the president, manager or board (Art. 143), as does the Art. 72 prohibition on loans to them (Art. 144). | A single director (president or manager) is permitted; there is no minimum board size for an SJSC. Counterparty authority check: identify from the articles whether a president, manager or chairman signs, and whether delegation to a CEO is authorised by the articles. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 145-149 | The SJSC articles list the matters reserved to shareholders, but capital increase or reduction, transformation, merger, division, dissolution, auditor appointment, discussion of the financial statements, dividends and amendment of the articles must always be decided by the shareholders; the articles set the quorum for meetings and decisions, may set different quorums for different matters, and must specify the matters requiring unanimity (Art. 145). Meetings are called by the president/manager/board under the articles, and may be requested by the auditor or by holders of 10% of voting shares; notice to all shareholders at least 5 days before, with place, date, time and agenda (and optionally a second-meeting date); by registered letter or technology unless the articles provide otherwise; for reserved matters each shareholder may obtain the relevant documents during the 5 days before the meeting (or longer per the articles); meetings may be held at the head office, elsewhere or by technology; holders of all voting shares may meet without notice formalities (Art. 146). Financial statements and the activity report (with dividend proposal) plus the auditor's report are presented to shareholders within six months of fiscal year end and deposited per the Regulations (Art. 147). Deliberations and decisions, including those by circulation, are recorded in minutes in a special register signed by the president/manager/board; technology may be used; the decisions specified by the Regulations must be registered with the Commercial Register within 15 days (Art. 148). The articles may allow decisions by circulation: the proposed decision and documents are sent to all shareholders (registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide otherwise) stating how to approve and the decision date; the articles fix the quorum for circulated decisions (Art. 149). | SJSC written consents are valid only under an articles clause and at the quorum the articles fix; there is no statutory fallback majority, so a silent articles document means no circulation route. Notice is only 5 calendar days. The statutory list of non-delegable shareholder matters is the minimum reserved-matters list for any SJSC SHA. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 150-154 | A single-shareholder SJSC: the owner's liability is limited to the capital allocated; he exercises the shareholders' powers by written decisions entered in a special register (Art. 150). The articles may restrict share disposals by (a) a lock-up of up to 10 years from issue, extendable by unanimity, and (b) a requirement of the company's or shareholders' consent before disposal; a disposal in breach is void (Art. 151). The articles may set conditions on which a shareholder can be compelled to transfer his shares, at fair value unless the articles provide otherwise, with non-financial rights suspended until transfer (Art. 152). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between shareholders or between the company and its president/manager/directors to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 153). Inserting or amending Arts. 151-153 provisions in the articles requires unanimity (Art. 154). | Founder lock-ups, consent rights, good/bad-leaver compulsory transfers and arbitration clauses are statutorily enforceable in an SJSC when placed in the articles — but only with unanimous consent when inserted or changed, so diligence should look for the unanimous resolution. Transfers in breach of a registered lock-up or consent clause are void, not merely a breach of contract. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 156-159, 174, 183-184 | LLC (الشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة): formed by one or more natural or legal persons; its estate is separate from the partners'; only the company is liable for its debts; a partner or sole owner is liable only to the extent of his interest in the capital (Art. 156). Single-owner LLC: the owner exercises the powers of the manager, board of managers and general assembly by written decisions entered in a special register; he may appoint one or more managers who represent the company and answer to him; a single-owner LLC has articles of association and references to articles of incorporation are read accordingly (Art. 157). Articles of incorporation must state: partners' names and details, name, head office, objects, capital and its division, the partners' declaration that the interests are paid, term (if any), management, assignment of interests, the means of notifying partners, partners' decisions, profit/loss distribution, fiscal year, dissolution, and other agreed terms; attachments: a compliance declaration and an accredited valuer's report on in-kind contributions (Art. 158), valued under Art. 141 (Art. 159). The partners fix the capital in the articles (no statutory minimum); it is divided into interests of equal value, indivisible and non-tradeable; co-owned interests must have a single representative or the company may suspend rights and ultimately sell the interest (Art. 174). A fixed-term LLC may be extended before expiry by partners holding half the interests (or more if the articles say so); if it continues trading without a resolution it is extended for a like term; a dissenting partner may exit at a value fixed under Art. 178, the extension taking effect only after his interests are sold and paid; third parties with an interest may object (Art. 183). Death, incapacity, insolvency, bankruptcy proceedings or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the LLC unless the articles so provide (Art. 184). | The LLC has no minimum capital under the Law; MISA licensing may nonetheless expect a stated capital for certain activities `[model knowledge — verify]`. The Law text read contains no cap on the number of LLC partners and no bar on an LLC owned by another single-owner LLC. The old 2015-Law requirement that a sole owner may not own more than one LLC is not in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 160-162 | An LLC is managed by one or more managers, partners or not, appointed by the partners in the articles or in a separate contract, for a fixed or indefinite term; the partners may constitute a board of managers where there are several (Art. 160). The articles or a partners' decision fix the management method and the majority for managers' decisions (Art. 161). The manager represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties and may delegate specific acts; a decision appointing, replacing or restricting the powers of a manager is effective against third parties only after registration with the Commercial Register; the company is bound by the manager's acts within its objects (Art. 162). | Counterparty authority for an LLC: the manager named on the Commercial Register binds the company within its objects; internal limits (SHA reserved matters, approval thresholds) do not protect the company against third parties unless registered. Closing checklist: register the change of manager and any power limits on the Register on completion. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 163-164; Regs Art. 63 | If a sole manager's office falls vacant, the partners must appoint a new manager within 15 days of learning of it; the auditor or any partner may call the general assembly for that purpose (Art. 163). The partners may remove the manager(s), whether appointed in the articles or a separate contract, and must appoint successors; a manager who is a partner may not vote on his own removal; partners holding at least one quarter of capital may petition the court for removal (Art. 164). Regs Art. 63: subject to the articles, removal of an LLC manager, even a partner named in the articles, is decided at the quorum the Law sets for partners' decisions (Art. 166: more than half of capital). | Removal of an LLC manager is an ordinary partners' decision (> 50% of capital), not an amendment of the articles, even where he is named in the articles; a manager-partner's interests are excluded from the vote on his removal. A 25% partner can seek court removal. The registered articles then need updating (Art. 162). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 165 | An LLC has a general assembly of all partners, convened by the manager(s) as the articles provide, at least once a year within six months after fiscal year end; it may be called at any time at the request of the managers, the auditor, or partners holding at least 10% of capital; notice goes to all partners by registered letter, modern technology or any other means in the articles at least 21 days before the meeting; partners representing all interests may meet without observing notice formalities and periods; deliberations, decisions and decisions by circulation are recorded in minutes entered in a special register kept for the purpose, and technology may be used to record them; meetings and voting may be held by technology. | LLC annual compliance: assembly within six months of year end (same window as the financial-statements deposit), 21 calendar days' notice unless 100% of interests attend, and a minutes register (paper or electronic) containing every resolution including written ones. The absence of a minutes register is a common diligence finding in foreign-owned LLCs. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 166, 171 | Partners' decisions are taken in the general assembly but may be taken by circulation without a meeting: the manager sends each partner the proposed decisions and documents for a written vote, by registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide another means. In all cases a decision is valid only if approved by partners holding more than half of the capital, unless the articles require a larger majority; if that majority is not reached in the first deliberation or consultation, the partners must be called to a meeting at which decisions pass by a majority of the interests represented, whatever their proportion of capital, unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 166). Each partner takes part in deliberations and voting with votes equal to his interests, and no contrary agreement is allowed; a partner may appoint another partner in writing as proxy unless the articles provide otherwise, and the articles may allow non-partner proxies; a non-manager partner may advise the manager and may inspect the company's business, records and documents at the head office twice per fiscal year, the company complying within 15 days; contrary clauses are void; information obtained is confidential (Art. 171). | Written-consent formula for an LLC ordinary decision: approvals from holders of > 50% of the capital (an absolute majority, not of those voting); if not reached, a called meeting decides by majority of interests present (the second-round rule can let a minority decide, so articles often exclude it — check). Voting proxies to non-partners (e.g. a parent's lawyer) need an articles clause. One-interest-one-vote cannot be varied. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 167-169; Regs Art. 65 | The manager prepares for each fiscal year the financial statements, an activity and financial-position report and dividend proposals, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual assembly; he provides partners with the statements, report and auditor's report (by technology or as the articles provide) at least 21 days before the annual assembly and deposits them as the Regulations specify (Art. 167). The annual agenda must include the manager's report, the financial statements, the auditor's report and the dividend proposal (Art. 168). The assembly may not deliberate outside the agenda unless facts emerge at the meeting; a partner may require an item to be added, and may appeal to the assembly if refused; every partner may discuss and question, the manager must answer, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 169). Regs Art. 65: the manager sends the activity report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual assembly. | LLC annual timeline mirrors the JSC: D-45 to auditor; D-21 to partners and the Ministry; assembly and deposit within six months of year end. The Ministry filing of the manager's activity report is frequently overlooked in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 172-173; Regs Art. 64 | The articles of incorporation, including capital increases and reductions, may be amended with the approval of partners holding at least three quarters of the capital, unless the articles require more; on a cash increase by new interests each partner has pre-emption pro rata to his holding under the Regulations; an increase by raising the nominal value of interests, or suspension of pre-emption, requires unanimity (Art. 172). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between partners or between the company and its managers to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 173). Regs Art. 64: a partner who takes less than his entitlement leaves the balance to the other partners pro rata; the partners file a draft amendment with a declaration that the new interests are paid, and the increase is effective on registration and publication with the Commercial Register. | Formula for LLC special decisions: ≥ 75% of capital (absolute) to amend the articles, change capital, transform, merge or divide (Arts. 220, 225, 232 refer back to the amendment quorum); unanimity to suspend pre-emption or raise nominal value. The 2015-Law rule requiring unanimity to change the company's nationality or increase partners' obligations is not in the Art. 172 text read `[model knowledge — verify]`; Art. 3 fixes Saudi nationality in any event, and a JSC-style "no increased financial burdens without all shareholders" rule appears only in Art. 85. A 25%+1 partner holds a veto over every amendment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 175-177 | Interests carry equal rights to net profits and liquidation surplus unless the articles provide otherwise; the assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; entitlement and payment dates are stated in the resolution (Art. 175). The assembly may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses; in the loss case a manager's statement of reasons, liabilities and effect with an auditor's report is read (or circulated); where the reduction returns surplus capital, every manager must sign a solvency statement confirming, on examination, that nothing would render the company unable to pay its debts and that it can pay debts falling due within the next 12 months, delivered to partners at least 15 days before the decision; the partners file a draft amendment with the Register within 15 days of the decision, attaching the statements, and the reduction is effective on registration and publication (Art. 176). The articles may provide for a reserve; the annual assembly may create reserves and allocate amounts for employees' social purposes (Art. 177). | An LLC has no creditor-objection window on a capital reduction (contrast Art. 135 for JSCs); the safeguard is the managers' personal solvency statement, which exposes managers criminally under Art. 260(a) if false. The statutory reserve of the 2015 Law is no longer mandatory in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`; reserves are optional. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 178 | A partner may assign his interest to another partner on the terms in the articles. To assign to a non-partner, with or without consideration, the partner must notify the other partners through the manager of the assignee's or buyer's name and the terms; the manager notifies the partners immediately; each partner may within 30 days of the manager's notification of the agreed price demand to redeem the interest and pay its value, or have the company buy it; if several partners redeem, the interest is divided pro rata to their capital; if the value is disputed it is fixed by an accredited valuer's fair-value report at the cost of the redeeming partner or the company; if the period expires without redemption, payment or company purchase, the partner may assign to the third party. The articles may provide other notification procedures, another valuation method, or a longer period for redemption, payment or company purchase. The redemption right does not apply to transfers by inheritance, will or court judgment. | Statutory pre-emption in every multi-partner LLC: a third-party sale of interests requires the manager-routed notice and the 30-day redemption window (or waivers from all other partners). Closing checklist for an LLC acquisition: written waivers of Art. 178 rights (and any longer articles period) from every non-selling partner, then the amended articles registered with the Commercial Register (Art. 25). A change of control of a corporate partner is not an "assignment" under the text and does not trigger Art. 178 `[model knowledge — verify]`, though SHAs often add it contractually. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Art. 182 | If an LLC's losses reach half of its capital, the manager must call the general assembly of partners to meet within 60 days of learning that losses reached that level, to consider whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | Losses trigger for LLC diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of registered capital → assembly within 60 days of knowledge. The text imposes no automatic dissolution and no partner liability for the company's debts for failing to act (the 2015-Law joint liability of partners has been dropped in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`), but the manager commits an offence under Art. 261(h) if he fails to call the assembly. Foreign-owned LLCs funded by shareholder loans often sit above the threshold; a recapitalisation or loss-absorbing reduction (Art. 176) is the usual cure. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 185-186, 197-199, 216-218 (notes) | Non-profit companies: a public non-profit company must be a JSC and spend its profits on public-benefit purposes fixed with the National Center for Non-Profit Sector; a private non-profit company may be an LLC, JSC or SJSC spending profits on stated non-profit purposes; neither may offer shares to the public; otherwise the rules of the chosen form apply (Arts. 185-186). Professional companies: formed by one or more persons licensed to practise a liberal profession, alone or with others, in any of the five forms; the form's rules apply save as Part 8 provides; a partner is not a merchant by reason of his holding (Arts. 197-199); Regs Part 5 sets conditions for single-owner professional companies, multi-profession firms, participation of non-licensed persons and foreign professional firms (headings read). Holding companies: a JSC, SJSC or LLC that forms or holds subsidiaries; a company is a subsidiary where the holder has a majority of voting rights, alone controls appointment or removal of the manager or a majority of the board, controls a majority of votes by agreement with other holders, or is a subsidiary of a subsidiary; a subsidiary may not hold interests or shares in its parent (acquisitions to the contrary are void) and must dispose of pre-existing holdings within 12 months without voting them, with exceptions for CMA-licensed persons (Arts. 216-218). | Group structuring: circular holdings are void; a control test (voting majority, board control, or control by agreement) defines subsidiaries for Art. 17 information rights and for the Art. 19 audit exemption. Professional-firm and non-profit specifics beyond these definitions were not read in detail. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 220-224 | A company may transform into another form by a resolution passed under the rules for amending its articles, after satisfying the incorporation, registration and publication requirements of the new form; transformation into an SJSC requires unanimity; sole proprietors may transfer their assets into any company form without being released from prior debts unless creditors expressly agree; a general partnership, limited partnership or LLC wholly owned by relatives (or including waqf/bequest interests) may transform into a JSC on the request of holders of more than half the capital (Art. 220). A private non-profit company may transform, spending surplus on its non-profit purposes and refunding exemptions; any company may become a non-profit company by unanimity (Art. 221; Regs Art. 86 requires proof and a special auditor's report). Partners or shareholders who object to the transformation may exit by written request within 15 days of the resolution, paid at the agreed value or an accredited valuer's fair value at the transformation date, unless the articles provide otherwise, with recourse to the court (Art. 222). Transformation creates no new legal person; the company keeps its rights and remains bound by prior obligations (Art. 223). General partners are not released from pre-transformation debts unless creditors expressly agree or fail to object within 30 days of notice by registered letter or technology (Art. 224). | An LLC-to-JSC/SJSC conversion (common pre-IPO or pre-VC round) needs a 3/4 partners' vote (unanimity for SJSC), keeps contracts in place without assignment, and gives dissenters a 15-day appraisal exit — a cash-out contingency for the deal model. Contract reviewers: change-of-form clauses in counterparties' contracts are not triggered by a new legal entity, but may still be drafted to bite. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 225-229; Regs Art. 87 | Merger is by absorption into an existing company or by amalgamation into a new company; a merger proposal must be approved by each party under its articles-amendment rules and must set out the terms, the nature and value of the consideration including the interests/shares allotted, and each party's ability to pay its debts; a company in liquidation may merge; the merger is valid only after valuation of each party's assets; consideration is interests or shares in the absorbing or new company; the competent authority may regulate cash for fractions, compensation of dissenters and voting by conflicted holders (Art. 225). The Regulations govern mergers into a 100% parent or between companies with identical owners (Art. 226): Regs Art. 87 — a wholly-owned subsidiary is merged by a resolution of the parent alone, with a managers'/board solvency statement for each party, without a merger proposal or asset valuation; sister companies with identical owners merge by resolutions of each, without asset valuation. Each party must announce the merger at least 30 days before the date set for the decision; creditors of the merged company may object by registered letter (or the means stated in the announcement) within 15 days of the announcement, and must be paid if due or adequately secured if not; an unpaid/unsecured objector may apply to the court no later than 10 days before the decision date, and the court may order payment or security, or suspend or postpone the merger before it takes effect, or award damages afterwards (Art. 227). The merger takes effect on registration of the merged company's data in the absorbing company's Commercial Register entry, or on registration of the new company (Art. 228); all rights, obligations, assets and contracts of the merged company pass to the absorbing/new company as successor (Art. 229). | Integration timeline for a statutory merger: valuation → proposal approved by each company (LLC 3/4 of capital; JSC 3/4 of represented votes at an EGA) → public announcement ≥ 30 days before the vote → creditor objections within 15 days of the announcement → court applications up to 10 days before the vote → vote → registration (effective date; universal succession, so contracts transfer without counterparty consent unless they contain change-of-control or anti-assignment-by-merger terms). Intra-group mergers of wholly-owned subsidiaries are simplified. Competition clearance (GAC economic-concentration filing) and CMA rules for listed parties are separate gates `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 230; Regs Art. 88 | Squeeze-out and sell-out in JSCs (subject to the Capital Market Law): a person, or persons acting in concert, who reach 90% or more of voting shares directly or indirectly, or unconditionally contract to buy that proportion, must disclose it to shareholders; any shareholder may within 90 days of the disclosure require the holder to make an offer for his shares (sell-out). The 90% holder may within 60 days apply to the competent authority for approval of a compulsory offer forcing the remaining shareholders to sell (squeeze-out); shareholders may contest the price in court within 60 days of the offer; only the competent authority may suspend a compulsory offer; settlement within 7 days after the objection period unless the court orders otherwise. Regs Art. 88: the disclosure goes through the board, which informs the other shareholders; a sell-out offer must be made in cash within 60 days of the request at not less than the highest price the holder paid in the preceding 12 months; the squeeze-out application to the Ministry must attach the offer and price, likewise not below the highest price paid in the preceding 12 months; after the objection period the purchaser completes through the board, which places the cash in a bank account for the affected shareholders and updates the register. | Acquisitions of 90%+ of an unlisted JSC carry both a minority put (90 days) and a squeeze-out call (application within 60 days), priced at no less than the highest price paid in the prior 12 months — relevant to price-protection and earn-out structures. There is no equivalent for LLCs or SJSCs in the text read; drag-along under Art. 113/181 is the LLC/SJSC route. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 231-234; Regs Arts. 89-92 | A company, even in liquidation, may divide into two or more companies of any form (Art. 231); the division resolution is passed under the articles-amendment rules and must state the number of partners/shareholders, their shares in the resulting and the divided company, and the allocation of rights, assets and liabilities (Art. 232). Resulting companies succeed the divided company to the extent allocated, but creditors of the divided company may claim against any of the resulting companies, which are jointly liable, unless creditors agree to the transfer of their claims to a specific resulting company (Art. 233). Regs Arts. 89-92: the division resolution requires the amendment quorum; owners receive interests/shares pro rata unless they agree otherwise; the manager/board prepares a proposal stating reasons, the assets and liabilities divided, an accredited valuer's fair-value report (not needed for a pro rata division), the shares to be received, and any creditor agreements; the proposal goes to owners at least 21 days before the vote; the division takes effect on registration of the amended articles of the divided company and of the resulting company. | Carve-outs by statutory division leave all resulting companies jointly liable to pre-division creditors unless each creditor consents to the allocation — a post-closing indemnity point for buyers of a divided entity. No creditor objection period is stated for division in the text read; the protection is joint liability. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 235-241 | Foreign companies carrying on activity in the Kingdom are subject to the Law (other than its incorporation provisions), without prejudice to treaties and other laws (Art. 235), and operate through a branch, representative office or other form under the Investment Law (Art. 236). Each branch/representative office must show its Saudi address and the foreign company's full name, address and head office on all papers (Art. 237). The branch registration application must state its fiscal year; a branch (not a representative office) must prepare financial statements for its Saudi activity under Saudi standards and deposit them with the auditor's report within six months of the fiscal year end per the Regulations; the branch manager may appoint the auditor under delegation from the foreign company (Art. 238). The branch/office is the foreign company's domicile for its Saudi activity and subject to all Saudi laws (Art. 239). If the foreign company operates before licensing and registration, or beyond its licence, the company and the persons who carried on the activity are jointly liable (Art. 240). A foreign company present to perform specific works for a limited period is registered temporarily and deregistered on completion after settling its rights and obligations, unless it regularises for continuation; the Ministry and the Ministry of Investment set the rules (Art. 241). | A branch needs a MISA licence and Commercial Register entry before signing Saudi contracts; the individuals who sign for an unregistered branch are personally liable with the company. Branch annual compliance: audited branch accounts deposited within six months; the branch manager's authority comes from the foreign company's registered power of attorney `[model knowledge — verify]` and should be checked on counterparty authority reviews. Temporary registrations (project branches) lapse on project completion. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 242-245 | Before the partners, assembly or shareholders resolve to dissolve, the managers or directors must prepare a statement confirming they have examined the company's position and that its assets suffice to pay its debts by the end of the proposed liquidation and that the company is not distressed under the Bankruptcy Law; it is put to the owners within 30 days of preparation; if it shows insufficiency or distress, the owners may not resolve to dissolve, on pain of joint liability for remaining debts (Art. 242). General grounds of dissolution: expiry of term unless extended; agreement of the partners/shareholders to dissolve; a final judgment dissolving or annulling the company (Art. 243). On dissolution the company enters liquidation and keeps personality for that purpose; the owners must start liquidation; the Art. 242 statement must be prepared (or be no older than 30 days); an insolvent or distressed company must apply to the court to open Bankruptcy Law liquidation; liquidation in breach makes the owners/managers/directors jointly liable for remaining debts; a public non-profit company needs Ministry approval (Art. 244). Liquidation follows the articles or the owners' agreement, failing which the Law (Art. 245). | Voluntary liquidation is available only for solvent companies, gated by a managers' solvency statement (false statements are criminal under Art. 260(a)); insolvent entities must go through the Bankruptcy Law. Post-acquisition clean-up of dormant entities: use the Regs Art. 93 short-form route (see Arts. 252-259 row). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 246-251 | On dissolution the managers'/board's authority ends but they continue to manage and are treated as liquidators towards third parties until a liquidator is appointed; assemblies continue with powers not conflicting with the liquidator's; owners keep inspection rights (Art. 246). One or more liquidators (owners or not) are appointed; liquidation may not exceed three years, extendable only by court order (Art. 247). The liquidator is appointed by the owners under the articles-amendment rules within 60 days of dissolution, failing which by the court on application of any owner or interested person; where dissolution is by judgment, the court appoints; the court first requires the Art. 242 statement or equivalent evidence within 30 days and opens Bankruptcy Law proceedings if assets are insufficient; the appointment decision must fix powers, fees, restrictions and the liquidation period (Art. 248). The liquidator registers and publishes his appointment with the Commercial Register, without which it cannot be relied on against third parties (Art. 249); he is removed in the manner appointed, or by the court on an owner's or creditor's application, with a replacement named (Art. 250); multiple liquidators act jointly and unanimously unless authorised otherwise (Art. 251). | Liquidation checklist: solvency statement (≤ 30 days old) → dissolution and liquidator appointment resolution at the amendment quorum (3/4 for an LLC) within 60 days → Commercial Register registration → maximum three years. Directors remain de facto liquidators, with liquidator liability, until the appointment is registered. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 252-259; Regs Art. 93 | The liquidator represents the company, converts assets to cash (auction or best-price sale), may sell assets en bloc or contribute them to another company only with the appointing body's authority, may not start new business except to complete existing business, binds the company within his powers, and his powers end with the liquidation or its period, whichever first (Art. 252). Managers/directors hand over records; within 90 days of starting he prepares an inventory of assets, rights and liabilities with an auditor's report (extendable by the appointing body); annually he prepares financial statements and a report, files copies with the Commercial Register and submits them to the owners (Art. 253). If assets prove insufficient he must immediately inform owners and creditors and apply to open Bankruptcy Law proceedings (Art. 254). He pays due debts by priority, reserves for deferred or disputed debts (liquidation debts rank first), returns capital and distributes the surplus per the articles or pro rata (Art. 255). On completion he files a detailed financial report; liquidation ends on the appointing body's approval; he registers and publishes the end of liquidation, effective against third parties only on deregistration (Art. 257). He is liable for exceeding his powers or for errors (Art. 258), with a five-year limitation from deregistration except for forgery and fraud (Art. 259). Regs Art. 93: a company that never carried on any activity may be dissolved by a unanimous owners' resolution submitted to the Ministry containing a declaration that it never traded, has no assets, debts (present, future or disputed), zakat, tax or fees due, plus a joint and several undertaking by all owners to pay any debts that emerge from their own funds; the undertaking counts as the final liquidation report, the closing accounts and the deregistration request under Art. 257. | Dormant-shell clean-up after integration: the Regs Art. 93 short-form route replaces a full liquidation but makes all owners jointly liable for any later-discovered debt. Zakat/tax clearance in practice is required before deregistration `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 260-261 | Imprisonment up to three years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000 for: (a) any manager, officer, director, auditor or liquidator who deliberately records false or misleading information in the financial statements, reports, capital-reduction statements, solvency statements or other statements put to the owners, or deliberately omits a material fact to misrepresent the financial position; (b) any manager, officer or director who uses the company's funds, powers or votes knowingly against its interests for personal ends, to favour a company or person, or to benefit from a transaction in which he has an interest; (c) a liquidator who does likewise or prefers a creditor without lawful cause (Art. 260). Imprisonment up to one year and/or a fine up to SAR 1,000,000 for, among others: an auditor who fails to report apparent criminal violations to the company; vote-buying; falsely announcing registration of an unregistered company; a public official disclosing company secrets; publishing false names to attract subscriptions; deciding, paying or receiving dividends in bad faith contrary to the Law or the articles (and an auditor who fails to report it); false declarations on in-kind valuations, allocation of interests/shares or payment of their value; any manager, officer, director or auditor who fails to call (or to take steps to call) the assembly on learning that losses reached the Arts. 132/182 threshold; exploiting or disclosing company secrets to harm the company; deliberately obstructing persons entitled to inspect records; a court-appointed inspector who falsifies or omits material facts (Art. 261). | Personal criminal exposure of nominee managers/directors: false financial statements or solvency statements (3 years/SAR 5m) and failure to convene the losses assembly (1 year/SAR 1m). Diligence on a loss-making target should therefore check the losses-assembly minutes; integration plans should regularise before new managers are appointed. Public Prosecution investigates these offences (Art. 265). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 262-267, 269-271; Regs Art. 94 | Fine up to SAR 500,000 for violations including: obstructing the calling or holding of an assembly or preventing an owner from attending or voting; failing to call the annual assembly within the statutory period; accepting or retaining JSC board membership contrary to the Law (and directors who knew and did not object); a director obtaining a loan or guarantee contrary to the Law; failing to keep accounting records or to prepare or deposit financial statements; failing to provide the competent authority with required documents; failing to make documents available to owners; failing to prepare and record minutes; omitting the Art. 12 particulars; acting as auditor despite a disqualification; failing to register the company or an amendment of its articles with the Commercial Register; deliberately recording untrue data in the articles, the application or supporting documents (and those who sign or register them knowingly); professional-company breaches; a liquidator failing to register his appointment or the end of liquidation; failing to take corrective action after notification of a violation; an auditor failing in statutory duties; any company or officer failing to apply the Law and Regulations or to comply with the competent authority's rules and decisions without reasonable cause (Art. 262). A Ministry committee of at least three members chaired by a legally qualified person imposes these fines (except for listed JSCs, where the CMA Board does); the Minister may designate violations penalised directly without the committee; the penalised person may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of notification; Regs Art. 94: notification may be electronic to a mobile number or e-mail registered with the Commercial Register or the National Information Center (Art. 267). Penalties are graduated by gravity and circumstances; repeat offences under Arts. 260-261 within three years of a final conviction double the penalty (Art. 263). The court may, in addition to or instead of Arts. 260-261 penalties, warn, order preventive or corrective steps, order cessation, or ban from the board of a listed JSC; the competent authority may warn or order corrective steps instead of Art. 262 fines (Art. 264). The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes Arts. 260-261 offences (Art. 265). The competent court hears all civil and criminal cases under the Law and Regulations except for listed JSCs, which go to the Committee for the Resolution of Securities Disputes (Art. 266). Penalties do not prejudice civil claims for damages (Art. 269). The competent authority supervises companies' compliance with the Law and their articles, may inspect, examine accounts and demand records and minutes through its officers or experts, and may send observers to general assemblies (Art. 270); all company officers must give Ministry (and, for listed or listing companies, CMA) representatives access to records and information (Art. 271). | The administrative-fine list is effectively the entity-compliance checklist: annual assembly on time, financial statements deposited, minutes kept, amendments registered, documents bearing the Art. 12 particulars, auditor properly qualified. Committee decisions are notified electronically to the registered mobile/e-mail, so keep Commercial Register contact data current or appeal windows (30 days) will be missed. Forum: Commercial Court for unlisted companies (the "competent court" under the Commercial Courts Law `[model knowledge — verify]`), CRSD for listed JSCs. Ministry inspection powers extend to minutes and registers, which is why registers must exist in Arabic-readable form. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 274, 277 | The CMA supervises listed JSCs and issues their rules, including merger rules where a party is listed; the competent authority issues a corporate-governance regulation for JSCs (and the Ministry may issue one for other companies); the Ministry sets rules to obtain beneficial-ownership (المستفيد الحقيقي) information from companies other than listed JSCs; the Minister and the CMA Board issue the necessary rules (Art. 274). The Minister and the CMA Board issue the Regulations within 180 days of publication, covering rules, periods, procedures, documents and the use of technology for notices, meetings, participation and voting; any procedure under the Law or Regulations may be electronic, including incorporation and amendment applications, registration and publication, signature of applications, documents and registers, and deposit of financial statements (Art. 277). | Closing checklist: the UBO register/filing with the Ministry must be updated after a share transfer; the Ministry's UBO rules (issued under Art. 274(3)) were not read for this file `[model knowledge — verify]` — obtain them before advising on thresholds or deadlines. Electronic execution and filing of corporate documents has express statutory backing. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 280-281; Royal Decree M/132 cl. 2-3; Regs Art. 95 | The Law replaces the Companies Law (M/3 of 28/1/1437H) and the Professional Companies Law (M/17 of 26/1/1441H) and repeals conflicting provisions (Art. 280); it enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 281). Decree cl. 2: the Law does not prejudice the powers of the Saudi Central Bank and the CMA. Decree cl. 3: companies existing at the effective date must conform to the Law within two years from that date; exceptionally, the Ministry and the CMA determine which provisions apply to them during that period. Regs Art. 95: the Regulations apply from the Law's effective date. | The transition ended 19 January 2025; a company whose registered articles still follow the 2015-Law templates (e.g. old statutory-reserve or partner-liability-on-losses wording, "one-person company" terminology) is now non-conforming and its amendment is a closing-checklist item. Corporate documents dated before 19 January 2023 must be read against the 2015 Law, whose rules differed on several points flagged in this file. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 

FILE anti-concealment-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Art. 1 (definitions) | "Non-Saudi" means a natural or legal person who does not hold Saudi nationality and is not treated as a holder of it. "Economic activity" means any activity aimed at profit for which approvals or licences from the competent authorities are required, whether commercial, investment, service, professional, industrial, agricultural, or otherwise. "Proceeds" means funds arising directly or indirectly from the crimes in Art. 3(a) and (b), including funds converted wholly or partly into similar funds. | A foreign-owned company (legal person) is itself a "non-Saudi" for this Law, so a Saudi-registered company that is beneficially foreign-controlled without an investment registration is within scope. Which persons are "treated as" Saudi nationals (e.g. GCC nationals, Premium Residency holders for permitted activities) is not defined in the Law; it depends on other instruments and the Implementing Regulation `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 2 | Definition of tasattur: an agreement or arrangement through which a person enables another, non-Saudi, person to practise an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, using the licence or approval issued to the concealer. | The offence has three elements a reviewer should test: (1) an agreement or arrangement (formal or informal); (2) a non-Saudi practising an activity for which the non-Saudi holds no licence/approval of their own; (3) use of the Saudi party's licence, approval, CR, or name. The gating question is therefore whether the non-Saudi is themselves licensed, which is why the Investment Law registration (see the Investment Law row below) is decisive. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(a) | Crime of the enabler: any person enabling a non-Saudi to practise, for the non-Saudi's own account, an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, including enabling the non-Saudi to use the enabler's name, licence or approval, commercial register, trade name, or the like. | The enabler is "any person" (شخص), not only a Saudi national: a licensed foreign investor who lends its licence to an unlicensed non-Saudi is also caught. "For the non-Saudi's own account" (لحسابه الخاص) is the dividing line between concealment and a lawful employment or agency relationship; who bears the profit and loss is the practical test `[model knowledge — verify]` for how courts apply it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(b)–(d) | (b) Crime of the non-Saudi: practising an economic activity for their own account in the Kingdom without a licence, through the person who enabled them. (c) Participation: anyone who incites, assists, or gives advice for the commission of either crime, with knowledge, is an accomplice whenever the crime is committed or continued on the basis of that incitement, assistance, or advice. (d) Obstructing or preventing enforcement officers from performing their duties by any means, including non-disclosure of information or providing incorrect or misleading information, is a crime. | Paragraph (c) exposes advisers, accountants, and nominee-service providers who knowingly structure a concealment arrangement. Paragraph (d) means that a company's response to a Ministry inspection (withholding records, false statements) is itself a crime carrying the Art. 9 penalties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4 | Administrative violations (not crimes): (a) an establishment irregularly granting a non-Saudi instruments that lead to absolute disposal over the establishment; (b) a non-Saudi irregularly holding or using such instruments; (c) an establishment using, in the dealings of its economic activity, a bank account that does not belong to it. The Regulation sets the detail, taking into account cases where the instruments were granted or held in good faith. | These are the statutory red flags a diligence review must check in a Saudi target: (i) unlimited powers of attorney, blank-signed documents, or bank mandates giving a non-Saudi absolute control; (ii) revenue collected into, or expenses paid from, accounts in a person's name rather than the establishment's. Each is a violation on its own even if no Art. 3 crime is proved. The good-faith carve-out lives in the Implementing Regulation, which was not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 5 | Competences: the Ministry (of Commerce) has oversight, receives reports, and detects crimes and violations; the Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes crimes; the Criminal Court hears crimes; a Ministry committee of at least three members, its chair and at least one member legally qualified, decides Art. 4 violations and imposes Art. 14 penalties. | Two tracks: crimes (Art. 3) go through Public Prosecution to the Criminal Court; violations (Art. 4) are decided administratively by the Ministry committee, with appeal to the Administrative Court (Art. 14(5)). A target's litigation disclosure should be checked against both. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6 | Enforcement officers designated by the Minister from the Ministry, Municipal and Rural Affairs, HRSD, Environment/Water/Agriculture, the General Authority of Zakat and Tax, and other competent bodies have judicial-enforcement status and may: enter and inspect suspected establishments, offices, branches, warehouses, and vehicles; examine and seize records; view CCTV recordings; require disclosure of information from any person or body; seal premises and safes pending inspection; summon and record statements; and call on the police. Inspection procedures are confidential. Rules for financial rewards to enforcement staff are issued by Council of Ministers resolution. | Inspections are multi-agency and can arrive via labour (HRSD) or zakat/tax (now ZATCA, the successor to the authority named in the text `[model knowledge — verify]`) channels. A diligence request list should ask for any inspection visit records, seizure receipts, or summonses in the last five years. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 7 | Crimes and violations may be proved by all means of proof, including electronic evidence. | WhatsApp messages, bank-app logs, and email approvals showing who really directs the business are admissible; there is no documentary-only rule to hide behind. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 8 | The Ministry may ask the Public Prosecution to impose a travel ban on a person suspected of a crime under the Law (conditions per the Regulation). The Public Prosecution may, on its own motion or at the request of enforcement officers, order a precautionary seizure of funds liable to confiscation for up to sixty days, extendable by order of the Criminal Court; the funds may be left under the management of the interested party or transferred to a competent body. | Seizure can freeze the target's operating funds mid-transaction; an escrow or closing-funds-flow check should confirm no seizure order is outstanding. Travel bans on non-Saudi principals affect signing logistics. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 9 | Penalty for any crime under the Law: imprisonment up to five years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000, having regard to the size of the activity, its revenues, the period of practice, and the effects. Penalties are doubled for recidivism (a further crime within three years of a final judgment). The Criminal Court may reduce penalties where the accused, after the Ministry learns of the crime, provides evidence or information that could not otherwise have been obtained and that is relied on to prove the crime. | Headline exposure for a finding: five years / SAR 5m per convicted person (enabler, beneficiary, and each accomplice), doubled for repeat offenders. The penalty is "without prejudice to any penalty in any other law" (labour, tax, investment). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 10 | On conviction for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime, the proceeds are confiscated by court judgment whether held by the convicted person or another party; if the proceeds cannot be found or are mixed with lawful funds, other funds of equivalent value are confiscated. The Criminal Court may annul or block any act, contractual or otherwise, whose parties knew or should have known it would impair recovery of confiscable proceeds. Confiscated funds go to the Public Treasury, subject to the rights of good-faith third parties. | A purchase price paid for a concealed business can be reached as "proceeds" or "equivalent value", and the sale itself can be annulled if the buyer should have known. Buyer good faith is the only shield, which is why a documented tasattur review is part of the buyer's protection, not just the seller's. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11 | The judgment convicting for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime must order publication of its summary once final; the Ministry publishes it in the medium it sees fit. A non-Saudi convicted of any Art. 3 crime is deported and barred from re-entry, per the applicable rules and the Regulation, after serving the sentence and paying fees, taxes, and other obligations set by the court. | Publication is mandatory, so a conviction is reputationally public; deportation removes the non-Saudi operator, which for a founder-run business is an existential key-person risk. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 12 | Conviction for the enabler's crime (Art. 3(a)) entails: (a) dissolution of the establishment, cancellation of its licence and approval, and deletion of the convicted person's commercial register, unless the Criminal Court decides otherwise; (b) a ban on the convicted person practising the activity concerned and any other commercial business for five years from finality. Those convicted under Art. 3(a) or (b) are jointly liable for zakat, taxes, fees, and any other obligation of the establishment. Judgments are copied to the Ministry and to the zakat and tax authority. | The target entity itself is at risk of liquidation and CR deletion, which is why a tasattur finding is a "deal-structure" issue rather than a price issue: the acquired vehicle may cease to exist. Joint tax liability means back-taxes assessed on the concealed business (income tax on the non-Saudi share, retroactively) attach to both parties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 13 | If a perpetrator reports the crime or the other perpetrators to the competent authorities before discovery, and the report leads to their arrest or to seizure of the funds, instruments, or proceeds, the Criminal Court may exempt them from the Art. 9(1) penalties under rules prepared by the Ministry and issued by Council of Ministers resolution; the exemption does not cover zakat and tax obligations. | This is the ongoing "leniency" route after the Royal Decree's one-time correction window closed (see the Royal Decree row). It is discretionary, requires pre-discovery reporting, and never waives tax. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 14 | Penalty for an Art. 4 violation: a fine up to SAR 500,000 and/or closure of the establishment for up to ninety days. The committee may additionally require the violator to submit to the Ministry, for up to five years, the establishment's financial statements, bank statements, and employee payroll, and may penalise non-compliance with that order. Factors: size, revenues, duration, gravity, repetition, effect. If the committee sees indications of a crime it refers that part to the competent authority. A penalised person may appeal to the Administrative Court within sixty days of notification. | An outstanding five-year reporting order is a continuing compliance obligation that survives a share sale; ask whether the target is under one. Ninety-day closure is a business-interruption risk that belongs in the closing-checklist critical path. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15 | Without prejudice to the rights of good-faith third parties, every contract or act whose object or purpose is tasattur is void. | Side letters, nominee declarations, profit-split agreements, and "management agreements" that in substance hand the business to an unlicensed non-Saudi are unenforceable between the parties. A diligence reviewer who finds such a document should treat the target's ownership and profit entitlement as unsettled, not merely as a covenant breach. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 17–18 | Every authority that issues licences for an economic activity must monitor its licensees and report suspected crimes or violations to the Ministry (Art. 17). The identity of reporters is kept in a confidential register and excluded from the case file (subject to the Public Prosecution's right to seek disclosure with safeguards). By decision of the Minister, a reporter who is not an enforcement official and not convicted in the matter receives a reward of up to 30 % of the fine collected, if the information was fit to open an investigation and a final judgment (crime) or final decision (violation) issued; the Regulation sets reporting procedures and division among multiple reporters (Art. 18). | Disgruntled employees, ex-partners, and competitors have a financial incentive to report; MISA, municipalities, and sector regulators have a statutory duty to. Internal-investigation and HR-separation processes should assume that concealment allegations will reach the Ministry. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Royal Decree M/4, clause "Second" | The Ministries of Commerce and Interior, with HRSD, Investment, and the Premium Residency Center, were to prepare within sixty days a Council of Ministers regulation with a mechanism for violators under the old 1425H law who continued after this Law took effect to correct their status, providing: the available correction options; exemption from the penalties of both laws and from retroactive income tax for anyone who applied to the Ministry of Commerce within 180 days of this Law's effective date; exclusion of those who first offended under this Law, those already caught, and those referred to the Public Prosecution or court; treatment of corrected persons wishing to leave the Kingdom permanently; and preservation of private rights arising from their dealings. | The statutory window ran 180 days from 24/02/2021; it was later extended by government decision to August 2021 `[model knowledge — verify]`. It is closed. A target that "corrected its status" should be able to produce the Ministry's correction certificate and evidence of the option taken (e.g. conversion to a MISA-registered foreign entity, sale to a Saudi, or exit); absence of it after a historic concealment is a live exposure. The correction regulation itself was not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 19–20 | The Minister of Commerce, in agreement with the Minister of Interior, issues the Implementing Regulation within 180 days of publication, to apply from the Law's effective date (Art. 19). The Law replaces the Anti-Concealment Law of Royal Decree No. M/22 dated 4/5/1425H, repeals conflicting provisions, and enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 20). | Conduct before 24/02/2021 is judged under the 1425H law; conduct after it under this Law. The Implementing Regulation (good-faith cases, travel-ban controls, deportation, reward procedures) governs much of the practical exposure and was not in the source set. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Investment Law (M/19 of 16/1/1446H) Arts. 1, 7, 8, 11, 16 — interplay | "Foreign investor" is any natural or legal person making an investment who is not a "local investor" (a person holding Saudi nationality) (Art. 1). A foreign investor must register with the Ministry of Investment before making any investment, per the Regulation, except investment in securities under the Capital Market Law (Art. 7(2)). Before investing in an activity on the Excluded Activities list (prohibited or restricted), or changing ownership of an investment in a restricted activity, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval (Art. 8). Breaches of Arts. 7–8 carry warning, a fine up to SAR 300,000 (doubled on repetition), or cancellation of registration (Art. 11). The Law took effect 180 days after publication on 16/08/2024 and repealed the Foreign Investment Law of M/1 of 1421H (Art. 16). | A non-Saudi who holds a MISA registration covering the activity is "licensed to practise it" and therefore cannot be an Art. 2 concealment beneficiary for that activity; the concealment analysis collapses to: (i) is there a MISA registration (formerly "licence"); (ii) does it cover the activity actually carried on; (iii) does the target's CR activity match. A gap on (ii) or (iii), or activity on the Excluded Activities list, reopens tasattur exposure. The Anti-Concealment Law still speaks of "licence or approval" (ترخيص أو موافقة); reading MISA "registration" as the relevant licence is the natural construction under the 2024 Investment Law but is not stated in either text `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Investment Law text read today from `boe/investment-law-ar.md`; portal shows 0 amended articles) 
 Diligence checklist (practice) | Indicators a reviewer should test against Arts. 2–4 and 15: a non-Saudi (or foreign entity) without MISA registration who (a) holds a general or irrevocable power of attorney or is the sole bank signatory; (b) receives the business's revenues or pays its expenses through personal accounts; (c) appears as "general manager" with de facto ownership rights in side agreements; (d) funds the business by "loans" repaid from all profits; (e) is the counterparty in a trust/nominee declaration over the Saudi partner's shares. A UBO register that names a non-Saudi natural person as controller of a company whose CR shows only Saudi owners is a direct cross-check (see `ultimate-beneficial-ownership-rules.md`). | Each indicator maps to an Art. 4 violation (a)–(c) or to the Art. 15 nullity, so findings can be reported by article rather than by impression. Whether Premium Residency holders or GCC nationals are "treated as" Saudi for particular activities must be confirmed under the relevant rules before a red flag is raised against them. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 

FILE investment-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Art. 1 (definitions: الاستثمار، رأس المال، المستثمر الأجنبي); Reg. Art. 2 | "Investment" is the use of capital to establish, expand, develop, finance, own (in whole or part) or manage an investment project in the Kingdom for economic gain. "Capital" is any asset of material value (cash, in-kind or intangible), in particular shares and interests in companies, contractual rights, fixed or movable assets, IP rights, and rights granted by law such as licences; it excludes loans, bonds, financing sukuk and public or private debt instruments. A "local investor" is a natural or legal person investing who holds Saudi nationality; a "foreign investor" is any investor who is not a local investor. | A lender or sukuk holder is not an "investor" and gets none of the Art. 4 protections. The nationality test is on the person investing, so a Saudi-incorporated company is not automatically "local": MISA practice treats a Saudi company with any non-Saudi (non-GCC) shareholder as a foreign investor that must register `[model knowledge — verify]`, and GCC nationals and GCC-owned companies are treated as local under the GCC Economic Agreement and Council of Ministers decisions `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 2 (الهدف من النظام) | The Law's purposes include facilitating establishment of, ownership of assets in, and exit from or liquidation of an investment; guaranteeing investor rights; guaranteeing equal treatment between local and foreign investors; transparent, efficient and fair procedures; and competitive neutrality. | Purpose clause; cite it when a regulator's practice discriminates between local and foreign-owned entities without a statutory basis. It is programmatic and grants no directly enforceable right by itself. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3 (حرية الاستثمار) | Subject to Arts. 8 and 9 and to other laws, an investor may invest in any sector or activity open to investment. | The default is openness; restrictions must come from the excluded-activities list (Art. 8), national-security suspension (Art. 9) or a sector law (Art. 14). Diligence should ask for the specific legal basis of any claimed foreign-ownership cap rather than assume one. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4(1)(a)–(b), 4(2); Reg. Arts. 3–4 | Investors enjoy equal treatment with other investors, and equal treatment between local and foreign investors, in similar circumstances; and fair and equitable treatment. Reg. Art. 3(2) tests "similar circumstances" by the goods/services produced or consumed, the target sector, investment size, and impact on the local economy or environment; Reg. Art. 3(3) preserves the Ministry's right to regulate for the public interest (national security, public safety, public order). Reg. Art. 4 deems denial of justice, substantial breach of due process, manifest arbitrariness (harassment, compulsion) and unjustified targeted discrimination to be breaches of fair and equitable treatment. Under Art. 4(2) the competent authority must observe these rights when taking public-interest measures. | These are treaty-style standards written into domestic law. Reg. Art. 3(3) is the carve-out a regulator will cite; Reg. Art. 4 gives an investor-side checklist for a complaint under Reg. Arts. 24–26. Domestic enforcement is through the competent court or the MISA complaint system, not an investor–state tribunal, unless a BIT applies (Art. 13). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(c); Reg. Art. 5 | No confiscation of an investment, in whole or part, except by final judicial judgment; no direct or indirect expropriation except for the public interest, in accordance with legal procedures, and against fair compensation. Reg. Art. 5 defines indirect expropriation (measure with effect equivalent to direct expropriation, judged by economic impact — adverse effect on value alone is not enough — duration, and nature/purpose) and excludes non-discriminatory measures protecting public health, safety and the environment. | Expropriation protection now exists in domestic statute for local and foreign investors alike. In a diligence memo, note that the standard of "fair compensation" (تعويض عادل) is undefined in the Law and Regulations; valuation practice is untested `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(d); Reg. Art. 6 | Freedom to transfer funds into and out of the Kingdom without delay, through legal channels, in any currency recognised by the Saudi Central Bank, including investment returns, profits, and sale or liquidation proceeds. Reg. Art. 6(1) lists initial and additional capital, profits, capital gains, dividends, royalties, fees, contract and loan payments, liquidation/sale proceeds, and remuneration of personnel engaged from abroad. Reg. Art. 6(2) permits delay or prevention under equitable, non-discriminatory, bona fide laws on bankruptcy/creditor protection, securities dealing, criminal offences, and compliance with judgments; Reg. Art. 6(3) preserves prudential measures in financial services. | No exchange-control approval is required for dividend or exit repatriation; the practical gate is the bank's AML/KYC and, for dividends, the zakat/tax clearance and withholding-tax position `[model knowledge — verify]`. Contract reviewers can rely on this row when a foreign counterparty asks for a "currency convertibility" representation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(e)–(g), 4(3); Reg. Arts. 7, 24–26 | Freedom to manage the investment, dispose of it lawfully and own what is needed to run the business; protection of IP and confidential commercial information; facilitation of administrative procedures and support from the competent authority. The Ministry must provide available statistics (within 30 working days of a request, Reg. Art. 7) and handle investor complaints under a transparent procedure: acceptance or rejection notified within 10 working days, recommendations within 30 working days of acceptance (Reg. Arts. 25–26); a complaint is not a judicial proceeding and does not prejudice court or ADR rights (Reg. Art. 24(2)). | The MISA complaint route is a cheap first step against another government body's decision affecting an investment; it does not stop limitation periods for court or Board of Grievances actions `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 5 (التزامات المستثمر) | The investor must comply with all laws and legislation of the Kingdom and with the Kingdom's obligations under international agreements to which it is party. | The only free-standing obligation article; the operative obligations are in Arts. 7 and 8 (register; obtain approval for excluded activities). Company-law, labour, tax and Saudization compliance are policed by their own laws, not by MISA penalties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6; Reg. Art. 8 | Investment incentives are granted by the competent authority under objective, specific and fair eligibility criteria; the competent authority publishes the criteria through appropriate channels. | Incentives (tax holidays, land, RHQ benefits) come from the sector authority or ZATCA under their own instruments, not from this Law. Reg. Art. 36(3) names the Regional Headquarters programme and Special Economic Zones as regimes whose licensing continuity MISA must preserve; the RHQ programme itself (RHQ licence from MISA, 30-year 0% corporate income tax and withholding tax on qualifying RHQ activities, and the 1 January 2024 restriction on government contracting with companies lacking a KSA RHQ) is `[model knowledge — verify]` and is not in the sources read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 7(1), 7(3), 7(4); Reg. Arts. 9–10, 21; Decree para. 5 | The Ministry establishes and maintains a confidential National Register of Investors (السجل الوطني للمستثمرين) recording all information on investors and their investments; competent authorities must supply data, including through platform integration; the Ministry may make its services conditional on the investor updating its register data (Reg. Art. 9(4)). Through its comprehensive service centre (مركز الخدمة الشاملة) the Ministry receives applications for any approvals, licences or permits needed to practise an activity and coordinates their issue with the competent authority. Decree para. 5 requires the Ministry to exercise this function consistently with Art. 4 of the Saudi Business Center organisation (CoM Res. 456 of 11/8/1440H) and by agreement with the Center. | The register is a MISA database, not a public register; a diligence reviewer cannot search it and must obtain the target's registration certificate and latest annual-update confirmation from the target. Sector licences are still issued by the sector regulator; MISA only routes. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 7(2); Reg. Arts. 11–12; Art. 16 | A foreign investor must register with the Ministry before making any investment, as specified in the Regulations; this does not apply to investment in securities governed by the Capital Market Law. Registration data for a legal person: name and place of incorporation, business scope and economic activities to be practised, capital, expected capital contribution, particulars of owners/shareholders/controllers or the ultimate beneficial owner, plus anything the Investor Guide requires; the applicant must confirm the Ministry's declarations including accuracy of information (Reg. Art. 11). The Ministry notifies registration within 10 working days of completion; an incomplete file must be completed within a period of not less than 15 working days or is deemed cancelled (Reg. Art. 12). Art. 16 repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) under which foreign investors needed a licence. | This is the shift from foreign investment licence (ترخيص الاستثمار الأجنبي) to investment registration. Neither the Law nor the Regulations set a minimum capital, a licence fee, or a licence term; MISA issues a "شهادة تسجيل الاستثمار" (investment registration certificate) and has been removing the minimum-capital requirements that attached to the old licence categories `[model knowledge — verify]`. Portfolio investment through the Tadawul is outside the registration duty. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Reg. Art. 13 (annual update) | The registered investor must submit an annual update of the declarations signed at registration and of any change to its register data; the Ministry notifies the result within 5 working days. The update may be filed within the 60 working days before the deadline; the Ministry reminds the investor 30 working days before; a grace period of up to 30 working days may be granted; a lapsed registration may be reactivated within three years of the end of that period, after which the investor must re-register under the Investor Guide procedure. | This replaces the annual licence renewal (and its fee) of the old regime `[model knowledge — verify]`. For an entity tracker: the annual update is the MISA compliance event; its due date is investor-specific (anniversary of registration) and must be taken from the registration certificate. A target whose registration has lapsed for more than three years cannot simply update — it must re-register, which is a closing-checklist item. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` 
 Reg. Art. 14 (deregistration) | An investor may apply for voluntary deregistration with the documents in the Investor Guide (incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days). The Ministry must deregister on (a) cessation of all registered investments by a final decision or unappealable judgment, (b) a final national-security suspension decision, or (c) a final committee penalty cancelling registration; notice within 5 working days. A deregistered investor may not carry on any investment activity unless it re-registers; deregistration does not waive third-party claims. | Exit sequencing: MISA deregistration should follow, not precede, commercial-register deletion and liquidation, because the deregistered investor loses the right to operate while creditors' rights survive. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` 
 Art. 8(1)–(2); Reg. Arts. 15–19 | The competent authority (the Examination Committee, per Decree para. 3) issues and updates the list of excluded activities (قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة) — activities prohibited to, or restricted for, foreign investors — and the Ministry publishes it (Reg. Art. 15: in the Investor Guide). Before investing in a listed activity a foreign investor must apply to the Ministry, which refers the application to the Examination Committee; the Committee may request further information (Reg. Art. 16); incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days (Reg. Art. 17); the decision is notified within 5 working days of receipt (Reg. Art. 18); a rejected applicant may reapply citing the first application and new information (Reg. Art. 19). | Investing in an excluded activity without approval is a "serious violation" (Reg. Art. 30(b)) exposing the investor to cancellation of registration. Diligence must map every activity code on the target's commercial register against the current list, not the list at the time of licensing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Excluded-activities list — contents | The list itself was not among the sources read. From memory it descends from the "negative list" of the 2000 regime and the Investor Guide and includes, among others: oil exploration, drilling and production (except certain services); manufacture of military equipment and explosives; security and investigation services; real-estate investment in Makkah and Madinah; Hajj and Umrah tour guidance and services; recruitment and employment offices; certain fisheries, printing and publishing, and audiovisual/media activities; and restricted (conditional) activities such as wholesale/retail trade, transport, and professional services with Saudi-participation or capital conditions. | Do not rely on this row for any activity classification. The skill must obtain the current list from MISA's Investor Guide and record which version was used. Restricted activities carry conditions (Saudi shareholding, capital, experience) that also drive the Art. 8(3) ownership-change approval below. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20; Reg. Art. 30(c) | Before any change in the ownership of its investment in a *restricted* activity on the list, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval, which the Ministry refers to the Examination Committee under the same procedure as new applications (Reg. Arts. 15–18 apply). Changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval is a serious violation. | Merger-control-style condition precedent for M&A: any share transfer, capital increase diluting a Saudi partner, or change of control in a foreign-owned entity operating a restricted activity needs MISA/Examination Committee approval before closing. Add to the closing checklist alongside GAC clearance and MoC amendment of the articles. The Law does not set a review period for this approval. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 9; Reg. Arts. 22–23 | The Ministry may suspend any foreign investment to protect national security, on an objective basis, consistently with the Kingdom's international-agreement obligations and under the Regulations' procedure. The Ministry may coordinate with competent authorities including the Examination Committee before, during and after; may request information and must keep it confidential; may discuss alternative measures with the investor before suspending (Reg. Art. 22); the suspension is notified and may be appealed under the laws in force (Reg. Art. 23). | This is a general foreign-investment screening power without sector thresholds or deadlines; the Law gives no review period and no time bar. Reg. Art. 14(3)(b) turns a final suspension into deregistration. Flag for any target in defence, critical infrastructure, data or telecoms even where the activity is not on the excluded list. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 10 (الوسائل البديلة لتسوية المنازعات) | An investor party to a dispute — including a dispute with a competent authority — may resort to the competent court unless the parties agree otherwise; investors may agree to settle disputes by alternative means including arbitration, mediation and conciliation. | Statutory endorsement of arbitration clauses in investor agreements, including with government bodies "unless agreed otherwise". Arbitration by a government entity still requires the approval mandated by Art. 10(2) of the Arbitration Law (Prime Minister's approval) `[model knowledge — verify]`; the Investment Law does not dispense with it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11(1)–(3); Reg. Arts. 27–30 | For a non-serious violation of Art. 7 or Art. 8 the Ministry notifies the investor to remedy it within the period set by the Regulations (not less than 30 working days, extendable on justification: Reg. Art. 28; the list of non-serious violations goes in the Investor Guide: Reg. Art. 29). Failure to remedy in time, or a serious violation, is punishable — without prejudice to any severer penalty in another law — by one or more of: warning; a fine of up to SAR 300,000, which may be doubled on repetition; cancellation of registration. Serious violations (Reg. Art. 30): investing without registration; investing in an excluded activity without approval, after its expiry or cancellation, or in breach of its conditions; changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval; giving false or misleading information to the Ministry or competent authority; obstructing detection officers. Detection officers are appointed by the Minister and may obtain records, analyse reports, record complaints, verify supplier/customer contracts and consult the licensing authority (Reg. Art. 27). | The financial penalty is modest; the real exposure is cancellation of registration, which under Reg. Art. 14(5) ends the right to operate. Operating a foreign-owned business through a Saudi nominee to avoid registration is a serious violation here and separately an offence under the Anti-Concealment Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 11(4)–(5); Reg. Arts. 31–35 | The Minister forms one or more committees of at least three members, at least one a legal specialist, to hear violations and impose the Art. 11(2) penalties, weighing gravity, repetition and the size of the enterprise. Members serve three years renewable once (Reg. Art. 31); sessions are in camera, valid with a majority including the chair or deputy, decisions by majority (Reg. Art. 32); representation is under the Code of Law Practice (Reg. Art. 33); the committee decides within 30 working days of receiving the violation report, extendable once, and the Minister or delegate issues the penalty decision within 30 working days of the committee's report (Reg. Art. 34); the decision is notified by the Ministry's approved means (Reg. Art. 35). | Expect a decision within roughly 60–90 working days of a violation report. A pending committee matter against a target should be disclosed as a contingent liability and, if it could end in cancellation of registration, treated as a closing condition. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 12 (التظلم) | A person against whom the Ministry issues a penalty decision may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of being notified of the decision. | Thirty calendar days, from notification. The competent court for administrative penalty appeals is the administrative court (Board of Grievances) `[model knowledge — verify]`. National-security suspensions (Reg. Art. 23) are appealed "in accordance with the laws in force", which the text does not specify. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 13–14 | The Law does not prejudice the Kingdom's obligations under any international agreement in force to which it is party; nor the special regimes of other authorities applying to specific economic activities or to special economic zones, provided the investor enjoys at least the rights in the Law. | A bilateral investment treaty or the GCC framework can give a foreign investor more than this Law; the Law is a floor for SEZ (e.g. King Abdullah Economic City, Riyadh Integrated SEZ) and sector regimes (SAMA, CMA, CST, energy). Check which regime governs the target's licence before applying these rows. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15; Art. 16; Decree paras. 2 and 4; Reg. Arts. 36–37 | The Minister issues the Regulations within 180 days of publication, effective with the Law; MISA prepares them after coordinating with relevant bodies and the Ministry of Energy co-drafts the provisions on Art. 6 and Art. 7(3)–(4) (Decree para. 4). The Law takes effect 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette, repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) and conflicting provisions. Rights acquired before the effective date, including any licences or incentives granted, are preserved until their terms expire (Decree para. 2). Reg. Art. 36 requires the Minister to issue and publish an Investor Guide listing MISA services, their classification, method and fees; Reg. Art. 37 brings the Regulations into force with the Law. | A pre-2025 MISA foreign investment licence remains valid until its stated expiry; on expiry the holder moves to registration. How MISA migrated existing licence-holders into the register, and whether it did so automatically, is a platform practice not in the text `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Diligence checklist (synthesis of Art. 7, Art. 8, Reg. Arts. 11, 13, 14, 20, 30) | For a target with any foreign ownership, a corporate diligence must verify: (1) a current MISA investment registration certificate and the date and result of the last annual update (Reg. Art. 13) — a lapse of more than 3 years means re-registration; (2) that the economic activities on the commercial register match the activities registered with MISA (Reg. Art. 11(2)(b)) — a mismatch is a violation of Art. 7; (3) whether any activity is on the excluded list and, if restricted, that Examination Committee approval exists and its conditions are met (Art. 8; Reg. Art. 30(b)); (4) that every past change of ownership in a restricted activity was pre-approved (Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20); (5) that the owner/controller/UBO particulars filed with MISA match the cap table (Reg. Art. 11(2)(e)); (6) any notice of violation, committee proceeding, suspension or deregistration (Reg. Arts. 14, 22, 27–35). | Minimum-capital and licence-fee checks that diligence memos carried under the old regime are no longer statutory requirements; where MISA still applies capital conditions to specific restricted activities this comes from the Investor Guide, not the Law `[model knowledge — verify]`. The registration certificate and the annual-update confirmation are MISA portal documents; request both, not just the certificate. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.); practice points inline `[model knowledge — verify]` 

FILE social-insurance-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Art. 1 (new law) | Definitions. "Employer": every natural or legal person who employs one or more workers or employees for a wage or salary. "Worker": every natural person working for an employer under the employer's management or supervision for a wage. "Contributor": anyone subject to any insurance branch, savings program or insurance product. "Minister": the Minister of Finance. | A single employee triggers the employer's registration and contribution duties; there is no headcount threshold. The "management or supervision" test mirrors Labor Law Art. 50, so a worker-classification finding that a contractor is in substance a worker carries a GOSI back-contribution exposure (Art. 9 fine plus Art. 59 penalty). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3, 4, 5 | The law has three branches: annuities; occupational hazards and additional compensations; unemployment insurance. The Board may create optional savings programs. The Council of Ministers may, on the Board's proposal, create compulsory insurance products for (a) Saudi self-employed and professionals and (b) non-Saudi workers beyond what Art. 6 and the occupational-hazards branch already cover. | Non-Saudi workers today are covered only by the occupational-hazards branch (2% employer-borne, row Art. 28-29); Art. 5(b) is the hook for any future compulsory product for expatriates. A reviewer should not assume any pension contribution for a non-Saudi employee unless a Council decision under Art. 5 is produced. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6 | Coverage of the following categories is decided by the Board under the Regulation: workers in foreign diplomatic, political or military missions; agricultural, forestry and herding workers; seafarers including fishermen; domestic workers; non-Saudi workers entering on a temporary or seasonal work visa of no more than 60 days; home-workers; players and coaches of sports clubs and federations. | These categories are not excluded outright (unlike the 1421H law Art. 5, which excluded them): they are covered if and when the Board says so. For a corporate employer the relevant ones are short-visit (≤60-day visa) assignees and, for sports entities, players and coaches. Whether the Board has switched coverage on for any category was not verifiable from the law text. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 7 | The Regulation sets: how employers and contributors register; a grace period for employers to register contributors who meet the conditions and to de-register those who no longer do; a grace period for a contributor to register or de-register himself if the employer fails or delays; and the rules for registration after the grace periods lapse. | The registration deadline is in the Regulation, not the law; the GOSI practice deadline is 15 days from joining and 15 days from leaving, applied through the GOSI online portal `[model knowledge — verify]`. Under Art. 31(2) an injury before registration and after the grace period is uncompensated by GOSI, which pushes the cost onto the employer. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 8 | Contributions for every branch are calculated on the basic wage or salary; the Regulation may add other elements and allowances the contributor receives, provided the base is not below the minimum wage set by the competent authority. The wage ceiling subject to contribution is SAR 45,000 per month, adjustable by Council of Ministers decision on the Board's proposal after actuarial review. The employer must submit wage data to GOSI in the manner and within the period the Regulation sets. | The law fixes only "basic wage" plus whatever the Regulation adds; the Regulation has historically added the housing allowance (or in-kind housing valued at two months' basic wage per year) and commissions, and the same definition carries over `[model knowledge — verify]`. When reviewing an offer letter, split basic wage and housing from other allowances: the first two drive GOSI cost, the rest generally do not. Wage under-declaration to GOSI (declaring basic only, paying more) is a common diligence finding and is penalised under Art. 59. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 9 | The employer must pay the full contributions due from itself and from the contributor within the period the Regulation sets and is solely liable to GOSI for payment; it may deduct the contributor's share from the wage each time the wage is paid. Late payment attracts a fine of 2% of the contribution due for each month or part of a month of delay; the Regulation sets the cases of full or partial exemption. Only periods for which contributions are paid count towards benefits; counting is suspended when unpaid periods reach the length set by the Board (not less than 6 months), unless an event prevents the establishment from paying. | The 2% per month is a delay fine, not interest, and is not capped in the law (the 1421H law Art. 19(5) left the cap to the Regulation). The employee's share, once deducted, is held for GOSI; failing to remit is the employer's liability alone. The payment deadline is in the Regulation; under the 1421H law it was the first 15 days of the following month (row on legacy Art. 19) and GOSI practice keeps that deadline `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 10 | Government bodies, public institutions and authorities, state-owned companies and companies in which the State holds more than 50% of the capital must require any employer dealing with them to produce a GOSI certificate showing the establishment is registered and has met all its obligations (or is not subject to the law) when the employer: (a) applies to collect its dues; (b) bids for public works, supply, operation, maintenance or other projects; (c) applies to amend, renew or cancel its commercial register; (d) applies for a State subsidy; (e) seeks liquidation of its establishment; (f) applies for or renews a project licence; (g) applies to recruit workers from abroad. | This is the statutory basis for "GOSI arrears block services". In diligence, a target with GOSI arrears cannot cleanly renew or amend its commercial register, obtain new recruitment visas, collect government receivables, or be liquidated until the arrears are settled; the block is implemented electronically through the Ministry of Commerce, Qiwa and government-payment integrations rather than by a paper certificate `[model knowledge — verify]`. Ask for the GOSI compliance certificate (شهادة الالتزام) and the GOSI statement of account as standard closing deliverables. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11 | Contributions and late-payment fines are secured by a lien in GOSI's favour ranking immediately after the lien for wages. GOSI may, through the competent bodies and by the applicable judicial and statutory procedures, attach and enforce against any assets owned by or owed to the employer, once the schedules of amounts due are officially certified by the Minister or his delegate. | GOSI ranks second only to wage claims in an insolvency or enforcement waterfall (cross-check the Bankruptcy Law priority rules before relying on this in a distressed-target memo). Certified GOSI schedules are enforceable through the Enforcement Court without a prior judgment on the merits; the employer's remedy is objection under Art. 60(3). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 14 | The annuities branch applies compulsorily to (a) all Saudi employees (public sector) and (b) all Saudi workers working inside the Kingdom, or outside it for an employer whose head office is in the Kingdom, provided the worker or employee is below the statutory entitlement age when the law first applies to him. It applies optionally to Saudis working abroad with no Kingdom-based employer. | A Saudi national seconded abroad by a Saudi-headquartered employer stays in the annuities branch. A Saudi hired locally by a foreign affiliate with no Saudi head office is outside compulsory coverage (may opt in). A Saudi hired at or after the statutory age is outside the branch. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15 with Royal Decree M/273 clause Third(1)-(3) | Annuities contribution: 22% of the contributory wage, 11% employer and 11% contributor (optional contributors bear the whole 22%). The Decree phases the rate in for those covered by the new law: 18% during the 12 months from the effective date; 19% from the month following the lapse of 12 months; 20% from the month following 24 months; 21% from the month following 36 months; 22% from the month following 48 months; the employer bears 50% and the contributor 50% of the compulsory contribution at each step. The Council of Ministers may amend the rates on the Board's proposal after an actuarial study. | For employees who joined the scheme on or after 3 July 2024: 9% + 9% (from 3 July 2024), 9.5% + 9.5% (from mid-2025), 10% + 10% (from mid-2026), 10.5% + 10.5% (from mid-2027), 11% + 11% (from mid-2028). The exact switch-over month is ambiguous in the text: "the month following the lapse of twelve months from the effective date" reads as August 2025 on a strict count from 3 July 2024, while GOSI has applied each step from July `[model knowledge — verify]`. Employees with pre-July 2024 periods stay on the 1421H rate of 9% + 9% (row on legacy Art. 18); the Decree's phase-in applies only to "those covered by the new law". A hiring-review cost model therefore needs the hire date and prior-GOSI status of each Saudi employee, not just the salary. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 16 with Council of Ministers Decision 1022 clause Third(5) | The statutory entitlement age is 65. A contributor is entitled to a pension on reaching 65 with at least the minimum contribution period set by Council of Ministers decision; Decision 1022 sets that minimum at 180 months. Early pension: up to 120 months before 65 if the contributor has stopped work subject to the law and has at least 360 months of contributions (subject to the Art. 17(3) reduction). | Retirement age for new entrants is 65 (both sexes), against 60 under the 1421H law. Labor Law Art. 74(4), as amended by Royal Decree M/134 of 27/11/1440H, now ends the contract on "the worker reaching retirement age as provided in the Social Insurance Law, unless the parties agree to continue" (the original 60 men / 55 women wording is no longer in force), so the Labor Law retirement trigger follows this row: 65 for new-law contributors, 60 for legacy contributors, or the Decree clause Fifth phased age in between. Termination-review must first classify the employee (new-law, legacy, or phased) before treating "retirement age" as a lawful end of contract. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 17, 26 | Pension = 2.25% × average contributory wage (average of the highest 180 months of contributory wages, Art. 26) × months of contribution ÷ 12, capped at 100% of that average; the Board may add benefits above the cap. Minimum pension SAR 4,000 per month at 480 months, reduced proportionately for each 12 months (and each month) short of 480, with a floor of SAR 2,000 at the 180-month qualifying period. Early pension is reduced by 3% for each 12 months (pro rata per month) before 65 or short of 480 months, whichever is less, for the life of the pension; a pension claimed after continuing work beyond 65 is increased by 3% per 12 months. | Compared with the 1421H law (2.5% accrual on the last 24 months' average, Art. 38 legacy), the new formula accrues more slowly and on a longer averaging window. Relevant to termination-review only as context for early-retirement negotiations; the numbers are GOSI's to compute, and a skill must not compute an individual's pension from this row. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 25, 27 | A contributor who reaches 65 (or is disabled under Art. 21) and stops covered work without qualifying for a pension receives: with 60 months or more of contributions, a compensation based on the total employer and contributor contributions revalued (not less than the total paid), payable monthly or as a lump sum at his option; with less than 60 months, a lump sum on the same basis. On death without pension entitlement the family receives the full lump sum. A contributor who loses Saudi nationality receives a lump sum. | The employer's contributions are not refundable to the employer in any scenario; they follow the contributor. No employer-side action is needed at termination beyond de-registration (Art. 7). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 28, 29 with Decree clause Third(4) | The occupational hazards and additional compensations branch applies compulsorily to all employees and all workers working inside the Kingdom (no nationality condition). Contribution: 2% of the contributory wage, borne by the employer; the Board may, for a fixed period, raise it up to double for employers who refuse to comply with instructions on contributors' safety and health; the Council of Ministers may amend the rate after an actuarial study. The date on which the branch applies to public employees is set by Council of Ministers decision. | Every non-Saudi employee costs the employer 2% of contributory wage (capped at SAR 45,000 base) and nothing else at GOSI. Every Saudi employee costs 2% plus the annuities and unemployment shares. The doubling power is the only GOSI-side sanction tied to OSH non-compliance; the main one is Labor Law Art. 229 as amended by Royal Decree M/46 of 1436H (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure up to 30 days or permanently, per-person multiplication). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 30, 31 | "Work injury" is any accident occurring during or because of work, including on the usual route between home and work, between work and the usual place of meals or prayer, and on travel for an assignment given by the employer; occupational diseases listed in the Board's schedule count as work injuries, the date of first medical observation being the injury date. Benefits: medical care; daily allowances for temporary disability; monthly annuities and lump-sum compensation for permanent total or partial disability. Benefits are due only if the injury occurred after the contributor's registration with GOSI was completed, or within the employer's registration grace period, or within the contributor's self-registration period where the employer failed to register him. | Commuting accidents are covered. The registration condition in Art. 31(2) is the sharpest exposure for an employer that registers late: an injury to an unregistered worker after the grace period is not compensated by GOSI and the worker will pursue the employer under Labor Law Arts. 132-140 and general liability `[model knowledge — verify]`. Art. 32(3) separately obliges the employer to provide first aid. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 33 | Temporary disability from a work injury reported within the Regulation's deadlines: a daily allowance for every day off work including holidays, from the day after the injury until recovery, confirmation of permanent disability, or death. The allowance is 100% of the daily contributory wage of the month before the injury (or the joining month), reduced to 75% while the injured is treated at GOSI's expense as an in-patient. | GOSI, not the employer, pays wages during work-injury absence once the injury is reported in time; the employer should not run injury absence through sick leave under Labor Law Art. 117. Late reporting (deadlines in the Regulation) shifts the cost back to the employer `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 35, 36, 39 | Permanent total disability: a monthly annuity of 100% of the average contributory wage of the three months before the injury (not below the Art. 17 minimum). Permanent partial disability of 50% or more: the total-disability annuity multiplied by the disability percentage. Below 50%: a lump sum of 60 times the corresponding monthly annuity (not less than 36 times), reduced by one monthly annuity for each year of age above 45 at the injury date. Annuities are provisional for five years with periodic re-examination, increased 50% where constant attendance is needed, and may be combined with wages. A non-Saudi entitled to an annuity (or his family on death) receives a lump sum instead: 84 times the monthly annuity for total disability, 60 times for partial, 84 times on death. | The non-Saudi lump-sum substitution (Art. 39) is the rule an HR adviser most often needs when an expatriate is injured: the family of a deceased expatriate receives 84 months' annuity as a lump sum from GOSI, on top of the employer's Labor Law Art. 40(4) repatriation duty (which the employer is excused from where GOSI pays under Art. 40 of this law). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 41, 42 with Decree clause Sixth(1) | Maternity compensation: a female contributor who gives birth receives compensation for 3 months (plus one month where the child is sick or disabled and needs constant care), if she has at least 12 months of contributions, continuous or not, in the 36 months before the birth and is in covered employment at the birth, and the birth is at least six months into the pregnancy. The compensation is 100% of the average contributory wage of the 12 months before the birth, payable from the first day of the birth month (claimable one month before the expected date), and may not be combined with wages. The Decree extends maternity compensation to contributors under the 1421H law's occupational-hazards branch, counting the qualifying period from 3 July 2024. | GOSI now pays three months of maternity compensation for qualifying Saudi employees (and, through the Decree, for 1421H-law contributors in the occupational-hazards branch, which includes non-Saudi women). How this interacts with the employer's paid maternity leave under Labor Law Art. 151 (12 weeks fully paid after the 1446H amendment `[model knowledge — verify]`) is a live question: the law bars combining the compensation with wages, which suggests the employer's wage obligation is displaced for the covered months, but GOSI's implementing rules were not available. Do not advise on the split without the Regulation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 43, 44 with Decision 1022 clause Third(6) | Unemployment insurance (SANED) applies compulsorily to all Saudi workers in the Kingdom who are under 64 when the law first applies to them; contributions stop when the contributor reaches the pension entitlement age. Contribution in the law: 2% of the contributory wage, 1% employer and 1% contributor; the Council of Ministers may vary it between 0.5% and 2% split equally. Decision 1022 sets the rate at the effective date at 1.5%, 0.75% employer and 0.75% contributor. | The operative SANED rate is 0.75% + 0.75%, set by the Council decision, not the 1% + 1% printed in Art. 44. Total employer GOSI cost for a new-law Saudi employee in 2026: annuities 10% (mid-2026 step) + occupational hazards 2% + SANED 0.75% = 12.75% of contributory wage; employee share 10% + 0.75% = 10.75% `[computed from the rows above; the annuities step date is the ambiguity noted at Art. 15]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 45-49 | SANED benefit conditions: Saudi nationality; qualifying period met; not dismissed for a reason attributable to the worker; no private business or work income; did not leave voluntarily; able to work; below pension age; registered within the Regulation's deadline after leaving; actively seeking work; complying with training. Qualifying periods: 12 months in the 36 before the first claim, 18 for the second, 24 for the third, 36 in 48 thereafter. Benefit: up to 12 months per entitlement within 24 months; 60% of the average monthly wage (1/24 of the last 24 months' contributory wages) for the first three months and 50% thereafter, capped at SAR 9,000 then SAR 7,500 per month. Payment stops on death, pension entitlement, or loss of any condition. | Termination-review point: the reason code the employer records when de-registering the employee at GOSI (resignation, Art. 77 termination, Art. 80 dismissal) determines SANED eligibility; a dismissal recorded under Labor Law Art. 80 disqualifies the worker, so employees contest the coding `[model knowledge — verify]`. Mutual-separation agreements should state the exit category and who submits the GOSI notification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 54, 57 | Claims for daily injury allowances and travel/accommodation allowances lapse one year after they fall due; claims for other benefits lapse after five years, unless GOSI accepts an excuse. Cash benefits may be attached only by court judgment, up to 25% for one creditor and 50% for several, maintenance debts ranking first; GOSI itself has a priority right to set off its own claims up to 25% (more where the benefit is a lump sum or the debt arose from incorrect data). | An employer creditor (loan, training bond) cannot reach GOSI benefits except through a judgment and within the 25% cap. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 58 | GOSI inspectors appointed by the Governor monitor compliance; employers must give inspectors the information and documents needed, including records on the nature of work, the numbers of workers and employees, their wages and salaries, and how they are calculated and paid. Inspectors report violations; GOSI investigates. Inspectors and staff are bound to confidentiality, with exceptions for enforcement, consent, or judicial and public-authority requests. A financial reward may be given to anyone, inspector or not, who helps uncover a violation. | Payroll records must reconcile to GOSI wage declarations; the whistle-blower reward makes under-declaration a reportable risk from inside the workforce. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 59, 60, 61 | Penalties, without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law: a fine up to SAR 50,000 for any violation of the law or Regulation, doubled for a repeat within three years, and multiplied by the number of contributors concerned. Registering a person who does not actually work for the employer: the same fine or up to twice the total contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person, with the contributions refunded to the employer. Where a violation causes benefits to be paid without right: the fine or the amount of the benefits, whichever is higher, plus restitution. No fine after five years from the violation (correction and restitution still due). The Board issues a schedule classifying violations; GOSI may settle and waive fines. A three-member committee (at least one legal specialist) imposes penalties; appeal lies to the competent court. Publication of the decision at the violator's expense may be ordered once final. | The per-contributor multiplier makes the ceiling effectively unlimited for systemic non-registration (10 unregistered workers = up to SAR 500,000). The fictitious-registration offence targets "ghost Saudis" registered to inflate the Nitaqat Saudization count; it is the GOSI-side complement to the HRSD penalties for fake Saudization and is a standard diligence red flag when Nitaqat headcount and payroll do not match. The five-year limitation runs from the violation, so a diligence look-back of five years of GOSI filings is the right scope. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Royal Decree M/273 clauses Second, Fourth, Fifth | The new law does not apply to (1) contributors with contribution periods before 3 July 2024 under the Civil Retirement Law or the 1421H law that have not been compensated, or (2) pensioners under those laws; for them the 1421H law, the Civil Retirement Law and the Unemployment Insurance Law of 1435H remain in force. By exception, contributors with fewer than 240 months of such periods who were under 50 Hijri years (48 years 6 months Gregorian) on 3 July 2024 get a phased pension age: under 29 → 65; 29 → 64y 8m; 30 → 64y 4m; 31 → 64; 32 → 63y 8m; 33 → 63y 4m; 34 → 63; 35 → 62y 8m; 36 → 62y 4m; 37 → 62; 38 → 61y 8m; 39 → 61y 4m; 40 → 61; 41 → 60y 8m; 42 → 60y 4m; 43 → 60; 44 → 59y 8m; 45 → 59y 4m; 46 → 59; 47 → 58y 8m; 48 to under 48y 6m → 58y 4m (age bands are "X years or more and under X+1"). Early-pension qualifying period for that group: fewer than 180 months at 3 July 2024 → 360 months; 180-191 → 348; 192-203 → 336; 204-215 → 324; 216-227 → 312; 228-239 → 300. | The "who is on which law" test is: any GOSI or civil-retirement contribution month before 3 July 2024 that was not paid out as a lump sum keeps the employee on the 1421H law (rates, formula, branches), but with the new retirement age from this table if the employee was under 48.5 and had under 240 months on that date. An employee aged 48.5 or more, or with 240+ months, keeps age 60. A workforce census by hire date, age on 3 July 2024 and prior contribution months is the only way to cost retirement timing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 1421H law Art. 4, 5, 7 (legacy contributors) | Occupational-hazards branch applies compulsorily to all workers without distinction of sex, nationality or age; annuities branch compulsorily to all Saudi workers under 60 when first covered (special rules for returners aged 60-65). Condition: work under a contract of employment for one or more employers, whatever the contract's term, nature, form or wage, performed mainly inside the Kingdom, or a Saudi working abroad for a Kingdom-headquartered employer. Excluded: civil servants and military under their own schemes, foreign staff of foreign missions, agricultural and herding workers, seafarers, domestic servants, foreign workers on jobs normally under three months, home-workers, family members in family-only businesses (with opt-in for the last two). Employers must apply the law from the day the conditions are met; the Regulation sets registration. | For pre-July 2024 hires the exclusion list is closed (domestic workers, sub-three-month foreign assignments, etc.). For a short-term foreign assignee under the legacy regime the three-month "normally" test applies; under the new law the test is a visa of 60 days or less subject to a Board decision (Art. 6). Because new hires are on the new law, the legacy exclusions matter only for the existing population. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 1421H law Art. 18, 19, 65 (legacy contributors) | Occupational hazards: 2% of the contributory wage, employer-borne, may be doubled for employers refusing OSH instructions. Annuities: 18% of the contributory wage, 9% employer and 9% worker (Art. 65 phased the worker's share 6% → 7.5% → 9% over the first two years after 2001). Contributions are calculated on the monthly basic wage as defined by the Regulation, which may add allowances and in-kind benefits; ceiling SAR 45,000 per month; wage increases after age 50 count only within the Regulation's limits. The employer pays both shares and is solely liable; it may deduct the worker's share at each wage payment, and if it fails to deduct at the time it may not recover later. Contributions are due within the first 15 days of the following month; late payment carries a 2% fine per month or part, with the cap and exemptions in the Regulation. The employer must file monthly wage schedules within the same deadline. State bodies and companies in which joint-stock companies hold shares must demand a GOSI certificate for dues collection, tenders, commercial-register changes, subsidies, liquidation, recruitment approvals and licensing. | Legacy population cost: employer 9% + 2% = 11% for Saudis (plus SANED 0.75% under the 1435H Unemployment Insurance Law, which remains in force for this group by Decree clause Fourth; the 0.75% figure is from the SANED regime as amended and was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`), 2% for non-Saudis. The "no later deduction" rule in Art. 19(4) means an employer that forgot to withhold the worker's 9% cannot claw it back from later wages; it is an employer loss. The 15-day deadline and monthly wage schedule are the operational rhythm GOSI still runs `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 1421H law Art. 20, 38 (as amended by M/49 of 1431H and M/134 of 1440H), 62 (as replaced by M/55 of 1438H and M/81 of 1445H) (legacy contributors) | Contributions and delay fines are secured by a lien ranking after wages; GOSI may attach the employer's assets after a court order once schedules are certified by the Minister. Retirement pension at 60 with at least 120 months of contributions; early pension before 60 with 300 months for a contributor no longer covered; sentence of imprisonment with 120 months lets the family draw it; a contributor at 60 with at least five years may buy up to five notional years (para 2 as reworded by M/49). The portal records that M/49 of 1431H reworded para 1(c) (a female contributor no longer covered may draw her pension at 55 with 120 months) and that M/134 of 1440H then deleted a paragraph of the article; read with M/134's simultaneous amendment of Labor Law Art. 74(4), the deleted paragraph is the women-at-55 provision, so the legacy age is 60 for both sexes `[model knowledge — verify]` for the identification of the deleted paragraph, which the portal note does not name. Pension formula: 1/40 of the average wage of the last 24 months per year of contribution, capped at 100% of that average, the average not to exceed 150% of the wage at the start of the last five years. Penalties (Art. 62 in force): a fine up to SAR 10,000 for any violation of the law or regulations, including submitting incorrect data or withholding data GOSI requests, doubled on repetition, multiplied by the number of contributors concerned and by the number of data items submitted or withheld; registering a person who does not work for the employer: that fine or up to twice the contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person; wrongful benefits recovered plus a fine up to their amount; office committees investigate and refer to the Governor, who issues the decision, with objection within 30 days under Art. 61; publication of the decision may be ordered; no fine after five years; GOSI may settle and waive fines (para 8, added by M/81 of 19/5/1445H). | The original Art. 62 (SAR 5,000 per violation with a SAR 50,000 aggregate cap) is not in force; since M/55 of 1438H the legacy ceiling is SAR 10,000 per violation with per-contributor and per-data-item multipliers and no aggregate cap, and the ghost-registration offence and the five-year limitation already existed under the legacy law. The new law's Art. 59 raises the per-violation ceiling to SAR 50,000. Which regime applies to a violation concerning a legacy contributor after 3 July 2024 is not settled by the text (Decree clause Fourth keeps the 1421H "provisions" in force for those contributors); assume the new law's Art. 59 for anything GOSI enforces today and flag the point `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE saudization-nitaqat
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Labor Law Art. 26 | All establishments, whatever their activity or headcount, must work to attract and employ Saudis, provide the means for them to stay in work, and give them the opportunity to prove their fitness through guidance, training and qualification. Saudi workers must be not less than 75% of the employer's total workforce; the Minister may temporarily reduce the percentage where technical competencies or academic qualifications are unavailable or posts cannot be filled by nationals. | The 75% is the statutory default; every Nitaqat threshold below 75% is an exercise of the Minister's reduction power, which is why HRSD can raise quotas by decision without amending the law. A contract or diligence memo should cite Art. 26(2) as the source of the duty and Nitaqat as the current measure of it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Implementing Regulation Art. 8 (to Art. 26) | In implementation of Art. 26(2), the Saudization ratios establishments must achieve are set through programs the ministry designs, which track the ratio for each private-sector activity and withhold all or some ministry services from establishments that do not achieve the set ratio, "such as the Program for Incentivising Establishments to Localise Jobs (Nitaqat)"; the ministry updates these programs periodically whenever needed. | This is the regulatory anchor for Nitaqat and for the service-withholding sanction; it also signals that the program's parameters are administrative and change without notice in the law. Any Nitaqat percentage quoted in a memo must carry a date and the phase it belongs to. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Labor Law Art. 28 (as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) with Implementing Regulation Art. 9 | An employer with 25 or more workers whose work allows it must employ vocationally rehabilitated persons with disabilities at not less than 4% of its workforce, whether through employment channels or otherwise, and must send the ministry a statement of the posts they occupy and their wages (M/44 substituted "channels" for "units", "the ministry" for "the competent labour office", and "persons with disabilities" for the older term; the duty is otherwise unchanged). The Regulation defines disability by a Ministry of Health or public-hospital report or an HRSD identification card, and provides that a person with a disability is counted as more than one worker under Nitaqat only if he holds an HRSD card stating the type and degree of disability and the job matches it; disability may not be a ground to refuse hiring, promotion or training. | Two separate duties: the 4% disability quota (Art. 28) and the Nitaqat multiplier for disabled Saudi employees (Regulation Art. 9(4)). The multiplier is four (one disabled Saudi counts as four) in HRSD practice `[model knowledge — verify]`; the Regulation confirms only that it is "more than one" and conditions it on the HRSD card and job fit. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 28; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 9 
 Nitaqat guide, Terms | "Firm": every enterprise managed by a natural or legal person employing one or more employees for a salary of any kind. "Entity": the unit the ministry deals with for calculating Saudization, recruiting expatriates and transferring services; it is all branches of the same economic activity owned by one firm. "Expatriate employee": an employee who is not a GCC citizen. "Saudization rate": total averages of Saudi employees in the entity's branches ÷ (averages of Saudi employees + averages of expatriate employees) × 100. "Range": the entity's classification based on its Saudization rate. | The denominator is Saudis plus non-GCC expatriates; GCC nationals are outside both numerator and denominator. The rate is computed on "averages", i.e. a rolling average of headcount rather than a point-in-time count (26 weeks in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`), which is why a new hire lifts the rate only gradually. A firm with several activities has one entity per activity; a firm with one activity across many branches is a single entity, so a Red branch cannot be cured by ring-fencing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Nitaqat guide, Technical Details A | Nitaqat Mutawar (a) keeps five ranges: Red, Low Green, Medium Green, High Green and Platinum; (b) uses the consolidated economic activities in Annex 1; (c) abolishes fixed size bands and computes required Saudization from the entity's headcount using fixed values per activity; (d) publishes the coefficients for three years so firms can plan. | There is no Yellow range in the 2021 design; the amendment-watch lead that "Yellow was removed" in 2026 does not match this text and should be treated as unreliable until HRSD's 2026 material is read. The three-year visibility promise expired with the 2024 coefficients, so the 2026-2028 phase (row below) is a new cycle. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Nitaqat guide, Technical Details B(1)-(3) | The minimum Saudization for each range is y = m × ln(x) + c, where y is the range's minimum rate, m the activity's curve gradient, c the intercept for the activity and year (the third-year value is used in the third year and beyond), x the entity's total workforce, ln the natural logarithm. The formula gives the start of Low Green, Medium Green, High Green and Platinum. Range assignment with n = the entity's rate: Red if n < y(Low Green); Low Green if y(LG) ≤ n < y(MG); Medium Green if y(MG) ≤ n < y(HG); High Green if y(HG) ≤ n < y(Platinum); Platinum if n ≥ y(Platinum). | The required rate rises with headcount continuously (no cliff at 500 workers). Worked example in the guide: a manufacturing entity of 400 workers at 35.00% Saudization is High Green in 2023 (thresholds 22.15 / 30.07 / 34.93 / 40.83) and drops to Low Green in 2024 (27.15 / 35.07 / 37.93 / 45.33) with no change in its workforce. A skill may reproduce this arithmetic only with coefficients whose phase and date are stated. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Nitaqat guide, Annex 1 (selected 2024 coefficients) | Coefficients (m; c for Jan 2024 onwards, Low Green / Medium Green / High Green / Platinum): Manufacturing 1.68-2.08; 17.08 / 23.87 / 25.47 / 32.87. Construction and Cleaning −0.37 to 0.00; 12.17 / 14.17 / 17.00 / 22.00. Retail and Wholesale 2.47-2.84; 24.25 / 28.72 / 32.91 / 40.91. Business Services 1.03-2.19; 33.28 / 42.12 / 47.12 / 57.82. Finance 2.60; 50 / 57 / 62 / 65. IT Solutions 2.19-3.22; 26.76 / 32.54 / 41.02 / 48.15. Operations and Maintenance 0.14-0.76; 17.62 / 21.12 / 26.96 / 31.09. Restaurants (excluding fast food) 1.58-1.67; 13.87 / 17.38 / 21.66 / 26.81. Accommodation, Leisure, Tourism 2.42-2.59; 26.10 / 32.52 / 37.90 / 44.02. Medical Labs and Health Services 0.35; 25.24 / 29.74 / 33.24 / 34.24. Employment, Recruitment and Security Services 0.34; 74.50 / 77.50 / 80.50 / 84.50. Combined Entities 2.23; 12.99 / 24.40 / 35.81 / 46. Full table: 30 activities in the guide. | These are the last primary-text coefficients available and belong to the phase that ran to 2025. The 2026-2028 phase reportedly raised sector quotas `[model knowledge — verify]`; do not use these numbers to state a target's current range. Use them only to explain the mechanism or to test a target's 2024-2025 historical classification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` (2023-2024 values; superseded in practice) 
 Nitaqat guide, Services by Range | Platinum, High Green and Medium Green: may apply for new expatriate visas, change expatriate workers' occupations, renew work permits, transfer expatriates' sponsorship into the entity from any other entity, and benefit from immediate counting in Nitaqat. Low Green: cannot apply for new visas or change occupations; can renew existing work permits; immediate counting. Red: cannot change occupations, cannot transfer expatriates in, cannot apply for new visas, cannot issue work permits for new expatriates, cannot renew work permits of existing expatriates. Use of each service is subject to its own regulations. | Red is existential for an expatriate-heavy business: existing work permits lapse at renewal, which makes continued employment of the expatriate unlawful (Labor Law Art. 33) and exposes the employer to per-person penalties under Art. 229 as amended (fine up to SAR 100,000, closure) and the worker to removal through the Ministry of Interior referral in Art. 39 as amended. Low Green freezes growth (no new visas, no occupation change) but keeps the current workforce. "Immediate counting" means a Green-or-better entity gets credit for a new Saudi hire at once, whereas a Red entity is counted on the rolling average only `[model knowledge — verify]` for the mechanics beyond the guide's one-line list. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Implementing Regulation Art. 14 First (recruitment), items 1-7, 12 | Recruitment from abroad may not compete with or displace Saudi labour; an employer's failure to attract Saudis is not a ground for approving recruitment. An establishment wishing to recruit must have achieved the Saudization ratio that enables recruitment under Nitaqat; visas may not be issued if adding them would push the ratio below the ministry's required level; the number of workers an establishment may recruit is set under Nitaqat; recruitment is confined to activities listed in Nitaqat; applications go through the ministry's approved platform. The ministry may refuse recruitment where there is collective delay in paying wages, proven commercial concealment (tasattur), workers left to work for others or for themselves, failure to meet the minimum Saudization ratio, false data (all recruitment suspended up to five years, visas cancelled), sale of visas (five-year suspension), unrenewed work permits or iqamas, or any other case Nitaqat provides. | The regulation turns the Nitaqat band into a legal condition of recruitment, and adds independent refusal grounds (WPS delays, tasattur, unrenewed permits) that bite even in a Green band. For diligence: a target's visa history and any five-year recruitment suspension for false data are discoverable from its Qiwa establishment file `[model knowledge — verify]` and should be requested. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Implementing Regulation Art. 14 Second item 10 and Third item 2(d) | An establishment requesting the transfer of a non-Saudi worker's services to it must have achieved the Saudization ratio required for its activity or entity under Nitaqat. A change of occupation for a non-Saudi worker also requires that the establishment has achieved the required Nitaqat ratio (and is not to a profession reserved for Saudis). | In an acquisition where the buyer entity will take over expatriate staff by transfer of services (rather than by an entity change under Art. 14 Second item 19), the buyer's own Nitaqat band gates the transfer. Plan the receiving entity's band before signing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Implementing Regulation, part-time work rules First(5) and flexible-work rules Second(8) | A Saudi employed by the employer in one of its entities on a part-time contract is counted in Saudization at the rates adopted in the Nitaqat rules, provided he is registered with GOSI as a part-time worker. For flexible-work (hourly) contracts, which are for Saudis only: the establishment earns one full point for every 160 completed flexible-work hours by a worker or group of workers, and the ministry sets how the ratio is computed against total headcount; the flexible-work contract must be documented on the ministry's portal. | Part-time Saudis count at a fraction (one half in HRSD practice `[model knowledge — verify]`) and only if GOSI-registered as part-time; flexible-work hours aggregate to whole points at 160 hours. Neither route can substitute for the Qiwa-documented full-time contract in the 2026 counting rule below `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Implementing Regulation, labour-services licensing rules (workers supplied to clients) | Workers whom a licensed labour-services company supplies to a client establishment are included in the workforce on which the client's Saudization ratios are computed, unless the ministry sets specific conditions for counting such workers; licensees must comply with the Saudization requirements the ministry issues for their clients and may not provide services that would breach a client's required ratio. | Outsourced expatriate labour (Ajeer-type secondment from a licensed company) does not escape the client's Nitaqat denominator by default. A vendor-agreement review of a manpower contract should ask which entity's Nitaqat file the seconded workers sit on and whether the ministry has set counting conditions for that arrangement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Labor Law Art. 229, 230, 231 (as restructured by M/46 of 1436H; Art. 230(5) as amended by M/44 of 1446H) | Without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law, anyone who breaches any provision of the Law, its Regulation or the decisions issued under it is liable to one or more of: a fine not exceeding SAR 100,000; closure of the establishment for up to 30 days; permanent closure. The penalty may be doubled on repetition, and fines multiply by the number of persons concerned (Art. 229). The ministry, by decision of the Minister or his delegate, may itself impose the fine and the temporary closure up to half their maximum, with appeal to the competent administrative court; the Minister issues a schedule of violations and penalties within that half-maximum, graduated by gravity, and a second schedule of violations exceeding half the maximum or warranting permanent closure, which the ministry must take to the competent court; the ministry and the violator may settle by paying the fine the ministry assesses, by decision of the Minister or his delegate (Art. 230). The violator must remove the violation within the period the Regulation sets, failing which it is a new violation (Art. 231). Fines are collected as public funds and go to the Human Resources Development Fund (Art. 232). The original Arts. 230-241 with specific fines per article (SAR 3,000-10,000 for Saudization breaches, etc.) are no longer in force. | The statutory ceiling for a Saudization breach is SAR 100,000 per person concerned, but the amount actually charged comes from the Minister's schedule under Art. 230(2), which HRSD issues by ministerial decision (the current schedule, reportedly revised with effect from 25 February 2026, was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`). The operative sanction remains the Nitaqat service block (Regulation Art. 8) and, since M/44, refusal to renew work permits under Art. 35 (row above). Fake Saudization (registering Saudis who do not work) is penalised under the HRSD schedule and separately under Social Insurance Law Art. 59(2) (see `social-insurance-law.md`). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Labor Law Arts. 32-34; Art. 35 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H and again by M/44 of 1446H) | Recruitment from abroad requires ministry approval. A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the ministry, granted only if the worker entered lawfully and is authorised to work, has qualifications or skills the country needs and that nationals lack or lack in sufficient numbers (or is ordinary labour the country needs), and is contracted with and under the responsibility of an employer. Other licences do not substitute for the work permit. Art. 35 in force: the ministry may, for considerations it assesses, refuse to renew a work permit where the employer has breached the job-localisation standards the ministry sets or any other requirements or controls in the Regulation; the Regulation sets procedures ensuring the worker is not harmed by the non-renewal, including the possibility of transferring the worker's services to another employer without the violating employer's consent. (The original Art. 35, requiring a check that no qualified Saudi job-seeker wants the job before renewal, is no longer in force.) | Art. 35 as amended is the direct statutory basis for the Nitaqat service block on renewals: a Red entity is denied the Art. 33 permit for new hires and the Art. 35 renewal for existing ones, and the worker may then transfer out without the employer's consent (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 6, see `platform-obligations.md`). The employer therefore loses the workforce, not only the growth headroom, when it falls to Red. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Nitaqat guide, Technical Details (electronic range check) | An entity can identify its range electronically through the ministry's online tool (the guide links to it). | The tool now sits inside Qiwa as the Nitaqat calculator and establishment dashboard, which shows the current band, the rate, the thresholds for the next band and the number of Saudis needed to move up `[model knowledge — verify]`. A diligence request list should ask for a dated Qiwa Nitaqat screenshot or export for each entity rather than a self-declared percentage. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the existence of an electronic range check; Qiwa location is `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Counting rules for Saudi employees (HRSD Nitaqat rules, not in the source set) | A Saudi employee counts as one unit if registered with GOSI on a wage of at least SAR 4,000 per month; a wage between SAR 3,000 and 3,999 counts as one half; below SAR 3,000 counts as zero. Additional multipliers and categories in the Nitaqat rules: persons with disabilities (four units, subject to Regulation Art. 9(4)), students on approved programs, released prisoners, Saudi women in certain roles, remote workers, and part-time workers at one half (with GOSI part-time registration). Owners and partners registered on the establishment are counted under separate rules. | The SAR 4,000 threshold is why the effective minimum wage for Saudis in the private sector is SAR 4,000 even though the Labor Law sets no minimum: below it the employee gives no Nitaqat credit. Offer letters for Saudis at SAR 3,000-3,999 should be flagged in hiring-review. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Qiwa contract documentation as a condition of Nitaqat credit (from 15 April 2026, as claimed) | From 15 April 2026 a Saudi employee is counted in the Nitaqat Saudization rate only if his employment contract is documented (authenticated) on Qiwa; GOSI registration alone no longer earns credit. | If confirmed, an entity's Nitaqat rate can fall without any change in headcount simply because contracts were never authenticated on Qiwa. Diligence should reconcile GOSI-registered Saudis with Qiwa-documented contracts. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (eiglaw.com note), unconfirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or qiwa.sa. See `platform-obligations.md` for the underlying documentation duty (Regulation Art. 18(2)), which is settled. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Nitaqat 2026-2028 phase (from 26 April 2026, as claimed) | A new Nitaqat phase for 2026-2028 with higher required Saudization rates by sector, announced by HRSD with a lead time before each annual step; the lead also claims the Yellow band was removed (inconsistent with the 2021 guide, which already had no Yellow band). Entities that were Green under the 2024-2025 coefficients may fall a band on 26 April 2026 without a workforce change, as happened in the guide's own 2023-to-2024 example. | Any current-band statement for a target must rest on the Qiwa dashboard after 26 April 2026, not on the 2024 coefficients in this file. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.5 (middleeastbriefing.com), unconfirmed. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Profession-specific Saudization decisions (outside Nitaqat) | HRSD ministerial decisions localise named professions independently of the entity's Nitaqat band, typically in phases: accounting professions 40% in phase 1 (reported from October 2025) rising in later phases to 70%; engineering professions 30% for establishments with five or more engineers (reported from July 2025); pharmacy 35% in community pharmacies and 65% in hospitals; dentistry 45%; technical engineering roles 25%; and earlier decisions on sales outlets, customer-service, consultancy, tourism, and the 100%-Saudi occupations list. Compliance is checked by profession code on Qiwa and non-compliance blocks the affected work permits and attracts the HRSD violations schedule. | These sit on top of the entity-level Nitaqat requirement; a Platinum entity can still be blocked from renewing an expatriate accountant's permit if its accounting-profession ratio is below the decision's threshold. Percentages and dates here are from memory of 2025 announcements and must be verified against the HRSD decisions before use. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Revised work-visa quotas for new establishments (26 August 2026, as claimed) | Instant-visa caps for newly established businesses tied to company age, participation in the Establishment Programme, and Nitaqat band. | Relevant to hiring-review for a newly incorporated Saudi entity that expects to recruit expatriates in its first year. Source: amendment-watch lead 1.2 (visasupdate.com, low reliability), unconfirmed. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 

FILE platform-obligations
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Labor Law Art. 15, 16 | On starting work in any establishment the employer must notify the competent labour office in writing of: the establishment's name, type, head office and correspondence address; the licensed economic activity with commercial-register or licence number, date and issuer (copy attached); the number of workers to be employed; the name of the responsible manager; any other data the ministry requires. The employer must designate a responsible representative at the workplace where it cannot act itself, notify the labour office of the partner's or manager's name, and notify any change within seven days. | The "labour office file" is now the Qiwa establishment account (ملف المنشأة), opened against the commercial register and used for every later platform step; the seven-day manager-change notice is done by updating the establishment's authorised manager on Qiwa `[model knowledge — verify]`. In diligence, an establishment number that does not exist on Qiwa, or a listed manager who has left, is an Art. 15-16 breach and a practical block on all HRSD services. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Labor Law Art. 51 (as amended by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 18(2) | The employment contract must be written in two copies, one kept by each party, and must be documented in accordance with the relevant statutory provisions and as the Regulation sets out; the contract exists even if unwritten, in which case the worker alone may prove it and his rights by all means of proof, and either party may demand a written contract at any time. Regulation Art. 18(2): the employer must document workers' contracts electronically through the ministry's approved platform. | Qiwa authentication of every employee's contract is a legal duty on the employer, not a convenience: the amended Art. 51 says "يجب توثيقه" and the Regulation names the platform. An undocumented contract remains valid and enforceable against the employer (the worker can prove it by any means); the sanction falls on the employer: Art. 229 (fine up to SAR 100,000 per person, closure) applied through the Minister's schedule under Art. 230 (the current schedule amount is `[model knowledge — verify]`), and loss of Nitaqat credit for undocumented Saudi contracts from April 2026 `[model knowledge — verify]`, see `saudization-nitaqat.md`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 51; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 18 
 Labor Law Art. 52 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H, para (1) as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 18(1) | The ministry issues a unified template for each type of employment contract containing at least: the employer's name and place; the worker's name, nationality, identity data and residential address; the agreed wage including benefits and allowances; the type and place of work; the joining date; the term if fixed; and each party's basic rights and obligations (subject to Art. 37 for non-Saudis). The contract must follow the template; the parties may add clauses that do not conflict with the Law, the Regulation and the decisions issued under it. Regulation Art. 18(1) adopts the unified templates in Annex 5. | The Qiwa contract is the unified template; a bespoke English-language contract is permissible only as additional clauses attached to (and not conflicting with) the Qiwa template. Where the Qiwa contract and a side letter differ on wage, term or notice, expect the labour court to apply whichever is more favourable to the worker and to treat the Qiwa wage as the declared wage for WPS and GOSI `[model knowledge — verify]`. Hiring-review should check the Qiwa contract, not only the offer letter. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 52; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 18 
 Labor Law Art. 37 (as replaced by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 12 | A non-Saudi's employment contract must be written and fixed-term; if it states no term, its term is one year from the worker's actual start date, and if work continues after that year it is deemed renewed for a similar period. Regulation: a non-Saudi's contract is fixed-term however long its term or renewals, and never becomes indefinite even if the parties so agree. (The original rule that the work-permit term filled the gap is no longer in force.) | On Qiwa an expatriate's contract is entered with a term; where none is stated the statutory default is one year from actual start, rolling annually. Termination-review must treat every expatriate contract as fixed-term for Art. 77 compensation purposes regardless of what the parties wrote, and must compute the unexpired term from the actual start date, not the permit date. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 37; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 12 
 Labor Law Art. 33, 34; Art. 35 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H and M/44 of 1446H) | A non-Saudi may not work, and may not be allowed to work, without a work permit from the ministry on its form, granted only if he entered lawfully with work authorisation, has needed qualifications not available among Saudis (or is needed ordinary labour), and is contracted with and under the responsibility of an employer. No other licence substitutes for the work permit. The ministry may, for considerations it assesses, refuse to renew a work permit where the employer has breached the ministry's job-localisation standards or any other requirement or control in the Regulation; the Regulation sets procedures so the worker is not harmed by non-renewal, including transfer of his services to another employer without the violating employer's consent. | Work permits are issued and renewed on Qiwa (annually, against the work-permit fee and the expatriate levy, and only if the entity's Nitaqat band allows it) and the iqama is then issued or renewed on Muqeem/Absher against the valid work permit `[model knowledge — verify]`. Art. 35 is the statutory hook for the Nitaqat renewal block and for the worker's exit route when the employer is non-compliant (Regulation Art. 14 Second item 6). Employing an expatriate whose permit has lapsed is an Art. 33 breach penalised per person under Art. 229 and blocks recruitment and transfers for the whole establishment (Regulation Art. 14 First item 12(e), Second item 2). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Labor Law Art. 38 with Regulation Art. 14 Third | An employer may not employ a worker in an occupation other than that on his work permit, and the worker may not work outside his occupation before completing the statutory occupation-change procedure. Regulation: occupation change requires a valid licence or commercial register registered with the labour office and an active business; the new occupation named per the Saudi occupational classification; certified qualifications (with two exceptions); the establishment's Nitaqat ratio achieved; not to a Saudi-only occupation or one whose change is barred; application through the ministry's approved platform; and the consent of any sector regulator where the occupation is licensed. | Job-title mismatches between the Qiwa/iqama occupation and the actual role are among the most common findings in a Saudi workforce audit; each is an Art. 38 breach penalised per person under Art. 229 through the Minister's schedule and, for licensed professions (engineering, accounting, health), a sector-regulator issue too. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 38; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the Regulation 
 Labor Law Art. 39 (as replaced by M/44 of 1446H) with Regulation Art. 13; Art. 30 (as replaced by M/44) with Art. 229 bis | Without following the prescribed rules and procedures, an employer may not let its worker work for others or for his own account, a worker may not work for another employer or for his own account, and an employer may not employ another's worker; the ministry inspects establishments, records violations and applies the Law's penalties, referring matters within the Ministry of Interior's competence to it, and the Ministry of Interior refers violating employers it detects to HRSD for penalties. Regulation Art. 13: a non-Saudi whose work the ministry approves through the Ajeer program is not in breach. Art. 30 as replaced: no natural or legal person may carry on the activity of employing Saudis, recruiting workers, or "outsourcing activity" (نشاط الإسناد) without an HRSD licence; the Regulation sets the licensing conditions and licensee obligations. Art. 229 bis (added by M/44): breach of Art. 30(1) is fined SAR 200,000 to 500,000, without prejudice to Art. 229. (The original Art. 233 fine of SAR 5,000-20,000 with deportation at the employer's expense is no longer in force; removal of the worker now runs through the Ministry of Interior referral.) | Intra-group secondment of an expatriate between two Saudi entities, or the use of another company's staff on site, is lawful only through Ajeer (temporary work notices on Qiwa) or a formal transfer of services; informal arrangements expose both companies to Art. 229 penalties and the worker to removal via the Ministry of Interior. Since M/44, supplying workers to third parties is a licensed "outsourcing activity" under Art. 30, and doing it unlicensed carries the SAR 200,000-500,000 fine of Art. 229 bis, which is the largest fixed fine in the Law. Worker-classification and vendor-agreement reviews should ask for the Ajeer notice for any on-site third-party expatriate and for the supplier's Art. 30 licence. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Arts. 30, 39, 229 bis; `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for Regulation Art. 13 
 Labor Law Art. 40 (para (1) as reworded by M/44 of 1446H) | The employer bears the non-Saudi worker's recruitment fees, iqama and work-permit fees and their renewal, the fines for delay in those that the employer causes, occupation-change fees, exit and re-entry fees, and the return ticket after the relationship ends; the worker bears his return cost if unfit for work or if he wishes to return without a legitimate reason. The employer bears the transfer-of-services fee for a worker who wishes to transfer to it. The employer bears the cost of preparing and repatriating a deceased worker's body unless GOSI does. | Every platform fee in this file (Qiwa work permit and levy, Muqeem iqama, Absher exit/re-entry, transfer fee) is by law an employer cost; deduction from the worker's wage or clawback at exit is unlawful, and clauses purporting to do so are a standard hiring-review and termination-review finding. The M/44 rewording limits the employer's liability for delay fines to those "caused by the employer", which opens a narrow argument that a fine caused by the worker (for example, failing to present for iqama renewal) may be charged to him; the only other costs the worker may bear are his own return ticket in the two Art. 40(2) cases. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 First items 10, 12, 16 (recruitment) | Recruitment applications are submitted through the ministry's approved platform in accordance with Nitaqat. The ministry may refuse where the establishment has a collective delay in paying wages, proven commercial concealment, workers left to work for others or themselves, an unmet minimum Saudization ratio, has given false data (all recruitment stopped for up to five years, visas cancelled, no permits for workers recruited on them, criminal referral for forgery), has sold visas (five-year stop), has not renewed its workers' permits or iqamas, or in any other case Nitaqat provides. No worker may be recruited or transferred without actual work existing with the employer. | Visa issuance on Qiwa is conditioned on WPS compliance (collective wage delay), permit renewal discipline, and Nitaqat; a target's five-year recruitment suspension for false data is a material diligence finding. The "no recruitment without actual work" rule is the legal basis for the tasattur (visa trading) enforcement. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 1, 11, 14, 15, 16, 17 (transfer of services with consent) | A non-Saudi worker may move to another employer without any minimum service period if the current employer consents, unless Nitaqat provides otherwise. Transfer requests are submitted electronically through the ministry's approved platform. Both the former and the new employer must keep the worker's written consent to the transfer before the procedure. The current employer's consent must be given electronically on the transfer request or by a paper waiver attested by an official body addressed to the requesting employer, and may not be made subject to any condition; the consent is not required in the no-consent cases below or for workers moving to a new contractor under continuing government operation, maintenance and cleaning contracts. If the transfer is not approved after the consent was given, the worker stays with the original employer. No decision or final judgment inconsistent with the transfer may exist before it is completed. | The transfer runs on Qiwa: the receiving employer requests, the current employer accepts electronically, the worker accepts. "May not be conditioned" bars the practice of demanding payment or a release as the price of a Qiwa release; a clause in a settlement agreement conditioning the transfer approval on a waiver is unenforceable as a condition of the approval itself `[model knowledge — verify]` for court practice. Keep the worker's written consent on file (item 14). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 5, 6, 7, 8, 18, 21 (transfer without the employer's consent) | A non-Saudi may transfer without the current employer's consent: (5) as a newly arrived worker for whom no work permit has been issued; (6) where his work permit or iqama has expired; (7) on expiry of the documented employment contract (subject to item 21); (8) by decision of the Minister or delegate where the employer prolongs a pending court case (absence from two hearings without accepted excuse, or proven delay), on the court's recommendation, where the establishment has not paid three consecutive months' wages or is late past the third month's due date (application within one Hijri year), where the employer is absent (travel, imprisonment, death or otherwise) leaving the establishment unable to meet its obligations for three months without an appointed representative, for categories or nationalities with special instructions on expiry or employer termination, for an accompanying dependant after the end of the relationship, for spouse reunification, where the worker reports commercial concealment with evidence, or in any other public-interest case the Minister decides. If a transfer in an item (7) case is not approved, the Minister may license the worker to work for another employer. (21) Subject to Art. 77, a non-Saudi may move without consent once he has completed twelve months from entry and has given the current employer not less than ninety days' notice before ending the relationship, unless otherwise agreed. Item 2 bars transfers to an establishment with unrenewed permits or iqamas, or a worker three months in the Kingdom without a permit; item 4 bars transfers to an employer with collective wage delay, tasattur, or workers left to work for others. | This is the 2021 "labour mobility" reform as now codified in the Regulation: an expatriate can leave at contract expiry, or after twelve months in the Kingdom on ninety days' notice, and Qiwa processes the transfer without the employer's release. The employer's protection is Art. 77 (compensation for early termination of a fixed-term contract, preserved by item 21) and the Qiwa contract term. The three-months-unpaid-wages ground links directly to WPS data on Mudad. Item 2 and item 4 mean a receiving employer with lapsed permits or WPS arrears cannot take anyone in, whatever the worker's rights. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Regulation Art. 14 Second items 19, 20 (corporate changes are not transfers) | The following are name amendments, not transfers of services: continuation of a deceased owner's business by heirs (with power of attorney or conversion to a company, Ministry of Commerce approval); change of the establishment's name, objects, all or some partners, or company type with the commercial register retained (MoC approval); merger of two or more establishments whether or not the legal entity changes, stated in the constitutional document (MoC approval); separation of a partner with a share of activities as a sole business (MoC approval); conversion of a sole establishment into a company by admission of partners; transfer of ownership of the establishment with all workers continuing and moving to the new owner with the register retained (MoC approval). Moving non-Saudi workforce registration between branches of the same entity (without limit) or between entities of the same establishment is a workforce-registration transfer, not a transfer of services, under Nitaqat. | For M&A: a share deal, a merger, or a business transfer with the commercial register retained does not require individual Qiwa transfers or the workers' consent under item 14, and does not consume the buyer's Nitaqat headroom; an asset deal into a new commercial register does, worker by worker, with each expatriate's consent and the receiving entity's Nitaqat band as gates. Structure the deal with this table in view and put the Qiwa "name amendment" step on the closing checklist. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Labor Law Art. 90 (as replaced by M/46 of 1436H) | Wages and all sums due to the worker must be paid in the official currency: daily-paid workers at least weekly, monthly-paid workers monthly, piece-work with weekly instalments proportionate to work done and the balance within the week after delivery, others at least weekly. Establishments are obliged to pay wages into the workers' accounts through banks approved in the Kingdom, provided the due dates above are not exceeded; the Minister may exempt some establishments. (The original text, which made bank payment optional with the worker's consent and required payment at the workplace during working hours, is no longer in force.) | Bank payment of wages is a statutory duty since 2015, not a WPS convenience: cash wages, or wages paid after the monthly due date, breach Art. 90 regardless of the Wage Protection System, and are penalised under Art. 229 through the Minister's schedule (the original Art. 235 fine of SAR 500-3,000 per worker is no longer in force). The WPS adds the monthly reporting and matching layer (next two rows). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Regulation, Model Work-Organisation Regulation Art. 15 (Annex to the Regulation) | Subject to any procedures or arrangements the Wage Protection Program provides, workers' wages are paid in the official currency on a fixed date each month and deposited into the workers' accounts through banks approved in the Kingdom. (The model regulation is the mandatory template for the employer's work-organisation regulation under Labor Law Art. 12; establishments with fewer than fifty workers may adopt it as is.) | This is the only reference to the Wage Protection Program in the primary texts read: it presupposes the program and folds the Art. 90(2) bank-deposit duty into every employer's internal regulation. The WPS itself (monthly wage file uploaded through Mudad or a bank, matched against Qiwa and GOSI data, compliance percentage thresholds, service suspension and fines for non-compliance) rests on HRSD ministerial decisions not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Wage Protection System via Mudad (HRSD ministerial decisions, not in the source set) | Every private-sector establishment must upload a monthly wage file covering all workers (Saudi and non-Saudi) through Mudad or an approved bank, showing the wage per worker matched to the Qiwa contract and GOSI declaration; HRSD computes a compliance percentage and, below a threshold (80% in the phased rollout) or after a defined period of non-upload, suspends Qiwa services (visas, transfers, permit renewals) and applies the violations schedule; deductions above the Art. 92 limits and unexplained variances are flagged. Establishments with very few workers were phased in last. Non-payment for three consecutive months is also a transfer-without-consent ground (row above, settled). | WPS compliance is the single most consequential platform duty in practice: a lapse blocks visas and transfers for the whole establishment and is used as evidence in wage claims. Diligence should request twelve months of Mudad compliance reports and reconcile them to payroll. The thresholds, decision numbers and current fine amounts must be confirmed from HRSD. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Wage-clause direct enforcement (MHRSD-MoJ framework; amendment-watch lead 1.1) | Under a joint HRSD-Ministry of Justice framework the wage clause of a Qiwa-documented employment contract is treated as an enforceable instrument: a worker (or employer) can apply to the Enforcement Court through the Qiwa-Najiz-Mudad link for unpaid documented wages without a labour-court judgment. Phase 3 reportedly extends this to open-ended contracts from 6 August 2026. | If confirmed, Qiwa documentation plus Mudad wage records convert unpaid wages into a directly enforceable claim, which raises the stakes of accurate Qiwa wage entries and timely WPS uploads. Source: King & Spalding note in the amendment watch; unconfirmed on hrsd.gov.sa or moj.gov.sa. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Regulation, flexible-work rules Second items 14, 15 | A flexible-work (hourly, Saudi-only) contract must be electronic and fixed-term with an hourly wage; employing a flexible-work worker requires the contract to be documented on the electronic portal the ministry designates, and anyone employing a flexible-work worker without a documented contract is liable to the penalties in Ministerial Decision No. 178743 of 27/9/1440H as amended. | This is the one place the Regulation itself names the penalties decision for undocumented contracts (MD 178743 of 1440H is the HRSD violations and penalties schedule issued under Labor Law Art. 230(2)-(3) `[model knowledge — verify]` for the identification, reportedly revised from 25 February 2026). Flexible-work contracts are documented on the flexible-work portal, not the standard Qiwa contract flow `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Social Insurance Law Arts. 7, 8(4), 9, 10 (GOSI online) | The employer must register its establishment and each contributor with GOSI within the Regulation's grace period, submit wage data in the manner GOSI sets, and pay both shares of contributions within the Regulation's period (2% per month delay fine); government bodies and state-controlled companies must demand a GOSI certificate before paying dues, awarding tenders, amending or renewing the commercial register, licensing, or approving recruitment from abroad. See `social-insurance-law.md`. | Registration, monthly wage updates and payment run through the GOSI online portal, which is integrated with Qiwa (a new Qiwa contract is expected to match a GOSI registration at the same wage) and with the Ministry of Commerce (commercial-register renewal checks GOSI compliance) `[model knowledge — verify]` for the integrations. The statutory duty and the certificate rule are settled. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Exit/re-entry and final-exit visas (Absher / Muqeem; Labor Law Art. 40) | The Labor Law fixes only that the employer bears exit and re-entry fees and the return ticket at the end of the relationship (Art. 40). The visas themselves are Ministry of Interior residency acts issued on Absher (business) or Muqeem: single or multiple exit/re-entry during employment, and a final-exit visa (خروج نهائي) when the relationship ends, after which the iqama is cancelled. Since the 2021 labour-mobility reform an expatriate can request exit/re-entry and final exit himself through Absher with notice to the employer, removing the employer's veto. | Termination-review: the final-exit visa is the operational end of an expatriate's employment and is normally issued after the end-of-service settlement; issuing it before settlement, or withholding it after settlement, are both common dispute triggers. Only the fee-allocation in Art. 40 is settled; the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and the worker-initiated exit rule are practice points to verify. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` for Art. 40 fees; `[model knowledge — verify]` for the Absher/Muqeem mechanics and worker-initiated exit 
 Musaned (domestic workers; note only) | The Regulation's rules for licensed recruitment and labour-services companies define the "approved platform" as the electronic platform through which licensees' operations are documented, "such as Musaned and Ajeer Recruitment and any platform the ministry approves", and require licensees to conclude and document the worker's contract through the approved platform; providing labour services without documenting them through the Ajeer Recruitment platform is a listed violation. Domestic workers are excluded from the Labor Law (Art. 7) and governed by the Domestic Workers Regulation; corporate employers encounter Musaned only when sponsoring household staff for executives. | Out of scope for the consuming skills. If an executive's employment package includes a company-sponsored domestic worker, the contract and visa run on Musaned under the Domestic Workers Regulation, not on Qiwa, and the company should not be the sponsor of record `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` for the definitional reference; the rest `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Labor Law Arts. 229, 230, 231, 232, 233 (penalties chapter as restructured by M/46 of 1436H; Art. 230(5) as amended by M/44 of 1446H) | Without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law, any breach of the Law, its Regulation or the decisions under it is punishable by one or more of: a fine up to SAR 100,000; closure up to 30 days; permanent closure; the penalty may be doubled on repetition and fines multiply by the number of persons concerned (Art. 229). The ministry, by decision of the Minister or his delegate, may impose the fine and temporary closure up to half the maximum, with appeal to the competent administrative court; the Minister issues a graduated schedule of violations and penalties within that half, and a second schedule of violations exceeding it or warranting permanent closure, which the ministry must take to the competent court; the ministry and the violator may settle by payment of the fine the ministry assesses, by decision of the Minister or his delegate (Art. 230). The violator must remove the violation within the period the Regulation sets, failing which it is a new violation (Art. 231). Fines are collected as public funds and go to the Human Resources Development Fund (Art. 232). The Minister may reward anyone who helps uncover a violation with up to 25% of the fine collected (Art. 233). Arts. 234-235 (added by M/14 of 1440H): labour claims are time-barred twelve months after the end of the relationship absent an accepted excuse or an admission; the employer may not change the working conditions to the worker's detriment while a case is pending. (The original Arts. 230-241, with fixed fines per article such as SAR 2,000-5,000 per worker for work-permit, occupation and expatriate-contract breaches, are no longer in force.) | Every platform failure in this file (no work permit, wrong occupation, no fixed-term expatriate contract, unnotified manager change, undocumented contract, cash wages) is punished under the same Art. 229 ceiling of SAR 100,000 per person concerned, with the actual amount taken from the Minister's schedule under Art. 230(2) (HRSD applies size-graduated amounts; the schedule in force, reportedly revised 25 February 2026, was not in the source set `[model knowledge — verify]`). Ministry-imposed penalties are capped at SAR 50,000 per person (half the maximum); anything above, or permanent closure, needs a court. The 25% informant reward (Art. 233) makes undocumented or cash-paid workers a live reporting risk. The operative commercial sanction remains the Qiwa service block. The Arabic file shows renumbering by M/46: the removal duty is "Art. 231 currently (formerly Art. 240)" and the Fund allocation "Art. 232 currently (formerly Art. 242)". | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE commercial-agencies-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Practical effect | Tag 
---|---|---|---
 Art. 1 (unamended) | Non-Saudis, whether natural or legal persons, may not be commercial agents in the Kingdom. Saudi companies carrying on commercial agency business must have wholly Saudi capital, and their board members and authorised signatories must be Saudi. | On the face of the Law, which the portal shows as unamended on this point, a MISA-licensed foreign-owned LLC cannot be registered as a commercial agent, and a Saudi JV with a foreign shareholder is also excluded. `[model knowledge — verify]`: the Ministry of Commerce has, through amendments to the Implementing Regulations and its electronic "commercial agencies" service (2023-2024), permitted foreign-invested companies licensed by MISA to register agencies, and wholesale/retail trade is open to 100% foreign ownership under conditions; whether this rests on regulation, ministerial exception or non-enforcement of Art. 1 must be confirmed with counsel because the statute has not been amended. A foreign principal selling directly through its own licensed Saudi subsidiary is not "a commercial agent" and this article does not stop it. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; relaxation `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 2 (unamended) | Agents operating when the Law was issued who did not meet Art. 1 were given up to two years from entry into force to wind up and transfer their business to Saudi agents, the Minister fixing the period per agent. | Transitional only; spent. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3 (unamended), first sentence | No one may carry on the work of a commercial agent unless entered in the register kept for that purpose at the Ministry of Commerce; the Minister creates the register by decision. | Registration is a condition of lawfully *acting* as an agent or distributor (see the 1400H Added Art. 1 row), not a condition of the validity of the contract between principal and agent (see Art. 4 row). The Law does not itself fix a deadline; `[model knowledge — verify]`: the Implementing Regulations require the agent/distributor to apply for registration within three months of signing the agreement, and late applications have been treated as violations attracting the Art. 4 fine. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; three-month deadline `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 3, register contents | The register records the name of the merchant or company, the type of goods covered, the principal company or establishment, the date of the appointment and its term if the agency is for a fixed period. | Diligence on a Saudi distributor should ask for the registration certificate for each principal/product line: it evidences the principal, the goods and the term. A new product line, a change of principal or a renewal needs an updated entry `[model knowledge — verify]`. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3, application, refusal, appeal | Applications with supporting documents go to the Deputy Minister. Registration may be refused only to a non-Saudi or to a Saudi barred from or unfit to practise trade; a refused applicant may appeal to the Minister. | The Ministry has no discretion under the Law to refuse a qualifying Saudi applicant on commercial grounds (for example because another agent is already registered for the same product), which is the textual basis for saying the Law imposes no exclusivity. `[model knowledge — verify]`: the Regulations historically required the application to attach the agency contract (in or conforming to the Ministry's model form, see Added Art. 4) and a certified, legalised copy from the principal; the current electronic service accepts the parties' own contract with the required data fields. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; documentary requirements `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 4 (in force: as amended by Royal Decree M/32 of 10/8/1400H) | Anyone who violates the Law or its Implementing Regulations is fined not less than SAR 5,000 and not more than SAR 50,000, with publication of the penalty at the violator's expense, without prejudice to the right of anyone harmed by the violation to claim compensation. If the violation is attributed to a foreigner or to a Saudi company with one or more non-Saudi partners, the penalty is in addition the administrative liquidation of the business, with a possible permanent or fixed-term ban from trade; the Minister of Interior may order the foreigner's deportation in light of the penalty imposed, and the Ministry of Commerce must report the foreigner or non-Saudi partner to him. (Original 1382H text: fine SAR 1,000-5,000; superseded.) | The sanctions are administrative and personal to the violator; the Law does not declare an unregistered agency or distribution contract void, and the amended Art. 4 expressly preserves civil damages claims by anyone harmed. Breach of the Regulations (for example late registration, or failing the spare-parts duty) is itself a statutory offence since 1400H. `[model knowledge — verify]`: Saudi courts (Board of Grievances commercial circuits historically, Commercial Courts now) have enforced unregistered agency and distribution agreements between the parties as ordinary commercial contracts, treating non-registration as a regulatory breach by the agent rather than a defect in the contract; non-registration nonetheless matters because the agent cannot lawfully hold itself out as the registered agent, government buyers and some customs/SASO processes ask for the certificate, and the fine may be applied per violation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` for the amended text; validity inter partes `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 5 (in force: as amended by Royal Decree M/8 of 20/3/1393H) | Registration fee: SAR 500 for an individual merchant and for a company, paid once. (Original: SAR 50 / SAR 100.) | Nominal. `[model knowledge — verify]`: current platform service fees may be set separately by Ministry decision. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6 (original) and the 1389H addition (Royal Decree M/5 of 11/6/1389H) | The Law takes effect three months after publication. The 1389H decree added to this Law and to the Weights and Measures Law: (1) a committee of three members formed at the Ministry by the Minister's decision applies the penalties of both laws; (2) its decisions may be appealed to the Minister within fifteen days of notification, failing which they become final on expiry of that period and the Minister's ratification. | Penalties are imposed by a Ministry committee, not a court, with a short 15-day internal appeal. `[model knowledge — verify]`: a further challenge lies to the Administrative Court under the Board of Grievances Law within its general period. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Added Art. 1 (Royal Decree M/32 of 10/8/1400H) — **distributors are inside the Law** | Without prejudice to other laws, the Commercial Agencies Law and its amendments apply to everyone who contracts with the producer, or with whoever stands in the producer's place in its country, to carry on commercial business, whether as agent or as distributor in any form of agency or distribution. | Confirmed from the statute: a foreign principal signing a distribution agreement with a Saudi distributor triggers the Law (nationality rule, registration, penalties, spare-parts duty) for the distributor. Buy-sell distributorships, dealerships and exclusive resellers are all "distribution in any form". The principal has no registration duty of its own, but will be asked for a legalised appointment letter or signed contract for the filing. Whether a reseller of services (as opposed to a producer's goods) is covered turns on "المنتج" and is not settled by the text. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Added Art. 2 and para. "ثالثاً" (Royal Decree M/32 of 10/8/1400H) — **spare parts and maintenance** | Without prejudice to other rules on maintenance and spare parts, the agent and the distributor must: (a) permanently secure the spare parts consumers usually demand for the products of the agency, and other spare parts within a reasonable period, per the Regulations; (b) secure the necessary maintenance for the products and guarantee manufacturing quality and the conditions producers usually set, continuously throughout the agency and for one year after its expiry or the appointment of a new agent, whichever is earlier, per the Regulations. Paragraph "ثالثاً" of the decree extends Added Art. 2 to importers even if they are not agents or distributors, and to anyone who makes selling (directly or through others) a trade for profit, with the same penalties. | The Saudi agent or distributor carries a statutory after-sales duty to end customers that outlives the contract by a year; a distribution agreement should therefore allocate spare-parts supply, warranty support and post-termination run-off between principal and distributor, because the distributor's exposure to the Art. 4 fine and to consumer claims does not end when the principal appoints a successor. Importers and professional resellers who are not registered agents carry the same duty. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Added Arts. 3-4 (Royal Decree M/32 of 10/8/1400H) | The Minister of Commerce issues the Implementing Regulations of the Law, published in the Official Gazette. The Ministry prepares model contracts for the guidance of agents and distributors containing all essential contract data: the parties, subject matter, temporal and territorial scope, renewal and termination, the parties' obligations to each other and their obligations to consumers, especially maintenance and spare parts. | The model contract is "for guidance" (يسترشد بها) in the statute; `[model knowledge — verify]` the Regulations historically made it effectively mandatory by refusing registration of contracts lacking its minimum content, and the current electronic service requires the same data fields. The Regulations themselves (Ministerial Decision 1897 of 1401H as amended) were not read. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]`; regulatory practice `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Implementing Regulations — registration timing and renewal (not read) | Application within three months of signing; the registration is tied to the contract term and must be renewed or updated on renewal, amendment or change of principal; the Ministry issues an electronic registration certificate; deregistration on termination. | Diligence should check that the current term is registered and that amendments (territory, products, term) were filed. `[model knowledge — verify]`: since 2023-2024 the process runs on the Ministry of Commerce platform and the certificate is electronic. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Termination and compensation (no statutory rule) | Neither the original Law nor the 1389H/1400H additions contain a provision on termination, notice or compensation. There is no Saudi statutory goodwill or indemnity payment on termination of an agency or distributorship; claims are contractual and, under Sharia principles applied by the Commercial Courts, limited to proven actual loss caused by an abusive, bad-faith or non-compliant termination (for example, termination without the contractual notice, or during a fixed term without cause). The amended Art. 4 preserves damages claims for harm caused by a *violation of the Law or Regulations*, which is a separate head. | Contrast the GCC "agency protection" statutes (UAE, Kuwait, Qatar): Saudi law does not lock a foreign principal into an agent or block registration of a successor, and courts award damages, not goodwill compensation. `[model knowledge — verify]`: Saudi courts have awarded lost profits for the balance of a fixed term and reimbursement of demonstrable unrecouped investments; consequential and speculative losses are generally refused. A clear notice period, an express non-exclusivity statement and a "no compensation on expiry" clause are enforceable as written. Remember the distributor's one-year post-termination spare-parts and maintenance duty (Added Art. 2) when drafting run-off terms. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Exclusivity | Neither the Law nor (to my knowledge) the Regulations require exclusivity or restrict a principal to one agent; Art. 3 lets the Ministry refuse registration only for nationality or unfitness, so multiple agents or distributors for the same product can be registered. | Exclusivity is a matter of contract. Parallel imports by non-agents are not prohibited by this Law `[model knowledge — verify]`, though importers carry the Added Art. 2 after-sales duty. Under the Government Tenders and Procurement Law Art. 90, an authorised distributor or agent of the original manufacturer is not treated as a prohibited intermediary. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Interaction with the Anti-Concealment Law and the Foreign Investment framework | A foreign principal that controls a nominally Saudi agent through side agreements, or that operates through a Saudi front to satisfy Art. 1, risks the Anti-Concealment Law (تستر) offences for both parties. The lawful routes are a MISA-licensed subsidiary or a genuine arm's-length Saudi distributor. | Diligence on a distributor relationship should look for profit-sharing, management-control or power-of-attorney arrangements that make the foreign principal the real operator of the "Saudi" agent. See the anti-concealment file for the offence elements (not read for this file). | `[model knowledge — verify]` 

