FILE companies-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Tag 
---|---|---
 Art. 1 | Definitions. "Regulations" are the regulations issued under the Law; "Ministry/Minister" = Commerce; "CMA" = Capital Market Authority; "competent authority" = the Ministry, except for JSCs listed on the Exchange, where it is the CMA. "Relatives" = parents and grandparents (ascending), children and their descendants, and spouses. "Day" = calendar day, whether or not a working day. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 2, 4, 3, 5 | A company is a legal entity formed under articles of incorporation or articles of association by two or more persons who each contribute money or work to a profit-seeking venture; exceptionally a company may be formed by the unilateral will of one person, and non-profit companies may be formed under Part 7 (Art. 2). Permitted forms: general partnership, limited partnership, JSC, SJSC, LLC (Art. 4). A company incorporated under the Law is Saudi and its head office must be in the Kingdom (Art. 3). Trade name may be in Arabic or another language, derived from the object, distinctive, or the name of present or former partners (with their or their heirs' consent for former partners); the name must be accompanied by the company form; a name change follows the amendment procedure and does not affect existing rights, obligations or proceedings (Art. 5). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 6 | A founder is anyone who actually participates in incorporation and contributes cash or in kind. Founders file the incorporation and registration application with the Commercial Register with the articles and required documents; the Register decides on a complete application; a refusal must be reasoned; founders may appeal to the Ministry within 60 days of notification, and to the competent judicial authority if the appeal is refused or not decided within 30 days. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 7 | Every company has articles of incorporation (عقد تأسيس), except a JSC, an SJSC and a single-owner LLC, which each have articles of association (نظام أساس). The articles must contain the provisions required by the Law for the form; they must be in Arabic and may be accompanied by a translation; the Ministry issues model articles. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Art. 8; Regs Art. 2 | Articles and every amendment must be in writing or are void; incorporation or amendment takes effect after the requirements in the Law and Regulations are met. Founders, partners, managers or directors (as applicable) must register the articles and every amendment with the Commercial Register, which publishes what the Law requires; those who cause non-registration are jointly liable for resulting damage. Registered data are available to third parties and are evidence against the company and third parties; unregistered articles or amendments cannot be relied on against third parties, and an unregistered particular alone is ineffective against them. Regs Art. 2: identity of founders/partners is verified through the National Information Center, the Commercial Register, or the notarisation authority in cases the Ministry sets with the Ministry of Justice; amendment of a JSC/SJSC's articles of association requires submitting the extraordinary general assembly (or shareholders') decision with the required documents to the Commercial Register. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 9, 10, 12 | Legal personality arises on registration with the Commercial Register; during incorporation the company has personality to the extent needed to incorporate, provided incorporation is completed. On registration all contracts and acts done by the founders for its account pass to the company, which bears the incorporation expenses. If incorporation is not completed, those who dealt in the company's name are personally and jointly liable for their acts (Art. 9). The company may carry on its objects after registration and after obtaining any required licences (Art. 10). Contracts, receipts and other documents issued by the company must state its name, form, head-office address, e-mail (if any) and Commercial Register number, and its capital and paid-up capital (not required for general and limited partnerships), and "under liquidation" during liquidation (Art. 12). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 11; Regs Art. 4 | Founders, partners or shareholders may, during or after incorporation, (a) conclude one or more agreements regulating their relations among themselves or with the company, including how their heirs enter the company, and (b) conclude a family charter (ميثاق عائلي) regulating family ownership, governance, management, work and family-employment policies, dividends, disposal of interests or shares, and dispute resolution. The agreement or charter is binding and may form part of the articles; it must not contradict the Law or the articles. Regs Art. 4: where the agreement or charter forms part of the articles, its amendment quorum is that for amending the articles of the relevant company form. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 13-15 | Contributions may be cash, in kind or both; except in JSCs and SJSCs a partner may contribute work in return for a share of profits fixed in the articles, but never reputation or influence; only cash and in-kind contributions form the capital; interests or shares may be issued to a person for work or services benefiting the company (Art. 13). Warranty rules for in-kind contributions follow sale (ownership/real rights) or lease (personal right of use) unless agreed otherwise; a work contributor owes his work and its earnings, but not IP rights unless agreed (Art. 14). Every partner is a debtor for the contribution promised; on delay the company may demand performance or suspend the rights attached to the interests (dividends, voting), without prejudice to damages (Art. 15). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 16-17; Regs Art. 5 | Fiscal year is 12 months fixed in the articles; the first fiscal year may be 6 to 18 months from registration (Art. 16). The company must keep accounting records and supporting documents at its head office or a place fixed by the manager/board; financial statements must be prepared at the end of each fiscal year under the accounting standards approved in the Kingdom and deposited, as the Regulations specify, within six months of the fiscal year end; a controlling or holding company may require information from its subsidiary/investee for its own statements (Art. 17). Regs Art. 5: the manager or chairman deposits the financial statements and the auditor's report (if any) with the Saudi Business Center through the electronic financial-statements deposit programme. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 18-20; Regs Arts. 6-9 | Every company has one or more licensed auditors appointed (with fees, term and scope) by the partners, general assembly or shareholders; the Regulations cap the term. The appointing body may remove the auditor (subject to damages if unjustified) and the manager/chairman must notify the competent authority of the removal and its reasons within 5 days; an auditor may resign by written notice, must state reasons to the company and the competent authority, and the manager/board must convene the partners/assembly to consider the reasons and appoint a replacement (Art. 18). Micro and small companies are exempt from mandatory appointment unless their articles require it, they are listed, issue tradeable debt/sukuk, preferred or redeemable shares, are required by other laws, are foreign, or are a parent/subsidiary (unless the whole group is micro/small); the exemption requires the status in the first fiscal year or two consecutive years; holders of 10% of voting interests may demand an auditor; general partnerships are exempt except in stated cases (Art. 19). The auditor must be independent, may not be a founder, director, manager, partner, employee or relative, may not trade the company's shares during the audit, may not do technical, administrative or consulting work except as the Regulations allow, has access to all records at any time, must report obstruction and may ask the manager/board to convene the partners/assembly and may convene it himself if they fail to do so within 30 days; he reports on the financial statements, management's cooperation and violations found, reads the report or a summary at the annual assembly (or presents it by circulation), must keep secrets, and is liable (jointly if several) for errors (Art. 20). Regs Art. 6: individual auditor max 10 consecutive fiscal years; audit firm max 10, extendable to 20 with audit-committee recommendation or competing bids, engagement partner max 10; a 2-year cooling-off. Regs Art. 7: micro/small = any two of annual revenue ≤ SAR 10 million, assets ≤ SAR 10 million, employees ≤ 49; a statement of exemption accompanies the deposited financial statements. Regs Art. 8: the 10% demand is made in writing to the manager/board at least 30 days before fiscal year end, and the manager/board must then convene a vote. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 22-24; Regs Art. 10 | Annual or interim dividends may be distributed out of distributable profits in JSCs, SJSCs and LLCs; if distributed otherwise, creditors may claim against the company and the company may reclaim from every partner or shareholder, even in good faith; lawfully distributed dividends need not be returned if later losses occur (Art. 22). Partners share profits and losses in proportion to capital; a clause excluding a partner from profit or exempting him from loss is void, but the articles may set unequal ratios; a work-only partner may be exempted from loss if unpaid (Art. 23-24). Regs Art. 10: interim dividends require an enabling provision in the articles, an annual delegation to the manager/board, reasonable liquidity, and distributable profits per the latest financial statements; distributable profits = retained earnings in the last statement of financial position plus distributable reserves (profit-based reserves not earmarked or whose purpose was cancelled). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 25 | Ownership of interests in general partnerships, limited partnerships and LLCs passes by registration with the Commercial Register and is not effective against the company or third parties before that date. Shares in unlisted JSCs and SJSCs are transferred by entry in the shareholders' register under Art. 112 with the same effect; listed shares transfer under the Capital Market Law. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 26; Regs Art. 11 | Managers and directors owe duties of care and loyalty, in particular: act within their powers; work for the company's interest and success; decide and vote independently; exercise reasonable care, attention, diligence and skill; avoid conflicts; disclose any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings; accept no benefit from third parties in connection with their role. Regs Art. 11 elaborates: exercise powers for their proper purpose, act in good faith to maximise value for partners/shareholders, act objectively, perform with the skill of a prudent person and the knowledge expected of the office, avoid and disclose conflicts, and not exploit the position for benefits from third parties. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 27; Regs Arts. 12-13, 16-20 | A manager or director may not have a direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts, nor compete with the company in any branch of its activity, except with authorisation from the partners, general assembly or shareholders or their delegate; he may not exploit the company's assets, information or investment opportunities offered to him or the company. Exceptions to the interest bar: public tenders; personal-needs dealings on the company's standard terms within its ordinary activity; other cases set by the Regulations. Breach of the interest bar: the company may seek annulment and disgorgement; breach of the competition bar: damages (Art. 27). Regs Art. 12: an opportunity is off-limits if within the company's usual activities or if the company wishes or is expected to benefit from it. Regs Art. 13: a general-partnership manager needs authorisation from all partners for self-dealing (case by case) and for competing activity (renewed annually). Regs Art. 16: the general assembly may delegate the authorisation of related-party dealings to the board only where the dealing (or aggregate for the year) is below 1% of revenue per the latest financial statements and below SAR 10 million, is in the ordinary course and on non-preferential terms; delegation lasts at most one year or to the end of the board term; directors may not vote on the delegation. Regs Art. 17: indirect interest includes dealings benefiting the director's relatives, partnerships/LLCs in which he or a relative is a partner, JSCs/SJSCs in which he or relatives hold 5% or more, non-company establishments owned or managed by him or relatives, and companies where he or a relative is a manager, director or senior executive. Regs Arts. 18-19: competition authorisations may be delegated to the board with criteria, for at most one year; the director must notify the board (recorded in the minutes), abstain, and the licence is renewed annually. Regs Art. 20: if authorisation is refused the director must resign within the period set or his membership ends, unless he abandons the dealing or competition. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 28 | Managers and directors are jointly liable to the company, partners/shareholders and third parties for damage caused by breach of the Law or the articles or by their errors, negligence or default; any contrary clause is void. Liability is personal to the individual, or shared by all where the decision was unanimous; where taken by majority, dissenters who expressly recorded their objection in the minutes are not liable; absence is no defence unless the absentee proves he did not know of the decision or could not object once he knew. The company may insure its managers/directors against liability arising from their capacity. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 29-32 | The company may sue managers/directors for breach of the Law or the articles or for errors, negligence or default; the partners/assembly/shareholders decide to sue and appoint the representative (the liquidator during liquidation; the legal representative in Bankruptcy Law proceedings). Partners/shareholders holding 5% of capital (or less if the articles so provide) may bring the company's action if the company does not, provided the action serves the company, has a proper basis, is in good faith and the claimant holds at the time of filing; they must notify the manager/board at least 14 days before filing; a partner may also sue personally for special damage (Art. 29). A discharge (إبراء ذمة) voted by the partners/assembly does not bar these actions; except for forgery and fraud, the action is time-barred five years from the end of the fiscal year in which the harmful act occurred or three years from the end of the manager's/director's tenure, whichever is later (Art. 30). Business-judgement rule: a manager/director who decided or voted in good faith is deemed to have performed his duty if he had no interest in the matter, was informed to the extent appropriate in the circumstances, and rationally and firmly believed the decision served the company; the claimant bears the burden of proving otherwise (Art. 31). The court may order the company to bear a good-faith claimant's costs whatever the outcome (Art. 32). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 33-34 | A partner's or shareholder's personal creditor may ask the court to be paid from the debtor's share of distributed net profits and, on dissolution, from his share of the surplus (Art. 33). Subject to the Movable Assets Security Law, the creditor may also ask the court to sell enough of the debtor's interests (other partners have a statutory right to redeem them) or shares; in an unlisted JSC or SJSC the other shareholders have priority to buy within 15 days of the offer if the articles so provide (Art. 34). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 35-38, 42-46, 50 | General partnership (شركة التضامن): two or more natural or legal persons personally and jointly liable with all their assets for the company's debts; each partner is a merchant (Art. 35). Managed by the partners or by one or more managers (partners or not) appointed in the articles or a separate contract; multiple managers may each act alone unless otherwise provided, with objections resolved by majority of managers, then by the partners; the manager conducts all business within the objects and represents the company, and the company is bound by his acts within its objects unless the counterparty acted in bad faith (Art. 37). Partners' decisions pass by numerical majority; amendment of the articles requires unanimity unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 38). Removal of a partner-manager named in the articles requires unanimity of the other partners; other managers by numerical majority; court removal always possible; removal does not dissolve the company unless the articles say so (Art. 42); a manager may resign on 60 days' written notice (Art. 43). Interests cannot be represented by negotiable instruments; assignment requires compliance with the articles' restrictions or the consent of the other partners, is void otherwise, and must be registered and published (Art. 44). An incoming partner is liable for prior debts unless exempted by unanimous agreement registered with the Register; a withdrawing or expelled partner remains liable for debts before registration of his exit; an assignor is released unless creditors object within 30 days of notice (Art. 45). A partner may withdraw on 60 days' notice unless the articles provide otherwise; expulsion follows the articles or a court order on application of a numerical majority; the court may dissolve where continuation is impossible (Art. 46). Death, incapacity, insolvency, expulsion or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the partnership unless the articles so provide; if only one partner remains he has 90 days to admit another partner or transform, failing which the company is dissolved by operation of law (Art. 50). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 51, 53, 55-56 | Limited partnership (شركة التوصية البسيطة): at least one general partner with unlimited joint liability and at least one limited partner liable only to the extent of his contribution and who is not a merchant; general-partnership rules apply to general partners and, absent special provision, to the company (Art. 51). A limited partner may inspect twice a year but may not take part in external management, even under a power of attorney, on pain of unlimited liability for the resulting obligations; internal management participation is allowed if the articles so provide, unless it leads third parties to believe he is a general partner (Art. 53). Unless the articles provide otherwise, amendments require unanimity of general partners plus the majority in capital of limited partners; other decisions by numerical majority of general partners; a limited partner may not seek dissolution or vote on appointment/removal of the manager (Art. 55). A limited partner may assign to other partners freely; to third parties only with consent of all general partners and the majority in capital of limited partners, unless the articles provide otherwise; a general partner assigns under the same rule; new partners are admitted with the consent of all general partners (Art. 56). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 58-60 | JSC (شركة المساهمة): formed by one or more persons, capital divided into tradeable shares, the company alone liable for its debts, shareholders liable only to pay up their shares (Art. 58). Issued capital must be at least SAR 500,000 and at least one quarter must be paid at incorporation (Art. 59). The company has issued capital (subscribed shares) and may have authorised capital fixed in the articles; the board may increase issued capital within the authorised capital by board resolution provided the issued capital is fully paid (Art. 60). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 61-66; Regs Art. 3 | The JSC articles of association must state at least: name, head office, objects, authorised (if any), issued and paid-up capital, number, types and classes of shares with nominal value and rights, term (if any), management and number of directors, fiscal year, and other agreed terms; the application must attach founders' names/addresses/nationalities, a statement of incorporation expenses, the founders' declaration of subscription to all issued shares and amounts paid, a bank certificate of deposit of the paid amount with a licensed Saudi bank, the founders' decision appointing the first board (names, nationalities, addresses, dates of birth) and, where required, the first auditor, a compliance declaration, and an accredited valuer's report on any in-kind contributions with the other founders' acceptance (Art. 61). Shares not taken by founders must be offered under the Capital Market Law (Art. 62); the paid amount is deposited in the name of the company under incorporation and may be used only by the board after registration; if registration fails subscribers recover their money and founders are jointly liable (Art. 64). Once registered the company is validly incorporated and no nullity action lies for breach of the Law or the articles (Art. 65). In-kind contributions at incorporation or capital increase must be valued by one or more accredited valuers; the report is put to the founders or the extraordinary assembly; contributors may not vote on it; a reduction of the agreed consideration needs the contributors' consent; the Regulations cap the time between report and issue (Art. 66) — Regs Art. 3: six months for JSC, SJSC and LLC. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 67-70; Regs Arts. 14-15, 22 | A JSC is managed by a board of at least three members; any shareholder may nominate himself or others (Art. 67). Directors are elected by the ordinary general assembly, must be natural persons; the Regulations fix the voting method; the articles may set a board-formation method within Regulation limits; the articles fix the term, not exceeding four years, with re-election unless the articles provide otherwise; the ordinary assembly may remove all or some directors even if the articles provide otherwise, and must then elect replacements (Art. 68). The board must call the ordinary assembly in good time before its term ends; if no election takes place the outgoing board continues for the period set by the Regulations; a director resigns by written notice to the chairman (the chairman to the other members and the secretary), effective on the date stated; casual vacancies may be filled by the board on a temporary basis, notified to the Commercial Register (and the CMA if listed) within 15 days and put to the next ordinary assembly, the appointee completing his predecessor's term; if numbers fall below the quorum minimum the remaining members must call the assembly within 60 days; failing an election any interested person may ask the court to appoint supervisors to convene an assembly within 90 days or to dissolve the company (Art. 69). The assembly, on the board's recommendation, may end the membership of a director absent from three consecutive or five separate meetings without an excuse accepted by the board (Art. 70). Regs Art. 14: election is by cumulative voting unless the articles provide for ordinary voting; the articles may give a shareholder above a stated threshold the right to reserve board seats, in which case he does not vote for the other seats. Regs Art. 15: an expired board continues at most 90 days; a resigning whole board continues at most 120 days. Regs Art. 22: a director removed for absence forfeits remuneration after his last meeting attended and must repay amounts received for that period; remuneration based on incorrect or misleading information must be repaid. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 71-72 | A director must inform the board immediately of any direct or indirect interest in the company's dealings and contracts; the disclosure is recorded in the minutes; he may not vote on the matter in the board or the assemblies; the board reports such dealings to the assembly with a special auditor's report; failure to disclose entitles the company or any interested person to seek annulment or disgorgement; liability for damage falls on the interested director and on directors who were negligent or where the dealing was unfair; dissenters recorded in the minutes are exempt (Art. 71). A JSC may not lend to a director in any form or guarantee or secure his loans with third parties, nor to his relatives; such contracts are void and the company may claim damages; exceptions for banks and finance companies in the ordinary course, and for employee incentive programmes approved under the articles or by the assembly; the competent authority may restrict loans or guarantees to shareholders (Art. 72). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 73-79 | Shareholders supervise the board through the assembly and may not interfere in board or executive management unless they sit on the board or work in management (Art. 73). The board may borrow for any term, sell or mortgage the company's assets or business, and release debtors, unless the articles or the assembly restrict it (Art. 74); sale of assets exceeding 50% of total assets, in one or several transactions within the preceding 12 months, needs assembly approval, the approval attaching to the transaction that crosses the threshold (Art. 75). The board has the widest powers within the objects except matters reserved to the assembly by the Law or the articles, may delegate specific acts to members or third parties, and the company is bound by the board's acts even outside its powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew the act was outside them (Art. 77). At its first meeting the board appoints a chairman from among its members and may appoint a managing director or a CEO from among them; the articles allocate their powers, failing which the board does; a listed JSC must appoint a vice-chairman; the board appoints a CEO and a secretary (members or not) and sets their powers and pay if the articles are silent; the board may relieve any of them of office without ending board membership (Art. 78). The chairman represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties; the articles may give representation to the managing director or CEO; any of them may delegate representation; the chairman may delegate some powers in writing to other members or third parties unless the articles provide otherwise; the vice-chairman deputises (Art. 79). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 76; Regs Art. 21 | The articles set the method of directors' remuneration (fixed sum, attendance allowance, benefits in kind, a percentage of net profits, or a combination) and may cap it; the ordinary assembly fixes the amount, which must be fair, motivating and proportionate to performance; the board's annual report must itemise everything each director received (remuneration, allowances, expenses, executive or consulting pay) and attendance at each meeting. Regs Art. 21: remuneration must be proportionate to duties, activity, company size and experience, may differ between members, must not for independent directors be a percentage of profits or otherwise tied to profitability, and the policy and amounts must be disclosed in the annual report. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 80-81 | The JSC board meets at least four times a year on the chairman's call under the articles (the competent authority may vary the number); the chairman must call a meeting when any member so requests in writing. Quorum is at least half the members present in person or by proxy, unless the articles require more. Resolutions pass by majority of those present in person or by proxy, the chair of the meeting having a casting vote unless the articles provide otherwise. The board fixes the venue and may meet by modern technology (Art. 80). A director may not appoint a proxy to attend or vote, except that the articles may allow him to appoint another director, who may hold only one proxy; a board resolution takes effect on issue unless it specifies another time or conditions (Art. 81). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 82 | The board may pass resolutions on urgent matters by circulation to all members, unless a member requests in writing that the board meet to deliberate. Such resolutions pass with the approval of a majority of the board's members (not merely of those responding), unless the articles require a higher proportion or number, and must be tabled at the next board meeting for recording in that meeting's minutes. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 83 | Board deliberations and resolutions are recorded in minutes prepared by the secretary and signed by the chair of the meeting, the directors present and the secretary; minutes are entered in a special register signed by the chairman and the secretary; modern technology may be used for signature, recording of deliberations and keeping the minutes. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 84; Regs Arts. 24-30 | The general assembly is chaired by the chairman, the vice-chairman in his absence, or a board delegate, failing which a person chosen by the shareholders by vote; every shareholder may attend regardless of the articles and may appoint a proxy who is not a director; meetings, deliberation and voting may be held by modern technology (Art. 84). Regs Art. 24: electronic participation requires live audio-visual transmission and real-time ability to hear, follow, speak and vote; the company may offer electronic voting on agenda items to shareholders who do not attend, opening after the notice and for at least three days before the meeting, closing per item as each is voted; electronic meetings do not remove the right to attend in person at the venue in the notice. Regs Art. 25: the board must verify the identity and entitlement of electronic participants and voters. Regs Art. 26: electronic participants and e-voters count towards quorum and majorities. Regs Arts. 27-30: proxies in writing to a shareholder or non-shareholder who is not a director, on the form annexed (Form 1) or under a notarised power of attorney expressly covering assembly attendance and voting; a proxy is valid for the next assembly and any adjournment; a resident natural person or a Saudi-incorporated legal person must have the signature authenticated by the chamber of commerce, a licensed bank or CMA-licensed person where the principal holds an account, a notary or licensed notarising person, or another body approved by the Ministry; non-residents and foreign entities authenticate through a Saudi mission abroad, except where an international convention exempts; a shareholder may not both attend and appoint a proxy; a copy of the proxy must reach the company at least two days before the meeting (the company may accept later proxies received before registration closes). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 85-88 | The extraordinary general assembly (EGA) amends the articles of association, except that it may not deprive a shareholder of, or modify, his fundamental rights (dividends, liquidation surplus, attending and voting, disposing of shares under the Law, inspection, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging assembly decisions), and amendments increasing shareholders' financial burdens require all shareholders' consent; the EGA also decides continuation or dissolution and approves buybacks (Art. 85); the EGA may decide ordinary matters under ordinary rules (Art. 86). The ordinary general assembly (OGA) handles everything else, in particular election and removal of directors, appointment, fees, reappointment and removal of the auditor, board and auditor reports, financial statements, dividend proposals, and reserves (Art. 87). The annual OGA must be held at least once within six months after fiscal year end, with a mandatory agenda: board report, financial statements, auditor's report, dividend proposal; an EGA held within the six months with that agenda satisfies the requirement (Art. 88). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 90-91; Regs Art. 23 | General and special assemblies are convened by the board under the articles; the board must call the OGA within 30 days of a request by the auditor or by shareholders holding at least 10% of voting shares, and the auditor may convene it himself if the board fails to do so within 30 days of his request; the request must state the items to be voted on. The competent authority may itself call the OGA where the six-month period passed without a meeting, where violations of the Law or the articles or management defects (including a board below quorum) appear, or where the board ignored a valid request; it may take the steps needed to hold the meeting and chair it. (Art. 90). Notice of an assembly (Art. 91) must be given at least 21 days before the meeting under the Regulations: by registered letters to the addresses in the shareholders' register or by announcement through modern technology; a copy of the notice and agenda goes to the Commercial Register (and to the CMA if listed) on the date of the notice. The notice must state who may attend and the right to appoint a non-director proxy, the right to discuss and question and how to vote; place, date and time; the type of assembly; and the agenda with the items to be voted on. In an unlisted JSC, shareholders representing all voting shares may hold an assembly without observing the notice formalities and periods. Regs Art. 23: electronic notice must allow proof of content, date and time of sending and identification of sender and recipient. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 92 | OGA quorum: shareholders representing at least one quarter of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding one half. If not met, a second meeting is called in the same manner within the 30 days following the first date; it may be held one hour after the first meeting's slot if the articles allow and the first notice said so; the second meeting is valid whatever the number of voting shares represented. OGA resolutions pass by majority of the voting rights represented at the meeting. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 93 | EGA quorum: shareholders representing at least half of voting shares, or a higher proportion in the articles not exceeding two thirds. If not met, a second meeting (one hour later if the notice said so) is valid with at least one quarter of voting shares; if still not met, a third meeting is valid whatever the number represented. EGA resolutions pass by two thirds of the voting rights represented, except that resolutions to increase or reduce capital, extend the company's term, dissolve before the end of its term, merge with another company or divide into two or more companies require three quarters of the voting rights represented. The board must register with the Commercial Register the EGA resolutions specified by the Regulations within 15 days of their issue. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 94-98 | Assembly resolutions take effect on issue unless the Law, the articles or the resolution provide otherwise (Art. 94). The articles fix the voting method; directors may not vote on resolutions concerning dealings in which they have a direct or indirect interest or a conflict (Art. 95). The board must consider items shareholders wish to add; holders of 10% of voting shares may add items when the agenda is prepared; each subject must be a separate item; substantially different matters may not be bundled, and related-party dealings may not be bundled into one item; every shareholder may discuss and question the board and the auditor, any contrary articles clause is void, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 96). Minutes must record the number of shareholders present in person or by proxy, the shares and votes they hold, the resolutions adopted with votes for and against, and an adequate summary of the discussion; minutes are entered regularly after each meeting in a special register signed by the chair of the assembly, its secretary and the vote collectors; the competent authority may regulate minutes, secretaries and vote collectors (Art. 97). Where a JSC has a single shareholder (at incorporation or later), he exercises the assemblies' powers; his decisions are issued in writing without convening an assembly and are entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 98). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 99, 102, 170 | Any JSC shareholder may ask the court to annul an assembly resolution contrary to the Law or the articles, if he objected at the meeting or was absent with an acceptable excuse; the action is not heard after 90 days from the resolution; the claimant must remain a shareholder throughout (Art. 99). Shareholders holding at least 5% of capital may ask the court to order an inspection of the company where the conduct of the directors or the auditor raises suspicion; the court may order inspection at the applicant's expense (with security if the company requests), and if the complaint is proved may order precautionary measures, convene the assembly, remove directors and the auditor, and appoint supervisors to convene an assembly for a new board (Art. 102). The same 90-day annulment action lies for LLC partners, but only those who objected in writing or could not object after learning of the resolution (Art. 170). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 100-101 | In an unlisted JSC the articles may allow the chairman to propose a general-assembly resolution by circulation to the shareholders without a meeting, unless any shareholder requests a meeting in writing; election and removal of directors, appointment and removal of the auditor, and review and discussion of the financial statements always require a meeting; the company must send the proposed resolution with the relevant documents to all shareholders, stating how to approve and the date by which the resolution is to be issued (Art. 100). Circulated resolutions pass, for OGA matters, with the approval of shareholders holding a majority of voting rights and, for EGA matters, with at least 75% of voting rights, unless the articles require more; they are recorded in minutes and entered in the Art. 97 register (Art. 101). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 103-112; Regs Arts. 51-55 | JSC shares are registered (nominal) and indivisible as against the company; co-owners appoint one representative and are jointly liable; the articles fix the nominal value, equal within a type or class; shares may be split or consolidated under the competent authority's rules; an unlisted JSC must issue paper or electronic share certificates (Art. 103). Subscription implies acceptance of the articles and assembly resolutions (Art. 104). Shares are issued against cash or in kind; at least one quarter of the nominal value of cash shares must be paid, the certificate showing the amount paid, and the balance within five years of issue; in-kind shares are issued only after full payment and transfer of the assets (Art. 105). Shares may not be issued below nominal value; a premium is allowed if the articles or the EGA permit, credited to a separate equity item used per the Regulations (Art. 106). Attached rights include disposal, attendance and voting, dividends, electing directors, inspection of records without breaching confidentiality, supervising the board, suing directors, challenging resolutions and a share of assets on liquidation, subject to the Law and the articles (Art. 107). Share types: ordinary, preferred and redeemable, with classes carrying different rights, privileges or restrictions under the articles and the Regulations (Art. 108); conversion between types/classes needs an articles provision and EGA approval (or automatic conversion terms), and conversion into redeemable shares needs all shareholders (Art. 109); modification of class rights requires a special assembly of the affected class under EGA rules plus EGA approval; new shares ranking ahead of preferred or redeemable shares need the affected class's consent (Arts. 89, 110). The CMA may restrict transfers in companies seeking listing; the articles may restrict share transfers, including redemption rights, but may not impose an absolute ban (Art. 111). An unlisted JSC keeps a shareholders' register (names, nationalities, details, residence, occupations, number, numbers and paid amount of shares), which may be outsourced but must be kept in the Kingdom; the Commercial Register must be given the register data and every change within 15 days of registration or of the change (Art. 112). Regs Art. 51: preferred or redeemable shares (and classes of them) may be issued only if the articles allow, issued capital is fully paid, the affected class's special assembly and the EGA approve, the proposal with the rights, privileges or restrictions accompanies the EGA notice and is reflected in the articles, the preferred and redeemable shares together never exceed 50% of capital, and they are fully paid on subscription; the EGA resolution states the redemption terms including the company's option, and redeemed shares are cancelled with a capital reduction. Regs Art. 52: conversion between types/classes needs an articles clause, must be into a type in Art. 108, needs the affected class's special assembly and the EGA, keeps preferred and redeemable shares within 50% of capital, and is supported by a board statement on the conversion ratio and its effect. Regs Art. 53: preferred and redeemable shares carry preferential rights but no vote at general assemblies; if a fixed or enhanced dividend is not paid for three consecutive years while profits suffice, their special assembly may resolve that they attend and vote (one vote per share) until arrears are paid. Regs Art. 54: share splits or consolidations need an articles clause, a board proposal sent to shareholders 21 days before the EGA, and EGA approval. Regs Art. 55: a shareholder in default has 30 days from the board's demand to pay; the auction is notified to the Ministry, advertised 15 to 30 days ahead in a daily newspaper or as the Ministry directs, and minuted. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 113, 181 | With the approval of holders of at least 90% of voting shares (JSC) or 90% of capital (LLC), the articles may provide (a) drag-along: the majority may compel the minority to accept a bona fide buyer's offer for all shares/interests at the same price, terms and conditions as the majority's; (b) tag-along: the minority may compel the majority to procure the sale of the minority's shares/interests when the majority sell, at the same price, terms and conditions. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 114-116, 180; Regs Arts. 34-39, 43-50, 66-69 | A JSC may buy back or take a pledge over its own shares if the articles allow; treasury shares carry no vote (Art. 114(1)); shares may be pledged, the pledgee collecting dividends and exercising share rights unless the pledge agreement provides otherwise, but never attending or voting at assemblies (Art. 114(2)). A shareholder who defaults on a call may, after notice, have his shares sold at public auction (or on the Exchange), the articles may give other shareholders priority; rights are suspended until sale or payment; the company recovers its dues and pays over the balance (Art. 115). The company may not demand more than the shareholder committed on issue, even if the articles say otherwise (Art. 116). An LLC may buy or take a pledge over its own interests if the articles allow; purchased interests carry no vote; a pledgee of interests collects dividends unless agreed otherwise (Art. 180). Regs Arts. 34-39: an unlisted JSC may buy its ordinary or preferred shares if the articles allow, only to reduce capital or to hold ordinary shares as treasury shares; treasury shares may never exceed 10% of the class, must be fully paid, and their cost may not exceed retained earnings; the buyback is approved by the EGA with a maximum number and purposes and a board authority to complete within 12 months, announced immediately; treasury shares may be used only to satisfy convertible debt/sukuk, as consideration for acquiring shares, interests or assets, for an employee share programme, for cancellation, or for another Ministry-approved purpose; shareholders must receive adequate information and a fair opportunity to tender; purchased preferred shares are cancelled; the EGA may fix a holding period. Regs Arts. 43-45: treasury shares are sold by board resolution if the articles allow and consistently with the EGA resolution, existing shareholders having pro rata priority on a cash sale; the company may take a pledge over its own shares only if the articles allow, as security for a debt owed to it, in the interest of the company and shareholders as the board judges, with general-assembly approval (which may be given in advance for several transactions). Regs Arts. 46-50: a shareholder may pledge his shares after any required regulatory approvals; a pledge is created by a written contract stating the parties and their identity numbers and addresses, the number, value and numbers of the pledged shares and the issuer's Commercial Register number, the secured debt and any cap, the debtor if different, the date, release terms and other terms; the pledgee and pledgor each file an application with the chairman (or the outsourced registrar) on Forms 2 and 3 with authenticated signatures (chamber of commerce, licensed bank or CMA-licensed person, notary or licensed notarising person, or a Saudi mission abroad for non-residents, subject to treaty exemptions); the pledge is entered in the shareholders' register and annotated on the certificates; release is by expiry or by the pledgee's Form 4; the pledge is effective against third parties only once entered in the shareholders' register; Regs Art. 66: an LLC buyback needs an articles clause, partners' approval with waiver of redemption rights and a manager's authority to complete within 30 days; Regs Art. 67: an LLC may take a pledge over its own interests only if the articles allow, as security for a debt to the company, in the interest of the company and partners, with partners' approval at the amendment quorum; Regs Arts. 68-69: a partner's pledge of interests requires the necessary regulatory approvals and a written contract stating the parties, the number and value of pledged interests and the Commercial Register number, the secured debt, the debtor, date, release terms and other agreed terms. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 121-122, 125; Regs Arts. 31-32 | At the end of each fiscal year the board prepares the financial statements and a report on the company's activity and financial position with the proposed dividend, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual OGA; they are signed by the chairman, the CEO and the CFO (if any) and copies are kept at the head office for shareholders (Art. 121). The chairman must provide shareholders with the signed statements, board report and auditor's report at least 21 days before the annual OGA unless published by modern technology, and deposit them as the Regulations specify (Art. 122). The assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; the resolution states the entitlement (record) date and the distribution date; dividends belong to holders registered at the end of the record date; the Regulations cap the time for the board to implement (Art. 125). Regs Art. 31: the chairman sends the board report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual OGA. Regs Art. 32: dividends must be paid to registered shareholders within 15 days of the entitlement date. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 126-131; Regs Arts. 40-42, 57-61 | Capital is increased by new shares for cash or in kind; by shares for ascertained and due debts with the creditors' consent, at a value set by the EGA after an expert or accredited valuer's opinion and a board statement with an auditor's report; by capitalising reserves (bonus shares pro rata); or by shares for debt instruments or sukuk (Art. 126). The EGA may increase issued or authorised capital only if issued capital is fully paid (except unpaid shares from pending conversions); it may allocate new shares to employees of the company and its subsidiaries, without pre-emption for shareholders; new shares have the same nominal value as existing shares of the type/class (Art. 127). Shareholders at the date of the EGA (or board) resolution have pre-emptive rights over new cash shares in proportion to their type/class and are notified by registered letter or technology of the terms and subscription period (Art. 128); the EGA may suspend pre-emption or grant it to non-shareholders if the articles allow (Art. 129); pre-emption rights may be sold or waived under the Regulations (Art. 130); allocation is pro rata to rights, then to over-subscribers, then to third parties unless the EGA or the Capital Market Law provide otherwise (Art. 131). Regs Art. 40: a company holding treasury shares (other than for an employee programme), or with an unrevoked EGA buyback approval, may not increase issued capital by a rights issue; Regs Art. 41: treasury shares participate in a capitalisation issue. Regs Art. 42: an employee share programme using treasury shares needs an articles clause, EGA approval of the programme (the board may be delegated its terms and the allocation price), exclusion of non-executive directors, and abstention of executive directors from board votes on it. Regs Arts. 57-61: pre-emption rights accrue to shareholders registered at the end of the day of the EGA (or board) resolution, in proportion to their shares and by type/class, but not for employee-allocated shares or where the EGA has suspended or reallocated the right; each right entitles the holder to subscribe for one new share at the price fixed in the resolution; subject to the articles, a registered shareholder may sell or assign rights to anyone, shareholder or not, at an agreed price during the subscription period, which may not be shorter than 15 days; the seller must notify the company in writing with the buyer's name, nationality, residence, occupation and number of rights, and the buyer steps into the subscription right; unexercised rights lapse at the end of the last subscription day. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 132 | If a JSC's losses reach half of its issued capital, the board must within 60 days of learning of it disclose the fact and its recommendations, and call the EGA to meet within 180 days of that date to decide whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 133-137 | Capital is reduced by cancelling shares, reducing nominal value by the amount of losses, reducing nominal value by refund or release of unpaid amounts, or by buying and cancelling shares (Art. 133). The EGA may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses (only then below the SAR 500,000 minimum), after a board statement of reasons, liabilities and the effect on them with an auditor's report is read at the assembly (or circulated where the resolution is by circulation) (Art. 134). Where the reduction is for surplus capital, creditors must be invited to object at least 45 days before the EGA, with a statement of capital before and after and the effective date; an objecting creditor who lodges his documents in time must be paid if due or given adequate security if not, and may apply to the court before the EGA, which may order payment, security or postponement; the reduction is not effective against a timely-objecting creditor until he is paid or secured (Art. 135). Shareholders of the same type/class are treated equally (Art. 136); reduction by buyback requires an invitation to all shareholders, pro rata scaling and, for unlisted companies, purchase at fair value (Art. 137). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 138-141 | The SJSC (شركة المساهمة المبسطة) is governed by the JSC rules except Arts. 61, 63, 67-71, 74-88, 90-94, 95(1), 96-98, 100, 101, 111(2), 121 and 122; shareholders organise the structure and operation in the articles; the shareholders replace the OGA and EGA and may allocate those powers in the articles; the president, manager or board exercises the JSC board's powers (Art. 138). The articles fix issued and paid-up capital and may provide authorised capital; the JSC minimum capital does not apply (Art. 139). Articles must cover name, head office, objects, capital, shares (types/classes/rights), term, management, share assignment, shareholders' meetings and their quorum, shareholders' decisions and their quorum, fiscal year; attachments mirror the JSC list including a bank deposit certificate and the appointment of the president/manager/board (Art. 140). In-kind contributions not exceeding half the capital need no accredited valuer unless the founders/shareholders decide otherwise; above half, an accredited valuer's report is required, contributors do not vote, and a reduction of the agreed value needs their consent; without a proper valuation the founders/shareholders are personally liable to third parties for the fairness of the valuation and for paying the difference in cash, for five years from registration or the increase (Art. 141). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 142-144 | The articles fix how the SJSC is managed — a president, one or more managers, a board or otherwise — and how the managers are appointed and removed, their powers and working method; if silent, the shareholders decide; the president/manager/board has the widest powers except matters reserved to shareholders, may delegate specific acts; the president, manager or chairman represents the company and may delegate representation if the articles allow; the company is bound even by acts outside the manager's powers unless the counterparty acted in bad faith or knew (Art. 142). JSC board liability rules apply to the president, manager or board (Art. 143), as does the Art. 72 prohibition on loans to them (Art. 144). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 145-149 | The SJSC articles list the matters reserved to shareholders, but capital increase or reduction, transformation, merger, division, dissolution, auditor appointment, discussion of the financial statements, dividends and amendment of the articles must always be decided by the shareholders; the articles set the quorum for meetings and decisions, may set different quorums for different matters, and must specify the matters requiring unanimity (Art. 145). Meetings are called by the president/manager/board under the articles, and may be requested by the auditor or by holders of 10% of voting shares; notice to all shareholders at least 5 days before, with place, date, time and agenda (and optionally a second-meeting date); by registered letter or technology unless the articles provide otherwise; for reserved matters each shareholder may obtain the relevant documents during the 5 days before the meeting (or longer per the articles); meetings may be held at the head office, elsewhere or by technology; holders of all voting shares may meet without notice formalities (Art. 146). Financial statements and the activity report (with dividend proposal) plus the auditor's report are presented to shareholders within six months of fiscal year end and deposited per the Regulations (Art. 147). Deliberations and decisions, including those by circulation, are recorded in minutes in a special register signed by the president/manager/board; technology may be used; the decisions specified by the Regulations must be registered with the Commercial Register within 15 days (Art. 148). The articles may allow decisions by circulation: the proposed decision and documents are sent to all shareholders (registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide otherwise) stating how to approve and the decision date; the articles fix the quorum for circulated decisions (Art. 149). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 150-154 | A single-shareholder SJSC: the owner's liability is limited to the capital allocated; he exercises the shareholders' powers by written decisions entered in a special register (Art. 150). The articles may restrict share disposals by (a) a lock-up of up to 10 years from issue, extendable by unanimity, and (b) a requirement of the company's or shareholders' consent before disposal; a disposal in breach is void (Art. 151). The articles may set conditions on which a shareholder can be compelled to transfer his shares, at fair value unless the articles provide otherwise, with non-financial rights suspended until transfer (Art. 152). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between shareholders or between the company and its president/manager/directors to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 153). Inserting or amending Arts. 151-153 provisions in the articles requires unanimity (Art. 154). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 156-159, 174, 183-184 | LLC (الشركة ذات المسؤولية المحدودة): formed by one or more natural or legal persons; its estate is separate from the partners'; only the company is liable for its debts; a partner or sole owner is liable only to the extent of his interest in the capital (Art. 156). Single-owner LLC: the owner exercises the powers of the manager, board of managers and general assembly by written decisions entered in a special register; he may appoint one or more managers who represent the company and answer to him; a single-owner LLC has articles of association and references to articles of incorporation are read accordingly (Art. 157). Articles of incorporation must state: partners' names and details, name, head office, objects, capital and its division, the partners' declaration that the interests are paid, term (if any), management, assignment of interests, the means of notifying partners, partners' decisions, profit/loss distribution, fiscal year, dissolution, and other agreed terms; attachments: a compliance declaration and an accredited valuer's report on in-kind contributions (Art. 158), valued under Art. 141 (Art. 159). The partners fix the capital in the articles (no statutory minimum); it is divided into interests of equal value, indivisible and non-tradeable; co-owned interests must have a single representative or the company may suspend rights and ultimately sell the interest (Art. 174). A fixed-term LLC may be extended before expiry by partners holding half the interests (or more if the articles say so); if it continues trading without a resolution it is extended for a like term; a dissenting partner may exit at a value fixed under Art. 178, the extension taking effect only after his interests are sold and paid; third parties with an interest may object (Art. 183). Death, incapacity, insolvency, bankruptcy proceedings or withdrawal of a partner does not dissolve the LLC unless the articles so provide (Art. 184). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 160-162 | An LLC is managed by one or more managers, partners or not, appointed by the partners in the articles or in a separate contract, for a fixed or indefinite term; the partners may constitute a board of managers where there are several (Art. 160). The articles or a partners' decision fix the management method and the majority for managers' decisions (Art. 161). The manager represents the company before courts, arbitral tribunals and third parties and may delegate specific acts; a decision appointing, replacing or restricting the powers of a manager is effective against third parties only after registration with the Commercial Register; the company is bound by the manager's acts within its objects (Art. 162). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 163-164; Regs Art. 63 | If a sole manager's office falls vacant, the partners must appoint a new manager within 15 days of learning of it; the auditor or any partner may call the general assembly for that purpose (Art. 163). The partners may remove the manager(s), whether appointed in the articles or a separate contract, and must appoint successors; a manager who is a partner may not vote on his own removal; partners holding at least one quarter of capital may petition the court for removal (Art. 164). Regs Art. 63: subject to the articles, removal of an LLC manager, even a partner named in the articles, is decided at the quorum the Law sets for partners' decisions (Art. 166: more than half of capital). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 165 | An LLC has a general assembly of all partners, convened by the manager(s) as the articles provide, at least once a year within six months after fiscal year end; it may be called at any time at the request of the managers, the auditor, or partners holding at least 10% of capital; notice goes to all partners by registered letter, modern technology or any other means in the articles at least 21 days before the meeting; partners representing all interests may meet without observing notice formalities and periods; deliberations, decisions and decisions by circulation are recorded in minutes entered in a special register kept for the purpose, and technology may be used to record them; meetings and voting may be held by technology. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 166, 171 | Partners' decisions are taken in the general assembly but may be taken by circulation without a meeting: the manager sends each partner the proposed decisions and documents for a written vote, by registered letter, personal delivery, e-mail or technology unless the articles provide another means. In all cases a decision is valid only if approved by partners holding more than half of the capital, unless the articles require a larger majority; if that majority is not reached in the first deliberation or consultation, the partners must be called to a meeting at which decisions pass by a majority of the interests represented, whatever their proportion of capital, unless the articles provide otherwise (Art. 166). Each partner takes part in deliberations and voting with votes equal to his interests, and no contrary agreement is allowed; a partner may appoint another partner in writing as proxy unless the articles provide otherwise, and the articles may allow non-partner proxies; a non-manager partner may advise the manager and may inspect the company's business, records and documents at the head office twice per fiscal year, the company complying within 15 days; contrary clauses are void; information obtained is confidential (Art. 171). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 167-169; Regs Art. 65 | The manager prepares for each fiscal year the financial statements, an activity and financial-position report and dividend proposals, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual assembly; he provides partners with the statements, report and auditor's report (by technology or as the articles provide) at least 21 days before the annual assembly and deposits them as the Regulations specify (Art. 167). The annual agenda must include the manager's report, the financial statements, the auditor's report and the dividend proposal (Art. 168). The assembly may not deliberate outside the agenda unless facts emerge at the meeting; a partner may require an item to be added, and may appeal to the assembly if refused; every partner may discuss and question, the manager must answer, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 169). Regs Art. 65: the manager sends the activity report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual assembly. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 172-173; Regs Art. 64 | The articles of incorporation, including capital increases and reductions, may be amended with the approval of partners holding at least three quarters of the capital, unless the articles require more; on a cash increase by new interests each partner has pre-emption pro rata to his holding under the Regulations; an increase by raising the nominal value of interests, or suspension of pre-emption, requires unanimity (Art. 172). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between partners or between the company and its managers to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 173). Regs Art. 64: a partner who takes less than his entitlement leaves the balance to the other partners pro rata; the partners file a draft amendment with a declaration that the new interests are paid, and the increase is effective on registration and publication with the Commercial Register. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 175-177 | Interests carry equal rights to net profits and liquidation surplus unless the articles provide otherwise; the assembly fixes the dividend after reserves; entitlement and payment dates are stated in the resolution (Art. 175). The assembly may reduce capital where it exceeds needs or after losses; in the loss case a manager's statement of reasons, liabilities and effect with an auditor's report is read (or circulated); where the reduction returns surplus capital, every manager must sign a solvency statement confirming, on examination, that nothing would render the company unable to pay its debts and that it can pay debts falling due within the next 12 months, delivered to partners at least 15 days before the decision; the partners file a draft amendment with the Register within 15 days of the decision, attaching the statements, and the reduction is effective on registration and publication (Art. 176). The articles may provide for a reserve; the annual assembly may create reserves and allocate amounts for employees' social purposes (Art. 177). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Art. 178 | A partner may assign his interest to another partner on the terms in the articles. To assign to a non-partner, with or without consideration, the partner must notify the other partners through the manager of the assignee's or buyer's name and the terms; the manager notifies the partners immediately; each partner may within 30 days of the manager's notification of the agreed price demand to redeem the interest and pay its value, or have the company buy it; if several partners redeem, the interest is divided pro rata to their capital; if the value is disputed it is fixed by an accredited valuer's fair-value report at the cost of the redeeming partner or the company; if the period expires without redemption, payment or company purchase, the partner may assign to the third party. The articles may provide other notification procedures, another valuation method, or a longer period for redemption, payment or company purchase. The redemption right does not apply to transfers by inheritance, will or court judgment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Art. 182 | If an LLC's losses reach half of its capital, the manager must call the general assembly of partners to meet within 60 days of learning that losses reached that level, to consider whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Arts. 185-186, 197-199, 216-218 (notes) | Non-profit companies: a public non-profit company must be a JSC and spend its profits on public-benefit purposes fixed with the National Center for Non-Profit Sector; a private non-profit company may be an LLC, JSC or SJSC spending profits on stated non-profit purposes; neither may offer shares to the public; otherwise the rules of the chosen form apply (Arts. 185-186). Professional companies: formed by one or more persons licensed to practise a liberal profession, alone or with others, in any of the five forms; the form's rules apply save as Part 8 provides; a partner is not a merchant by reason of his holding (Arts. 197-199); Regs Part 5 sets conditions for single-owner professional companies, multi-profession firms, participation of non-licensed persons and foreign professional firms (headings read). Holding companies: a JSC, SJSC or LLC that forms or holds subsidiaries; a company is a subsidiary where the holder has a majority of voting rights, alone controls appointment or removal of the manager or a majority of the board, controls a majority of votes by agreement with other holders, or is a subsidiary of a subsidiary; a subsidiary may not hold interests or shares in its parent (acquisitions to the contrary are void) and must dispose of pre-existing holdings within 12 months without voting them, with exceptions for CMA-licensed persons (Arts. 216-218). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 220-224 | A company may transform into another form by a resolution passed under the rules for amending its articles, after satisfying the incorporation, registration and publication requirements of the new form; transformation into an SJSC requires unanimity; sole proprietors may transfer their assets into any company form without being released from prior debts unless creditors expressly agree; a general partnership, limited partnership or LLC wholly owned by relatives (or including waqf/bequest interests) may transform into a JSC on the request of holders of more than half the capital (Art. 220). A private non-profit company may transform, spending surplus on its non-profit purposes and refunding exemptions; any company may become a non-profit company by unanimity (Art. 221; Regs Art. 86 requires proof and a special auditor's report). Partners or shareholders who object to the transformation may exit by written request within 15 days of the resolution, paid at the agreed value or an accredited valuer's fair value at the transformation date, unless the articles provide otherwise, with recourse to the court (Art. 222). Transformation creates no new legal person; the company keeps its rights and remains bound by prior obligations (Art. 223). General partners are not released from pre-transformation debts unless creditors expressly agree or fail to object within 30 days of notice by registered letter or technology (Art. 224). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 225-229; Regs Art. 87 | Merger is by absorption into an existing company or by amalgamation into a new company; a merger proposal must be approved by each party under its articles-amendment rules and must set out the terms, the nature and value of the consideration including the interests/shares allotted, and each party's ability to pay its debts; a company in liquidation may merge; the merger is valid only after valuation of each party's assets; consideration is interests or shares in the absorbing or new company; the competent authority may regulate cash for fractions, compensation of dissenters and voting by conflicted holders (Art. 225). The Regulations govern mergers into a 100% parent or between companies with identical owners (Art. 226): Regs Art. 87 — a wholly-owned subsidiary is merged by a resolution of the parent alone, with a managers'/board solvency statement for each party, without a merger proposal or asset valuation; sister companies with identical owners merge by resolutions of each, without asset valuation. Each party must announce the merger at least 30 days before the date set for the decision; creditors of the merged company may object by registered letter (or the means stated in the announcement) within 15 days of the announcement, and must be paid if due or adequately secured if not; an unpaid/unsecured objector may apply to the court no later than 10 days before the decision date, and the court may order payment or security, or suspend or postpone the merger before it takes effect, or award damages afterwards (Art. 227). The merger takes effect on registration of the merged company's data in the absorbing company's Commercial Register entry, or on registration of the new company (Art. 228); all rights, obligations, assets and contracts of the merged company pass to the absorbing/new company as successor (Art. 229). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Art. 230; Regs Art. 88 | Squeeze-out and sell-out in JSCs (subject to the Capital Market Law): a person, or persons acting in concert, who reach 90% or more of voting shares directly or indirectly, or unconditionally contract to buy that proportion, must disclose it to shareholders; any shareholder may within 90 days of the disclosure require the holder to make an offer for his shares (sell-out). The 90% holder may within 60 days apply to the competent authority for approval of a compulsory offer forcing the remaining shareholders to sell (squeeze-out); shareholders may contest the price in court within 60 days of the offer; only the competent authority may suspend a compulsory offer; settlement within 7 days after the objection period unless the court orders otherwise. Regs Art. 88: the disclosure goes through the board, which informs the other shareholders; a sell-out offer must be made in cash within 60 days of the request at not less than the highest price the holder paid in the preceding 12 months; the squeeze-out application to the Ministry must attach the offer and price, likewise not below the highest price paid in the preceding 12 months; after the objection period the purchaser completes through the board, which places the cash in a bank account for the affected shareholders and updates the register. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 231-234; Regs Arts. 89-92 | A company, even in liquidation, may divide into two or more companies of any form (Art. 231); the division resolution is passed under the articles-amendment rules and must state the number of partners/shareholders, their shares in the resulting and the divided company, and the allocation of rights, assets and liabilities (Art. 232). Resulting companies succeed the divided company to the extent allocated, but creditors of the divided company may claim against any of the resulting companies, which are jointly liable, unless creditors agree to the transfer of their claims to a specific resulting company (Art. 233). Regs Arts. 89-92: the division resolution requires the amendment quorum; owners receive interests/shares pro rata unless they agree otherwise; the manager/board prepares a proposal stating reasons, the assets and liabilities divided, an accredited valuer's fair-value report (not needed for a pro rata division), the shares to be received, and any creditor agreements; the proposal goes to owners at least 21 days before the vote; the division takes effect on registration of the amended articles of the divided company and of the resulting company. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 235-241 | Foreign companies carrying on activity in the Kingdom are subject to the Law (other than its incorporation provisions), without prejudice to treaties and other laws (Art. 235), and operate through a branch, representative office or other form under the Investment Law (Art. 236). Each branch/representative office must show its Saudi address and the foreign company's full name, address and head office on all papers (Art. 237). The branch registration application must state its fiscal year; a branch (not a representative office) must prepare financial statements for its Saudi activity under Saudi standards and deposit them with the auditor's report within six months of the fiscal year end per the Regulations; the branch manager may appoint the auditor under delegation from the foreign company (Art. 238). The branch/office is the foreign company's domicile for its Saudi activity and subject to all Saudi laws (Art. 239). If the foreign company operates before licensing and registration, or beyond its licence, the company and the persons who carried on the activity are jointly liable (Art. 240). A foreign company present to perform specific works for a limited period is registered temporarily and deregistered on completion after settling its rights and obligations, unless it regularises for continuation; the Ministry and the Ministry of Investment set the rules (Art. 241). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 242-245 | Before the partners, assembly or shareholders resolve to dissolve, the managers or directors must prepare a statement confirming they have examined the company's position and that its assets suffice to pay its debts by the end of the proposed liquidation and that the company is not distressed under the Bankruptcy Law; it is put to the owners within 30 days of preparation; if it shows insufficiency or distress, the owners may not resolve to dissolve, on pain of joint liability for remaining debts (Art. 242). General grounds of dissolution: expiry of term unless extended; agreement of the partners/shareholders to dissolve; a final judgment dissolving or annulling the company (Art. 243). On dissolution the company enters liquidation and keeps personality for that purpose; the owners must start liquidation; the Art. 242 statement must be prepared (or be no older than 30 days); an insolvent or distressed company must apply to the court to open Bankruptcy Law liquidation; liquidation in breach makes the owners/managers/directors jointly liable for remaining debts; a public non-profit company needs Ministry approval (Art. 244). Liquidation follows the articles or the owners' agreement, failing which the Law (Art. 245). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 246-251 | On dissolution the managers'/board's authority ends but they continue to manage and are treated as liquidators towards third parties until a liquidator is appointed; assemblies continue with powers not conflicting with the liquidator's; owners keep inspection rights (Art. 246). One or more liquidators (owners or not) are appointed; liquidation may not exceed three years, extendable only by court order (Art. 247). The liquidator is appointed by the owners under the articles-amendment rules within 60 days of dissolution, failing which by the court on application of any owner or interested person; where dissolution is by judgment, the court appoints; the court first requires the Art. 242 statement or equivalent evidence within 30 days and opens Bankruptcy Law proceedings if assets are insufficient; the appointment decision must fix powers, fees, restrictions and the liquidation period (Art. 248). The liquidator registers and publishes his appointment with the Commercial Register, without which it cannot be relied on against third parties (Art. 249); he is removed in the manner appointed, or by the court on an owner's or creditor's application, with a replacement named (Art. 250); multiple liquidators act jointly and unanimously unless authorised otherwise (Art. 251). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 252-259; Regs Art. 93 | The liquidator represents the company, converts assets to cash (auction or best-price sale), may sell assets en bloc or contribute them to another company only with the appointing body's authority, may not start new business except to complete existing business, binds the company within his powers, and his powers end with the liquidation or its period, whichever first (Art. 252). Managers/directors hand over records; within 90 days of starting he prepares an inventory of assets, rights and liabilities with an auditor's report (extendable by the appointing body); annually he prepares financial statements and a report, files copies with the Commercial Register and submits them to the owners (Art. 253). If assets prove insufficient he must immediately inform owners and creditors and apply to open Bankruptcy Law proceedings (Art. 254). He pays due debts by priority, reserves for deferred or disputed debts (liquidation debts rank first), returns capital and distributes the surplus per the articles or pro rata (Art. 255). On completion he files a detailed financial report; liquidation ends on the appointing body's approval; he registers and publishes the end of liquidation, effective against third parties only on deregistration (Art. 257). He is liable for exceeding his powers or for errors (Art. 258), with a five-year limitation from deregistration except for forgery and fraud (Art. 259). Regs Art. 93: a company that never carried on any activity may be dissolved by a unanimous owners' resolution submitted to the Ministry containing a declaration that it never traded, has no assets, debts (present, future or disputed), zakat, tax or fees due, plus a joint and several undertaking by all owners to pay any debts that emerge from their own funds; the undertaking counts as the final liquidation report, the closing accounts and the deregistration request under Art. 257. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 260-261 | Imprisonment up to three years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000 for: (a) any manager, officer, director, auditor or liquidator who deliberately records false or misleading information in the financial statements, reports, capital-reduction statements, solvency statements or other statements put to the owners, or deliberately omits a material fact to misrepresent the financial position; (b) any manager, officer or director who uses the company's funds, powers or votes knowingly against its interests for personal ends, to favour a company or person, or to benefit from a transaction in which he has an interest; (c) a liquidator who does likewise or prefers a creditor without lawful cause (Art. 260). Imprisonment up to one year and/or a fine up to SAR 1,000,000 for, among others: an auditor who fails to report apparent criminal violations to the company; vote-buying; falsely announcing registration of an unregistered company; a public official disclosing company secrets; publishing false names to attract subscriptions; deciding, paying or receiving dividends in bad faith contrary to the Law or the articles (and an auditor who fails to report it); false declarations on in-kind valuations, allocation of interests/shares or payment of their value; any manager, officer, director or auditor who fails to call (or to take steps to call) the assembly on learning that losses reached the Arts. 132/182 threshold; exploiting or disclosing company secrets to harm the company; deliberately obstructing persons entitled to inspect records; a court-appointed inspector who falsifies or omits material facts (Art. 261). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 262-267, 269-271; Regs Art. 94 | Fine up to SAR 500,000 for violations including: obstructing the calling or holding of an assembly or preventing an owner from attending or voting; failing to call the annual assembly within the statutory period; accepting or retaining JSC board membership contrary to the Law (and directors who knew and did not object); a director obtaining a loan or guarantee contrary to the Law; failing to keep accounting records or to prepare or deposit financial statements; failing to provide the competent authority with required documents; failing to make documents available to owners; failing to prepare and record minutes; omitting the Art. 12 particulars; acting as auditor despite a disqualification; failing to register the company or an amendment of its articles with the Commercial Register; deliberately recording untrue data in the articles, the application or supporting documents (and those who sign or register them knowingly); professional-company breaches; a liquidator failing to register his appointment or the end of liquidation; failing to take corrective action after notification of a violation; an auditor failing in statutory duties; any company or officer failing to apply the Law and Regulations or to comply with the competent authority's rules and decisions without reasonable cause (Art. 262). A Ministry committee of at least three members chaired by a legally qualified person imposes these fines (except for listed JSCs, where the CMA Board does); the Minister may designate violations penalised directly without the committee; the penalised person may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of notification; Regs Art. 94: notification may be electronic to a mobile number or e-mail registered with the Commercial Register or the National Information Center (Art. 267). Penalties are graduated by gravity and circumstances; repeat offences under Arts. 260-261 within three years of a final conviction double the penalty (Art. 263). The court may, in addition to or instead of Arts. 260-261 penalties, warn, order preventive or corrective steps, order cessation, or ban from the board of a listed JSC; the competent authority may warn or order corrective steps instead of Art. 262 fines (Art. 264). The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes Arts. 260-261 offences (Art. 265). The competent court hears all civil and criminal cases under the Law and Regulations except for listed JSCs, which go to the Committee for the Resolution of Securities Disputes (Art. 266). Penalties do not prejudice civil claims for damages (Art. 269). The competent authority supervises companies' compliance with the Law and their articles, may inspect, examine accounts and demand records and minutes through its officers or experts, and may send observers to general assemblies (Art. 270); all company officers must give Ministry (and, for listed or listing companies, CMA) representatives access to records and information (Art. 271). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 
 Arts. 274, 277 | The CMA supervises listed JSCs and issues their rules, including merger rules where a party is listed; the competent authority issues a corporate-governance regulation for JSCs (and the Ministry may issue one for other companies); the Ministry sets rules to obtain beneficial-ownership (المستفيد الحقيقي) information from companies other than listed JSCs; the Minister and the CMA Board issue the necessary rules (Art. 274). The Minister and the CMA Board issue the Regulations within 180 days of publication, covering rules, periods, procedures, documents and the use of technology for notices, meetings, participation and voting; any procedure under the Law or Regulations may be electronic, including incorporation and amendment applications, registration and publication, signature of applications, documents and registers, and deposit of financial statements (Art. 277). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 280-281; Royal Decree M/132 cl. 2-3; Regs Art. 95 | The Law replaces the Companies Law (M/3 of 28/1/1437H) and the Professional Companies Law (M/17 of 26/1/1441H) and repeals conflicting provisions (Art. 280); it enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 281). Decree cl. 2: the Law does not prejudice the powers of the Saudi Central Bank and the CMA. Decree cl. 3: companies existing at the effective date must conform to the Law within two years from that date; exceptionally, the Ministry and the CMA determine which provisions apply to them during that period. Regs Art. 95: the Regulations apply from the Law's effective date. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] 

FILE anti-concealment-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Tag 
---|---|---
 Art. 1 (definitions) | "Non-Saudi" means a natural or legal person who does not hold Saudi nationality and is not treated as a holder of it. "Economic activity" means any activity aimed at profit for which approvals or licences from the competent authorities are required, whether commercial, investment, service, professional, industrial, agricultural, or otherwise. "Proceeds" means funds arising directly or indirectly from the crimes in Art. 3(a) and (b), including funds converted wholly or partly into similar funds. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 2 | Definition of tasattur: an agreement or arrangement through which a person enables another, non-Saudi, person to practise an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, using the licence or approval issued to the concealer. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(a) | Crime of the enabler: any person enabling a non-Saudi to practise, for the non-Saudi's own account, an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, including enabling the non-Saudi to use the enabler's name, licence or approval, commercial register, trade name, or the like. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(b)–(d) | (b) Crime of the non-Saudi: practising an economic activity for their own account in the Kingdom without a licence, through the person who enabled them. (c) Participation: anyone who incites, assists, or gives advice for the commission of either crime, with knowledge, is an accomplice whenever the crime is committed or continued on the basis of that incitement, assistance, or advice. (d) Obstructing or preventing enforcement officers from performing their duties by any means, including non-disclosure of information or providing incorrect or misleading information, is a crime. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4 | Administrative violations (not crimes): (a) an establishment irregularly granting a non-Saudi instruments that lead to absolute disposal over the establishment; (b) a non-Saudi irregularly holding or using such instruments; (c) an establishment using, in the dealings of its economic activity, a bank account that does not belong to it. The Regulation sets the detail, taking into account cases where the instruments were granted or held in good faith. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 5 | Competences: the Ministry (of Commerce) has oversight, receives reports, and detects crimes and violations; the Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes crimes; the Criminal Court hears crimes; a Ministry committee of at least three members, its chair and at least one member legally qualified, decides Art. 4 violations and imposes Art. 14 penalties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6 | Enforcement officers designated by the Minister from the Ministry, Municipal and Rural Affairs, HRSD, Environment/Water/Agriculture, the General Authority of Zakat and Tax, and other competent bodies have judicial-enforcement status and may: enter and inspect suspected establishments, offices, branches, warehouses, and vehicles; examine and seize records; view CCTV recordings; require disclosure of information from any person or body; seal premises and safes pending inspection; summon and record statements; and call on the police. Inspection procedures are confidential. Rules for financial rewards to enforcement staff are issued by Council of Ministers resolution. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 7 | Crimes and violations may be proved by all means of proof, including electronic evidence. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 8 | The Ministry may ask the Public Prosecution to impose a travel ban on a person suspected of a crime under the Law (conditions per the Regulation). The Public Prosecution may, on its own motion or at the request of enforcement officers, order a precautionary seizure of funds liable to confiscation for up to sixty days, extendable by order of the Criminal Court; the funds may be left under the management of the interested party or transferred to a competent body. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 9 | Penalty for any crime under the Law: imprisonment up to five years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000, having regard to the size of the activity, its revenues, the period of practice, and the effects. Penalties are doubled for recidivism (a further crime within three years of a final judgment). The Criminal Court may reduce penalties where the accused, after the Ministry learns of the crime, provides evidence or information that could not otherwise have been obtained and that is relied on to prove the crime. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 10 | On conviction for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime, the proceeds are confiscated by court judgment whether held by the convicted person or another party; if the proceeds cannot be found or are mixed with lawful funds, other funds of equivalent value are confiscated. The Criminal Court may annul or block any act, contractual or otherwise, whose parties knew or should have known it would impair recovery of confiscable proceeds. Confiscated funds go to the Public Treasury, subject to the rights of good-faith third parties. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11 | The judgment convicting for the Art. 3(a) or (b) crime must order publication of its summary once final; the Ministry publishes it in the medium it sees fit. A non-Saudi convicted of any Art. 3 crime is deported and barred from re-entry, per the applicable rules and the Regulation, after serving the sentence and paying fees, taxes, and other obligations set by the court. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 12 | Conviction for the enabler's crime (Art. 3(a)) entails: (a) dissolution of the establishment, cancellation of its licence and approval, and deletion of the convicted person's commercial register, unless the Criminal Court decides otherwise; (b) a ban on the convicted person practising the activity concerned and any other commercial business for five years from finality. Those convicted under Art. 3(a) or (b) are jointly liable for zakat, taxes, fees, and any other obligation of the establishment. Judgments are copied to the Ministry and to the zakat and tax authority. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 13 | If a perpetrator reports the crime or the other perpetrators to the competent authorities before discovery, and the report leads to their arrest or to seizure of the funds, instruments, or proceeds, the Criminal Court may exempt them from the Art. 9(1) penalties under rules prepared by the Ministry and issued by Council of Ministers resolution; the exemption does not cover zakat and tax obligations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 14 | Penalty for an Art. 4 violation: a fine up to SAR 500,000 and/or closure of the establishment for up to ninety days. The committee may additionally require the violator to submit to the Ministry, for up to five years, the establishment's financial statements, bank statements, and employee payroll, and may penalise non-compliance with that order. Factors: size, revenues, duration, gravity, repetition, effect. If the committee sees indications of a crime it refers that part to the competent authority. A penalised person may appeal to the Administrative Court within sixty days of notification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15 | Without prejudice to the rights of good-faith third parties, every contract or act whose object or purpose is tasattur is void. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 17–18 | Every authority that issues licences for an economic activity must monitor its licensees and report suspected crimes or violations to the Ministry (Art. 17). The identity of reporters is kept in a confidential register and excluded from the case file (subject to the Public Prosecution's right to seek disclosure with safeguards). By decision of the Minister, a reporter who is not an enforcement official and not convicted in the matter receives a reward of up to 30 % of the fine collected, if the information was fit to open an investigation and a final judgment (crime) or final decision (violation) issued; the Regulation sets reporting procedures and division among multiple reporters (Art. 18). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Royal Decree M/4, clause "Second" | The Ministries of Commerce and Interior, with HRSD, Investment, and the Premium Residency Center, were to prepare within sixty days a Council of Ministers regulation with a mechanism for violators under the old 1425H law who continued after this Law took effect to correct their status, providing: the available correction options; exemption from the penalties of both laws and from retroactive income tax for anyone who applied to the Ministry of Commerce within 180 days of this Law's effective date; exclusion of those who first offended under this Law, those already caught, and those referred to the Public Prosecution or court; treatment of corrected persons wishing to leave the Kingdom permanently; and preservation of private rights arising from their dealings. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 19–20 | The Minister of Commerce, in agreement with the Minister of Interior, issues the Implementing Regulation within 180 days of publication, to apply from the Law's effective date (Art. 19). The Law replaces the Anti-Concealment Law of Royal Decree No. M/22 dated 4/5/1425H, repeals conflicting provisions, and enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 20). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Investment Law (M/19 of 16/1/1446H) Arts. 1, 7, 8, 11, 16 — interplay | "Foreign investor" is any natural or legal person making an investment who is not a "local investor" (a person holding Saudi nationality) (Art. 1). A foreign investor must register with the Ministry of Investment before making any investment, per the Regulation, except investment in securities under the Capital Market Law (Art. 7(2)). Before investing in an activity on the Excluded Activities list (prohibited or restricted), or changing ownership of an investment in a restricted activity, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval (Art. 8). Breaches of Arts. 7–8 carry warning, a fine up to SAR 300,000 (doubled on repetition), or cancellation of registration (Art. 11). The Law took effect 180 days after publication on 16/08/2024 and repealed the Foreign Investment Law of M/1 of 1421H (Art. 16). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Investment Law text read today from `boe/investment-law-ar.md`; portal shows 0 amended articles) 
