 Art. 1 (definitions: الاستثمار، رأس المال، المستثمر الأجنبي); Reg. Art. 2 | "Investment" is the use of capital to establish, expand, develop, finance, own (in whole or part) or manage an investment project in the Kingdom for economic gain. "Capital" is any asset of material value (cash, in-kind or intangible), in particular shares and interests in companies, contractual rights, fixed or movable assets, IP rights, and rights granted by law such as licences; it excludes loans, bonds, financing sukuk and public or private debt instruments. A "local investor" is a natural or legal person investing who holds Saudi nationality; a "foreign investor" is any investor who is not a local investor. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 2 (الهدف من النظام) | The Law's purposes include facilitating establishment of, ownership of assets in, and exit from or liquidation of an investment; guaranteeing investor rights; guaranteeing equal treatment between local and foreign investors; transparent, efficient and fair procedures; and competitive neutrality. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3 (حرية الاستثمار) | Subject to Arts. 8 and 9 and to other laws, an investor may invest in any sector or activity open to investment. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4(1)(a)–(b), 4(2); Reg. Arts. 3–4 | Investors enjoy equal treatment with other investors, and equal treatment between local and foreign investors, in similar circumstances; and fair and equitable treatment. Reg. Art. 3(2) tests "similar circumstances" by the goods/services produced or consumed, the target sector, investment size, and impact on the local economy or environment; Reg. Art. 3(3) preserves the Ministry's right to regulate for the public interest (national security, public safety, public order). Reg. Art. 4 deems denial of justice, substantial breach of due process, manifest arbitrariness (harassment, compulsion) and unjustified targeted discrimination to be breaches of fair and equitable treatment. Under Art. 4(2) the competent authority must observe these rights when taking public-interest measures. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(c); Reg. Art. 5 | No confiscation of an investment, in whole or part, except by final judicial judgment; no direct or indirect expropriation except for the public interest, in accordance with legal procedures, and against fair compensation. Reg. Art. 5 defines indirect expropriation (measure with effect equivalent to direct expropriation, judged by economic impact — adverse effect on value alone is not enough — duration, and nature/purpose) and excludes non-discriminatory measures protecting public health, safety and the environment. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(d); Reg. Art. 6 | Freedom to transfer funds into and out of the Kingdom without delay, through legal channels, in any currency recognised by the Saudi Central Bank, including investment returns, profits, and sale or liquidation proceeds. Reg. Art. 6(1) lists initial and additional capital, profits, capital gains, dividends, royalties, fees, contract and loan payments, liquidation/sale proceeds, and remuneration of personnel engaged from abroad. Reg. Art. 6(2) permits delay or prevention under equitable, non-discriminatory, bona fide laws on bankruptcy/creditor protection, securities dealing, criminal offences, and compliance with judgments; Reg. Art. 6(3) preserves prudential measures in financial services. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 4(1)(e)–(g), 4(3); Reg. Arts. 7, 24–26 | Freedom to manage the investment, dispose of it lawfully and own what is needed to run the business; protection of IP and confidential commercial information; facilitation of administrative procedures and support from the competent authority. The Ministry must provide available statistics (within 30 working days of a request, Reg. Art. 7) and handle investor complaints under a transparent procedure: acceptance or rejection notified within 10 working days, recommendations within 30 working days of acceptance (Reg. Arts. 25–26); a complaint is not a judicial proceeding and does not prejudice court or ADR rights (Reg. Art. 24(2)). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 5 (التزامات المستثمر) | The investor must comply with all laws and legislation of the Kingdom and with the Kingdom's obligations under international agreements to which it is party. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 6; Reg. Art. 8 | Investment incentives are granted by the competent authority under objective, specific and fair eligibility criteria; the competent authority publishes the criteria through appropriate channels. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 7(1), 7(3), 7(4); Reg. Arts. 9–10, 21; Decree para. 5 | The Ministry establishes and maintains a confidential National Register of Investors (السجل الوطني للمستثمرين) recording all information on investors and their investments; competent authorities must supply data, including through platform integration; the Ministry may make its services conditional on the investor updating its register data (Reg. Art. 9(4)). Through its comprehensive service centre (مركز الخدمة الشاملة) the Ministry receives applications for any approvals, licences or permits needed to practise an activity and coordinates their issue with the competent authority. Decree para. 5 requires the Ministry to exercise this function consistently with Art. 4 of the Saudi Business Center organisation (CoM Res. 456 of 11/8/1440H) and by agreement with the Center. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 7(2); Reg. Arts. 11–12; Art. 16 | A foreign investor must register with the Ministry before making any investment, as specified in the Regulations; this does not apply to investment in securities governed by the Capital Market Law. Registration data for a legal person: name and place of incorporation, business scope and economic activities to be practised, capital, expected capital contribution, particulars of owners/shareholders/controllers or the ultimate beneficial owner, plus anything the Investor Guide requires; the applicant must confirm the Ministry's declarations including accuracy of information (Reg. Art. 11). The Ministry notifies registration within 10 working days of completion; an incomplete file must be completed within a period of not less than 15 working days or is deemed cancelled (Reg. Art. 12). Art. 16 repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) under which foreign investors needed a licence. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Reg. Art. 13 (annual update) | The registered investor must submit an annual update of the declarations signed at registration and of any change to its register data; the Ministry notifies the result within 5 working days. The update may be filed within the 60 working days before the deadline; the Ministry reminds the investor 30 working days before; a grace period of up to 30 working days may be granted; a lapsed registration may be reactivated within three years of the end of that period, after which the investor must re-register under the Investor Guide procedure. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` 
 Reg. Art. 14 (deregistration) | An investor may apply for voluntary deregistration with the documents in the Investor Guide (incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days). The Ministry must deregister on (a) cessation of all registered investments by a final decision or unappealable judgment, (b) a final national-security suspension decision, or (c) a final committee penalty cancelling registration; notice within 5 working days. A deregistered investor may not carry on any investment activity unless it re-registers; deregistration does not waive third-party claims. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MISA]` 
 Art. 8(1)–(2); Reg. Arts. 15–19 | The competent authority (the Examination Committee, per Decree para. 3) issues and updates the list of excluded activities (قائمة الأنشطة المستثناة) — activities prohibited to, or restricted for, foreign investors — and the Ministry publishes it (Reg. Art. 15: in the Investor Guide). Before investing in a listed activity a foreign investor must apply to the Ministry, which refers the application to the Examination Committee; the Committee may request further information (Reg. Art. 16); incomplete applications lapse after 15 working days (Reg. Art. 17); the decision is notified within 5 working days of receipt (Reg. Art. 18); a rejected applicant may reapply citing the first application and new information (Reg. Art. 19). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Excluded-activities list — contents | The list itself was not among the sources read. From memory it descends from the "negative list" of the 2000 regime and the Investor Guide and includes, among others: oil exploration, drilling and production (except certain services); manufacture of military equipment and explosives; security and investigation services; real-estate investment in Makkah and Madinah; Hajj and Umrah tour guidance and services; recruitment and employment offices; certain fisheries, printing and publishing, and audiovisual/media activities; and restricted (conditional) activities such as wholesale/retail trade, transport, and professional services with Saudi-participation or capital conditions. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20; Reg. Art. 30(c) | Before any change in the ownership of its investment in a *restricted* activity on the list, the foreign investor must apply to the Ministry for approval, which the Ministry refers to the Examination Committee under the same procedure as new applications (Reg. Arts. 15–18 apply). Changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval is a serious violation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 9; Reg. Arts. 22–23 | The Ministry may suspend any foreign investment to protect national security, on an objective basis, consistently with the Kingdom's international-agreement obligations and under the Regulations' procedure. The Ministry may coordinate with competent authorities including the Examination Committee before, during and after; may request information and must keep it confidential; may discuss alternative measures with the investor before suspending (Reg. Art. 22); the suspension is notified and may be appealed under the laws in force (Reg. Art. 23). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 10 (الوسائل البديلة لتسوية المنازعات) | An investor party to a dispute — including a dispute with a competent authority — may resort to the competent court unless the parties agree otherwise; investors may agree to settle disputes by alternative means including arbitration, mediation and conciliation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11(1)–(3); Reg. Arts. 27–30 | For a non-serious violation of Art. 7 or Art. 8 the Ministry notifies the investor to remedy it within the period set by the Regulations (not less than 30 working days, extendable on justification: Reg. Art. 28; the list of non-serious violations goes in the Investor Guide: Reg. Art. 29). Failure to remedy in time, or a serious violation, is punishable — without prejudice to any severer penalty in another law — by one or more of: warning; a fine of up to SAR 300,000, which may be doubled on repetition; cancellation of registration. Serious violations (Reg. Art. 30): investing without registration; investing in an excluded activity without approval, after its expiry or cancellation, or in breach of its conditions; changing ownership in a restricted activity without prior approval; giving false or misleading information to the Ministry or competent authority; obstructing detection officers. Detection officers are appointed by the Minister and may obtain records, analyse reports, record complaints, verify supplier/customer contracts and consult the licensing authority (Reg. Art. 27). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 11(4)–(5); Reg. Arts. 31–35 | The Minister forms one or more committees of at least three members, at least one a legal specialist, to hear violations and impose the Art. 11(2) penalties, weighing gravity, repetition and the size of the enterprise. Members serve three years renewable once (Reg. Art. 31); sessions are in camera, valid with a majority including the chair or deputy, decisions by majority (Reg. Art. 32); representation is under the Code of Law Practice (Reg. Art. 33); the committee decides within 30 working days of receiving the violation report, extendable once, and the Minister or delegate issues the penalty decision within 30 working days of the committee's report (Reg. Art. 34); the decision is notified by the Ministry's approved means (Reg. Art. 35). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Art. 12 (التظلم) | A person against whom the Ministry issues a penalty decision may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of being notified of the decision. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 13–14 | The Law does not prejudice the Kingdom's obligations under any international agreement in force to which it is party; nor the special regimes of other authorities applying to specific economic activities or to special economic zones, provided the investor enjoys at least the rights in the Law. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15; Art. 16; Decree paras. 2 and 4; Reg. Arts. 36–37 | The Minister issues the Regulations within 180 days of publication, effective with the Law; MISA prepares them after coordinating with relevant bodies and the Ministry of Energy co-drafts the provisions on Art. 6 and Art. 7(3)–(4) (Decree para. 4). The Law takes effect 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette, repeals the Foreign Investment Law (M/1 of 1421H) and conflicting provisions. Rights acquired before the effective date, including any licences or incentives granted, are preserved until their terms expire (Decree para. 2). Reg. Art. 36 requires the Minister to issue and publish an Investor Guide listing MISA services, their classification, method and fees; Reg. Art. 37 brings the Regulations into force with the Law. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law and Decree); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.) 
 Diligence checklist (synthesis of Art. 7, Art. 8, Reg. Arts. 11, 13, 14, 20, 30) | For a target with any foreign ownership, a corporate diligence must verify: (1) a current MISA investment registration certificate and the date and result of the last annual update (Reg. Art. 13) — a lapse of more than 3 years means re-registration; (2) that the economic activities on the commercial register match the activities registered with MISA (Reg. Art. 11(2)(b)) — a mismatch is a violation of Art. 7; (3) whether any activity is on the excluded list and, if restricted, that Examination Committee approval exists and its conditions are met (Art. 8; Reg. Art. 30(b)); (4) that every past change of ownership in a restricted activity was pre-approved (Art. 8(3); Reg. Art. 20); (5) that the owner/controller/UBO particulars filed with MISA match the cap table (Reg. Art. 11(2)(e)); (6) any notice of violation, committee proceeding, suspension or deregistration (Reg. Arts. 14, 22, 27–35). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Law); `[authority — MISA]` (Reg.); practice points inline `[model knowledge — verify]` 

