FILE companies-law
 Art. 178 | A partner may assign his interest to another partner on the terms in the articles. To assign to a non-partner, with or without consideration, the partner must notify the other partners through the manager of the assignee's or buyer's name and the terms; the manager notifies the partners immediately; each partner may within 30 days of the manager's notification of the agreed price demand to redeem the interest and pay its value, or have the company buy it; if several partners redeem, the interest is divided pro rata to their capital; if the value is disputed it is fixed by an accredited valuer's fair-value report at the cost of the redeeming partner or the company; if the period expires without redemption, payment or company purchase, the partner may assign to the third party. The articles may provide other notification procedures, another valuation method, or a longer period for redemption, payment or company purchase. The redemption right does not apply to transfers by inheritance, will or court judgment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 
 Art. 182 | If an LLC's losses reach half of its capital, the manager must call the general assembly of partners to meet within 60 days of learning that losses reached that level, to consider whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] 

FILE anti-concealment-law
 Art. 2 | Definition of tasattur: an agreement or arrangement through which a person enables another, non-Saudi, person to practise an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, using the licence or approval issued to the concealer. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(a) | Crime of the enabler: any person enabling a non-Saudi to practise, for the non-Saudi's own account, an economic activity in the Kingdom that the non-Saudi is not licensed to practise, including enabling the non-Saudi to use the enabler's name, licence or approval, commercial register, trade name, or the like. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 3(b)–(d) | (b) Crime of the non-Saudi: practising an economic activity for their own account in the Kingdom without a licence, through the person who enabled them. (c) Participation: anyone who incites, assists, or gives advice for the commission of either crime, with knowledge, is an accomplice whenever the crime is committed or continued on the basis of that incitement, assistance, or advice. (d) Obstructing or preventing enforcement officers from performing their duties by any means, including non-disclosure of information or providing incorrect or misleading information, is a crime. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4 | Administrative violations (not crimes): (a) an establishment irregularly granting a non-Saudi instruments that lead to absolute disposal over the establishment; (b) a non-Saudi irregularly holding or using such instruments; (c) an establishment using, in the dealings of its economic activity, a bank account that does not belong to it. The Regulation sets the detail, taking into account cases where the instruments were granted or held in good faith. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 9 | Penalty for any crime under the Law: imprisonment up to five years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000, having regard to the size of the activity, its revenues, the period of practice, and the effects. Penalties are doubled for recidivism (a further crime within three years of a final judgment). The Criminal Court may reduce penalties where the accused, after the Ministry learns of the crime, provides evidence or information that could not otherwise have been obtained and that is relied on to prove the crime. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 12 | Conviction for the enabler's crime (Art. 3(a)) entails: (a) dissolution of the establishment, cancellation of its licence and approval, and deletion of the convicted person's commercial register, unless the Criminal Court decides otherwise; (b) a ban on the convicted person practising the activity concerned and any other commercial business for five years from finality. Those convicted under Art. 3(a) or (b) are jointly liable for zakat, taxes, fees, and any other obligation of the establishment. Judgments are copied to the Ministry and to the zakat and tax authority. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 14 | Penalty for an Art. 4 violation: a fine up to SAR 500,000 and/or closure of the establishment for up to ninety days. The committee may additionally require the violator to submit to the Ministry, for up to five years, the establishment's financial statements, bank statements, and employee payroll, and may penalise non-compliance with that order. Factors: size, revenues, duration, gravity, repetition, effect. If the committee sees indications of a crime it refers that part to the competent authority. A penalised person may appeal to the Administrative Court within sixty days of notification. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 15 | Without prejudice to the rights of good-faith third parties, every contract or act whose object or purpose is tasattur is void. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
