FILE social-insurance-law
 Art. 10 | Government bodies, public institutions and authorities, state-owned companies and companies in which the State holds more than 50% of the capital must require any employer dealing with them to produce a GOSI certificate showing the establishment is registered and has met all its obligations (or is not subject to the law) when the employer: (a) applies to collect its dues; (b) bids for public works, supply, operation, maintenance or other projects; (c) applies to amend, renew or cancel its commercial register; (d) applies for a State subsidy; (e) seeks liquidation of its establishment; (f) applies for or renews a project licence; (g) applies to recruit workers from abroad. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 11 | Contributions and late-payment fines are secured by a lien in GOSI's favour ranking immediately after the lien for wages. GOSI may, through the competent bodies and by the applicable judicial and statutory procedures, attach and enforce against any assets owned by or owed to the employer, once the schedules of amounts due are officially certified by the Minister or his delegate. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 59, 60, 61 | Penalties, without prejudice to any harsher penalty in another law: a fine up to SAR 50,000 for any violation of the law or Regulation, doubled for a repeat within three years, and multiplied by the number of contributors concerned. Registering a person who does not actually work for the employer: the same fine or up to twice the total contributions for the period, whichever is higher, per person, with the contributions refunded to the employer. Where a violation causes benefits to be paid without right: the fine or the amount of the benefits, whichever is higher, plus restitution. No fine after five years from the violation (correction and restitution still due). The Board issues a schedule classifying violations; GOSI may settle and waive fines. A three-member committee (at least one legal specialist) imposes penalties; appeal lies to the competent court. Publication of the decision at the violator's expense may be ordered once final. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE saudization-nitaqat
 Nitaqat guide, Services by Range | Platinum, High Green and Medium Green: may apply for new expatriate visas, change expatriate workers' occupations, renew work permits, transfer expatriates' sponsorship into the entity from any other entity, and benefit from immediate counting in Nitaqat. Low Green: cannot apply for new visas or change occupations; can renew existing work permits; immediate counting. Red: cannot change occupations, cannot transfer expatriates in, cannot apply for new visas, cannot issue work permits for new expatriates, cannot renew work permits of existing expatriates. Use of each service is subject to its own regulations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Implementing Regulation, labour-services licensing rules (workers supplied to clients) | Workers whom a licensed labour-services company supplies to a client establishment are included in the workforce on which the client's Saudization ratios are computed, unless the ministry sets specific conditions for counting such workers; licensees must comply with the Saudization requirements the ministry issues for their clients and may not provide services that would breach a client's required ratio. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]` 
 Qiwa contract documentation as a condition of Nitaqat credit (from 15 April 2026, as claimed) | From 15 April 2026 a Saudi employee is counted in the Nitaqat Saudization rate only if his employment contract is documented (authenticated) on Qiwa; GOSI registration alone no longer earns credit. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Nitaqat 2026-2028 phase (from 26 April 2026, as claimed) | A new Nitaqat phase for 2026-2028 with higher required Saudization rates by sector, announced by HRSD with a lead time before each annual step; the lead also claims the Yellow band was removed (inconsistent with the 2021 guide, which already had no Yellow band). Entities that were Green under the 2024-2025 coefficients may fall a band on 26 April 2026 without a workforce change, as happened in the guide's own 2023-to-2024 example. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Revised work-visa quotas for new establishments (26 August 2026, as claimed) | Instant-visa caps for newly established businesses tied to company age, participation in the Establishment Programme, and Nitaqat band. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 

