 Royal Decree M/191 para. 5 (transitional) | The Law applies to all facts that occurred before it came into force, except (a) where a statutory provision or a judicial principle relating to the fact conflicts with the Law and a party relies on it, and (b) where the rule concerns a limitation period that began to run before the Law came into force. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 98-99 | The contract's effect extends to the parties and their universal successors (subject to inheritance rules) unless the contract, the nature of the transaction or statute shows otherwise; personal rights and obligations attached to a thing pass to a particular successor with the thing if they are among its requisites and the successor knew of them. A contract imposes no obligation on third parties but may confer a right on them. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 248-254 | Assignment of debt (hawalat al-dayn) transfers the obligation from the original debtor to the new debtor. Concluded between the old and new debtor, it is effective against the creditor only if the creditor accepts; it may also be concluded between the old debtor and the creditor, effective against the new debtor only on its acceptance. The debt passes with its attributes and accessories and the original debtor is discharged; securities remain except those given by third parties; the new debtor may raise the old debtor's defences except those personal to it; the old debtor warrants the new debtor's solvency at the relevant time unless agreed otherwise. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 
 Arts. 255-258 | A party may transfer its position as party to a contract to another with the other party's consent; consent may be given in advance if it identifies the contract, in which case the transfer takes effect against the remaining party when the transferor notifies it. If the remaining party consents, the transferor is released for the future; if not, the transferor remains bound jointly and severally with the transferee unless agreed otherwise or the transfer was conditional on that consent. The transferee may raise defences relating to the debt but not those personal to the transferor; the remaining party may raise all defences it had against the transferor. Third-party securities lapse on release of the transferor. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] 

