 Companies Law Art. 274(3) (legal basis) | The Ministry may set the rules and procedures needed to ensure it obtains beneficial-owner information from companies subject to the Companies Law, except in relation to joint stock companies listed on the Exchange. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Rules Art. 4(1) (guide 3.4, 3.5) — tier 1, ownership | Every natural person who owns, directly or indirectly, 25 % or more of the company's capital is a UBO. Indirect holdings are computed by multiplying percentages down the chain (e.g. 90 % of company B which holds 85 % of company A = 76.5 % of A); direct and indirect holdings of the same person are aggregated (20 % + 17 % = 37 %); holdings through several intermediate entities are summed; and persons acting by agreement, or close family members (spouses, siblings, children) holding together, are treated as holding jointly. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 4(2) (guide 3.4, 3.5 "second") — tier 2, effective control | If no natural person meets the 25 % test, or there is doubt whether a person who does is actually the one exercising control, every natural person who exercises ultimate effective control over the company by any means, directly or indirectly, is a UBO. Examples given: shareholder agreements or informal understandings (including within a family) giving control over important decisions; dominant influence to appoint or remove the majority of management; a chain of majority holdings that controls the general assembly at every level even where the multiplied percentage is below 25 % (51 % × 51 % × 51 % = 13.26 % but still a UBO); control through voting or economic rights; personal ties to holders; financing or debt-repayment relationships; use or enjoyment of the company's assets. Control may be presumed from indicators even if not actually exercised. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 4(3) (guide 3.4, example 13) — tier 3, management fallback; sequencing | If no UBO can be identified under the ownership or control criteria, the UBO is the company's manager, board member, or chairman, as the case may be. The company must apply the criteria in sequence and move to the next criterion only when the previous one cannot be applied. Where more than one senior manager controls the company, all are UBOs; if the managers act on the instructions of another (nominee managers), the person giving the instructions is the UBO. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Nominee partners and shareholders (guide 2.5, 2.6, 3.3) | A partner or shareholder who exercises the rights attached to shares (voting, dividends) on behalf of and on the instructions of another person (the "principal" partner/shareholder) must disclose to the company the identity and data of that principal, within 15 days of acquiring the nominee capacity, and disclose changes within 15 days; if the principal is a legal person, the disclosure must include the principal's own UBO. The company must collect: the identity of the nominee; the principal's full name, date and place of birth (or CR/licence data for a legal person), residence or head-office address, and, for a legal-person principal, its UBO data. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Annual confirmation (guide 2.3) | The company must confirm its disclosed UBO data to the Ministry every year; the confirmation is a continuing obligation that renews annually. The due date is the anniversary of the company's registration in the Commercial Register, and the confirmation may be submitted up to 30 days before that date. Example: company registered 08/12/2025 must confirm by end of 07/12/2026, and may file from 30 days before. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Change notification (guide 2.4) | If disclosed UBO data changes or is amended, the company must submit an update request to the Ministry within 15 days of the change, stating the reasons for the change. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 

FILE competition-law
 Art. 7; Reg. Art. 12 and Guidelines s.6 (thresholds) | Establishments wishing to participate in an economic concentration must notify GAC at least ninety days before completion if the total annual sales value of the participating establishments exceeds the amount set by the Regulations. Reg. Art. 12(1) (as quoted): the threshold is total annual sales of all participating entities exceeding SAR 200,000,000; Reg. Art. 12(2): where sales cannot be estimated or the business has not run a full fiscal year, annual sales are estimated from the activity; Reg. Art. 12(3): the Board may set reporting criteria where sales cannot be determined, published at least 30 days before entry into force. The Guidelines then state "additional notifying criteria" that must *all* be met: (A) total worldwide annual sales of the parties exceed SAR 200 million; **and** (B) total worldwide annual sales of the target exceed SAR 40 million (for mergers and full-function JVs: at least two parties each exceed SAR 40 million); **and** (C) total annual sales in the Kingdom of all parties exceed SAR 40 million (for acquisitions, "and the Target contributes to this"). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 7); `[authority — GAC]` (Reg. Art. 12 wording and the additional criteria) 
 Guidelines s.6 (which entities count; how sales are measured) | Participating entities are all that form part of the concentrated entity after completion: for a merger, the merging entities in full; for an acquisition, the entire acquiring group plus the target (or the acquired division), but *not* the seller; for a full-function JV, all parents acquiring joint control plus the JV itself. "Annual sales" is gross revenue from ordinary business in the most recent audited financial statements (SOCPA or equivalent), less sales rebates and VAT/sales-related taxes; intra-group sales are excluded; foreign currency converted at the Saudi Central Bank average rate for the financial year; special measures apply to banks, insurers and other financial institutions; the acquirer is assessed as a "single economic entity" including all entities under common control. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — GAC]` 
 Art. 6 with Reg. Art. 10 as quoted in Guidelines s.10 (dominance; 40%) | Dominance in the relevant market is established by either (1) a market share of 40% or more held by a single establishment or a group acting with a common will, or (2) the ability to influence the market (prices, production, demand), assessed by factors including shares of the firm and competitors, actual/potential competition, supply and demand growth, entry barriers, buyer power and access to inputs. Art. 6 prohibits abuse of a dominant position, listing below-cost pricing to exclude rivals, imposing resale prices or conditions, manipulating available quantities, discriminating between establishments in like contracts, refusal to deal without objective reason, exclusive-dealing conditions, and tying. In concentration review a post-merger share of 40% or more is treated as likely to create or strengthen dominance. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Art. 1, Art. 6); `[authority — GAC]` (Reg. Art. 10 wording) 
 Art. 5 (prohibited agreements — note only) | Practices, agreements or contracts between establishments, written or oral, express or implied, whose object or effect is to prejudice competition are prohibited, in particular: fixing or proposing prices or sale/purchase terms; limiting volumes or output; restricting flow of goods to or from markets (hiding, hoarding, refusal to deal); conduct obstructing entry or excluding an establishment; withholding available goods from particular establishments; dividing markets by area, distribution centre, customer type, or season; freezing or limiting production, development, distribution, marketing or investment; collusion or coordination in tenders, government or otherwise. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 

FILE bankruptcy-law
 Arts. 200-205, 207-209 — **director and officer liability** | A natural-person debtor, and any manager, board member, officer, founder or person in a similar position, breaches the Law if, before the opening (and leading to it) or during it, they harm any party's rights by: mismanaging or withholding assets or abusing powers; running the business with intent to defraud creditors; *continuing to carry on the business when liquidation could no longer be avoided*; using reckless methods to avoid or delay liquidation to creditors' detriment, including selling goods below market for cash; entering transactions for no or unfair consideration; paying some creditors to the prejudice of others; or abusing a procedure. Art. 201 adds concealment or misappropriation of assets, falsifying, destroying or failing to keep proper books, fictitious accounts, fraudulent increase of liabilities or reduction of assets, misleading the trustee, court or Commission, and disposing of assets or paying debts contrary to the Law, a judgment or the plan. Creditors are liable for fraudulent or inflated claims and for collusive preferential arrangements (Art. 202). Penalty: up to 5 years' imprisonment and/or a fine up to SAR 5 million, and the court may for up to 5 years ban the offender from managing any profit-seeking entity, from voting on board nominations, and from owning shares that would carry management; the Commission keeps a public register of such judgments (Art. 204(2); Regs Art. 90). The court may also annul the act, order restitution and award compensation (Art. 205). Other violations of the Law or Regulations: fine up to SAR 500,000 (Art. 207). Recidivism within 3 years doubles the penalties (Art. 209). The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes; the Commercial Court sentences (Art. 208). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Arts. 210-212 — **suspect period / clawback** | Any interested party may object to the court to any of the following dispositions made by the debtor within the 12 months before the opening with a non-related party, or within 24 months with a related party: (a) total or partial waiver of assets, rights or security granted to it; (b) transactions for no consideration or below fair value; (c) transactions settling debts before maturity or unfairly; (d) granting security for debts before they were established; (e) partial or total release of a debtor of the debtor. The objection must be brought within 24 months of the opening. The court annuls the disposition and its effects unless it served the debtor's interest and the debtor was neither distressed nor insolvent at the time, and orders recovery of assets and proceeds (or fair value), return of security, repayment of money received to the trustee, or reinstatement of released guarantees. Rights acquired by bona fide third parties who were not party to the disposition are unaffected. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 227; Regs Arts. 18, 89 | The Commission establishes the Bankruptcy Register, in which everything the Law requires is deposited; its contents are publicly accessible, electronically, on the Commission's website. Regs Art. 18: on the opening of any procedure the debtor (or, in liquidation, the trustee/Commission) must state the type of procedure on every document issued in the debtor's name. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 

