FILE ultimate-beneficial-ownership-rules
 Article | Rule (English) | Tag 
---|---|---
 Companies Law Art. 274(3) (legal basis) | The Ministry may set the rules and procedures needed to ensure it obtains beneficial-owner information from companies subject to the Companies Law, except in relation to joint stock companies listed on the Exchange. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Definition (guide, introduction and item 3.1–3.2) | The ultimate beneficial owner is the natural person who owns or exercises ultimate effective control, directly or indirectly, over the company, or in whose name or for whose benefit the company is managed or transactions are conducted. A UBO is always a natural person; a legal person can never be the UBO and its structure must be looked through. "Ownership" means holding, directly or indirectly, at least 25 % of the company's capital; "control" means the ability to exercise effective influence or powers over the company's material or strategic decisions even without a capital share. Control may be legal (by instrument or agreement) or factual (by conduct). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Scope of companies (guide 1.1, 1.6) | A company must disclose UBO data to the Ministry if it is a general partnership, limited partnership, joint stock company, simplified joint stock company, limited liability company, or a foreign company practising its activity and business inside the Kingdom. The foreign company is subject to the disclosure duty and to all other duties in the Rules. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 3 (guide 1.2) — listed companies | A joint stock company listed on the Exchange is excluded from the application of the Rules. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 7 (guide 1.3–1.5) — exempt subsidiaries; narrowing of exemptions | A company is exempt from the disclosure requirement only where two criteria are met: (a) it is a subsidiary of a company listed on the Exchange, and (b) the subsidiary is subject to UBO disclosure requirements. An exempt company remains bound by the other duties, in particular: proving its entitlement to the exemption; the annual confirmation of the exemption; updating data when a change affects entitlement; and cooperating with the Ministry's requests. A company that enjoyed an exemption under the previous Rules that has now been cancelled must make its first disclosure at any time, but no later than its annual-confirmation due date. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` for the two-criteria exemption and residual duties; `[model knowledge — verify]` for any other exemption 
 Rules Art. 4(1) (guide 3.4, 3.5) — tier 1, ownership | Every natural person who owns, directly or indirectly, 25 % or more of the company's capital is a UBO. Indirect holdings are computed by multiplying percentages down the chain (e.g. 90 % of company B which holds 85 % of company A = 76.5 % of A); direct and indirect holdings of the same person are aggregated (20 % + 17 % = 37 %); holdings through several intermediate entities are summed; and persons acting by agreement, or close family members (spouses, siblings, children) holding together, are treated as holding jointly. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 4(2) (guide 3.4, 3.5 "second") — tier 2, effective control | If no natural person meets the 25 % test, or there is doubt whether a person who does is actually the one exercising control, every natural person who exercises ultimate effective control over the company by any means, directly or indirectly, is a UBO. Examples given: shareholder agreements or informal understandings (including within a family) giving control over important decisions; dominant influence to appoint or remove the majority of management; a chain of majority holdings that controls the general assembly at every level even where the multiplied percentage is below 25 % (51 % × 51 % × 51 % = 13.26 % but still a UBO); control through voting or economic rights; personal ties to holders; financing or debt-repayment relationships; use or enjoyment of the company's assets. Control may be presumed from indicators even if not actually exercised. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Rules Art. 4(3) (guide 3.4, example 13) — tier 3, management fallback; sequencing | If no UBO can be identified under the ownership or control criteria, the UBO is the company's manager, board member, or chairman, as the case may be. The company must apply the criteria in sequence and move to the next criterion only when the previous one cannot be applied. Where more than one senior manager controls the company, all are UBOs; if the managers act on the instructions of another (nominee managers), the person giving the instructions is the UBO. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Register contents (guide 2.1, 2.2) | The company must keep a dedicated UBO register recording, for each UBO: full name (first, father, grandfather, family) in Arabic and English; nationality; date and place of birth; identity data (national ID for Saudis; resident ID for residents; passport number, type, place and dates of issue and expiry, with a passport copy, for non-residents); residence address (national address for Saudis and residents; home-country address for non-residents); mobile number and e-mail; the criterion under Rules Art. 4 by which the person qualifies; the nature and extent of the ownership or control (a figure such as a percentage or voting share, or a description such as decision rights or board influence); and the date on which the person became a UBO. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Register duties (guide 2.2) | The company must: take steps to search for the natural person who owns or effectively controls it; verify each UBO's data by measures proportionate to the company's risk, the nature of its activity, and the complexity of its ownership structure, relying on documents and information from reliable and independent sources; keep supporting documents (copies of ID or passport, and others); update the register and documents whenever UBO data changes; keep the register and related documents inside the Kingdom; preferably use secure electronic systems; and give the Ministry, and financial institutions and designated non-financial businesses and professions, immediate access to the UBO data on request. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Nominee partners and shareholders (guide 2.5, 2.6, 3.3) | A partner or shareholder who exercises the rights attached to shares (voting, dividends) on behalf of and on the instructions of another person (the "principal" partner/shareholder) must disclose to the company the identity and data of that principal, within 15 days of acquiring the nominee capacity, and disclose changes within 15 days; if the principal is a legal person, the disclosure must include the principal's own UBO. The company must collect: the identity of the nominee; the principal's full name, date and place of birth (or CR/licence data for a legal person), residence or head-office address, and, for a legal-person principal, its UBO data. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Filing channel and incorporation (guide 2.7, 2.3 example, 4.1) | Disclosure of UBO data to the Ministry, its update or change, and the annual confirmation are made through the Ministry's approved electronic services. The guide's example is of a company that disclosed its UBO data to the Ministry together with its incorporation application. No fee is charged for the disclosure or the annual confirmation. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]`; platform name `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Annual confirmation (guide 2.3) | The company must confirm its disclosed UBO data to the Ministry every year; the confirmation is a continuing obligation that renews annually. The due date is the anniversary of the company's registration in the Commercial Register, and the confirmation may be submitted up to 30 days before that date. Example: company registered 08/12/2025 must confirm by end of 07/12/2026, and may file from 30 days before. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Change notification (guide 2.4) | If disclosed UBO data changes or is amended, the company must submit an update request to the Ministry within 15 days of the change, stating the reasons for the change. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — MoC]` 
 Penalties: Companies Law Arts. 262, 264 (guide 4.2) | The penalty in Companies Law Art. 262 and the measures in Art. 264 apply to a company that fails to: (a) disclose sufficient, accurate, and up-to-date UBO data within the prescribed period; (b) update the data within the prescribed period; (c) submit the annual confirmation within the prescribed period; (d) obtain sufficient, accurate, and up-to-date UBO data, record it in a dedicated register, and keep it, or provide the data to financial institutions or designated non-financial businesses and professions; or (e) cooperate with the Ministry in providing documents and information and responding to requests within the prescribed period. Art. 262: without prejudice to any penalty in another law, a fine not exceeding SAR 500,000 for the listed violations, including (ق) any company or officer that fails to apply the Law and its regulations or to comply with the controls or decisions of the competent authority without reasonable cause. Art. 264(2): the competent authority may, in addition to or instead of the Art. 262 penalty, issue a warning or require the person to take the steps necessary to avoid the violation or to remedy its effects. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (Arts. 262, 264) and `[authority — MoC]` (list of triggering failures) 
 Diligence implication (practice) | For each Saudi entity in a target group, obtain: the UBO register; the Ministry filing acknowledgement (initial disclosure) and the latest annual confirmation; any 15-day update filings since the last confirmation; and each shareholder-of-record's own-account confirmation or nominee disclosure. Reconcile the register against the cap table computed under the tier-1 method, then test tier 2 against the shareholders' agreement, powers of attorney, bank mandates, and financing arrangements. A mismatch between the filed UBO and the persons who in fact control the business is both a Rules breach (Art. 262 exposure) and a tasattur indicator where the controller is a non-Saudi without an investment registration. | `[model knowledge — verify]` 

FILE personal-data-protection-law
 Article | Rule (English) | Tag 
---|---|---
 Art. 1 (definitions; "sensitive data", "disclosure", "destruction", "data subject" as amended by M/148) | "Personal data": any data, whatever its source or form, that identifies an individual specifically or makes him identifiable directly or indirectly (name, ID number, addresses, contact numbers, licence and property numbers, bank and card numbers, images, and other personal data). "Processing": any operation on personal data by manual or automated means (collection, recording, storage, modification, use, disclosure, transfer, publication, erasure, destruction, etc.). "Controller": any public entity or private natural or juristic person that determines the purpose and manner of processing, whether it processes itself or through a processor. "Processor": any public entity or private natural or juristic person that processes personal data for the benefit of and on behalf of the controller. "Sensitive data" (amended): data on racial or ethnic origin, religious, intellectual or political belief, security and criminal data, biometric identification data, genetic data, health data, and data indicating unknown parentage. "Disclosure" (amended): enabling any person other than the controller or the processor, as the case may be, to obtain, use or view personal data. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 2 | The Law applies to any processing of personal data relating to individuals that takes place in the Kingdom by any means, including processing of personal data of individuals resident in the Kingdom by any entity outside the Kingdom; it includes data of the deceased if it identifies him or a family member. Excluded: an individual's processing for purposes not exceeding personal or family use, provided he does not publish or disclose it (Regulation Art. 2 defines personal and family use). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 5 (as amended: "explicit" replaces "written"), 7; Regulation Art. 11, 12 | Except in the cases the Law provides, personal data may not be processed, nor its purpose changed, without the data subject's consent; the Regulations set the conditions of consent, the cases where consent must be explicit, and guardian consent for persons lacking capacity. Consent may be withdrawn at any time. Consent may not be a condition of a service or benefit unless the service is related to the processing consented to. Regulation Art. 11: consent may be in any appropriate form (written, verbal, electronic) if freely given, for clear and specific purposes explained before or at the time of the request, by a person with full capacity, documented so it can be verified later, and separate for each purpose; consent must be explicit for sensitive data, credit data, and decisions based solely on automated processing. Regulation Art. 12: withdrawal must be as easy as giving consent; processing stops without undue delay; withdrawal does not affect prior processing or processing on another basis; the controller must notify recipients and ask them to destroy the data. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 6 (as amended: paragraph 4 added), 10 (as amended), 15 (as amended); Regulation Art. 16 | Processing does not require consent where: (1) it serves an actual interest of the data subject and contacting him is impossible or difficult; (2) it is under another law or in performance of a prior agreement to which the data subject is party; (3) the controller is a public entity and processing is required for security or judicial purposes; (4) (added by M/148) processing is necessary for the controller's legitimate interests, provided it does not prejudice the data subject's rights or conflict with his interests and the data are not sensitive; the Regulations set the controls. Art. 10 (amended) allows collection from a source other than the data subject and processing for a new purpose on the same grounds plus public availability, vital interests, public health or safety, anonymised data, and (7) legitimate interest on the same conditions. Art. 15 (amended) allows disclosure on the same grounds including (6) legitimate interest. Regulation Art. 16: legitimate interest is not available to public entities; conditions: lawful purpose, balancing, no sensitive data, within the data subject's reasonable expectations; examples: fraud detection, network and information security; a documented legitimate-interest assessment is required before processing. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 8 (as amended); Regulation Art. 17(1) | When choosing a processor, the controller must choose one that provides the guarantees necessary to implement the Law and Regulations, and must verify the processor's compliance with the Law and Regulations, without prejudice to the controller's own responsibility to the data subject or the Competent Authority; the Regulations set the rules, including for any subsequent contracts made by the processor. Regulation Art. 17(1): the controller must ensure the processor gives sufficient guarantees and that the agreement with the processor includes: (a) purpose of processing; (b) categories of personal data; (c) duration of processing; (d) the processor's commitment to notify the controller without undue delay of any personal data breach; (e) whether the processor is subject to regulations of other countries and the effect on its compliance with the Law; (f) that the data subject's prior consent is not required for disclosures mandatory under Saudi law, provided the processor notifies the controller of such disclosure; (g) identification of any subcontractors or other party to whom personal data will be disclosed. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Regulation Art. 17(2)-(5) | The controller must issue clear instructions to the processor; the processor must notify the controller in writing without undue delay of any breach of those instructions or of Saudi law. The controller must periodically assess the processor's compliance (it may appoint an independent third party) covering processing by the processor or third parties acting for it. A processor that breaches the controller's instructions or the agreement is treated as a controller and is directly accountable. Before engaging a sub-processor the processor must take sufficient guarantees that protection is not reduced, choose only sub-processors giving sufficient guarantees, and obtain the controller's prior acceptance, notifying the controller before contracting and allowing it to object within a period agreed between controller and processor. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 11 (paragraph 4 as amended), 14; Regulation Art. 19 | The purpose of collection must relate directly to the controller's purposes and not conflict with any legal provision; collection methods must be appropriate, direct, clear, secure and free of deception; content must be adequate and limited to the minimum necessary for the purpose; once data are no longer necessary, collection must stop and what was collected must be destroyed without delay. The controller may not process data without adequate steps to verify accuracy, completeness, currency and relevance. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 12 (as amended), 13 (as amended) | The controller must adopt a privacy policy and make it available to data subjects when collecting their data, covering purpose, content of data collected, method of collection, means of storage, manner of processing and destruction, the data subject's rights and how to exercise them. When collecting directly from the data subject the controller must inform him, at collection, of: the legal basis for collection; the purpose, whether collection is mandatory or optional, and that the data will not be processed inconsistently with the purpose except under Art. 10; the identity and address of the collector; the entities to which the data will be disclosed and whether the data will be transferred, disclosed or processed outside the Kingdom; the consequences of not completing collection; the Art. 4 rights; other elements set by the Regulations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 18 (paragraph 1 as amended) | The controller must destroy personal data without delay once the purpose of collection ends; it may retain the data if everything identifying the data subject is removed per the Regulations. It must retain the data after the purpose ends where a legal requirement mandates retention for a set period (destroy at the later of that period or the purpose's end) or where the data are closely connected to a case before a judicial body (destroy after proceedings end). | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 19; Regulation Art. 23 | The controller must take the organisational, administrative and technical measures necessary to protect personal data, including during transfer, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 23: implement security measures against breach; adopt the National Cybersecurity Authority's controls, standards and rules, or recognised best practice and cybersecurity standards if not bound by the NCA's. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 20 (as amended); Regulation Art. 24 | The controller must notify the Competent Authority on becoming aware of a leak, damage or unlawful access to personal data, per the Regulations; and must notify the data subject if the incident may cause harm to his data or conflict with his rights or interests, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 24: notify the Competent Authority within no more than 72 hours of becoming aware, if the incident potentially harms the data or the data subject or conflicts with his rights or interests; the notification must describe the incident (time, date, circumstances, time of awareness), data categories and numbers of affected data subjects, risks and mitigation measures, whether data subjects were notified, and contact details of the controller or DPO; missing information is supplied as soon as possible with reasons; the controller keeps a copy of reports and corrective measures; NCA reporting duties are unaffected; data subjects are notified without undue delay in clear language with a description, risks, contact details and recommendations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 22; Regulation Art. 25 | The controller must assess the effects of processing personal data for any product or service offered to the public, according to its activity, per the Regulations. Regulation Art. 25: a written impact assessment is required for processing sensitive data; combining or linking two or more datasets from different sources; large-scale processing of persons lacking capacity, constant monitoring, new technologies, or automated decision-making; and products or services likely to cause serious harm to privacy; minimum content listed; a copy must be given to any processor acting for the controller; re-assessment if harm is indicated. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 24 (paragraph 1 as amended), 25, 26 | Credit data: the Regulations must require verification of the data subject's explicit consent (M/148 replaced "written") for collection, change of purpose, disclosure or publication, and notice to him when a disclosure request is received. Direct marketing: personal contact means may be used to send advertising or awareness material only with the recipient's consent and with a clear opt-out mechanism. Marketing processing is allowed for non-sensitive data collected directly from the data subject with his consent under the Law, per the Regulations. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 29 (as amended by M/148) | (1) Subject to paragraph (2), a controller may transfer personal data outside the Kingdom or disclose it to a party outside the Kingdom for any of these purposes: (a) performance of an obligation under an agreement to which the Kingdom is party; (b) serving the Kingdom's interests; (c) performance of an obligation to which the data subject is party; (d) other purposes set by the Regulations. (2) Conditions: (a) the transfer or disclosure must not prejudice national security or the Kingdom's vital interests; (b) an appropriate level of protection for personal data must exist outside the Kingdom, not lower than that of the Law and Regulations, according to an assessment by the Competent Authority in coordination with the relevant bodies; (c) the transfer or disclosure is limited to the minimum data needed. (3) Paragraph (2) does not apply in cases of extreme necessity to protect the data subject's life or vital interests, or to prevent, examine or treat infection. (4) The Regulations set the rules, standards and procedures, including the cases in which controllers are exempted from conditions (2)(b) and (2)(c), and the controls and procedures for exemption and its withdrawal. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Transfer Regulation (not read) — mechanisms under Art. 29(4) | Under the Regulation on Personal Data Transfer Outside the Kingdom (as amended September 2024): "other purposes" include central processing within a group, provision of services or products to the data subject, scientific research, and other listed purposes; where the destination lacks a SDAIA adequacy decision, a controller may transfer under appropriate safeguards, namely SDAIA's Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Common Rules for intra-group transfers, or a certification of compliance, with a documented transfer risk assessment in prescribed cases; narrow exceptions exist for specific situations (e.g., performance of a contract with the data subject, vital interests). | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Localisation (no PDPL article) | The PDPL contains no general data-localisation or in-Kingdom hosting requirement; localisation obligations, where they exist, come from sector regulators and the NCA (e.g., the NCA's cloud controls and government-data rules, SAMA's outsourcing rules, CST's cloud framework and their data-classification levels). | `[model knowledge — verify]` 
 Art. 30 (as amended); Regulation Art. 32 | The Competent Authority supervises the Law and Regulations, without prejudice to the Saudi Central Bank's powers. The Regulations set the cases in which a controller must appoint or designate one or more persons as personal data protection officer and their responsibilities. Controllers must cooperate with the Competent Authority, which may request documents and information, use other bodies for support, set monitoring tools including a national register of controllers, provide services through that register for a fee, and delegate some tasks. Regulation Art. 32: a DPO is mandatory where the controller is a public entity providing services involving large-scale processing; where the controller's primary activities require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects; or where its core activities involve processing sensitive data. The DPO may be an executive, an employee or an external contractor; responsibilities include being the contact point for the Competent Authority, supervising impact assessments and audits, enabling data-subject rights, notifying breaches, maintaining records and handling violations. The Competent Authority issues DPO appointment rules. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 31; Regulation Art. 33, 34 | The controller must keep records of processing activities for a period set by the Regulations, available to the Competent Authority on request, containing at least: controller contact details; purpose; categories of data subjects; recipients of disclosure; whether data are transferred or disclosed outside the Kingdom; expected retention period. Regulation Art. 33: written records kept throughout processing and for five years after processing ends, accurate and current, produced to the Competent Authority on request; minimum content adds DPO information, data categories, retention periods per category, categories of recipients, description of transfers outside the Kingdom with legal basis and recipients, and security measures; SDAIA provides templates. Regulation Art. 34: SDAIA issues rules for registration in the National Register of Controllers, specifying which controllers must register. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 33 (as amended) | The Competent Authority sets the requirements for practising commercial, professional or non-profit activities related to personal data protection in the Kingdom; may license bodies to issue accreditation certificates to controllers and processors; may license bodies to audit or examine processing activities; and sets the tools and mechanisms for monitoring compliance by entities outside the Kingdom that process data of residents, and the procedures for enforcing the Law outside the Kingdom. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 35 (as amended) | Criminal offence: whoever discloses or publishes sensitive data in breach of the Law with intent to harm the data subject or to obtain a personal benefit is punished with imprisonment up to two years and/or a fine up to SAR 3 million. The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes before the competent court; the court may double the fine on recidivism, up to twice the maximum. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 36 (paragraph 2 as amended), 37, 38 | Administrative penalties for any other breach of the Law or Regulations by a private natural or juristic person: warning or a fine up to SAR 5 million; the fine may be doubled for repeat breaches up to twice the maximum (SAR 10 million). A committee formed by the head of the Competent Authority (at least three members including a technical specialist and a legal adviser) imposes them according to type, gravity and impact, subject to approval by the head of the Authority; decisions may be challenged before the competent court. Authority staff have inspection and seizure powers and may call on criminal-enforcement bodies. The court may confiscate proceeds; the court or committee may order publication of the judgment or decision summary at the offender's expense once final. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 40, 41 | Without prejudice to the penalties, anyone harmed by a breach of the Law or Regulations may claim compensation before the competent court for material or moral damage proportionate to the harm. Everyone who has carried out processing must keep data secrets confidential even after the end of his employment or contractual relationship. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` 
 Art. 4 (as amended), 9, 21; Regulation Art. 3 (note only) | Data subjects have the rights to be informed of the legal basis and purpose of collection; to access their data; to obtain a copy in a readable, clear format; to correction, completion or updating; and to destruction of data no longer needed, all per the Regulations. Controllers may set periods and limits for access in stated cases. Requests must be answered within the period and by the means set by the Regulations: Regulation Art. 3 gives 30 days, extendable by 30 days with notice where effort is disproportionate or requests are multiple, with identity verification and a record of all requests. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` `[authority — SDAIA]` 
 Art. 42, 43 (as amended); Council of Ministers Resolution 98 items Second and Fourth | The head of the Competent Authority issues the Regulations within 720 days of the Law's issue, after coordination with the ministries and authorities named. The Law is in force 720 days after publication. Council of Ministers Resolution 98: SDAIA is the Competent Authority for two years, during which transfer of supervision to the National Data Management Office is to be considered in light of implementation results and sector maturity; controllers must regularise within one year of the effective date, extendable by the Competent Authority. | `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]` `[BOE — Arabic]` (status of the NDMO transfer: `[model knowledge — verify]`) 

