71:| Art. 132 | If a JSC's losses reach half of its issued capital, the board must within 60 days of learning of it disclose the fact and its recommendations, and call the EGA to meet within 180 days of that date to decide whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | إذا بلغت خسائر شركة المساهمة (نصف) رأس المال المصدر، وجب على مجلس الإدارة الإفصاح عن ذلك وعما توصل إليه من توصيات بشأن تلك الخسائر خلال (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ علمه ببلوغها هذا المقدار، ودعوة الجمعية العامة غير العادية إلى الاجتماع خلال (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ العلم بذلك للنظر في استمرار الشركة ... أو حلّها. | Losses trigger for diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of issued capital (from the latest financial statements or management accounts) → check for the board disclosure within 60 days and the EGA within 180 days. Unlike the 2015 Law there is no automatic dissolution for failing to act `[model knowledge — verify]`, but failure to call the assembly is a criminal offence under Art. 261(h) and the assembly may resolve to dissolve. Continuation usually involves a capital reduction (Art. 134 allows reduction below SAR 500,000 for losses) or shareholder recapitalisation. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
80:| Art. 165 | An LLC has a general assembly of all partners, convened by the manager(s) as the articles provide, at least once a year within six months after fiscal year end; it may be called at any time at the request of the managers, the auditor, or partners holding at least 10% of capital; notice goes to all partners by registered letter, modern technology or any other means in the articles at least 21 days before the meeting; partners representing all interests may meet without observing notice formalities and periods; deliberations, decisions and decisions by circulation are recorded in minutes entered in a special register kept for the purpose, and technology may be used to record them; meetings and voting may be held by technology. | تنعقد الجمعية العامة للشركاء بدعوة من المدير أو المديرين ... على أن تنعقد مرة على الأقل في السنة خلال الأشهر (الستة) التالية لنهاية السنة المالية للشركة ... وذلك قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة (بواحد وعشرين) يومًا على الأقل. تثبت مداولات الجمعية العامة للشركاء وقراراتها أو قرارات الشركاء بالتمرير في محاضر تدون في سجل خاص تعده الشركة لهذا الغرض. | LLC annual compliance: assembly within six months of year end (same window as the financial-statements deposit), 21 calendar days' notice unless 100% of interests attend, and a minutes register (paper or electronic) containing every resolution including written ones. The absence of a minutes register is a common diligence finding in foreign-owned LLCs. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
82:| Arts. 167-169; Regs Art. 65 | The manager prepares for each fiscal year the financial statements, an activity and financial-position report and dividend proposals, and places them at the auditor's disposal at least 45 days before the annual assembly; he provides partners with the statements, report and auditor's report (by technology or as the articles provide) at least 21 days before the annual assembly and deposits them as the Regulations specify (Art. 167). The annual agenda must include the manager's report, the financial statements, the auditor's report and the dividend proposal (Art. 168). The assembly may not deliberate outside the agenda unless facts emerge at the meeting; a partner may require an item to be added, and may appeal to the assembly if refused; every partner may discuss and question, the manager must answer, and the assembly decides if an answer is inadequate (Art. 169). Regs Art. 65: the manager sends the activity report to the Ministry at least 21 days before the annual assembly. | يعد مدير الشركة عن كل سنة مالية القوائم المالية للشركة وتقريرًا عن نشاطها ومركزها المالي ... ويضع المدير هذه الوثائق تحت تصرف مراجع الحسابات -إن وجد- قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة في اجتماعها السنوي (بخمسة وأربعين) يومًا على الأقل. (اللائحة م65) على مدير الشركة أن يزود الوزارة بتقرير عن نشاط الشركة قبل الموعد المحدد لانعقاد الجمعية العامة السنوي (بواحد وعشرين) يومًا على الأقل. | LLC annual timeline mirrors the JSC: D-45 to auditor; D-21 to partners and the Ministry; assembly and deposit within six months of year end. The Ministry filing of the manager's activity report is frequently overlooked in practice `[model knowledge — verify]`. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
83:| Arts. 172-173; Regs Art. 64 | The articles of incorporation, including capital increases and reductions, may be amended with the approval of partners holding at least three quarters of the capital, unless the articles require more; on a cash increase by new interests each partner has pre-emption pro rata to his holding under the Regulations; an increase by raising the nominal value of interests, or suspension of pre-emption, requires unanimity (Art. 172). Except for criminal matters, the articles may refer disputes between partners or between the company and its managers to arbitration or other ADR (Art. 173). Regs Art. 64: a partner who takes less than his entitlement leaves the balance to the other partners pro rata; the partners file a draft amendment with a declaration that the new interests are paid, and the increase is effective on registration and publication with the Commercial Register. | يجوز تعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة بما في ذلك زيادة رأس مالها أو تخفيضه بموافقة شريك أو أكثر يمثلون (ثلاثة أرباع) رأس المال على الأقل، ما لم ينص عقد التأسيس على نسبة أكبر. لا يجوز زيادة رأس المال عن طريق رفع القيمة الاسمية لحصص الشركاء أو وقف العمل بحق الأولوية، إلا بإجماع الشركاء. | Formula for LLC special decisions: ≥ 75% of capital (absolute) to amend the articles, change capital, transform, merge or divide (Arts. 220, 225, 232 refer back to the amendment quorum); unanimity to suspend pre-emption or raise nominal value. The 2015-Law rule requiring unanimity to change the company's nationality or increase partners' obligations is not in the Art. 172 text read `[model knowledge — verify]`; Art. 3 fixes Saudi nationality in any event, and a JSC-style "no increased financial burdens without all shareholders" rule appears only in Art. 85. A 25%+1 partner holds a veto over every amendment. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
86:| Art. 182 | If an LLC's losses reach half of its capital, the manager must call the general assembly of partners to meet within 60 days of learning that losses reached that level, to consider whether the company continues (with measures to address the losses) or is dissolved. | إذا بلغت خسائر الشركة نصف رأس مالها، وجب على مدير الشركة دعوة الجمعية العامة للشركاء إلى الاجتماع خلال (ستين) يومًا من تاريخ العلم ببلوغ الخسارة هذا المقدار للنظر في استمرار الشركة مع اتخاذ أي من الإجراءات اللازمة لمعالجة تلك الخسائر، أو حلها. | Losses trigger for LLC diligence: accumulated losses ≥ 50% of registered capital → assembly within 60 days of knowledge. The text imposes no automatic dissolution and no partner liability for the company's debts for failing to act (the 2015-Law joint liability of partners has been dropped in the text read `[model knowledge — verify]`), but the manager commits an offence under Art. 261(h) if he fails to call the assembly. Foreign-owned LLCs funded by shareholder loans often sit above the threshold; a recapitalisation or loss-absorbing reduction (Art. 176) is the usual cure. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [BOE — official English] |
96:| Arts. 260-261 | Imprisonment up to three years and/or a fine up to SAR 5,000,000 for: (a) any manager, officer, director, auditor or liquidator who deliberately records false or misleading information in the financial statements, reports, capital-reduction statements, solvency statements or other statements put to the owners, or deliberately omits a material fact to misrepresent the financial position; (b) any manager, officer or director who uses the company's funds, powers or votes knowingly against its interests for personal ends, to favour a company or person, or to benefit from a transaction in which he has an interest; (c) a liquidator who does likewise or prefers a creditor without lawful cause (Art. 260). Imprisonment up to one year and/or a fine up to SAR 1,000,000 for, among others: an auditor who fails to report apparent criminal violations to the company; vote-buying; falsely announcing registration of an unregistered company; a public official disclosing company secrets; publishing false names to attract subscriptions; deciding, paying or receiving dividends in bad faith contrary to the Law or the articles (and an auditor who fails to report it); false declarations on in-kind valuations, allocation of interests/shares or payment of their value; any manager, officer, director or auditor who fails to call (or to take steps to call) the assembly on learning that losses reached the Arts. 132/182 threshold; exploiting or disclosing company secrets to harm the company; deliberately obstructing persons entitled to inspect records; a court-appointed inspector who falsifies or omits material facts (Art. 261). | يعاقب بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (ثلاث) سنوات وبغرامة لا تزيد على (5,000,000) خمسة ملايين ريال، أو بإحدى هاتين العقوبتين: كل مدير أو مسؤول أو عضو مجلس إدارة أو مراجع حسابات أو مصفّ، سجَّل متعمدًا بيانات أو معلومات كاذبة أو مضللة في القوائم المالية. يعاقب بالسجن مدة لا تزيد على (سنة) وبغرامة لا تزيد على (1,000,000) مليون ريال ... كل مدير أو مسؤول أو عضو مجلس إدارة أو مراجع حسابات، لم يدعُ الجمعية العامة للشركاء أو المساهمين ... عند علمه ببلوغ الخسائر الحدود المقدرة. | Personal criminal exposure of nominee managers/directors: false financial statements or solvency statements (3 years/SAR 5m) and failure to convene the losses assembly (1 year/SAR 1m). Diligence on a loss-making target should therefore check the losses-assembly minutes; integration plans should regularise before new managers are appointed. Public Prosecution investigates these offences (Art. 265). | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
97:| Arts. 262-267, 269-271; Regs Art. 94 | Fine up to SAR 500,000 for violations including: obstructing the calling or holding of an assembly or preventing an owner from attending or voting; failing to call the annual assembly within the statutory period; accepting or retaining JSC board membership contrary to the Law (and directors who knew and did not object); a director obtaining a loan or guarantee contrary to the Law; failing to keep accounting records or to prepare or deposit financial statements; failing to provide the competent authority with required documents; failing to make documents available to owners; failing to prepare and record minutes; omitting the Art. 12 particulars; acting as auditor despite a disqualification; failing to register the company or an amendment of its articles with the Commercial Register; deliberately recording untrue data in the articles, the application or supporting documents (and those who sign or register them knowingly); professional-company breaches; a liquidator failing to register his appointment or the end of liquidation; failing to take corrective action after notification of a violation; an auditor failing in statutory duties; any company or officer failing to apply the Law and Regulations or to comply with the competent authority's rules and decisions without reasonable cause (Art. 262). A Ministry committee of at least three members chaired by a legally qualified person imposes these fines (except for listed JSCs, where the CMA Board does); the Minister may designate violations penalised directly without the committee; the penalised person may appeal to the competent court within 30 days of notification; Regs Art. 94: notification may be electronic to a mobile number or e-mail registered with the Commercial Register or the National Information Center (Art. 267). Penalties are graduated by gravity and circumstances; repeat offences under Arts. 260-261 within three years of a final conviction double the penalty (Art. 263). The court may, in addition to or instead of Arts. 260-261 penalties, warn, order preventive or corrective steps, order cessation, or ban from the board of a listed JSC; the competent authority may warn or order corrective steps instead of Art. 262 fines (Art. 264). The Public Prosecution investigates and prosecutes Arts. 260-261 offences (Art. 265). The competent court hears all civil and criminal cases under the Law and Regulations except for listed JSCs, which go to the Committee for the Resolution of Securities Disputes (Art. 266). Penalties do not prejudice civil claims for damages (Art. 269). The competent authority supervises companies' compliance with the Law and their articles, may inspect, examine accounts and demand records and minutes through its officers or experts, and may send observers to general assemblies (Art. 270); all company officers must give Ministry (and, for listed or listing companies, CMA) representatives access to records and information (Art. 271). | يعاقب بغرامة لا تزيد على (500,000) خمسمائة ألف ريال: ... كل من لم يؤدِّ واجبه في دعوة الجمعية العامة للشركاء أو المساهمين إلى الانعقاد خلال المدة المقررة لانعقادها؛ ... كل من أهمل في أداء واجبه في إعداد محاضر الاجتماعات وتدوينها؛ ... كل من تخلف عن قيد تعديل عقد تأسيس الشركة أو نظامها الأساس لدى السجل التجاري. تتولى المحكمة المختصة النظر والفصل في جميع الدعاوى المدنية والجزائية والمنازعات الناشئة عن تطبيق أحكام النظام واللوائح ... وذلك فيما عدا ما يتعلق بشركات المساهمة المدرجة في السوق المالية. | The administrative-fine list is effectively the entity-compliance checklist: annual assembly on time, financial statements deposited, minutes kept, amendments registered, documents bearing the Art. 12 particulars, auditor properly qualified. Committee decisions are notified electronically to the registered mobile/e-mail, so keep Commercial Register contact data current or appeal windows (30 days) will be missed. Forum: Commercial Court for unlisted companies (the "competent court" under the Commercial Courts Law `[model knowledge — verify]`), CRSD for listed JSCs. Ministry inspection powers extend to minutes and registers, which is why registers must exist in Arabic-readable form. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
98:| Arts. 274, 277 | The CMA supervises listed JSCs and issues their rules, including merger rules where a party is listed; the competent authority issues a corporate-governance regulation for JSCs (and the Ministry may issue one for other companies); the Ministry sets rules to obtain beneficial-ownership (المستفيد الحقيقي) information from companies other than listed JSCs; the Minister and the CMA Board issue the necessary rules (Art. 274). The Minister and the CMA Board issue the Regulations within 180 days of publication, covering rules, periods, procedures, documents and the use of technology for notices, meetings, participation and voting; any procedure under the Law or Regulations may be electronic, including incorporation and amendment applications, registration and publication, signature of applications, documents and registers, and deposit of financial statements (Art. 277). | للوزارة وضع القواعد والإجراءات اللازمة التي تكفل حصولها على معلومات المستفيد الحقيقي من الشركات الخاضعة لأحكام النظام وذلك فيما عدا ما يتعلق بشركات المساهمة المدرجة في السوق المالية. يجوز أن يكون أي من الإجراءات المنصوص عليها في النظام، أو اللوائح؛ إلكترونيًّا. | Closing checklist: the UBO register/filing with the Ministry must be updated after a share transfer; the Ministry's UBO rules (issued under Art. 274(3)) were not read for this file `[model knowledge — verify]` — obtain them before advising on thresholds or deadlines. Electronic execution and filing of corporate documents has express statutory backing. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] |
99:| Arts. 280-281; Royal Decree M/132 cl. 2-3; Regs Art. 95 | The Law replaces the Companies Law (M/3 of 28/1/1437H) and the Professional Companies Law (M/17 of 26/1/1441H) and repeals conflicting provisions (Art. 280); it enters into force 180 days after publication in the Official Gazette (Art. 281). Decree cl. 2: the Law does not prejudice the powers of the Saudi Central Bank and the CMA. Decree cl. 3: companies existing at the effective date must conform to the Law within two years from that date; exceptionally, the Ministry and the CMA determine which provisions apply to them during that period. Regs Art. 95: the Regulations apply from the Law's effective date. | على الشركات القائمة عند نفاذ النظام ... تعديل أوضاعها وفقًا لأحكامه خلال مدة لا تزيد على (سنتين) تبدأ من تاريخ نفاذه. يعمل بالنظام بعد (مائة وثمانين) يومًا من تاريخ نشره في الجريدة الرسمية. (اللائحة م95) تسري أحكام اللائحة من تاريخ نفاذ النظام. | The transition ended 19 January 2025; a company whose registered articles still follow the 2015-Law templates (e.g. old statutory-reserve or partner-liability-on-losses wording, "one-person company" terminology) is now non-conforming and its amendment is a closing-checklist item. Corporate documents dated before 19 January 2023 must be read against the 2015 Law, whose rules differed on several points flagged in this file. | [settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — MoC] |
107:- LLC unanimity matters: Art. 172 requires unanimity only for raising nominal value and suspending pre-emption; confirm that no other provision (or Ministry model articles) reintroduces the 2015-Law unanimity for changing nationality or increasing partners' obligations, and whether Ministry model articles are mandatory in content.
108:- Losses rule consequences: confirm in practice whether the Ministry treats a failure to hold the Art. 182/132 assembly as grounds for administrative action beyond the Art. 261(h) offence, and whether partners face any residual liability (the 2015-Law joint liability is absent from the text read).
120:- **corporate-legal `entity-compliance`** — load at the step where the annual compliance calendar is built. Cite Arts. 16-17 (financial statements within six months; Regs Art. 5 electronic deposit), Arts. 18-20 (auditor appointment and rotation), Art. 88 (JSC annual assembly within six months), Art. 165 (LLC annual assembly within six months, 21-day notice), Arts. 121-122 and 167 with Regs Arts. 31, 65 (45-day auditor and 21-day shareholder/Ministry deliveries), Art. 147 (SJSC six-month presentation), Art. 238 (branch accounts within six months), Art. 112 (JSC register updates within 15 days), Arts. 93 and 148 (registration of resolutions within 15 days), Arts. 262-267 (fines and appeal window). Cross-reference `filing-calendar.md` for the consolidated dates; where that file and this file differ, this file's article rows control and the discrepancy must be flagged. Computed dates: "annual assembly deadline = fiscal year end + 6 months (Art. 88 / Art. 165)"; "auditor pack date = assembly date − 45 calendar days (Art. 121 / Art. 167)"; "shareholder/Ministry pack date = assembly date − 21 calendar days (Art. 122 / Art. 167; Regs Arts. 31, 65)"; all days are calendar days (Art. 1). Attach the tag of the rows used to every computed date.
121:- **corporate-legal `written-consent`** — load when drafting or validating a resolution by circulation. JSC board: Art. 82 (urgent matters only; majority of all members; ratification at next meeting) and Art. 83 (minutes register). JSC shareholders: Arts. 100-101 (articles clause, chairman's proposal, circulation to all, > 50% / ≥ 75% of all voting rights, excluded matters, register entry). SJSC: Art. 149 (articles clause and articles quorum only; no statutory fallback). LLC: Art. 166 (written vote; > 50% of capital; second-round meeting rule), Art. 172 (≥ 75% for amendments; unanimity items), Art. 165(5) (minutes register), Regs Art. 63 (manager removal at > 50%). Single-owner entities: Arts. 98, 150, 157 (written decisions entered in the register). Formula outputs must state the article and the tag. If the articles of incorporation/association are not available to the skill, it must say that the statutory default is being applied and that the articles may set a higher threshold.
122:- **corporate-legal `board-minutes`** — load at the template-selection step. JSC board: Art. 80 (call, quorum half, majority, casting vote, technology), Art. 81 (proxies only under an articles clause, one per member), Art. 83 (secretary drafts; signed by chair, attending directors and secretary; register), Art. 28 (named dissents), Art. 71 (interest declarations recorded, abstention), Art. 76 (attendance disclosure in board report). Assemblies: Arts. 90-91 and Regs Art. 23 (convening, 21-day notice content), Arts. 92-93 (quorum and majorities), Art. 97 (minutes content and signatories), Art. 84 and Regs Arts. 24-30 (electronic participation, proxies, authentication). LLC assemblies: Arts. 165, 168-169. Language of record: Art. 7 requires Arabic only for the articles; the Law is silent on minutes — the skill must flag the `[model knowledge — verify]` note in the Art. 83 row and default to bilingual minutes with an Arabic text.
123:- **corporate-legal `diligence-issue-extraction`** and **`tabular-review`** — load at the corporate-documents category step. Extraction targets and rows: share-transfer restrictions (Art. 178 LLC pre-emption and 30-day window; Art. 111 and Arts. 151-154 JSC/SJSC restrictions; Art. 44 and Art. 56 partnerships; Art. 25 registration as the transfer event); change-of-control and major-transaction approvals (Art. 75 sale of > 50% of assets; Art. 93 three-quarters matters; Art. 172 three-quarters matters; Art. 220 transformation; Art. 230 90% squeeze-out/sell-out); drag/tag (Arts. 113, 181, 90% insertion vote); losses trigger (Art. 132 JSC: 60-day disclosure and 180-day EGA; Art. 182 LLC: 60-day assembly; Art. 261(h) offence); related-party approvals (Art. 27 with Regs Arts. 16-17 thresholds; Art. 71 disclosure and abstention; Art. 72 director loans void; Regs Art. 62 LLC application); auditor status (Arts. 18-19; Regs Arts. 6-7); registers and minutes (Arts. 83, 97, 112, 148, 165); unpaid capital (Arts. 15, 105, 115); capital reductions (Arts. 135, 176); historic dividends (Art. 22); pledges (Art. 114, Art. 180, Regs Arts. 46-50, 66-69); conformity with the 2022 Law after the transition (Decree cl. 3 row). Materiality wording must cite the article; where a finding depends on the target's articles, the skill must request them rather than assume the statutory default.
124:- **corporate-legal `closing-checklist`** — load at checklist generation. LLC share purchase: Art. 178 waivers or expiry of the redemption window; Art. 172 approval of the amended articles (≥ 75% or higher per articles); Art. 8 and Regs Art. 2 (written amendment, identity verification, Commercial Register registration); Art. 25 (transfer effective on registration); Art. 162 (register new manager and any power limits); Art. 274(3) UBO update (`[model knowledge — verify]` for the Ministry's rules); Art. 12 stationery and document particulars; Art. 11 SHA/family-charter conformity. Unlisted JSC/SJSC: Art. 112 register entry and 15-day Commercial Register update; Art. 91(3) or Art. 146(6) waiver meeting; Art. 93(5) or Art. 148(2) 15-day registration of resolutions; Regs Arts. 46-50 pledge entry or release in the shareholders' register. Pre-closing corporate approvals of the seller/buyer: Art. 75 (asset sale > 50%), Art. 93 (3/4 matters), Art. 27/71 (related-party authorisation). MISA licence amendment and other regulator consents are outside this file and must be flagged as such.
125:- **corporate-legal `integration-management`** — load at the legal-entity rationalisation step. Statutory merger: Arts. 225-229 and Regs Art. 87 (proposal at the amendment quorum; valuation; 30-day announcement before the vote; 15-day creditor objection from the announcement; court application ≥ 10 days before the vote; effective on Commercial Register registration; universal succession; simplified intra-group merger). Division: Arts. 231-234 and Regs Arts. 89-92 (21-day proposal circulation; joint liability to pre-division creditors). Transformation: Arts. 220-224 (amendment quorum, unanimity for SJSC, 15-day dissenter exit, 30-day creditor notice for partnerships). Dissolution of surplus entities: Arts. 242-259 and Regs Art. 93 (solvency statement, 60-day liquidator appointment, 3-year cap, short-form for never-traded companies). Managers'/directors' personal exposure: Arts. 28, 260-261. Competition, CMA and MISA gates are flagged `[model knowledge — verify]` in the merger row and must be raised separately.

