| IP | None | 0 | Open |
| Data | None | 0 | Open |

The profile requires review of contracts **above SAR 500,000 annually** and all pending litigation. Each customer contract exceeds SAR 800,000 annually and qualifies. Their aggregate stated annual value exceeds **SAR 2.4 million** `[computed from user-supplied values: three contracts × more than SAR 800,000]`.

The management and distribution agreements are included despite missing fixed annual values because they determine control, economics and regulatory eligibility `[review]`. No currency conversion was necessary.

**Steps 2.5–5: Findings by house category**

Red denotes an issue affecting deal value, structure or ability to close; Yellow denotes an issue requiring attention or further evidence. Ratings are recommendations for counsel’s review.

**Corporate**

**Issue 1 — 🔴 Red: LLC losses exceed the statutory trigger `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§9 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Accumulated losses are SAR 280,000 against SAR 500,000 capital. The statutory threshold is SAR 250,000; losses are **56% of capital**, exceeding the threshold by SAR 30,000.

`[computed — companies-law.md Art. 182, settled 2026-09-04; inputs: capital SAR 500,000, losses SAR 280,000; 500,000 × 50% = 250,000; 280,000 ÷ 500,000 = 56%; excess = 30,000]`

Article 182 requires the manager to call a partners’ assembly to meet within **60 calendar days of learning** that losses reached half of capital, to consider continuation with remedial measures or dissolution. Its operative wording includes: “وجب على مدير الشركة دعوة الجمعية العامة للشركاء”. No management knowledge date, meeting notice, minutes or remedial resolution is supplied. Failure to take the required action engages Article 261(h), subject to the facts. Neither automatic dissolution nor unlimited partner liability is established by these extracts.

**Authority:** `companies-law.md`, Arts. 1, 182 and 261(h), `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. [BOE Companies Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/a8376aea-1bc3-49d4-9027-aed900b555af/1).

**Recommendation:** Obtain the knowledge date, current financial statements and the assembly record; require a documented continuation and funding plan before closing `[review]`. No meeting deadline can be calculated from “FY2025” alone.

The combination of losses, contribution arrears and related-party economics also warrants a solvency review. Examine relevant dispositions within the statutory **12-month non-related-party / 24-month related-party** windows before any bankruptcy opening, including recent payments under the management agreement. The agreement’s 2023 signature date does not exclude later payments from review. No opening date or avoidable transaction is established. **Authority:** `bankruptcy-law.md`, Arts. 210–212 and 227, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. [BOE Bankruptcy Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/68204119-84f1-4789-8fad-a9ec014c3788/1).

**Issue 2 — 🔴 Red: Both partners are needed for constitutional amendments `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§2, article 14 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The supplied articles require **75% of capital for partners’ decisions and unanimity for amendments**. Article 172 permits a higher constitutional threshold than its statutory three-quarters minimum: “ما لم ينص عقد التأسيس على نسبة أكبر”. Consequently, neither the 51% partner nor the 49% partner can alone approve an amendment. On the stated holdings, both partners must participate to satisfy either stated threshold.

This is an approval constraint, not evidence that either partner has refused consent. The full registered Arabic articles are needed to confirm reserved matters, voting exclusions and procedures `[review]`.

**Authority:** `companies-law.md`, Arts. 166, 171 and 172, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; target articles, article 14 `[user provided]`.

**Recommendation:** Secure the required partner approvals and signatures to the amended articles. Align signing and completion mechanics with that unanimity requirement `[review]`. Do not substitute JSC voting rules or assume the JSC asset-sale provision in Article 75 governs this LLC.

**Issue 3 — 🟡 Yellow: Third-party transfers require pre-emption procedures and registration `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§2, article 9 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Article 178 governs transfers of LLC interests to non-partners. It requires notification through the manager, including buyer identity and terms, and provides the statutory redemption/payment mechanism within the **30-day** period. The target’s articles also prescribe a same-price offer and 30 days to accept. A bare “acceptance” provision should not replace the full statutory procedure `[review]`.

No offer notices, delivery evidence, waivers or exercise records are supplied. Under Article 25, ownership passes through Commercial Register registration; a signed transfer instrument alone does not establish completed ownership.

**Authority:** `companies-law.md`, Arts. 1, 8, 25 and 178, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; target articles, article 9 `[user provided]`.

**Recommendation:** Obtain legally effective waivers or evidence that the applicable notification, redemption and payment process has been completed `[review]`. Require written amended articles and the updated CR as completion deliverables. No expiry date is calculable without the triggering notification evidence; Companies Law periods are calendar days.

**Issue 4 — 🔴 Red: Management agreement diverts economics and may require conflict authorisation `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1, D§2 and D§6 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Overseas Fleet GmbH owns 49% but receives 95% of net profits as a management fee and controls all operations and bank accounts. Mr. A receives SAR 60,000 annually and has no management role. These arrangements materially affect the earnings and control being acquired.

Article 27 requires authorisation where a manager has a direct or indirect interest in company contracts. Mr. B’s relationship with Overseas Fleet GmbH is not disclosed; common nationality alone proves no such interest. His affiliations, delegated powers and the agreement’s approvals require examination `[review]`.

The payment label does not establish its legal or accounting character. If payments are actually distributions, Articles 22–24 become relevant to distributable profits and permissible allocation. Unequal economics alone do not establish unlawful dividends.

**Authority:** `companies-law.md`, Arts. 22–24, 26–27 and 171, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. Regulations Arts. 16–17 and the LLC-specific application provisions require form-specific review; JSC delegation thresholds should not be applied automatically.

**Recommendation:** Obtain the complete agreement, approval minutes, manager affiliations, invoices, bank mandates and payment history. Agree termination or replacement, accrued-fee treatment and release of operational powers before closing; quantify any continuing leakage and consider specific contractual protection `[review]`. Cross-reference Issue 10.

**Issue 5 — 🟡 Yellow: Beneficial ownership and control cannot be reconciled `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§6; no target UBO register or filing supplied `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** On the disclosed ownership alone:

- **Mr. A → Target: 51% direct ownership**, meeting the 25% first-tier test `[computed from user-supplied cap table]`.
- **Natural person → Overseas Fleet GmbH → Target:** multiply that person’s holding through the upstream chain by 49%, then aggregate all other paths. Upstream percentages are missing; no indirect percentage can be calculated.
- Overseas Fleet GmbH is a legal person and cannot itself be the ultimate beneficial owner.

The management arrangement creates doubt whether registered ownership identifies ultimate effective control. Apply the second-tier control test to the natural persons directing operations, bank powers and profit entitlement. Do not identify Mr. B automatically merely because he is the manager `[review]`.

**Authority and method:** `ultimate-beneficial-ownership-rules.md`, Rules Arts. 4(1)–(3), `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — MoC]`; [MoC UBO Guidance, version 2](https://mc.gov.sa/ar/guides/Documents/BOG.pdf).

**Recommendation:** Obtain the full ownership chain, nominee declarations, target UBO register, initial filing, annual confirmation and subsequent updates. Reconcile them with MISA and bank records; prepare any transaction-driven update within the applicable **15-day** period. The filing deadline is from the file’s **Change notification / guide §2.4** row, rather than an invented Rules article number. Missing evidence establishes an open diligence item, not proven non-filing.

**Contracts**

**Issue 6 — 🔴 Red: Three material customers can terminate on change of control `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§8, all three customer contracts `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Each customer may terminate on written notice following a change in supplier control. On the assumed acquisition of control, all three rights are potentially triggered `[review]`.

A share sale ordinarily leaves the target as the contracting party; the termination exposure here comes from the express clauses. These are termination rights, not an express prohibition on completing the acquisition. The extracts contain no control definition, waiver, cure period or relevant exception.

**Authority:** `civil-transactions-law.md`, Art. 98 and the share-sale application in its practical-effect row, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; termination clauses `[user provided]`. [BOE Civil Transactions Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/655fdb42-8c96-422b-b8c4-b04f0095c94c/1).

**Recommendation:** Obtain each complete agreement and a written transaction-specific waiver or amendment covering the termination right. Identify the three customers separately in the closing checklist; the extract does not identify which customer has the affiliate-assignment clause. Consider the revenue-retention result in valuation and closing conditions `[review]`.

**Issue 7 — 🟡 Yellow: An asset structure would introduce assignment requirements `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§8 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** One agreement permits assignment to affiliates without consent; two are silent. Article 255 permits transfer of contractual position with counterparty consent, including advance consent identifying the contract and effective notification.

Silence is not advance consent. The affiliate clause may assist only if the proposed transferee satisfies its definition and the clause covers the intended transfer `[review]`. It does not waive the separate change-of-control termination right.

Article 256 provides prospective release when the remaining party approves the transfer; without approval, joint liability may remain, subject to the agreement and circumstances. This is more specific than assuming the assignor always remains liable until a separately titled release is signed. Article 252 also requires review of third-party security when debt is transferred.

**Authority:** `civil-transactions-law.md`, Arts. 252 and 255–256, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`.

**Recommendation:** If the structure remains a share purchase, record that contracts remain with the target. If assets are transferred, obtain consents or establish valid advance consent and notice, and document release and replacement security where needed `[review]`.

For the 2022 distribution and 2023 management agreements, assess the Civil Transactions Law’s transitional exceptions before relying on its application to historic facts: **Royal Decree M/191, paragraph 5**, in the same file, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`.

**Employment**

**Issue 8 — 🟡 Yellow: Low Green constrains recruitment and occupation changes `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§5, August 2026 Qiwa summary `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The reported band is **Low Green**. The HRSD services table restricts new visa applications and occupation changes while allowing existing work-permit renewals, subject to service conditions. The current 2026 guide confirms that distinction. This can affect staffing and growth, but no actual recruitment refusal or expired permit is evidenced.

Nine Saudis among 42 workers produce a simple headcount ratio of **21.43%** `[computed from user-supplied headcounts: 9 ÷ 42 × 100]`. That calculation is **not** the official weighted Nitaqat rate and does not establish the next-band hiring requirement.

**Authority:** `saudization-nitaqat.md`, **Services by Range** row, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — HRSD]`; `labor-law.md`, Art. 33, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. [HRSD 2026 Nitaqat guide, services table](https://www.hrsd.gov.sa/sites/default/files/2026-01/aldlyl-alajray-y-ntaqat-almtwr-alnskht-2026.pdf).

**Recommendation:** Obtain a current dated Qiwa export showing activity classification, official rate, band, service restrictions and Saudis required for the next band. Reconcile named workers to payroll and GOSI, inspect work permits and occupations, and cost the staffing plan `[review]`.

**Issue 9 — 🟡 Yellow: Two Saudi employment contracts lack Qiwa documentation `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§5 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Two Saudi employees’ contracts are not documented on Qiwa. Article 51 requires documentation; the implementing regulation specifies the approved electronic platform. The employment relationships and workers’ rights do not disappear because documentation is missing.

The repository’s separate claim that documentation became a condition of Nitaqat credit on **15 April 2026** is tagged `[model knowledge — verify]`. That proposition was independently confirmed in HRSD’s announcement this session, `[authority — HRSD]`. The two workers therefore require a credit reconciliation; their exclusion must not be used to invent a revised band. [HRSD documentation and Nitaqat announcement](https://www.hrsd.gov.sa/media-center/news/توثيق-عقود-العمل-إلكترونياً).

HRSD also announced an establishment documentation target of 90% by 30 June 2026. The extract does not establish the documentation status of all other workers, so it does not prove failure of that separate overall metric. [HRSD documentation thresholds](https://www.hrsd.gov.sa/media-center/news/ضوابط-الالتزام-بتوثيق-عقود-العمل-عبر-منصة-قوى), `[authority — HRSD]`.

**Authority:** `platform-obligations.md`, Labor Law Art. 51 and Regulation Art. 18(2); Art. 51 `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; Regulation row `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04]`. Labor Law Art. 229 supplies the general sanction framework, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; no actual fine is assessed here.

**Recommendation:** Complete documentation with both workers, obtain the accepted contract records and refresh Qiwa’s compliance and Nitaqat outputs before closing `[review]`.

**Regulatory**

**Issue 10 — 🔴 Red: Strong concealment indicators require an element-by-element assessment `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1, D§3 and D§6 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The combination of foreign control of all operations and bank accounts, a 95% net-profit fee, and a fixed payment to the Saudi majority partner is a significant **tasattur** indicator `[review]`.

Articles 2–3 require, among other elements, an economic activity the non-Saudi is not authorised to practise and operation for that person’s own account through an enabler. Foreign ownership or a foreign manager alone is insufficient. The activity mismatch and incomplete registration evidence in Issue 11 make the authorisation element material.

Article 4(a)–(b) separately addresses irregular instruments giving absolute control. Article 4(c), concerning accounts not belonging to the establishment, is **not established**: controlling the company’s accounts is different from using someone else’s account.

If the requisite elements are established, Article 15 provides: “يعد باطلًا كل عقد أو تصرف يكون محله أو غايته التستر”, subject to good-faith third-party rights. Article 9 permits imprisonment up to five years and/or a fine up to SAR 5 million; Article 12 includes enterprise and registration consequences following the specified conviction; Article 14 provides a separate administrative sanction regime for Article 4 violations.

**Authority:** `anti-concealment-law.md`, Arts. 2–4, 9, 12, 14 and 15, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; its Investment Law interplay row, Arts. 7–8. [BOE Anti-Concealment Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/bf9e0aae-6df6-4785-a305-ac2300bd0856/1).

**Recommendation:** Obtain Saudi counsel’s assessment using the full management agreement, historical authorisations, actual business activities, beneficial-owner chain, bank mandates, account ownership and payment evidence. Agree a lawful operating structure and treatment of historical exposure before closing `[review]`. Contract termination alone should not be assumed to eliminate historical liability.

**Issue 11 — 🔴 Red: Investment activity mismatch and missing current registration evidence `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§3 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The CR lists IT consulting and fleet-management services; the supplied MISA certificate covers only IT consulting. Hardware distribution is also evidenced elsewhere. The certificate was issued in August 2024 and renewed in August 2025; no 2026 update or current register status is supplied.

Assuming the renewal retained the stated one-year term, its next anniversary was **1 August 2026** `[review] [computed from user-supplied certificate dates: 2025-08-01 + one year]`. This does not establish automatic cancellation: obtain the current national-register record, transition treatment, annual-update notices and any grace period.

The skill routes this issue for **serious-violation review**. However, Regulation Article 30 does not expressly classify every clerical activity mismatch or missing annual-update document as a serious violation. Its categories include investment without registration and specified unauthorised restricted/excluded-activity conduct. The correct classification depends on the actual registered scope and activities `[review]`.

**Authority:** `investment-law.md`, Arts. 7(2), 8(3) and 11, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`; Regulations Arts. 11, 13, 20 and 30, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [authority — MISA]`. [BOE Investment Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/eda86cc3-3a00-4b90-900d-b1d000c8a863/1); [MISA implementing regulations](https://misa.gov.sa/app/uploads/2025/06/THE-INVESTMENT-LAW-IMPLEMENTING-REGULATIONS.pdf).

**Recommendation:** Require current registration evidence and correction or authorisation of the actual activities. Obtain the current excluded-activities list; the repository’s list contents remain `[model knowledge — verify]` and are not used to classify fleet management or distribution. Determine whether restricted-activity ownership approval and the buyer’s own registration are required `[review]`.

**Issue 12 — 🔴 Red: SAR 210,000 GOSI arrears affect liabilities and completion logistics `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§4, August 2026 statement `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Three months’ contributions remain unpaid, totalling **SAR 210,000 including late-payment charges**. This is a reported liability, not a newly calculated penalty. No current clearance certificate is supplied.

Article 10 creates a certificate requirement for specified dealings, including CR amendment and recruitment-related applications. Article 11 gives contributions and late-payment fines preferential ranking immediately after wages. These provisions make payment and certificate status relevant to completion.

Article 59 addresses penalties, ghost registration and a five-year fine look-back. There is no evidence of ghost workers, and 42 registered workers is not a proven affected-person count for multiplying penalties.

**Authority:** `social-insurance-law.md`, Arts. 10–11 and 59, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. Its Royal Decree M/273 transitional rows preserve the legacy regime for specified contributors; reconcile applicable cohorts before calculating contribution or penalty exposure `[review]`. [BOE Social Insurance Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/eaee8a20-3a54-4aaf-b0d9-b1ad00998962/1).

**Recommendation:** Obtain a current itemised account, reconcile worker records, settle or document an accepted resolution, and obtain the certificate needed for completion `[review]`. Reflect the liability in agreed debt/working-capital treatment without double counting charges already included.

**Issue 13 — 🔴 Red: Unregistered distribution and unresolved eligibility `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§7 `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** Target is EuroTrack S.A.’s exclusive Saudi distributor of telematics hardware. Registration is expressly absent. Added Article 1 extends the Commercial Agencies Law to distributors; Article 3 requires registration.

Article 1’s unamended statutory text requires wholly Saudi capital for Saudi commercial-agency companies. Target’s 49% foreign ownership conflicts with that requirement. The repository mentions possible administrative relaxation, but tags it `[model knowledge — verify]`; no applicable exception or approval is evidenced `[review]`.

Amended Article 4 provides a SAR 5,000–50,000 fine and additional consequences for specified foreign involvement, including administrative liquidation of the relevant business. The live amendment was checked; the original SAR 1,000–5,000 range is superseded.

Non-registration is a regulatory finding against the distributor. It does not justify declaring the contract void. The file’s proposition concerning enforceability between the parties remains `[model knowledge — verify]`.

Added Article 2 also imposes maintenance, spare-parts and quality obligations, including the post-term period ending at one year or appointment of a new agent, whichever is earlier. Allocation of those obligations is absent from the extract.

**Authority:** `commercial-agencies-law.md`, Arts. 1, 3 and amended 4; Added Arts. 1–2 under Royal Decree M/32, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`. [BOE Commercial Agencies Law and amendment blocks](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/b19a8aa6-7b50-43f0-ab8c-a9a700f1a446/1).

**Recommendation:** Resolve eligibility with Saudi counsel and MoC before treating registration as a routine cure. Obtain the full current-term agreement, registration route or applicable exception, product coverage, termination terms and after-sales allocation `[review]`. Any restructuring must also be screened against Issue 10.

**Issue 14 — 🟡 Yellow: Merger-control filing position remains undetermined `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§8 and D§9; no audited buyer-group or target revenue figures `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The required acquisition threshold analysis cannot be completed:

| Test | Threshold | Available result |
|---|---|---|
| Combined worldwide sales | Exceed SAR 200 million | Unknown |
| Target worldwide sales | Exceed SAR 40 million | Unknown |
| Combined Saudi sales | Exceed SAR 40 million | Unknown; target Saudi activity is evidenced but not quantified |

The tests are cumulative. Capital, losses and the three contracts’ minimum values cannot substitute for audited revenue. No test is demonstrated to fail, so a no-filing conclusion is unavailable.

**Authority:** `competition-law.md`, Art. 7, Regulation Art. 12 and Guidelines §6, `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic] [authority — GAC]`. [GAC Economic Concentration Review Guidelines, version 5](https://gacbep.gac.gov.sa/cms/b9376edc-79a1-4573-a36d-4f3effaba838.pdf).

**Recommendation:** Obtain the relevant audited buyer-group and target revenue schedules and complete a written filing analysis. If notification is required, incorporate the statutory notification and completion restrictions into the timetable. Articles 11 and 19 govern premature completion exposure; no completed transaction or gun-jumping is evidenced. `[settled — last confirmed 2026-09-04] [BOE — Arabic]`.

Also screen the exclusive distribution agreement for restrictive pricing, market allocation and foreclosure under Article 5, and market power under Article 6 / Regulation Article 10. Exclusivity alone does not establish a violation, and the 49% equity holding is not a market share. [BOE Competition Law](https://laws.boe.gov.sa/BoeLaws/Laws/LawDetails/e3605c0d-ef87-4cff-b5da-aa3f0102bbb4/1).

**Issue 15 — 🟡 Yellow: Tax standing and cross-border management-fee treatment are untested `[ksa]`**

**Documents:** D§1 and D§6; no target ZATCA materials `[user provided]`.

**Finding:** The mixed ownership and management-fee arrangement require review of the target’s zakat/income-tax position, withholding on actual non-resident payments, related-party pricing, VAT and e-invoicing standing. The record does not establish payments made, withholding, deductibility, treaty entitlement or arrears.

**Authority:** `filing-calendar.md`, **ZATCA zakat/income-tax, withholding-tax, transfer-pricing, VAT and e-invoicing rows**, all `[model knowledge — verify]`. The withholding row identifies Income Tax Law Art. 68 as an **unretrieved authority**; no substantive conclusion is drawn from that article here. The other rows do not supply verified article-level authority.

**Recommendation:** **Confirm with the accountant** using returns, payment receipts, assessments, certificates, invoices, bank records and related-party documentation. Quantify any exposure before agreeing tax indemnities, price treatment or clearance deliverables `[review]`. No tax rate, filing deadline or liability amount is assumed.

